-- main.lua — THE LOADER (single-file product). You run this one script; it figures
-- out what game state is on screen and runs ONLY the matching reader, switching
-- automatically as you play. This is the in-progress form of dist/pokemon-access.lua
-- (accessibility-docs/09).
--
-- WHY ONE FILE: BizHawk's Lua `require`/`dofile` path handling is fragile
-- (QUESTIONS-round-2 Q2; confirmed by Ola round-3 Q1), so everything lives here. Each
-- game state is a MODULE in the MODULES table; only the active module's update() runs
-- per frame. Inactive modules are dormant -> nothing speaks out of context.
--
-- SEAMLESS RULE (Ola, round-3 Q2): the tool speaks ONLY game content (dialogue, menu
-- options, ...). It does NOT announce its own state ("you are in a menu"), and readers
-- do NOT say "loaded/running". The single startup line is the one allowed exception.
-- Context transitions are written to the Lua console only (silent) as a dev trail.
--
-- HOW TO RUN: load Pokémon White (or Black) in our EmuHawk, Lua Console -> Open Script
-- -> this file. It speaks one startup line, then stays quiet until there's game content
-- to read for whatever screen you're on.
--
-- ADDING A READER: write a module { name, detect, update, on_enter?, on_exit? } and add
-- it to MODULES in priority order. Keep each module in its own scope — and read the next
-- paragraph before you pick which kind of scope, because it is not free.
--
-- ⛔ THE 200-LOCALS CEILING, AND HOW TO KEEP ADDING FEATURES ANYWAY (measured 2026-09-01,
-- after the Pokédex module hit it: "too many local variables (limit is 200) in main
-- function"). Lua allows 200 locals live at once PER FUNCTION, and the whole file is one
-- function — the main chunk. This file already declares ~145 locals at top level, which
-- live for the entire chunk, so everything else shares the ~55 that are left.
--   * A `do ... end` block's locals are spent from THAT shared 55. Fine for a small module,
--     and that is why most modules below are `do` blocks.
--   * `;(function() ... end)()` gives the module its own private 200 instead. It is
--     otherwise identical — the file-level names it uses simply become upvalues, which the
--     compiler resolves and luacheck's undeclared-global scan verifies — and it changes
--     behaviour only if the block has a top-level `return`, a `goto` crossing the boundary,
--     or uses `...`, none of which a module does.
--   MEASURED: with the Pokédex module as a plain `do` block the file had 33 locals of
--   headroom left; wrapping just that one module took it to 154. So the ceiling is not a
--   wall — convert the biggest block when you next need room, and each converted module
--   stops competing for the shared budget entirely.
--   The other lever, worth taking anyway: keep baked string tables as ONE table rather than
--   twenty locals (see ZK / ZS / ZA in the Pokédex module).
--
-- GATING STATUS (honest):
--   * overworld (map + coords) — gated on player-control + a loaded map.
--   * overworld dialogue       — gated on the engine's live print pointer; safely silent
--                                if the heap text buffer relocates.
--   * title menu               — gated on the title proc vtable + interactive state (RE'd, works).
--   * gender select            — gated on the gender proc vtable (RE'd); speaks Boy/Girl as you move.
--   * name entry (intro keyboard) — gated on the ov88 intro proc at EventState 3 + the ov194 keyboard
--                                child proc (RE'd, doc 12). Speaks the key under the cursor + typed name.
--                                LAYOUT partial: UPPER letters mapped; other keys say "row R, column C".
--   * the SAVE-PRESENT main menu has NO gate yet -> stays silent (logged to the console only).

------------------------------------------------------------------ CONFIG -----
local INSTANT_TEXT = true      -- romhack-style: zero the text printer's draw delay and max its
                               -- chars-per-frame so each page renders (and is spoken) at once. Writes
                               -- to the printer's speed bytes; set false if dialogue renders glitchy.

-- Hotkeys. Only R C U L N are verified safe on Ola's binds (CLAUDE.md gotcha:
-- F=Frame-Advance, P=Load-State, S=a game button all collide). Module-local keys
-- (like overworld "C" for coordinates) are handled inside the module's update().
local KEY_STOP    = "R"   -- stop current speech
local KEY_REPEAT  = "U"   -- repeat the last spoken game text

--------------------------------------------------------- CONTROLLER MOD LAYER -
-- Ola's design (2026-08-04): HOLD THE LEFT TRIGGER to turn the pad into an accessibility layer. While it
-- is held the game receives NO input at all, and the pad drives whatever list is currently live (the
-- overworld nav list out in the field, the battle HP list in a battle, anything we add later).
--
-- WHY THIS WORKS (verified in BizHawk's own source, not assumed — the DS has no L2 to bind the trigger to):
--   * READ:  input.get() returns RAW HOST inputs by name, filled in InputManager.ProcessInput BEFORE any
--            binding or hotkey lookup (HostInputCoalescer.Receive, InputManager.cs:173 -> InputApi.cs:21).
--            So an unbound trigger still reaches Lua. Names come from SDL2Gamepad.cs: an SDL-recognised
--            game controller gets the prefix "X1 " (Ola's pad does — confirmed live with pad-probe.lua),
--            a generic joystick would get "J1 " with B1/POV0U-style names instead.
--            The trigger is DIGITISED for us at 32768/6.5 (~15% pull, SDL2Gamepad.cs:179) -> a plain on/off
--            modifier, no analog handling needed.
--   * BLOCK: joypad.set{X=false} writes a hard override (JoypadApi.cs:69) that the controller chain applies
--            AFTER the physical latch (InputManager.cs:271, latch at :240), so the core never sees the press.
--            Overrides are wiped every frame (MainForm.cs:2944) and this script's loop runs after that wipe
--            (UpdateToolsBefore, :2952) and before the core reads input (FrameAdvance, :3017) -> re-asserting
--            the block each frame from our own loop lands on exactly the right frame.
--
-- ⛔ THE FACE-BUTTON NAMES ARE THE XBOX/SDL LAYOUT, NOT THE DS LAYOUT. This is the "two small ID spaces are
-- not the same ID space" trap in pad form: SDL normalises every pad to the 360 layout, so its Y is the TOP
-- face button and its X is the LEFT one — the exact opposite of the DS, where X is on top. Ola asked for the
-- TOP face button (called X on a DS), so the host name we want is "X1 Y". Never map these by letter.
local PAD         = "X1 "                    -- host-input prefix for Ola's pad (see above)
local PAD_MOD     = PAD .. "LeftTrigger"     -- the modifier: hold it to enter the accessibility layer
local PAD_MOD2    = PAD .. "RightTrigger"    -- RESERVED (Ola, Q3) for a second layer — battles. Unused today:
                                             -- nothing is bound to it and it blocks nothing, so it stays free
                                             -- until we give it actions.
-- Pad button -> the virtual key the readers already listen for. Mapping to the EXISTING letters is what
-- keeps this modular: every reader (overworld nav, battle HP, and whatever comes next) is untouched and
-- automatically gains controller support, and the keyboard keys keep working exactly as before.
-- One table, one entry per LAYER (the file is at Lua's 200-local cap, so the second layer cannot have a
-- local of its own — and keeping them together makes the two maps easy to compare anyway).
local PAD_ACTIONS = {
	LT = {                            -- LEFT trigger: the general accessibility layer
		[PAD .. "DpadLeft"]  = "I",   -- previous category  (battle: your side)
		[PAD .. "DpadRight"] = "O",   -- next category      (battle: enemy side)
		[PAD .. "DpadUp"]    = "J",   -- previous item
		[PAD .. "DpadDown"]  = "L",   -- next item
		[PAD .. "LeftThumb"] = "P",   -- pathfind to the selected item, press again to stop (L3 = left stick click)
		[PAD .. "Y"]         = "C",   -- place name + coordinates      (SDL Y = the TOP face button, DS "X")
	},
	-- RIGHT trigger: screens we drive with SYNTHETIC TOUCH (Ola, 2026-08-19). Same letters as the left
	-- layer on purpose — up and down step a list, left and right pick between choices everywhere in this
	-- tool, so the Musical inherits the habit — plus one new letter for "do it".
	-- ⚠ THE FACE-BUTTON NAMES ARE THE XBOX/SDL LAYOUT, NOT THE DS ONE (the same trap as the note above):
	-- Ola asked for the DS's A, the RIGHT-hand face button, and SDL calls that one B.
	RT = {
		[PAD .. "DpadUp"]    = "J",   -- previous prop
		[PAD .. "DpadDown"]  = "L",   -- next prop
		[PAD .. "DpadLeft"]  = "I",   -- previous action
		[PAD .. "DpadRight"] = "O",   -- next action
		[PAD .. "B"]         = "E",   -- confirm  (SDL B = the RIGHT face button = the DS's A)
	},
}
-- Which DS buttons we hold shut while the layer is active: Ola's answer to Q6 was BLOCK EVERYTHING, so we
-- ask the CORE for its full button list rather than typing one out (see pad_buttons in pad_step). A typed
-- list was the first attempt and the codex-review caught it: it left Touch and Microphone open on the
-- reasoning that they "are not pad-driven" — which is a binding assumption, and this layer is deliberately
-- binding-agnostic everywhere else. Deriving the list keeps "everything" literally true and cannot drift.

-------------------------------------------------------------- CORE HELPERS ---
local MM = 0x02000000                 -- RAM base (both games). Reads are RAW; the Black -0x20 lives on the
                                      -- FIXED addresses via DELTA (see below), NOT on the base — because a
                                      -- pointer VALUE read from Black RAM is already Black-native (shifting it
                                      -- again double-subtracts; that was the "only options worked" bug).
local DOMAIN = 0x400000               -- melonDS "Main RAM" size (4 MiB).
-- ⛔ NIL-TOLERANT ON PURPOSE (2026-09-01). A nil address is, self-evidently, not a RAM address, and
-- answering false is the fail-closed answer every caller already expects. Before this, one nil reaching
-- here raised a Lua error, and a Lua error does not silence one reader — it kills the SCRIPT, so a blind
-- player loses every bit of speech mid-session with no way back except reloading. That is the worst
-- failure this tool has, and it is not worth risking to catch a caller's bug half a second earlier.
-- (The real bug is still fixed at its source; this is the net under it.)
local function inram(a) return a ~= nil and a >= 0x02000000 and a < 0x02400000 end
-- inrange guards the domain OFFSET: once Black shifts the base, a stray/garbage pointer can compute an
-- out-of-domain offset; returning 0 instead of letting mainmemory throw keeps the frame loop alive. It
-- never triggers on White (every live read is already in-domain) -> White behaviour is byte-for-byte identical.
local function inrange(o) return o >= 0 and o < DOMAIN end
local function u8(a)  local o = a - MM; return inrange(o) and mainmemory.read_u8(o) or 0 end
local function u16(a) local o = a - MM; return inrange(o) and mainmemory.read_u16_le(o) or 0 end
local function u32(a) local o = a - MM; return inrange(o) and mainmemory.read_u32_le(o) or 0 end
local function w8(a, v) local o = a - MM; if inrange(o) then mainmemory.write_u8(o, v) end end

------------------------------------------------- GAME DETECT + BLACK DELTAS --
-- Detect White (IRAO) vs Black (IRBO) from the ROM header and set the per-CLASS address deltas that port
-- White's absolute addresses to Black. THE RULE (CLAUDE.md "PORTABILITY = OFFSETS, NOT ADDRESSES"): struct
-- OFFSETS port unchanged; only absolute ROOTS move. Reads are RAW; a delta is added to FIXED absolute
-- addresses ONLY (RAM work-area vars, ARM9 globals, overlay code/vtables). Pointer VALUES read out of RAM
-- are already Black-native, so derefs after a root stay RAW. Every delta is 0 on White -> White is
-- byte-for-byte identical. MEASURED offline vs both ROMs (research/re/black_*.py; byte/fn-ptr matched):
--   DELTA    = -0x20 : DATA + ARM9 globals (0x0214xxxx) + MOST overlays (ov88 has this base shift too, but
--                      see D_OV88VT; ov93/94 battle, ov194 name, ov197 bag all shift a clean -0x20).
--   D_OV204  = -0x40 : overlays ov89/203/204 (the intro-cutscene overlay) load 0x40 lower. Verified clean
--                      (intro_graphic fn-ptrs + gender handler literals all shift exactly -0x40).
--   D_OV88VT = -0x54 : ov88's title/gender/intro VTABLES specifically. ov88 differs HEAVILY between games
--                      (Reshiram vs Zekrom intro; 89% of bytes differ, file even a different size), so its
--                      vtable block relocated -0x54 (NOT the -0x20 base shift). All three vtables were
--                      located by matching their {Init,MainLoop,End} fn-pointer spacing (unique).
--   ARM9 CORE CODE (RenderText / Text_AddPrinter / String_*) shifts -0x1c / -0x18 and is byte-matched at
--   each use (RENDERTEXT / TF* below), NOT by these deltas.
local function detect_game()
	local c = {}
	for i = 0, 3 do c[i + 1] = string.char(memory.read_u8(0x0C + i, "ROM")) end
	local code = table.concat(c)
	if code == "IRAO" then return "white" end
	if code == "IRBO" then return "black" end
	local ok, name = pcall(function() return gameinfo.getromname() end)
	if ok and name then
		local n = string.lower(name)
		if n:find("white", 1, true) then return "white" end
		if n:find("black", 1, true) then return "black" end
	end
	return "unknown"
end
local GAME     = detect_game()
local DELTA    = (GAME == "black") and -0x20 or 0   -- data / ARM9 globals / most overlays
local D_OV204  = (GAME == "black") and -0x40 or 0   -- ov89/203/204 (intro cutscene overlay)
local D_OV88VT = (GAME == "black") and -0x54 or 0   -- ov88 title/gender vtable block (non-uniform; measured)

-- Live player (trainer) name via the RE'd save chain (see player-name-read memory): GameData (the no-scan
-- fixed chain below) -> SaveControl(gd+0) -> blockMgr(+0x10) -> live save block (u32(blockMgr+0x34)) + 0x19404
-- -> trainer profile; name = u16[8] @ +0 (ASCII charcodes, 0xFFFF-terminated). NO scanning at all: GameData
-- comes from a fixed global pointer chain (find_gd below); the whole path is pointer derefs. Reusable for the
-- trainer card / party OT / battle text later.
local PLAYER_PROFILE_OFF = 0x19404
local function read_name(p)
	if not inram(p) then return nil end
	local out = {}
	for i = 0, 7 do
		local c = u16(p + i * 2)
		if c == 0xFFFF or c == 0 then break end
		if c == 0xE9 then out[#out+1] = "e"
		elseif c >= 0x20 and c < 0x7F then out[#out+1] = string.char(c)
		else return nil end
	end
	return (#out >= 1) and table.concat(out) or nil
end
-- GameData via a FIXED global pointer chain — NO heap scan (RE'd 2026-06-29, Ghidra). The main game proc
-- (init FUN_02011d9c) stores the GameSystem (a 0x34-byte struct) into the fixed ARM9 global 0x02146268
-- (`*0x02146268 = GameSystem`; main.lua's overworld gate already trusts u32(0x02146268 + DELTA)), and GameData_New's
-- result is stored at GameSystem+0x1C (ctor FUN_02011f28: `GameSystem[+0x1C] = GameData_New()`; read back at
-- FUN_02011fb8 as `*(GameSystem+0x1C)`). So GameData = u32( u32(0x02146268 + DELTA) + 0x1C ) — one global, two
-- derefs, zero scanning. Validated by GameData's own "game_data.c" allocator tag at gd-0x18 (the exact
-- signature the legacy scan matched) plus SaveControl(u32(gd)) being a heap ptr; cached + cheaply
-- re-validated. The old one-time heap tag scan is kept ONLY as a self-healing last resort (must never run in
-- practice). Black: 0x02146268 -> 0x02146248 (a clean -0x20, byte-matched in research/re/black_globals.py),
-- applied via + DELTA; the +0x1C / -0x18 struct offsets are the same engine.
local GAMESYS_PTR = 0x02146268 + DELTA -- fixed ARM9 global; u32 here = GameSystem ptr (Black 0x02146248)
-- MOVED UP (2026-08-19): the cross-module registry has to exist before pad_step, which now asks it
-- whether a touch-driven screen is up before translating the right-trigger layer.
-- ── THE MANAGER REGISTRY (dialogue discovery, registry-ONLY in field play — Ola's "switch completely",
-- 2026-07-23 late) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Every field-context dialogue printer lives on one of the game's own task managers, reached by FIXED
-- chains (Codex registry RE, Claude-validated against canonical decompiles). While a Field exists the
-- registry is AUTHORITATIVE: no scan, no fallback — registry empty means "no box", full stop. Chains
-- (one absolute root + struct offsets; the root and the two FieldTask callback addresses carry DELTA):
--   field msgbg mgr (NPC/sign/script) : u32(u32(u32(u32(GAMESYS_PTR)+0x20)+0x28)+0x164)
--     (FUN_021881F8 stores FUN_0219020C's msgbg at Field+0x28; the mgr sits at msgbg+0x164, cap 1/4;
--      print path FUN_02190CB4. msgbg identity re-proven on change via its "field_msgbg.c" alloc tag.)
--   ctalk mgr (comm talk windows)     : u32(u32(msgbg+0x15C)+0xC98) — optional child, usually absent
--   shop mgr (Poké Mart windows)      : GS task cb SHOP_CB -> env -> u32(env) scriptWork ->
--                                       +0x3C shop [tag "scrcmd_shop.c"] -> mgr u32(shop+0x1B4)
--   report mgr (save prompts)         : GS task cb REPORT_CB -> env -> u32(env+0x18) -> mgr u32(+0)
-- ✅ BLACK VERIFIED (offline byte-match 2026-07-28): both callbacks are ov010 code addresses at the
-- standard -0x20 (SHOP_CB 0x021588DD, REPORT_CB 0x021616F1) — the White literal sits at the SAME file
-- offset in Black's ov010 holding exactly that value, and both function bodies are byte-identical
-- (94/96 and 95/96 bytes; the diffs are relocated branch/literal words). No assumption left here.
local reg = { SHOP_CB = 0x021588FD + DELTA, REPORT_CB = 0x02161711 + DELTA }   -- one local (200-cap)

local function gd_ok(gd)               -- cheap, scan-free validation: the "game_data.c" alloc tag + SaveControl
	return gd and inram(gd) and inram(u32(gd))
	   and u8(gd - 0x18) == 0x67 and u8(gd - 0x17) == 0x61   -- 'g','a' ...
	   and u8(gd - 0x16) == 0x6D and u8(gd - 0x15) == 0x65   -- ... 'm','e' of "game_data.c"
end
local gd_cache
local function find_gd()
	if gd_ok(gd_cache) then return gd_cache end
	local gs = u32(GAMESYS_PTR)                                 -- PRIMARY (no scan): fixed pointer chain.
	if inram(gs) then
		local gd = u32(gs + 0x1C)                              -- GameData = GameSystem + 0x1C
		if gd_ok(gd) then gd_cache = gd; return gd end
	end
	-- LAST-RESORT self-heal (should never run): the original one-time "game_data.c" heap tag scan.
	local lo, hi, T = 0x02100000, 0x023E0000, "game_data.c"
	local a = lo
	while a < hi do
		local len = math.min(0x40000, hi - a)
		local arr = mainmemory.read_bytes_as_array(a - MM, len + 16)
		local b = (arr[0] ~= nil) and 0 or 1
		for j = 0, len - 1 do
			if arr[b+j] == 0x67 and arr[b+j+1] == 0x61 then       -- 'g','a'
				local ok = true
				for k = 1, #T do if arr[b+j+k-1] ~= T:byte(k) then ok = false; break end end
				if ok then local gd = a + j + 0x18; if inram(u32(gd)) then gd_cache = gd; return gd end end
			end
		end
		a = a + len
	end
end
local function player_name()
	local gd = find_gd(); if not gd then return nil end
	local sc = u32(gd); if not inram(sc) then return nil end
	local mgr = u32(sc + 0x10); if not inram(mgr) then return nil end
	local blk = u32(mgr + 0x34); if not inram(blk) then return nil end
	return read_name(blk + PLAYER_PROFILE_OFF)
end

local last_said = ""
-- say(text[, interrupt]). interrupt omitted/true = cut current speech and speak now (default — used
-- while NAVIGATING, so fast cursor moves collapse to the latest). interrupt == false = QUEUE after
-- whatever is speaking (used for the message / edit field / what you LAND on, so they play in order
-- without cutting each other). Mirrors the project's prism queue convention (cf. the Digimon Story:
-- Time Stranger mod's SpeechManager.Speak(text, interrupt)). NOTE (Ola): dialogue stays interrupt-only
-- (a new line just replaces the old); the queue is only needed if an on-screen MESSAGE and a Yes/No
-- POPUP must both be spoken at once — handle that if/when it comes up.
local function say(m, interrupt)
	local s = tostring(m)
	last_said = s
	console.writeline(s)
	if speech and speech.say then
		if interrupt == false then speech.say(s, false) else speech.say(s) end
	end
end
local function stop_speech()
	if speech and speech.stop then speech.stop() end
end
-- Silent dev trail (console only, never spoken) — used for context transitions.
local function dbg(m) console.writeline("[ctx] " .. tostring(m)) end

-- Edge-detected hotkeys: returns { [key]=true } for keys that went DOWN this frame.
-- held_keys is the same poll's UNEDGED state (what is down right now) — the controller mod layer needs it,
-- because a modifier is about being HELD, not about the frame it was pressed.
local prev_keys = {}
local held_keys = {}
local function poll_keys()
	local now = input.get()
	local pressed = {}
	for k in pairs(now) do
		if not prev_keys[k] then pressed[k] = true end
	end
	prev_keys = now
	held_keys = now
	return pressed
end

-- CONTROLLER MOD LAYER (see the config block up top for the design + the BizHawk evidence).
-- Call once per frame BEFORE dispatching to the modules. Two jobs:
--   1. While the trigger is held, translate pad presses into the virtual keys the readers already use.
--   2. Keep the game's own buttons shut, and keep them shut past the release.
-- ⛔ THE RELEASE LATCH (Ola, Q4). Letting go of the trigger while still holding a direction must NOT hand
-- that direction to the game — you would take a step you never asked for. So on release we keep blocking
-- whatever is STILL physically down, until it is genuinely let go. Reading "still physically down" needs
-- care: at the moment our loop runs, the emulator's controller already has LAST frame's overrides baked
-- into it (RunControllerChain applies them at MainForm.cs:1004, and the wipe at :2944 only empties the
-- adapter, not the controller). A naive joypad.getimmediate() therefore reports our own blocked buttons as
-- released and the latch would never engage. joypad.set{} first UnSets every override AND re-latches the
-- controller straight from the physical input (JoypadApi.cs:68/79), so the read that follows is the true
-- state of the pad. Doing it through the emulator like this keeps us binding-AGNOSTIC: we never need to
-- know which pad button Ola has bound to which DS button.
-- ⛔ AND WE ALWAYS OWE ONE CLEARING PASS AFTER THE LAST BLOCKED FRAME (codex-review 2026-08-04, confirmed
-- against the source). An override does NOT expire on its own: RunControllerChain applies whatever is in
-- the adapter (MainForm.cs:1004) BEFORE the wipe at :2944, and the rest of the chain only reads THROUGH to
-- ActiveController — MovieSession.LatchInputToUser is literally `MovieOut.Source = MovieIn`
-- (MovieSession.cs:321), no re-latch anywhere. So a frame on which we call no joypad function at all
-- inherits the PREVIOUS frame's block, and the frame right after you let go of the trigger would eat your
-- input. It only showed up when NOTHING was latched (with something latched we call joypad.set anyway and
-- self-heal), which is the common case of tapping the trigger while touching nothing else. pad_dirty
-- tracks whether we wrote anything last frame and buys back the one clearing call we owe.
local pad_latched = {}     -- DS buttons held shut past the trigger's release, until physically let go
local pad_dirty   = false  -- did we write overrides last frame? (drives the clearing pass described above)
local pad_buttons = nil    -- every bool button the CORE has, asked once (see the Q6 note in the config)
local function pad_step(pressed)
	local mod_held = held_keys[PAD_MOD] and true or false

	if mod_held then
		for host, key in pairs(PAD_ACTIONS.LT) do
			if pressed[host] then pressed[key] = true end
		end
	end
	-- SECOND LAYER — the RIGHT trigger, for screens we drive with synthetic touch (Ola, 2026-08-19). The
	-- Musical's half is deliberately NOT blocking: that screen reads no buttons at all, so there is nothing
	-- to block, and gating it on the screen being up keeps the right trigger free everywhere else.
	-- ⛔ THE FLY MAP BREAKS THAT ASSUMPTION AND SO IT DOES BLOCK (codex-review 2026-08-24, MEDIUM,
	-- confirmed). Unlike the Musical, the town map reads the D-pad for its own free cursor and reads A for
	-- its own confirm — so on a non-blocking layer every RT chord ALSO reached the game: stepping to a place
	-- the cursor already held queued no gesture at all and simply moved the cursor away from the destination
	-- we had just announced, a direction still held when an eight-frame gesture ended came back as a fresh
	-- native press, and a physical A went straight to the app's own commit. Blocking, with the left layer's
	-- release latch, is what keeps RT+D-pad "the list" and the bare D-pad "the cursor" — which is exactly
	-- what Ola asked for. The Musical keeps its non-blocking behaviour, unchanged and already confirmed.
	local rt_fly   = held_keys[PAD_MOD2] and reg.flymap_active   and reg.flymap_active()   and true or false
	local rt_dress = held_keys[PAD_MOD2] and reg.dressup_active  and reg.dressup_active()  and true or false
	if rt_fly or rt_dress then
		for host, key in pairs(PAD_ACTIONS.RT) do
			if pressed[host] then pressed[key] = true end
		end
	end
	local blocking = mod_held or rt_fly

	-- Idle: no blocking layer is held and nothing is still latched. Zero cost in the common case (walking
	-- around), except for the single clearing call owed on the first idle frame after we last blocked.
	if not blocking and next(pad_latched) == nil then
		if pad_dirty then joypad.set({}); pad_dirty = false end
		return
	end

	joypad.set({})                          -- drop our overrides + re-latch from physical (see above)
	local phys = joypad.getimmediate()      -- now the TRUE physical state of every DS button
	if not pad_buttons then                 -- bool entries are buttons, number entries are axes
		local list = {}                     -- (Emulation.Common/Extensions.cs:410-411)
		for k, v in pairs(phys) do if type(v) == "boolean" then list[#list + 1] = k end end
		if #list > 0 then pad_buttons = list end   -- never cache an empty answer (no core = try again)
	end
	local block = {}
	if blocking then
		for _, b in ipairs(pad_buttons or {}) do
			block[b] = false
			pad_latched[b] = phys[b] and true or nil   -- remember what to hold past the release
		end
	else
		for b in pairs(pad_latched) do
			if phys[b] then block[b] = false else pad_latched[b] = nil end
		end
	end
	if next(block) ~= nil then joypad.set(block); pad_dirty = true else pad_dirty = false end
end

-- ⛔ FOUR OF THE GAME'S CHARCODES ARE PRIVATE FONT GLYPHS, NOT UNICODE. Every other non-ASCII code in
-- B/W's text really is its Unicode codepoint — 0x00E9 is the é of "Pokémon", the direction signs use the
-- real ← ↑ → ↓, and ♪ ● ◆ ◇ ■ ★ are themselves — so passing those straight through is correct. These four
-- are not: they index the game's own glyph sheet and happen to land inside Unicode's "parenthesized
-- number" block, which is why the battle intro spoke as "You are challenged by ⒆⒇ Trainer Cheren!"
-- (Ola, 2026-08-20). NVDA was reading ⒆⒇ exactly as written; the charcodes were never translated.
--   0x2486 + 0x2487 = the two-cell POKéMON ligature (the font draws that word as a glyph PAIR, so both
--     halves must map or the word comes out split). Proven in a/0/0/2: bank 9[69] is "PARTY <2486><2487>",
--     and bank 191 (trainer classes) holds "<2486><2487> Trainer" (ids 0,1,37,38,40,100,103,104),
--     "<2486><2487> Breeder" (17,18) and "<2486><2487> Ranger" (24,25) — Cheren is class 0, "some
--     trainers but not all" exactly as Ola described.
--   0x246D = ♂ and 0x246E = ♀. Proven in bank 70 (species): [29] "Nidoran<246E>", [32] "Nidoran<246D>";
--     also bank 191's "Clerk<246D>"/"Clerk<246E>" and "Swimmer<246D>"/"Swimmer<246E>".
-- I surveyed EVERY entry of EVERY bank in a/0/0/2: these four are the only 0x24xx codes the game uses,
-- so the table is complete rather than a sample. Ola's call (2026-08-20): emit the real ♂/♀ signs and let
-- NVDA's own symbol level decide how to voice them, rather than spelling out "male"/"female" for him.
-- It lives in utf8c so ONE fix covers every reader — battle text, dialogue, party, bag, and the default
-- nicknames, where "Nidoran♀" is decoded by this same path.
-- ⚠ Written as a compare chain and NOT as a `local GLYPH = {...}` lookup table, which is what I reached
-- for first: this chunk sits exactly at Lua's 200-locals-per-function ceiling, so one more top-level
-- local fails the whole file to compile ("too many local variables", caught by luacheck.py). The guard is
-- one integer compare that ASCII fails immediately, so the common path costs nothing.
-- BMP codepoint -> UTF-8 (shared by the text/keyboard readers).
local function utf8c(u)
	if u >= 0x246D and u <= 0x2487 then          -- the four private font glyphs (see above)
		if u == 0x2486 then return "Poké"        -- POKéMON, left cell
		elseif u == 0x2487 then return "mon"     -- POKéMON, right cell
		elseif u == 0x246D then return "♂"
		elseif u == 0x246E then return "♀" end
		-- anything else in the span is not a glyph we know: fall through and encode it as itself
	end
	if u < 0x80 then
		return string.char(u)
	elseif u < 0x800 then
		return string.char(0xC0 + math.floor(u / 0x40), 0x80 + (u % 0x40))
	else
		return string.char(0xE0 + math.floor(u / 0x1000),
			0x80 + (math.floor(u / 0x40) % 0x40), 0x80 + (u % 0x40))
	end
end

--------------------------------------------------------------- ADDRESS TABLE -
-- GAME + DELTA + D_OV204 + D_OV88VT are set up top (right after the mem helpers) so they exist before the
-- roots below. Two tables: A = RAM work-area addresses (all a clean -0x20 on Black); C = code/global/overlay
-- roots (per-CLASS deltas — proc_mgr/name are -0x20, the ov88 vtables -0x54, the ov204 handlers -0x40). Every
-- delta is 0 on White, so both tables are byte-identical there. Black addresses verified in research/re/black_*.py.

-- RAM variables — FIXED work-area addresses, so + DELTA (Black -0x20) applies directly.
local A = {
	player_control  = 0x0224F79D + DELTA,  -- u8: 0 = overworld control; 1 = menu/dialogue/etc.
	active_menu_ptr = 0x0224F784 + DELTA,  -- u32: == GameSystem+0x18, the GS TASK-STACK HEAD. 0 in free
	                                       -- roam; non-zero whenever ANY game task (script VM, message box,
	                                       -- save, field app) is live. NOT a pointer to menu data — do not
	                                       -- dereference it as one; reach menus by their own chains.
	map_id          = 0x0224F92C + DELTA,  -- u16: the RAW zone id the field is running. ⛔ NOT a sentinel:
	                                       -- 0 is a real zone (see the version-remap block below).
	zdsys           = 0x02146284 + DELTA,  -- u32 -> ZoneDataSystem (Black 0x02146264). +0x34 = the cached
	                                       -- zone (post-remap); +0x48/+0x49 = the two allow bytes.
	map_x           = 0x0224F932 + DELTA,  -- u16
	map_y           = 0x0224F93A + DELTA,  -- u16
	-- ⛔ kbd_row/kbd_col/name_buf (0x0224FBC0 / 0x0224FBBC / 0x0224FA50) DELETED 2026-09-01. They were
	-- never statics: Ghidra has no xref to any of them, and they are the ov194 naming subsystem's own
	-- +0x1D8 / +0x1D4 / +0x68 at the heap address the INTRO's instance happened to get. The name-entry
	-- module reads those offsets off the live proc now, which is what made the keyboard work outside the
	-- intro. Sitting in the static band is not evidence of being static — the xref is.
}

-- ── THE VERSION-EXCLUSIVE AREA: Black City / White Forest ─────────────────────────────────────────────
-- ⛔ ZONE 0 IS A REAL ZONE, AND IT IS NOT THE SAME PLACE IN THE TWO GAMES. B/W ship ONE set of map data
-- for both versions: in BOTH ROMs zone 0 is Black City (place name 50, script bank 2) and zone 424 is
-- White Forest (place name 51, bank 466). The zonedata, the matrix, the land data and the events are
-- byte-identical between the ROMs — of all 247 files in the ROM only five differ at all, and not one of
-- them is a zone table — and BOTH gates (375 and 379) name zone 0 as their destination unconditionally.
-- The engine fixes that up on the way IN to the data: ZoneData_GetHeaderCore (White 0x02013ae0) begins by
-- calling GetVersionedMapChangeZoneNum (0x02013aa4), which walks a TWO-record {u16 from, u16 to} table at
-- ARM9 0x020A72C8 (Black 0x020A72A8, byte-identical contents) = {0 -> 424} and {294 -> 295}. Record i
-- applies only while the "allow" byte at ZoneDataSystem + 0x48 + i is non-zero.
-- ⭐ THE ENTIRE VERSION DIFFERENCE IS THOSE TWO BYTES. White's InitZoneDataSystem calls
-- SetAllowVersionSpecificZone(1) and SetAllowVersionSpecificArea(1); BLACK'S DOES NOT — byte-compared,
-- Black's Init returns immediately after the same +0x34 store. ov010 toggles them again for the Entralink,
-- where you walk into the OTHER version's area, so we read the live bytes and never key on GAME.
-- ⛔ AND THIS IS WHY IT BIT US: the fixup lives INSIDE the accessor, so the field's own zone id (mappos_id)
-- keeps the RAW 0 while the game draws, names and scripts White Forest. Every table we bake per zone has
-- to be looked up with the EFFECTIVE id, or a player standing in White Forest is read as Black City — wrong
-- name, wrong collision grid, wrong warps, wrong buildings — and `map_id == 0` reads as "no map loaded",
-- which stood the whole overworld reader down in the one city each game has.
-- ⭐ WHY READING THE FLAGS IS ALSO THE SAFE CHOICE, not just the faithful one: these are the SAME two
-- bytes the engine's own zonedata lookups consult. Wherever the game names the place correctly, this
-- agrees with it by construction — if the flags were somehow set on Black, the GAME would be showing
-- White Forest in Black City too. A GAME-keyed guess would have no such backstop.
-- ⭐ AND IT COSTS BLACK NOTHING: the only absolute address here is A.zdsys, an anchor already
-- Black-verified, and the {0->424},{294->295} pair is byte-identical data in both ROMs. The one
-- genuinely per-ROM value in this whole fix is the 112/113 gate constant in zone_name.
-- (On `reg` and not in two file locals: this scope is at Lua's 200-local ceiling. [lua-syntax-check])
function reg.ez(z)                      -- a raw zone id -> the zone the ENGINE resolves it to
	if z ~= 0 and z ~= 294 then return z end   -- the only two ids in the table, so every other zone is free
	local sys = u32(A.zdsys)
	if not inram(sys) then reg.blind_warn(); return z end               -- a GUESS, and it reads as Black City
	if z == 0 then return (u8(sys + 0x48) ~= 0) and 424 or 0 end
	return (u8(sys + 0x49) ~= 0) and 295 or 294
end
-- True when the remap cannot be read AT ALL — so any 0 or 294 we resolve could be the wrong half of its
-- pair. Callers that SPEAK a place, or navigate by one, refuse rather than guess.
-- ⛔ THIS IS DELIBERATELY ABOUT THE RESOLVER, NOT ABOUT THE ZONE WE ARE STANDING IN. The first cut asked
-- "is the CURRENT zone 0 or 294?", and a verification pass caught the hole: the ambiguity belongs to the
-- VALUE, and plenty of ambiguous values are not the current zone. Standing in gate 375 — a perfectly
-- unambiguous zone — the nav list still names its destination through transition_name(0), and
-- reach.place_at resolves whatever id a matrix cell holds. Both would have sailed past a current-zone
-- test and spoken the wrong city with total confidence. Ask whether we can answer, not where we are.
function reg.blind_zone()
	if inram(u32(A.zdsys)) then return false end
	reg.blind_warn(); return true
end
-- ⛔ AND IT SAYS SO, ONCE. The state should be unreachable — ppZONEDATA_SYSTEM is live for as long as a
-- field is — but "should be unreachable" is what this project's worst bugs are made of, and a reader that
-- goes quiet is indistinguishable from a mis-gated one until somebody has burned a test round on it.
-- ⚠ THE WARNING LIVES HERE, NOT IN reg.ez, BECAUSE THE GUARD RUNS FIRST (verification pass, round 2). The
-- first cut logged from reg.ez, which overworld_active never reaches once blind_zone has stood it down —
-- so the stand-down would have been silent in exactly the case it was written for. A diagnostic that only
-- fires when the thing already works is not a diagnostic.
function reg.blind_warn()
	if reg.ez_warned then return end
	reg.ez_warned = true
	dbg("the ZoneDataSystem is unreadable, so Black City and White Forest cannot be told apart — place "
	    .. "names and routing are standing down until it comes back.")
end
function reg.zid() return reg.ez(u16(A.map_id)) end   -- the zone we are actually standing in
-- CODE/GLOBAL/OVERLAY roots — per-CLASS Black deltas (all 0 on White). proc_mgr + the ov194 keyboard
-- shift a clean -0x20 (DELTA); the ov88 title/gender/intro VTABLES shift -0x54 (D_OV88VT — ov88 is heavily
-- rebuilt between games); the ov204 intro-cutscene handlers shift -0x40 (D_OV204). All Black values
-- byte/fn-ptr-verified in research/re/black_overlay_verify.py + black_ov88_battle.py.
local C = {
	proc_mgr_ptr      = 0x021469B0 + DELTA,    -- Black 0x02146990. [here] = g_GameProcManager (heap).
	title_ctrl_vtable = 0x021BD6C0 + D_OV88VT, -- Black 0x021BD66C. title CONTROLLER proc = CurrentProc on title.
	title_vtable      = 0x021BD9C8 + D_OV88VT, -- Black 0x021BD974. list-menu sub-component (NOT the CurrentProc).
	gender_vtable     = 0x021BD86C + D_OV88VT, -- Black 0x021BD818. intro sequence proc (gender + name + Yes/No).
	gender_live_state = 0x021C03C0 + D_OV204,  -- Black 0x021C0380. list obj current-handler (gcur+0x20) when it
	                                -- takes D-pad input (vs 0x021C03A8 idle during Juniper's speech). Our
	                                -- "input is live" gate; verified by [gdiff gcur] +0x20:A8->C0.
	gender_confirm_state = 0x021C0420 + D_OV204, -- Black 0x021C03E0. same gcur+0x20 handler during the "You're
	                                -- a boy, right?" Yes/No confirm (vs 03C0 for the Boy/Girl pick).
	intro_graphic_vtable = 0x021BFD64 + D_OV204, -- Black 0x021BFD24. ov204 intro cutscene child proc (runs the
	                                -- Boy/Girl pick AND every intro Yes/No). Gate intro_navsub on this vtable.
	name_child_vtable = 0x021F55A0 + DELTA,     -- Black 0x021F5580. ov194 name-entry KEYBOARD child proc,
	                                -- launched by the intro proc at EventState 3 via [sub+0x30]. See doc 12.
	mictest_vtable    = 0x021BFB7C + D_OV204,   -- Black 0x021BFB3C. ov203 DEMO3D / Mic Test proc, launched
	                                -- on the GameSystem SUB proc manager (ov010 @0x02161C72 passes overlay
	                                -- 203 + this vtable to LaunchProc FUN_020120B4). Black MEASURED
	                                -- 2026-07-28: its {Init,Main,End} triple sits at the same ov203 file
	                                -- offset with all three pointers -0x40, and that triple is unique.
	-- ── THE ov194 KEYBOARD'S PARENTS + THE POST-CATCH POKÉDEX SCREEN (2026-09-01) ──────────────────
	-- ov194 is a REUSABLE naming app, not an intro screen: eight launch sites in the ROM, each running
	-- it on a DIFFERENT manager (Codex RE dispatch, every site found by an exact search for the ov194
	-- proc template 0x021F55A0 across the ARM9 and all 237 overlays, in BOTH ROMs). The vtables below
	-- are the PARENTS we have to recognise to find its private manager; they are validators, not roots.
	-- Every one is a clean -0x20 on Black and was read out of the Black ROM, never assumed.
	evbattleret_vtable = 0x021B9DB4 + DELTA,  -- Black 0x021B9D94 (measured: all 3 fn-ptrs -0x20). ov092
	                                -- "event_battle_return.c" — the field event that runs after a battle.
	                                -- It owns BOTH the post-catch Pokédex page and the nickname keyboard.
	zukantoroku_vtable = 0x0220BAD0 + DELTA,  -- Black 0x0220BAB0 (measured: all 3 fn-ptrs -0x20). ov207
	                                -- "zukan_toroku_graphic.c"/"zukan_nickname.c" = the Pokédex
	                                -- REGISTRATION screen + the "Give a nickname…" prompt.
	pdcreturn_vtable  = 0x021B98C4 + DELTA,   -- Black 0x021B98A4. ov227 "event_pdc_return.c" — the other
	                                -- parent that runs an ov207 child and then the keyboard.
	boxapp_vtable     = 0x021EF7AC + DELTA,   -- Black 0x021EF78C. ov170 box2 (PC boxes) — Box name entry.
	friendname_vtable = 0x021CA678 + DELTA,   -- Black 0x021CA658. ov128 field app 8 — "Your friend's name?".
	egghatch_vtable   = 0x021F7138 + DELTA,   -- Black 0x021F7118. ov215 egg_demo — the hatchling's nickname.
	-- ── THE POKÉDEX APP AND ITS FOUR SUB-SCREENS (2026-09-01) ─────────────────────────────────────
	-- The Pokédex opened from the X menu. It is FIELD APP 1 in ov010's app registry (base 0x021712EC,
	-- stride 0x18, record {overlayId, procVtable, buildFn, retFn, 0, commonFn}); row 1 is
	-- {212, 0x021C9958, 0x02160D8D, 0x02160DFD} on White and {212, 0x021C9938, ...} on Black.
	-- ⛔ ov208, ov209, ov210 AND ov211 ALL LOAD AT THE SAME BASE (0x021BC260) and are mutually
	-- exclusive — only one is resident at a time. That is why identity here is always the pair
