	-- Ola, 2026-08-26: "this gimmick is all about quick sand warps, fall into them and you get taken down
	-- to the next floor, there are specific ones you need to fall down to continue".
	-- A pit is a 5x5 patch of collision class **0x7C**, the sinking sand. That class exists in EXACTLY
	-- five zones — 160-164 — and NOWHERE else in the game (248 tiles, censused over every land record of
	-- every zone's matrix); ordinary Relic Castle floor is class 0x23. Each patch is covered by one
	-- TRIGGER record whose region is exactly those 25 tiles, and whose script is nothing but
	--     LockAll; **0xC1(destZone, xTile, zTile)**; WaitMoment; ReleaseAll; End
	-- Op 0xC1's handler is ov021 **0x021AFDCC**: three plain u16 operand reads (0x02011330 x3), operands
	-- 2 and 3 become a position (`lsls r7,#0x10` — tile to fx32), operand 1 goes to FUN_02184E60 as the
	-- destination zone, and the whole thing runs through the warp cluster at 0x0216284C with transition
	-- type 9. So a pit IS a warp; it just fires by being stepped on and cannot be walked back up.
	-- ⭐ THE POINT OF THE TABLE: the trigger record gives the REGION but not where it goes — the
	-- destination only exists inside the script — so the destination must be baked. The region is baked
	-- with it so build() can list the pit before pf_tile exists (the same constraint the warp code has).
	-- Generator + full evidence: research/re/gen_relic_pits.py (re-run it to regenerate this table).
	-- ⚠ WHITE AND BLACK ARE BYTE-IDENTICAL HERE — zonedata, entity file, script, matrix and land records
	-- for all of 160-165 (research/re/rc_black_check.py). Measured, not assumed: no DELTA, no per-game
	-- port. These are zone ids and tile coordinates, never RAM addresses.
	-- Row = { x0, z0, width, height, destination zone }.
	ow.PIT = {
		[160] = { {5,12,5,5, 161}, {14,13,5,5, 161} },                  -- 1F  -> B1F (both)
		[161] = { {24,14,5,5, 162} },                                   -- B1F -> B2F
		[162] = { {14,11,5,5, 163}, {9,20,5,5, 163} },                  -- B2F -> B3F (both)
		[163] = { {6,21,5,5, 164}, {2,13,5,5, 164}, {11,18,5,5, 164}, {17,23,5,5, 164} },   -- B3F -> B4F (all four)
		[164] = { {7,19,5,5, 165} },                                    -- B4F -> B5F
	}
	-- ⭐⭐ ONLY THE CENTRE TILE ALWAYS TAKES YOU. The engine has a quicksand-only event path: the ordinary
	-- per-step trigger check REFUSES class 0x7C outright (ov021 FUN_0218A7A4) and hands it to
	-- CheckQuicksandEvent (FUN_0218A8DC), which starts the fall unconditionally at exactly
	-- **(X + (W>>1), Z + (H>>1))** — the middle of the 5x5 — and on any OTHER tile of the patch only when
	-- FieldPlayerCore's ExState is nonzero or the actor's action command is in 0x58..0x5B or 0xA3..0xA6.
	-- Ola, from memory of playing it: "if you run it drags you in" — which is that gate's exact shape, and
	-- the best reading we have. ⚠ But it IS a reading: nobody has named those two command families from
	-- the code, so nothing may be built on "0x58..0x5B means running" as though it were proven
	-- (codex-review 2026-08-26 flagged precisely that). What IS proven is the centre.
	-- ⚠ AND THE PATCHES ARE NOT QUITE 25 TILES EACH: the ten records cover 250 cells, the census finds 248,
	-- and the two missing ones are (11,22) and (12,22) in zone 163, where B3F's third and fourth patches
	-- overlap a wall corner. No consequence — all ten CENTRES are class 0x7C and walkable in both ROMs —
	-- but ow.sink checks the live class as well as the baked coordinate rather than trust the pattern.
	-- (Codex, White ov021/ov010/ARM9, 2026-08-26; report-relic-quicksand-engine.md. The engine builds that
	-- centre out of the same four record fields we read, which independently confirms the layout.)
	-- So the rim is ordinary ground you can walk on and off, and the centre is the point of no return.
	-- ow.sink (is this tile the centre that always drops you?) is defined further down, next to the
	-- other tile predicates -- it has to read the LIVE map, and pf_tile is a local declared down there.
	function ow.sandy(p) return p ~= nil and p % 0x10000 == 0x7C end     -- on the sand at all?
	ow.NODIRS = {}                                                      -- expand no successors (a sink is a leaf)
	-- ⛔ AND SAND IS AVOIDED, NOT FORBIDDEN. Walking across a patch is legal and is the ONLY way into three
	-- sealed pockets of B3F — the ones holding the PP Up and the grunt the walkthrough sends you to, which
	-- have no other entrance in the whole floor. But an outer tile still drops you if you happen to be
	-- RUNNING, and we do not control how Ola crosses it. So A* pays PF_SAND extra per sand tile entered:
	-- any detour shorter than that is preferred, and a pocket only reachable across sand stays reachable.
	-- Soft on purpose — this must never be the thing that makes somewhere unroutable.
	ow.PF_SAND = 6
	-- Where each one puts you down, kept as the record of what the scripts do (the reader never needs it,
	-- because falling is a zone change and the overworld announcer names the new floor on its own):
	--   160 (5,12)->161 (10,10) | 160 (14,13)->161 (15,11) | 161 (24,14)->162 (22,17)
	--   162 (14,11)->163 (27,9) | 162 (9,20)->163 (21,19)
	--   163 (2,13)->164 (13,14) | 163 (11,18)->164 (21,19) | 163 (6,21)->164 (15,21) | 163 (17,23)->164 (26,26)
	--   164 (7,19)->165 (14,26) or (14,24) — that floor's script picks between two landings on a variable.
	-- zone id -> spoken name: a curated building name if we have one, else "<name>'s house/room" for the player's
	-- home, else the place name (a/0/0/2 bank 89), else "Zone N" (nameless zones 150/151/422 and OOB/nil ids).
	-- NOTE: this deliberately still returns "Castelia City" for the street zones — it is what the save panel
	-- wants, and the overworld announcer adds the street itself (see the zone_area block in the module below).
	local function zone_name(z)
		z = reg.ez(z)                       -- Black City / White Forest: name the zone the ENGINE resolves to
		local b = z and zone_building[z]; if b then return b end
		local suf = z and zone_player_room[z]
		if suf then local n = player_name(); if n and n ~= "" then return n .. suf end end
		local p = z and zone_pid[z]
		-- ⛔ THE GATE IS NAMED BY THE VERSION, NOT BY THE ZONE. Both gate zones into the version-exclusive
		-- city (375 on the Route 14 side, 379 on the Route 15 side) carry place name 112 "Black Gate" in the
		-- shared zonedata, and NO zone anywhere in the game uses 113 "White Gate" — the engine substitutes
		-- it. GetZoneLocationNameIdx (White 0x02013e68, Black 0x02013e4c) ends with
		--   if ((idx + 0xFF90) & 0xFFFF) <= 1 then idx = 0x71 end     -- i.e. idx 112 OR 113 -> 113
		-- and that one constant is the only byte in which the two ROMs' copies of the function differ
		-- (Black says 0x70). So it is a per-VERSION constant, not a runtime flag like the zone remap above.
		if p == 112 or p == 113 then p = (GAME == "black") and 112 or 113 end
		local nm = p and placenames[p]
		if nm and nm ~= "" then return nm end
		return "Zone " .. tostring(z or "?")
	end
	reg.zone_name = zone_name          -- exported for the save-panel line (reg.save_step)
	-- Name a WARP's destination for the Transitions list. Generic house interiors -> "house" (so a door doesn't
	-- read as the town name); curated buildings + the player's own house/room keep their specific name. Used ONLY
	-- for transitions, not the on-entry map announcement (zone_name) — entering still says the place/town name.
	local function transition_name(z)
		z = reg.ez(z)                       -- a warp record names zone 0 for BOTH cities; resolve it first
		-- A FLOOR KEEPS ITS BUILDING'S NAME (Ola, 2026-08-25: "put that text in the nav list after the map
		-- name, don't remove it"). Celestial Tower's five zones all share place name 56, so the floor label
		-- is what tells two list entries apart — but "3F" ALONE has no context in a list, so the entry reads
		-- "Celestial Tower 3F". Only the NAV LIST does this; the on-entry announcer keeps saying the building
		-- once and then the bare floor as you climb, the way it already does for Castelia's streets.
		-- ⭐ A DOOR BACK OUT ONTO AN OUTDOOR ZONE THAT HAS FLOORS OF ITS OWN. Ola, inside zone 219:
		-- "the transition to the outside doesn't say the floor number in nav list, it does speak when you
		-- go out ... the one out should say the floor as well." He is right, and the plain rule below
		-- cannot do it: Victory Road's whole mountain is ONE zone with no zone_area entry, so a warp to it
		-- reads "Victory Road" whichever of its seven terraces it opens onto.
		-- The terrace is knowable exactly, and needs no new data: a room's own floor label IS the terrace
		-- its outdoor door lands on -- that is how the labels were derived in the first place. Verified
		-- rather than assumed (research/re/vr_recon.py): no interior has doors onto two different
		-- terraces, and all fourteen labels agree with the terrace their door actually reaches.
		-- ⚠ 215/220/222 are on the bottom terrace and carry no label under the no-1F rule, so their way
		-- out stays plain "Victory Road" -- which is correct, and matches what the announcer says there.
		if z and ow.RAILISLE[z] then
			local here = reg.zid()
			local hf = zone_area[here]
			if hf and ow.FLOOR[here] then return zone_name(z) .. " " .. hf end
		end
		local a = z and zone_area[z]
		if a then
			if ow.FLOOR[z] then return zone_name(z) .. " " .. a end
			return a                                                    -- a Castelia door/mouth names its street,
		end
		if z and HOUSE[z] and not zone_building[z] and not zone_player_room[z] then return "house" end
		return zone_name(z)                                             -- not "Castelia City" twelve times over.
	end
	local prev_map, prev_name              -- prev_name = the last PLACE name decided. The last Castelia STREET
	                                       -- is kept apart, as ow.parea — see the announce block below. It
	                                       -- lives on `ow` and not in a local because this function is already
	                                       -- at Lua's 200-local ceiling (one more here fails to compile).
	local EVD_ANCHOR = 0x0224F970 + DELTA           -- fixed global -> current EventDataSystem ptr.
	-- EventDataSystem signature check (zone id + duplicated counts + heap array pointers).
	local function s16v(v) if v >= 0x8000 then return v - 0x10000 end return v end
	local function evd_ok(E, z)
		if not inram(E) then return false end
		local nF, nA, nW, nT = u16(E+0x12), u16(E+0x14), u16(E+0x16), u16(E+0x18)
		if nF>=512 or nA>=512 or nW>=512 or nT>=512 or (nF+nA+nW+nT)==0 then return false end
		if u16(E+0x2C)~=nF or u16(E+0x2E)~=nA or u16(E+0x30)~=nW or u16(E+0x32)~=nT then return false end
		if z and u16(E+0x10)~=z then return false end
		if not inram(u32(E+0x04)) then return false end
		for _, ca in ipairs({ {nF,0x1C},{nA,0x20},{nW,0x24},{nT,0x28} }) do
			if ca[1]>0 and not inram(u32(E+ca[2])) then return false end
		end
		return true
	end
	-- Fallback only (if the anchor ever fails): one signature scan of the field-heap band, on a key, not per frame.
	local NLO, NHI = 0x02100000, 0x023E0000
	local function evd_scan(z)
		local a = NLO
		while a < NHI do
			local len = math.min(0x10000, NHI - a)
			local arr = mainmemory.read_bytes_as_array(a - MM, math.min(len+0x40, NHI+0x40-a))
			local b = (arr[0] ~= nil) and 0 or 1
			local zlo, zhi = z%256, math.floor(z/256)
			for j = 0, len-4, 4 do
				if arr[b+j+0x10]==zlo and arr[b+j+0x11]==zhi then
					local E = a + j; if evd_ok(E, z) then return E end
				end
			end
			a = a + 0x10000
		end
		return nil
	end
	local evd_diag = false
	-- ⛔ EITHER ID IDENTIFIES THE VERSION-EXCLUSIVE CITY. We ask by the EFFECTIVE zone (424 in White
	-- Forest), but whether the EventDataSystem stamps its own +0x10 with that or with the RAW 0 the warp
	-- record carried is not something the ROM settles offline — and guessing wrong costs a evd_scan, the
	-- whole-heap scan this project forbids anywhere but a fallback that never runs. Accepting both is
	-- fail-closed: the signature check still has to pass, so a wrong object is still rejected.
	local function evd_find(z)
		local E = u32(EVD_ANCHOR)
		if evd_ok(E, z) then if not evd_diag then evd_diag=true; dbg("nav: anchor ok") end; return E end
		local alt = (z == 424 and 0) or (z == 295 and 294) or nil
		if alt and evd_ok(E, alt) then if not evd_diag then evd_diag=true; dbg("nav: anchor ok (raw id)") end; return E end
		local S = evd_scan(z); if S then dbg("nav: anchor failed -> scanned") end; return S
	end
	-- Build per-category lists (positions normalized to TILES). Rail entities are skipped for now (grid areas
	-- first; rail/bridge coords aren't a flat tile pair). Transitions get the destination place name; the rest
	-- (NPCs/Signs/Objects) are still generic placeholders.
	local CATS = { "Transitions", "NPCs", "Items", "HM", "Signs", "Objects", "Slides" }
	-- LIVE actors from the FieldActorManager (RE 2026-06-29 via the pokeplatinum Gen IV decomp `map_object.c` +
	-- White ov010; see games/white.lua "LIVE ACTOR SYSTEM"). The manager holds ONLY actors the game has actually
	-- spawned — so this shows exactly what is present and drops story NPCs / collected items the moment they go
	-- (the static ZoneNPC list could not: it lists everything a zone CAN have, over-counting in the bedroom). NO
	-- heap scan: GameData (fixed pointer chain, find_gd) + 0x1A8 -> manager; manager+0x18 -> FieldActor[] (stride
	-- 0x100, manager+0x04 = slot count). A slot is present+visible iff (status&1)~=0 [active] and (status&0x200)
	-- ==0 [not HIDE]; current tile = x@+0x3c, z@+0x40. CLASSIFY (data-mined from the ZONE_ENTITIES archive a/1/2/5,
	-- see white.lua): ModelID(+0xC) in {110,210,216} = a ground ITEM (110 = standard ball); else EvType(+0x10)==1
	-- = a TRAINER, otherwise a plain person. Returns {npcs=..., items=...} (each nearest-first) or nil to fall
	-- back to the static list.
	-- Per-ModelID kinds (RE'd from ZONE_ENTITIES a/1/2/5 + ov021 gfx-id checks; see white.lua "LIVE ACTOR SYSTEM"):
	--   ITEM = ground item ball (110 standard, 210/216 variants).
	--   HM   = HM field obstacle (Cut tree / Strength boulder / ...), value = its label. B/W has NO gfx->HM table
	--          and the field overlays never gfx-check these (the cut tree is handled by data/SCRID, not a switch) —
	--          so they were found by MAP DATA instead (research/re, a/1/2/5): **ModelID 206 = CUT TREE**, confirmed
	--          because across all 427 zones it appears ONLY in Pinwheel Forest (a 4-tile barrier at 106-109) and
	--          Victory Road — the two B/W cut-tree areas — with shared "cut" flags (652/653). Cut trees are static
	--          objects with a SpawnFlag, so they spawn into the live manager while uncut and vanish once cut (flag
	--          set) -> correct live behavior for free. (Strength boulder / Rock Smash models TBD by the same data
	--          method; cave data was murkier.) Label uses the localized move name "Cut" (bank 203) + English noun.
	local ITEM_MODELS = { [110]=true, [210]=true, [216]=true }
	-- HM obstacles (model -> label). ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-07-21 by RENDERING the actual sprites (OW archive
	-- a/0/4/9 via the a/0/4/8 id table — see white.lua "OW SPRITE ARCHIVE"): the old data-only guesses had
	-- the right labels on the WRONG ids (a user reported a cut tree spoken as an NPC — this was why).
	--   108 = CUT TREE (the green tree sprite): Dreamyard entrance, Route 2, Route 13 x3, Route 16 — all
	--         scr 10004 (the examine script), spawnflag 0 = regrows on re-entry, exactly cut-tree semantics.
	--   206 = BOULDER (the gray rock sprite): Pinwheel Forest 4-in-a-row + Victory Road, PERMANENT flags
	--         (652/653) = cleared-for-good obstacles. (The old note called this the cut tree.)
	--   89  = REMOVED — its sprite is a PERSON (woman in a white sun hat; story NPC in Cold Storage /
	--         Chargestone / Twist / N's Castle). She was being announced as "Boulder".
	--   8195 / 8197 = STRENGTH BOULDER, both of them, added 2026-09-01 after Ola met one in Mistralton Cave
	--         and the tool called it "Person" — model 8197 was in no table at all, so it fell through to the
	--         generic name, sat in the NPC list as a routing target, and (worse) counted as SOFT occupancy,
	--         i.e. something the router may assume will step aside. It is a rock.
	--         ⭐ NAMED FROM THE GAME'S OWN CODE, not from where they appear. Both models carry SCRID 10000,
	--         and the categorize table (ov010 0x021701c8) sends 10000..10089 to a/0/5/7 file **867** with
	--         text bank **280** — the field-move bank. Common script 10000 decodes to
