	local function trainer_label(scr)
		local tid
		if scr >= 3000 and scr < 5000 then tid = scr - 3000 end
		if not tid then return "Trainer" end
		return trainer_name[tid] or trainer_classname[trainer_class[tid] or 0] or "Trainer"
	end
	-- forward declaration: the rail subsystem is defined further down (after the grid collision
	-- primitives it sits beside), but live_actors below needs it to know it is in a rail area.
	local rail
	-- Zones where the reachability filter must not run: see the note at its call site in refresh().
	-- A zone belongs here when getting somewhere legitimately does NOT mean walking there.
	--   63 = Nimbasa Gym — five disconnected platforms joined only by roller-coaster rides.
	--   97 = Driftveil Gym — storeys of one mine shaft joined only by lift rides.
	--  108 = Mistralton Gym — FOURTEEN disconnected islands joined only by the cannons. Measured out of
	--        the ROM the same way as the other two (research/re/z108_recon.py): 605 walkable tiles in 14
	--        components with no tile path between any two, climbing 0 → 2 → 4 → 6 → 10 tile-heights from
	--        the door at (14,40) to Skyla on the top island at (15,5). The gym's own Gym Guide says the
	--        mechanic out loud (a/0/0/3 bank 113 entry 8): "to proceed in this Gym, you climb in the
	--        cannons to move forward. The cannons go up, down, left, and right."
	-- ⛔ ZONES WHERE WALKING IS NOT THE ONLY WAY TO GET SOMEWHERE. Nimbasa's platforms are joined only by
	-- coaster rides, Driftveil's storeys only by lift rides and Mistralton's islands only by cannon shots,
	-- so a walk-only BFS is right that the goal cannot be walked to and wrong to hide it — the leg planners
	-- are what get you there (Ola 2026-08-24: "turn off the reachability filter ... then I could target
	-- Clay right away"; the same request for Skyla on 2026-08-25).
	--  114 = Icirrus Gym — three rotating ice mechanisms gate eight tiles that hand you onto the rails
	--        (RE 2026-08-25, accessibility-docs/27), and ow.ig.leg is what actually gets you across.
	--        ⚠ I first wrote this entry as an INTERIM to be removed once the planner existed. That was
	--        wrong, and the other three entries say why: 63, 97 and 108 all HAVE planners and all keep
	--        their exemption permanently. The filter floods terrain; it cannot see a ride in any of these
	--        rooms, so the exemption is what stops it hiding the leader while the planner does the routing.
	--        It is the same answer here, and it is not temporary.
	--  121 = Opelucid Gym — three rail chains and four gallery arcs joined ONLY by three rotating dragon
	--        necks (RE 2026-08-27, accessibility-docs/28), and ow.dg.leg is what actually gets you across.
	--        Same permanent exemption as the four above, for the same reason: the filter floods terrain
	--        and cannot see a bridge that is one switch press away, so leaving it on would hide Iris.
	-- ── SPLIT ZONES + BAKED SURF ENTRIES (2026-08-31) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
	-- Ola, on Route 1: "the reachability filter doesn't seem to work there? There is a gate to the west but
	-- I am like 99% sure there should be surfing in the way there ... the surf spot here isn't even being
	-- picked up." His four N-dumps settled BOTH, and killed the theory I had been carrying:
	--   * the search is FINE — the matrix reads, the flood is correctly limited to the zone, 305..593 tiles;
	--   * the game keeps only **four map chunks** loaded (a 2x2 block, ~64 tiles square, centred on you);
	--   * the Route Gate is 66/79/89/119 tiles away in the four dumps, so its tiles are NEVER loaded and
	--     reach.filter takes its deliberate fail-open branch ("unreachable BUT a tile is not loaded")
	--     EVERY time, not occasionally;
	--   * and reach.surf_spots' +/-20 box cannot see an entry that is not loaded either — his dump #4, up
	--     near Accumula, reads "surf spots in range 20 = 0".
	-- So the tool can never answer this from live memory. It has to come from the ROM.
	-- ⛔ AND IT IS SAFE TO, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED (Ola raised exactly this: "accuracy is a must"):
	--   * the two overworld matrices (0, and 175 for the Dragonspiral story state) differ in ZERO cells of
	--     land data and 109 cells of zone ownership — a story change re-labels squares, it moves no walls;
	--   * a/0/0/8's 649 land members are all distinct and allocated one per chunk in map order, so there is
	--     no hidden set of seasonal copies being swapped in;
	--   * 51 live collision windows out of Ola's own dumps were compared tile-for-tile against this static
	--     data: 48 agree exactly, and the three that do not are the rooms already known to be dynamic
	--     (Driftveil's lift shaft, a two-layer Dragonspiral floor, one rail tile in Opelucid);
	--   * and Ola, asked directly: "it does not need to be season aware, as nothing walkable changes."
	-- ⭐⭐ TWO TABLES, TWO DIFFERENT KEYS, and conflating them would break Dragonspiral to fix Route 1.
	-- SPLIT is per ZONE because reach.for_ already limits its flood to `zone_at == zid`, so the only
	-- question the filter ever asks is about tiles inside the current zone. SURFPT is computed over the
	-- MATRIX and filed under the zone whose land you STAND on, because a Surf spot legitimately belongs to
	-- a different zone from the shore you press from — Dragonspiral Tower has ZERO surfable tiles of its
	-- own and the water Ola surfs there is Route 8's, one square over a chunk corner. That is also why
	-- SURFPT is a SUPPLEMENT and never a replacement: zone 205 bakes no entries at all, so any zone the
	-- live sweep already serves is left untouched. (Ola spotted this before a line of it was written.)
	-- SPLIT[zone][n] = run-length rows {z, x0, x1} of the n-th MINORITY island; a tile in none of them is
	-- on the zone's main island. SURFPT[zone] = {x, z, direction, island, water body}.
	-- Generated by research/re/gen_islands.py, which asserts White == Black for both tables.
