	ow.SPLIT_OFF = { [96] = true }
	ow.NOREACH = { [63] = true, [97] = true, [108] = true, [114] = true, [121] = true }
	-- ── DARK CAVES: the zones you cannot see in until you use FLASH ───────────────────────────────────
	-- Ola, 2026-09-01, in Mistralton Cave: "this cave needs you to use flash to be able to see where
	-- you're going ... make the path finder unavailable until I actually use the move."
	-- ⭐ THE GAME HAS A TABLE FOR THIS, and it is not in zonedata. InitZoneDataSystem (0x020139B0) loads
	-- NARC ARCID 149 = ROM file **a/1/4/9** member 0 whole (`movs r0,#0x95; movs r1,#0; blx 0x0204A2B0`),
	-- stores the pointer at ZoneDataSystem+0x44 and size>>2 at +0x43. GetZoneFlashFlags (0x02013F88)
	-- walks it at stride 4 — `u16 zone @ +0`, `u16 flags @ +2` — and returns 0 for a zone that is absent.
	-- The whole member is 32 bytes, EIGHT rows, BYTE-IDENTICAL in White and Black:
	--   (324, 0) (325, 1) (333, 1) (334, 1) (335, 0) (352, 1) (353, 1) (354, 1)
	-- Bit 0 = FLD_FLASH_ALLOW (dark, Flash may be used); bit 1 = FLD_FLASH_ACTIVE, set at runtime only.
	-- So SIX zones in the whole game are dark: Wellspring Cave B1F (325), Mistralton Cave's two rooms
	-- (333, 334) and all three of Challenger's Cave (352, 353, 354).
	-- ⚠ THE TWO EXPLICIT ZEROS ARE NOT PADDING and they are the surprising part: Wellspring Cave's
	-- entrance floor (324) and the GUIDANCE CHAMBER (335) — the Cobalion room, reached from dark 334 —
	-- are lit. They are spelled out rather than left absent because a zone load whose destination flags
	-- are 0 is exactly what CLEARS the used flag (see ow.flash_on), so walking 334 -> 335 turns Flash off
	-- and coming back leaves 334 dark again. That is the game's rule, not ours; it is on the test list.
	-- ⛔ BAKED, NOT READ LIVE, and deliberately: reading it would need a NEW absolute root per game
	-- (ZoneDataSystem, White 0x02146284 / Black 0x02146264) for eight rows of ROM data that cannot change
	-- and that both ROMs agree on byte for byte. The live half — whether Flash is burning right now — is
	-- read from the game, because that is the half that actually varies.
	-- (RE: Codex 2026-09-01, research/re/report_flash.md + research/re/flash_dark_zones.py; the table,
	-- the accessors and the complete writer set were each re-verified independently before this was wired.)
	ow.DARK = { [325] = true, [333] = true, [334] = true, [352] = true, [353] = true, [354] = true }
	-- Is the cave lit right now? true / false, or **nil when we cannot tell** — and the difference matters,
	-- see the gate in pf_start: an unreadable state must never produce the "too dark" line, because that
	-- line is a claim about the game's state and a wrong one is exactly the wrong-speech this project
	-- refuses. Chain from the root we already trust, no new absolute address:
	--   GameData (find_gd) -> FieldStatus +0x1B8 -> u8 +0x06 = IsFlashUsed
	-- proven at the point of use: GameData_GetFieldStatus 0x02012B38 is `movs r1,#0x6e; lsls r1,#2;
	-- ldr r0,[r0,r1]` (0x6E*4 = 0x1B8) and FieldStatus_Set/CheckFlashUsed 0x02014480/0x02014484 are
	-- `strb r1,[r0,#6]` / `ldrb r0,[r0,#6]`. Both offsets are identical in Black.
	-- ⭐ AND IT IS STABLE FRAME TO FRAME. An all-alignment BL scan of the ARM9 and every overlay finds
	-- exactly THREE writers of that byte in the whole game: the Flash field-move callback (ov010
	-- 0x0215ED60, writes 1), the destination-zone loader (ov010 0x02164424, writes 0 — and only when
	-- `GetZoneFlashFlags(destination) & 3` is zero), and PlayerSave_LoadFieldStatus restoring
	-- PlayerSaveData+0x72. So there is NO step timer and NO battle clear: Flash lasts until you walk into
	-- a zone the table says is not dark. (Scan reproduced independently of the RE report.)
	-- ⛔ VALIDATE THE BYTE THE CONSUMER READS, NOT JUST THE POINTER (codex-review 2026-09-01). Two holes,
	-- both cheap to close and both of the shape this project has been bitten by before:
	--   * inram(fs) allows fs within six bytes of the top of RAM, where u8() answers 0 for an unreadable
	--     address — and 0 here means "dark", so an out-of-domain read would SPEAK a false claim rather
	--     than stay quiet. Check the field, not the base.
	--   * the three writers found in the binary only ever store 0 or 1, so anything else means we are not
	--     looking at a FieldStatus. Answer UNKNOWN rather than treating a stray byte as "lit".
	-- ⛔⛔ AND **NOT** find_gd() HERE, however natural it looks (codex-review verification 2026-09-01).
	-- find_gd's last resort is the original 2.9 MiB "game_data.c" heap scan — a self-heal that must never
	-- run — and pf_update calls this EVERY FRAME while a route is live. A GameData chain that fails while
	-- routing in one of the six dark rooms would therefore scan the heap sixty times a second, which is
	-- precisely the per-frame scan this project's hardest rule forbids. So take the FIXED chain directly
	-- and stop: GameSystem -> +0x1C GameData, validated by the same cheap scan-free tag check find_gd uses
	-- for its own cache. No fallback, no scan, and an unreadable chain simply answers UNKNOWN.
	function ow.flash_on()
		local gs = u32(GAMESYS_PTR); if not inram(gs) then return nil end
		local gd = u32(gs + 0x1C); if not gd_ok(gd) then return nil end
		local fs = u32(gd + 0x1B8); if not inram(fs) or not inram(fs + 0x06) then return nil end
		local b = u8(fs + 0x06)
		if b > 1 then return nil end                                    -- not a FieldStatus: unknown, never a guess
		return b == 1
	end
	-- The one question the readers ask: is this place dark AND unlit right now? Only a POSITIVE read of
	-- both halves answers true.
	-- ⛔ AN UNREADABLE STATE OPENS THE GATE, and that is a considered choice rather than an oversight.
	-- Speaking "this area is too dark" is a claim about the game's state, so a read we do not trust must
	-- not make it — that is the fail-closed rule, applied to the thing we SAY. The other half of the
	-- trade is what withholding would cost: the nav list is how he finds out a room contains anything at
	-- all, and a list that goes silent with no explanation is indistinguishable from a dead script, which
	-- is the diagnosis this project has lost whole sessions to. Degrading to the tool's previous, working
	-- behaviour is the better failure.
	-- ⚠ MY FIRST JUSTIFICATION FOR THIS WAS TOO STRONG and the review was right to say so: I argued the
	-- unreadable branch could only be reached when GameData itself fails, in which case the whole list
	-- fails anyway. Not so — FieldStatus can be unreadable while GameData and the actor manager are fine,
	-- and then the list works and the gate silently does not. Rare, but real. So make it visible instead
	-- of arguing it away: one console line, once per session, naming exactly what is not being enforced.
	function ow.too_dark(zone)
		if not ow.DARK[zone] then return false end
		local lit = ow.flash_on()
		if lit == nil and not ow.flwarn then
			ow.flwarn = true
			dbg("flash: the FieldStatus chain did not read in a DARK zone — the darkness gate is standing "
			    .. "down, so the nav list and the pathfinder stay available whether or not Flash is on.")
		end
		return lit == false
	end
	-- The baked island containing this tile — index 0 is the zone's MAIN island — or nil for "not land".
	-- ⛔ nil USED TO MEAN TWO THINGS ("main island" or "not land"), which forced the filter to be
	-- asymmetric: standing on Route 1's beach it could not tell a main-island exit from an unplaceable one
	-- and kept both, so Ola was still offered the far side across the water. Every island is baked now, so
	-- nil means exactly one thing and the filter can be exact in both directions.
	function ow.isle_at(zone, x, z)
		local sp = ow.SPLIT[zone]; if not sp then return nil end
		for n, rows in pairs(sp) do
			for _, r in ipairs(rows) do
				if r[1] == z and x >= r[2] and x <= r[3] then return n end
			end
		end
		return nil
	end
	-- ── RAILISLE: zones whose nav list is cut to the rail ISLAND you are standing on ──────────────────
	-- ⛔ THIS DELIBERATELY INVERTS THIS FILE'S STANDING RULE, and only because Ola asked for it by name.
	-- Everywhere else "a door never listed is worse than a door the router cannot reach" — which is why
	-- reach.filter fails open and why the five NOREACH zones switch it off entirely rather than hide a
	-- place you are trying to get to. Victory Road is the one map where that trade flips, and it flips
	-- because of scale: the outdoor mountain is a SINGLE zone holding eighteen entrances spread over
	-- seven terraces, and no two terraces are joined by walking. Ola, 2026-08-28: "I want us to only see
	-- the ones on our current floor here ... this means we only see what we can see and not everything
	-- under us as well, since getting up again is a bit of a pain."
	-- Why the answer is exact rather than a judgement call: zone 214's rail graph splits into ELEVEN
	-- components and every one of them is PERFECTLY FLAT — measured, spread 0.00 tiles, not one spans
	-- even a tile of height (research/re/vr_floors.py). So "the island I am on" and "the terrace I am on"
	-- are the same set, and filtering by island cannot hide anything I could have walked to.
	-- ⚠ ISLAND, NOT HEIGHT BAND, and the difference is real: the bottom terrace is TWO disconnected
	-- islands (the main path in from Route 10, and a separate landing holding zone 222's door that you
	-- can only reach by sliding down onto it). Filtering by height would offer that door from the main
	-- path, where there is no way to walk to it; filtering by island does not.
	-- ⚠ Scoped by an opt-in table rather than applied to every rail zone, because the hybrid zones
	-- (Castelia, the League, Village Bridge, Opelucid...) cross between rail and grid mid-zone, so an
	-- island there is not a place you are stuck on. rail.isle_now returns nil off the rails and the
	-- filter then does nothing, but the table is the guard that says we MEANT this, per zone.
	-- Value = the graph FINGERPRINT this zone's bake belongs to (see ow.isle_certified). A table, so it
	-- is still truthy everywhere the flag is tested.
	ow.RAILISLE = { [214] = { nl = 65, np = 76 } }
	-- ⛔ CERTIFY THE GRAPH BEFORE FILTERING DESTRUCTIVELY. rail.sys() proves a graph is WELL-FORMED, not
	-- that it is THIS zone's graph: entering 214 while a previous area's rail data is still loaded would
	-- give a valid-looking graph, and the island numbers computed from it mean nothing here. Everything
	-- else on this feature fails open or fails closed harmlessly, but the Transitions filter DELETES
	-- entries, so it is the one place a wrong graph could leave Ola on a mountain with the wrong exits.
	-- rail.onrail's world cross-check already makes that nearly impossible (a wrong graph almost never
	-- converts to the tile the player really stands on), but "nearly" is not the standard for a
	-- destructive filter, and codex-review rightly called it out (HIGH).
	-- The certificate is the SHAPE plus a CONTENT marker that cannot appear elsewhere: class 0xA0/0xA1
	-- exists in rail area 9 and NOWHERE else in the game -- all 17 rail areas and every land record of
	-- every grid zone, in both ROMs (research/re/vr_slidetiles.py). So reading one at a baked coordinate
	-- proves the loaded graph is the one this table was baked from. Two u16 reads and one tile read.
	function ow.isle_certified(c, z)
		local want = ow.RAILISLE[z]
		if not (want and c and c.nl == want.nl and c.np == want.np) then return false end
		local run = ow.SLIDE[z] and ow.SLIDE[z][1]
		return run ~= nil and ow.slidetile(c, run[1][1], run[1][2], run[1][3])
	end
	-- What A* pays extra to step on a slide tile (see the note at the rail.astar call). Big enough to
	-- dominate any detour the three-wide mountain lane can force — the widest is a couple of tiles — and
	-- deliberately FINITE so it can only ever make the rim unattractive, never unreachable.
	ow.PF_SLIDE = 20
	-- Is this rail tile really a slide, live? Used to validate every baked ow.SLIDE coordinate at the
	-- point of USE, so a stale or mismatched rail area can never turn a baked triple into a "Slide"
	-- sitting on an ordinary tile. nil/anything-else reads as "no".
	-- ⛔ THE ENGINE ACCEPTS **TWO** CLASSES, NOT ONE. White's own predicate (FUN_021BB1A0, called from
	-- FUN_0218B26C at 0x0218B2A0) is an unsigned RANGE test — `(u16)(cls - 0xA0) <= 1` — so it takes
	-- 0xA0 AND 0xA1. B2W2's symbols name it IsTileSlopeSlip_. The first cut of this file shipped 0xA0
	-- only and left out the two 0xA1 cells on line 27, which are a real slide off the third terrace
	-- (they land on island 1). Excluding them contradicted the predicate; they are in now.
	-- The two forms differ only in Flags (0xA0 -> 0x0080, 0xA1 -> 0x0004); both have collision bit 0
	-- clear, so both are walkable, which is exactly why ow.PF_SLIDE exists.
	function ow.slidetile(c, l, f, s)
		local t = rail.tileat(c, l, f, s)
		if t == nil then return false end
		local cls = t % 0x10000
		return cls == 0xA0 or cls == 0xA1
	end
	-- ── VICTORY ROAD'S SLIDES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	-- Ola: "the gimmick here is slides you can slide down on the outside to get down faster and to other
	-- entrances", and on how they work: "you start just by walking, you can't steer, it takes you to the
	-- end of that slide no matter what as far as I know."
	-- A slide cell is collision class **0xA0 or 0xA1** on the rail tilemap (the engine's predicate is an
	-- unsigned RANGE test — see ow.slidetile). Those classes exist in rail area 9 and NOWHERE ELSE —
	-- censused over all 17 rail areas AND every land record of every grid zone in the game, in both ROMs
	-- (research/re/vr_slidetiles.py): 67 cells of 0xA0 plus 2 of 0xA1, all of them in zone 214. So the
	-- classes ARE this gimmick's signature, not shared ones we are reading out of context.
	-- ⭐ THE SHAPE IS THE GIVEAWAY. Drawn out (research/re/vr_slidemap.py) every mountain path is a
	-- three-tile-wide ledge — rock wall on the inner side, walkable lane, and then a CONTINUOUS RUN of
	-- launch cells along the OUTER RIM, up to nine tiles long. All 69 sit at side +2 or +3, never in the
	-- lane.
	-- ⚠ AND THE TRIGGER IS **PRESSING DOWN WHILE STANDING ON ONE**, not stepping onto it: White's
