		-- loaded, or loaded in the right place, for us to know where its hole is - which is the one thing
		-- the old status-only test could not survive across leaving the room and coming back.
		-- ⭐ VALIDITY IS THE ENGINE'S EXACT PREDICATE (0x0216E4B0): invalid only when ALL THREE words are
		-- -1. Testing the first word alone happened to agree, but "the same test the game makes" is free.
		-- ⚠ AND THE BLOCK IS MANDATORY, not best-effort. Without a record we cannot tell a rock sitting in
		-- its hole from one sitting beside it, and the two answers are opposite mistakes: route through a
		-- solid rock, or refuse a bridge that is already there. raw() fails as a whole everywhere else in
		-- this function; the save read is no different.
		-- ⛔⛔ EVERY AUTHORED ROCK, EXACTLY ONCE, OR NOTHING. The loop above collects the actors it FINDS,
		-- and nothing checked that it found them all — so an authored boulder whose actor is absent (or
		-- enumerated twice) produced a confident PARTIAL room. Unsaved, that plans a push through a rock
		-- nobody told us about; saved, it silently omits a bridge that is really there. Both contradict the
		-- whole-room contract this function states three comments above its own head, and neither would
		-- ever have been noticed from the outside (codex-review 2026-09-02, MEDIUM).
		-- The zone's authored UID list is the yardstick, and it is the same table that identifies a rock in
		-- the first place — so this costs one pass over at most four entries.
		local got = {}
		for _, r in ipairs(out) do
			if got[r.uid] then return nil end                            -- two actors claiming one boulder
			got[r.uid] = true
		end
		for uid in pairs(uids) do
			if not got[uid] then return nil end                          -- an authored rock we never saw
		end
