			-- on class 0x1D and which has no saved record is falling or about to. This is NOT the circular
			-- tile check the comment below warns about — that one asked whether a FALLEN rock's tiles were
			-- still holes, through a reader that deliberately reports them as floor. These tiles are not
			-- filled by anyone (this rock has no record yet), so `tl` shows them raw; if another rock had
			-- filled them `tl` would say floor and this clause simply would not fire, which is the safe way
			-- round. Skipped when no tile reader was handed in, which is sig()'s case.
			-- ⚠ THE RESIDUAL, STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED: this protects the PLANNER, which is where a wrong
			-- answer becomes wrong guidance. sig() has no tile reader, so in that one handoff window (if it
			-- exists at all — it is an unclosed proof, not an observed frame) it can still publish a changed
			-- layout and provoke one re-plan, which pf_build answers with an ordinary best-effort route.
			-- That line is superseded about twenty frames later when the rock lands and the signature
			-- changes again, so the cost is one transient instruction, not a dead end. Closing it properly
			-- means either proving the two phases are atomic in the engine or threading a tile reader
			-- through sig() and both its call sites; neither is worth doing blind.
