		-- EXISTS TO PREVENT (codex-review 2026-08-27, MEDIUM — confirmed). `settled` only says pose ==
		-- f(flags), and the pose is written at the START of the swing: so it goes true immediately and
		-- stays true for the whole animation, while the rail lines still describe the OLD geometry. A
		-- route built in that window is planned from the new poses over the old bridges — exactly the
		-- nonexistent-bridge instruction the design is meant to rule out.
		-- ⚠ AND pf_update's hold does not cover this. That hold protects an EXISTING route; pf_start runs
		-- after it, and with no active route pf_update returns before reaching the hold at all. So route
		-- CONSTRUCTION has to fail closed on its own. busy() is the test that spans the whole swing,
		-- because it compares the saved half against the live disabled set rather than trusting either.
		-- ⛔ busy() IS TRI-STATE AND ALL THREE STATES MATTER (codex-review 2026-08-27, HIGH — confirmed).
		-- `if ow.dg.busy()` discarded the second return, so `nil,"rail"` — the live disabled-line list
		-- could not be read at all — fell through as "not busy" and planning continued from the SAVED
		-- poses over rails nobody had checked. That is the nonexistent-bridge instruction this module
		-- exists to rule out, arrived at from the one direction I had not guarded. Route construction
		-- fails closed on an unreadable live state, full stop.
		local bsy, bwhy = ow.dg.busy()
		if bsy then return nil, "swinging" end
		if bwhy then return nil, bwhy end
		if not st.settled then return nil, "swinging" end   -- the flag has flipped, the pose has not yet
		-- Which handoffs and switches sit in a region, and where a handoff leads under a pose triple.
		-- ⛔⛔ THE RAIL RELATION IS DIRECTED, AND A GROUP STRING COULD NEVER SAY SO. This used to read a
		-- 21-character equivalence partition — same character means "mutually connected" — and that shape
		-- is simply wrong for this graph: 2000 of the 13440 unordered (pose, handoff-pair) relations are
		-- ONE-WAY, and 640 of them stay one-way even after excluding every pose-disabled line, because
		-- they are one-way rail.jump launches. So the old bake disagreed with the shipped rail search in
		-- 2704 of 26880 answers: it offered 704 rides the router then refuses (→ "Can't reach") and hid
		-- 2000 it would happily make (→ the leg planner and the router each insisting on a different next
		-- step, which is the one-tile loop). Forcing the partition to be conservative instead is no better
		-- — measured, it refuses the entrance→Iris route outright and drops exit success to 356/1344.
		-- rch is the directed relation, generated by asking the SHIPPED rail.astar 26880 ordered
