	-- ⛔ **HEIGHT IS THE HALF OF EVERY TILE WE NEVER READ.** Each permission record is EIGHT bytes and
	-- pf_tile only ever took the second four (Class + Flags). The first four say how high the ground is —
	-- and the engine refuses any step whose destination is 1.25 tile-heights or more from where the actor
	-- is standing (ActorRouteCollCheckCore, White 0x021638EC: target height in r0, live actor WPos.y from
	-- [r7+4], `abs` in r2, compared against 0x14000 and taken only on BLT — so equality also refuses).
	-- Ola, 2026-08-20, after two failed rounds: "it still wants me to go left when I can't." Cold Storage is
	-- a warehouse of raised platforms and to us it was all flat floor. In that one room there are 96 steps
	-- we called legal that the game refuses, and exactly two of them are ice-to-ice going left —
	-- (6,16)->(5,16) and (6,17)->(5,17), height 0 against height 196655, three tile-heights up. Found
	-- independently by Claude (from the map data) and Codex (from the compare instruction).
	-- ⚠ A TILTED RECORD IS A RAMP, AND IT HAS A HEIGHT TOO. I left ramps out at first, on the reasoning that
	-- a tilted plane's height varies across the tile — but the engine samples the target at its CENTRE, so a
	-- staircase gets one definite number just like flat ground does, and leaving them unknown is what let the
	-- router walk Ola into the side of a staircase (round 4: "it wants me to go right here and I can't").
	-- Only mode 2 (a corner-extension record, 1.1% of tiles) is still left unknown, and unknown never blocks.
	-- The rule leaves every room connected that was connected before, bar small strips that turn out to be
	-- genuinely unreachable (the six tiles behind the counter in every Pokémon Center, for instance); of the
	-- 138 rooms containing a staircase, decoding ramps costs reachable ground in exactly two.
	-- The height of ONE permission record at a tile centre, or nil when the game itself has no answer.
	-- Factored out of ow.height so the two-layer picker below can evaluate both candidates the same way.
	-- ⚠ The plane is measured from the CHUNK CENTRE (c.cx/c.cz), not from the map corner — on a tilted
	-- tile the x/z terms do not vanish, and getting that origin wrong turns every ramp into a cliff.
	function ow.hrec(c, rec, tx, tz, rd)
		local n, D
		if rd then
			-- 'RD' (Black City / White Forest) carries its TWO PLANES in the record itself instead of
			-- indexing the shared normal/distance tables: s16 normals at +0x00..+0x04 (plane 0) and
			-- +0x06..+0x0A (plane 1), s32 plane constants at +0x0C and +0x10. The getter negates the Z
			-- component of whichever it picks, exactly as the WB decoder does — which is why ow.HN is
			-- baked as {nx, ny, -nz} — so the same solve below serves both.
			-- ⭐ WHICH PLANE: the engine (FUN_021937B0) picks the SECOND when either
			--     Flags bit 15 set : fracX <= fracZ            else : fracX + fracZ > 0xFFFF
			-- and this reader only ever asks about a tile's CENTRE, where fracX == fracZ == 0x8000 (see
			-- the px/pz below, which add exactly 0x8000). At the centre BOTH tests are true — 0x8000 <=
			-- 0x8000, and 0x10000 > 0xFFFF — so the centre height always comes from plane 1, whatever the
			-- selector bit says. That is exact for the question we ask, not an approximation of it.
			n = { ow.s16(rec + 0x06), ow.s16(rec + 0x08), -ow.s16(rec + 0x0A) }
			D = ow.s32(rec + 0x10)
			if n[2] == 0 then return nil end                            -- degenerate plane: no height
		else
			local incl, di = u16(rec), u16(rec + 2)
			if (incl & 3) ~= 0 then return nil end                      -- mode 2 corner-extension: not decoded
			n, D = ow.HN[incl >> 2], ow.HD[di]
			if not n or not D then return nil end                       -- the game's own "no height" sentinels
		end
		local px = ((tx - c.cx) << 16) + 0x8000
		local pz = ((tz - c.cz) << 16) + 0x8000
		-- FLOOR-DIVIDE, matching the ARM original's arithmetic shift. Both operands go negative routinely:
		-- px/pz are measured from the chunk CENTRE, so they are negative across half of every chunk, and
		-- the plane normals carry negative components. Lua 5.4's `>>` is a LOGICAL shift on the 64-bit
		-- pattern, so it would turn a small negative into an astronomical positive; `// 4096` is the
		-- arithmetic shift the hardware does, and it is identical for every non-negative value.
		-- ⛔⛔ CORRECTION, 2026-09-02, AND IT RETRACTS A ROOT CAUSE THIS COMMENT USED TO CLAIM. It said the
		-- `>> 12` version returned -6371932322492175 in zone 121 where the true height is 32714, and that
		-- this was what made Ola unable to reach Iris. **That is not true of this expression.** For a
		-- negative x, `x >> 12` is exactly `(x // 4096) + 2^52`, and the `* 4096` on the very next line
		-- shifts that 2^52 out by 2^64 — which wraps away in Lua's 64-bit integers. So the two forms
		-- return BIT-IDENTICAL values. Proven, not argued: research/re/shiftcheck.py runs both formulas in
		-- Lua 5.4 over **every land record in the ROM — 628,272 of them — and they disagree on ZERO**.
		-- ⚠ WHAT THIS MEANS: the Opelucid cure was one of the other three faults found that day (the sparse
		--   HN/HD tables, rail.step's missing 021B9548 clamp, or rail.wtile's rounding), not this. The
		--   memory for that arc already said "the fix that worked did so for the WRONG REASON"; this is
		--   which one. `// 4096` stays, because it is the correct arithmetic and does not depend on an
		--   overflow coincidence — but nobody should look here for the reason a route was refused.
		-- ⚠ HOW IT WAS CAUGHT, which is the reusable part: repairing ice_pftest gave the height path a real
		--   assertion for the first time (check H), and reintroducing this "bug" as a negative control did
		--   not move a single tile. A root cause that a test cannot reproduce is a hypothesis.
		local sum = D + ((n[3] * pz + 0x800) // 4096) + ((n[1] * px + 0x800) // 4096)
		return (-sum * 4096) // n[2]
	end
	-- ⭐ THE ENGINE'S OWN LAYER CHOICE (FUN_0218DB0C): decode every candidate, skip the ones whose Class
	-- is 0xFF, and keep the one whose height is STRICTLY nearest the query position's current Y — so an
	-- equal-height tie keeps layer 0, and a WALL competes and can win (validity is not walkability). The
	-- winner supplies BOTH the TileType and the height; they are never chosen separately.
	-- ⛔ SO IT IS NEVER "walkable in either layer". Dragonspiral 2F has seven tiles that are floor in
	-- layer 1 and wall in layer 0 at exactly the same height: they tie, layer 0 wins, and they stay solid.
	-- What the second layer is really for is HEIGHT — walking the fallen columns at +6 while the ground
	-- underneath reads 0 (measured: research/re/ds_layer_reach.py).
	-- Returns tile, height, layerIndex for ONE chunk. With no y, or on a one-layer map, it answers layer 0
	-- exactly as this reader always has — so the 350-odd single-layer zones are byte-identical.
	function ow.cand(cs, c, tx, tz, y)
		local w, K = cs.w, cs.K
		local idx = ((tz - c.cz) + K) * w + ((tx - c.cx) + K)
		if c.rd then                                                    -- Black City / White Forest
			local rec = c.layer + 4 + idx * 0x18
			return u32(rec + 0x14) % 0x80000000, ow.hrec(c, rec, tx, tz, true), 0
		end
		local off = 4 + idx * 8
		local t0 = u32(c.layer + off + 4) % 0x80000000
		if not c.l2 or not y then return t0, ow.hrec(c, c.layer + off, tx, tz), 0 end
		local t1 = u32(c.l2 + off + 4) % 0x80000000
		local h0 = ow.hrec(c, c.layer + off, tx, tz)
		local h1 = ow.hrec(c, c.l2 + off, tx, tz)
		if h1 == nil or t1 % 0x10000 == 0xFF then return t0, h0, 0 end   -- no usable second candidate
		if h0 == nil or t0 % 0x10000 == 0xFF then return t1, h1, 1 end
		local d0 = h0 - y; if d0 < 0 then d0 = -d0 end
		local d1 = h1 - y; if d1 < 0 then d1 = -d1 end
		if d1 < d0 then return t1, h1, 1 end                            -- strictly nearer, so a tie keeps layer 0
		return t0, h0, 0
	end
	function ow.height(cs, tx, tz, y)                                   -- centre height, or nil = unknown
		-- Driftveil Gym above the shaft floor: one storey, one height, and it is the player's own. Two
		-- surfaces at the same floor are always steppable between; a platform parked at another floor is
		-- not reachable by walking at all, which pf_tile has already made solid. (Same branch as pf_tile,
		-- and it must be the same branch — a height read from the grid a hundred tiles below would make
		-- every step in the room fail the engine's 1.25-tile rule.)
		local dv = ow.dv.over(cs)
		if dv then return dv.y * 65536 end
		local w, K = cs.w, cs.K
		for _, c in ipairs(cs) do
			local lx, lz = (tx - c.cx) + K, (tz - c.cz) + K
			if lx >= 0 and lz >= 0 and lx < w and lz < w then
				local _, hy = ow.cand(cs, c, tx, tz, y)                 -- same choice pf_tile made, by construction
				return hy
			end
		end
		-- NOT LOADED IS NOT THE SAME AS HAS NO HEIGHT — the same distinction pf_tile draws two screens up,
		-- and the reason it matters is written out in full at ow.ic.hat. Live data still wins wherever it
		-- exists; this only fills tiles the engine has not streamed in, from the same layer the tile bake
		-- uses. Where the bake has nothing either (an unbaked zone, or a record the ROM gives no height
		-- to) the answer is still nil, and nil still fails open — which is what it should mean.
		if cs.zone then return ow.ic.hat(cs.zone, tx, tz, y) end
		return nil
	end
	-- Can the player step between these two tiles as far as HEIGHT is concerned? Unknown on either side
	-- means yes: this must never be the thing that refuses a route on a guess.
	-- (Fields on `ow`, not locals — this block is at Lua's 200-local ceiling; same rule as ow.ic/ow.slide.)
	-- ⛔ AND NO PER-ZONE EXCEPTIONS LIVE HERE. On 2026-08-27 I added six, for the Opelucid switch
	-- pedestals, on the reasoning that the gym's own save flags proved those steps must be walkable.
	-- Codex traced the movement path and refuted it: 0x14000 IS the player's rule (PlayerMoveCollCheck
	-- 0x021A5284 -> ActorRouteCollCheck -> the core at 0x021638EC, so the "ActorRoute" name was a red
	-- herring), it is symmetric, and it has no class or inclination dependence. The switch is reached UP
	-- ITS RAMP from the ledge — (35,22) h=0.503 to (36,22) h=1.000, a legal half-step — and the two-height
	-- drop onto the arm is the scripted hop, which writes the position directly and never consults this.
	-- ⭐ The geometry proves the same thing on its own: the hop moves you one tile across and two heights
	-- down, and only ONE starting tile makes it land on real floor — the pedestal. So you step onto the
	-- switch from the ledge, and the arm is where you are thrown, not where you come from.
	-- What my exception really was: a workaround for a reachability hole elsewhere that I had mislocated.
	-- The hole is real and still open (three ledges whose only rail entrance is gated by the switch that
	-- sits on them); it is not here.
	function ow.hstep(cs, ax, az, bx, bz, y)
		-- `y` = the height the mover is at, when the caller knows it. It decides which candidate each tile
		-- answers with on a two-layer map; without it both sides answer from layer 0, which is what every
		-- caller that does not track a height has always got.
		local ha = ow.height(cs, ax, az, y); if not ha then return true end
		local hb = ow.height(cs, bx, bz, y); if not hb then return true end
		local d = ha - hb; if d < 0 then d = -d end
		return d < 0x14000                                              -- the engine's own threshold
	end
	local function pf_walk(p)                                           -- can the player STAND on tile `p`?
		if not p then return false end                                 -- not loaded / off-map -> blocks
		if p % 0x10000 == 0xFF then return false end                   -- invalid tile Class
		return (math.floor(p / 0x10000) % 2) == 0                       -- Flags bit0 (collision) clear (ledges have it SET -> blocked)
	end
	-- ⚠ AND IT ASKS THE LIVE MAP AS WELL AS THE TABLE (codex-review 2026-08-26). The baked coordinates
	-- are only coordinates; the engine's precondition is that the tile IS quicksand. Requiring both means
	-- a wrong zone id, a patched map or a mis-generated table can only ever make this answer FALSE — the
	-- centre stops being a leaf and the router treats it as ordinary ground — instead of inventing a
	-- one-way sink on a tile the game would happily let you walk off. Fail closed, as everywhere else.
	-- (Measured: all ten centres really are class 0x7C and walkable, so this changes nothing today.)
	function ow.sink(cs, x, z)                                          -- is this the tile that always drops you?
		local ps = cs and ow.PIT[cs.zone]; if not ps then return false end
		for _, q in ipairs(ps) do
			if x == q[1] + (q[3] >> 1) and z == q[2] + (q[4] >> 1) then
				return ow.sandy(pf_tile(cs, x, z))
			end
		end
		return false
	end
	-- ── ICE: A STEP ONTO IT IS NOT A STEP, IT IS A SLIDE ─────────────────────────────────────────────
	-- Ola, 2026-08-20, standing in Cold Storage: "one step on ice slides you to you hit something, the
	-- path finder needs to understand this". Every tile query in the pathfinder assumed one press = one
	-- tile, which on ice is simply not what happens.
	-- ⭐ THE ENGINE'S OWN PREDICATE IS **IsTileSlidingIce = FUN_021AB368** (White), in the MapTile_* cluster,
	-- and it accepts exactly **Class 0x18 OR Class 0x10** — nothing else. Read out of the decompiler, and
	-- found the same way independently by Codex in the same session. Its result is not a bare boolean: the
	-- surface classifier FUN_021A5478 returns it as an INDEX (0 = ordinary ground, 1 = sliding) and
	-- FUN_021A55C8 dispatches through the fn-ptr table at DAT_021A55F8 with it, so index 1 selects the
	-- slide movement handler outright. ⚠ I found 0x18 first from the map data and nearly shipped it alone;
	-- 0x10 came only from reading the comparison. That is the OPTIMIZATION/no-guessing rule earning its
	-- keep — a histogram tells you where a class is used, never what the engine does with it.
	-- ⭐ THE SLIDE RULE, from the state machine (Codex, White ov021 + ARM9): the engine **locks the
	-- direction** (your input is ignored for the whole slide), **advances one tile at a time**, and stops
	-- **either before a collision or on the first non-ice tile**. That is exactly what ow.slide implements.
	-- WHERE THE CLASSES LIVE (research/re/ice_map.py re-derives this from the ROM at any time — every land
	-- record of a/0/0/8 through every zone's matrix):
	--   **0x18** — 882 tiles in three zones and nowhere else: **114 Icirrus Gym (700), 192 Cold Storage
	--     (170), 193 Cold Storage back room (12)**. Flags 0x80. ⭐ The game says so itself: zone 192's own
	--     script bank (a/0/0/3 bank 200) entry [6] is "When you walk through the containers, some places
	--     are frozen, so you go sliding around!" — the exact room Ola was standing in.
	--   **0x10** — 111 tiles in ONE zone, **363, the Route 10 Badge Check Gate**. Flags 0x40. ⚠ This one is
	--     wired on the ENGINE'S word alone: that hall reads like somewhere you simply walk through, not a
	--     puzzle, and treating it as slippery drops its standable tiles from 143 to 74. Both ways its exit
	--     stays reachable, so nothing is broken either way — but it is the one place this could be visible,
	--     and it is called out in todo.md for Ola to walk.
	--   ⚠ NOT the SNOW classes (0x17 / 0x40 / 0x0e / 0x0f, predicates FUN_021AB324/330/33C/348), which are
	--     a different group entirely and do not slide.
	function ow.ice(p)
		if p == nil then return false end
		local c = p % 0x10000
		return c == 0x18 or c == 0x10
	end
	-- ── WHICH CONTROLLER IS DRIVING THE PLAYER (the engine's own answer) ─────────────────────────────
	-- RE'd 2026-08-18 (Codex, White ov021; every claim re-checked offline against the ROM by Claude):
	--   Field+0x108 = the zone's FieldmapCtrl VTABLE, and its first word is a TypeID.
	--   TypeID -> mode through the four bytes at 0x021D35C0 (= 00 01 02 00): 0 GRID, 1 RAIL, 2 HYBRID.
	--   The vtable itself comes from the zone: zonedata byte +0x00 is an AreaID, and the area config
	--   table (stride 0x48, vtable pointer at +0x3C) hands out one of exactly three vtables. So the
	--   classification is authored data, and the whole game splits into 407 grid zones, 11 rail zones
	--   (28 Castelia avenue, 36, 66, 209, 211, 214, 241-244, 249 Skyarrow) and 9 HYBRID zones
	--   (30 Castelia Central Plaza, 114 Icirrus Gym, 121 Opelucid Gym, 137 the League,
	--   255 Village Bridge, 338-341 Celestial Tower).
	--   In a HYBRID zone the engine keeps a live controller object at Field+0x10C whose first word is
	--   the ACTIVE type, 0 GRID or 1 RAIL, and it switches WITHOUT a zone load: on an authored handoff
	--   tile (class 0xA3) an attempted step into a blocked side hands you to the other system, which is
	--   exactly how you leave Castelia's plaza for one of its streets. Zone 30's 0xA3 tiles are four
	--   bands, one per street mouth: x 3..8 and x 25..30 on row 19, x 11..14 and x 18..21 on row 22.
	-- Returns TWO answers: what is driving us right now ("grid"/"rail"/nil if unreadable) and what the
	-- zone is statically ("grid"/"rail"/"hybrid"/nil) — a caller that needs the whole map to be one
	-- thing must ask the second question, not the first.
	function ow.space()
		local gs = u32(0x02146268 + DELTA); if not inram(gs) then return nil end
		local fld = u32(gs + 0x20); if not inram(fld) then return nil end
		if u32(fld + 0x04) ~= gs then return nil end                    -- certify Field before trusting its fields
		local vt = u32(fld + 0x108); if not inram(vt) then return nil end
		local tid = u32(vt); if tid > 3 then return nil end             -- the mode table is four bytes long
		-- ⚠ Black is NOT -0x20 here: ov021's base shifts -0x20 but its CONTENT sits 8 bytes later in the
		-- Black overlay, so this table lands at 0x021D35A8 (measured 2026-07-28 by matching the 24 code
		-- bytes before the LDR literal — unique hit). Treat every ov021 absolute as measure-only.
		local m = u8(((GAME == "black") and 0x021D35A8 or 0x021D35C0) + tid)
		local static = (m == 0 and "grid") or (m == 1 and "rail") or (m == 2 and "hybrid") or nil
		if m == 2 then
			local hy = u32(fld + 0x10C); if not inram(hy) then return nil, static end
			m = u32(hy)                                                 -- ActiveTypeID, written by the switch itself
		end
		return (m == 0 and "grid") or (m == 1 and "rail") or nil, static
	end
	-- Is the RAIL controller driving us? The engine answers this outright; the validated live rail
	-- position is only the fallback for the frames where that chain is unreadable.
	function ow.onrail(rc)
		local now = ow.space()
		if now then return now == "rail" end
		return (rc and rail.onrail(rc)) and true or false
	end
	-- The grid map, but only where we may TRUST it. pf_chunks answers "is this a grid area"; in a MIXED
	-- area (Castelia's Central Plaza, Village Bridge, Celestial Tower, the League, two gyms) the answer is
	-- "partly", and the test that settles it is whether the collision map describes the tile the player is
	-- actually standing on. A rail corridor reads BLOCKED in the grid map — 155 of the 169 rail tiles that
	-- fall inside the plaza's map do — so this says no exactly when the grid is not what is moving us, and
	-- the caller falls back to the rail path. It is also the backstop for the one way rail.onrail can be
	-- wrong: if it called a live rail position stale, the tile under us reads blocked and we refuse rather
	-- than hand out grid directions from a rail.
	-- The player's own world Y, in fx32, or nil when it does not read sanely. +0x02 past map_x is the
	-- field ow.dg.y0 already trusts (map_y is at +0x08, so this is not it); the range guard is the same
	-- one, ±64 tile-heights, because a wild value must mean "unknown" and never "the ground floor".
	function ow.py()
		local y = ow.s32(A.map_x + 0x02)
		if not y or y > 0x400000 or y < -0x400000 then return nil end
		return y
	end
	-- ⛔⛔ THE HEIGHT A SEARCH STARTS FROM MUST BE A TILE'S, NOT THE PLAYER'S LIVE ONE.
	-- ow.py() is the live VecFx32 y, and MID-STEP that is an interpolated value on its way between two
	-- tiles. Worse, it lags the position: the engine writes the DESTINATION tile into the actor's current
	-- x/z at the START of a step (it keeps the old one in the origin fields, +0x36/+0x3A — see ow.cs.state)
	-- while y is still climbing. So on a ramp the player reports "tile (18,53)" with a height that still
	-- belongs to (17,53), and a search seeded from that pair starts one storey out of step with itself.
	-- Every other node in every one of our searches carries a TILE-CENTRE height (`ey = ny or cy`), so the
	-- seed was the one value in the whole search measured differently from the rest.
	-- ⭐ Ola, 2026-09-04, on Pinwheel's ramp: "it first said left, then up when the repel stopped working,
	-- so it didn't completely know where I was?" — a message box interrupts the follower, the re-plan that
	-- follows is seeded mid-step, and it can pick an equally good but different first move. It settles, so
	-- it is not what he was reporting; it is the benign face of the failure that ISN'T benign, which this
	-- file has already paid for once (the Icirrus loop: two stable endpoints, a deterministic re-plan, and
	-- a route that alternated for ever instead of settling). Removed at the source rather than guarded
	-- around, because a guard on "are we mid-step" would still leave the seed measured in the wrong space.
	-- The live y is still what CHOOSES the layer — it is the only thing that can say which floor you are on
	-- — and then that layer's own centre height is what the search carries. Unreadable falls through to
	-- exactly the old blind call, which is layer 0.
	function ow.seedy(cs, sx, sz)
		local y = ow.py()
		return ow.height(cs, sx, sz, y) or y
	end
	-- ⛔⛔ ASK AT THE PLAYER'S OWN HEIGHT, OR A TWO-STOREY MAP SHUTS THE WHOLE PATHFINDER DOWN.
	-- This validated the tile under the player with a HEIGHT-BLIND read, which on a two-layer map
	-- answers from layer 0 — the ground floor *beneath* him. On Opelucid's upper gallery (46,0) is a
	-- WALL in layer 0 and ordinary floor at height 6.001 in layer 1, so ow.grid returned nil, and every
	-- caller reads that as "Can't read the map here": the nav list, the routes, the lot, dead for the
	-- entire upper half of the gym — which is where three of the five switches are. Ola hit it two
	-- switches into the journey. (Zone 121 is the only zone with two permission layers, so this is
	-- inert everywhere else; and a nil height falls through to exactly the old call.)
	-- The sixth time this room has charged for the same mistake, and the first time in a function the
	-- whole game uses rather than in ow.dg.
	function ow.grid()
		local cs = pf_chunks(true); if not cs then return nil end
		if not pf_walk(pf_tile(cs, ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y), ow.py())) then return nil end
		return cs
	end
	-- STANDING IN FRONT OF A BLOCKED DOORWAY IS BEING AT IT. A warp region's own tiles are solid — that is
	-- how you trigger it — so no test against the region itself can ever be true, and which walkable tile
	-- beside it you end up on depends on the room. Any straight neighbour counts (never a diagonal: from
	-- there the doorway is not one step away).
	function ow.at_reg(rgn, x, z)
		if not rgn then return false end
		local inx = x >= rgn[1] and x <= rgn[1] + rgn[3] - 1
		local inz = z >= rgn[2] and z <= rgn[2] + rgn[4] - 1
		-- ⛔ AND ONLY FROM THE SIDE THE ENGINE ACCEPTS. rgn[5]/rgn[6] are the record's own approach code
		-- and transition type (see the build() comment). Types 0/1/5/6 fire from ON the tiles; everything
		-- else demands one specific side, and calling the other three "arrived" is what left Ola below a
		-- stairwell he could not walk into.
		local d, t = rgn[5], rgn[6]
		-- ⛔⛔ A STAND-ON DOORWAY ACCEPTS ONLY ITS OWN TILES. rgn[7] is set at build time for the
		-- types the engine fires from the tile the player is standing on, and only where that region is
		-- actually standable (the rule, its evidence and the two zone-136 exceptions are written out in
		-- the build() comment). Being beside one of these is not being at it — the engine's check bails
		-- out the moment it sees a walkable tile in front — and calling it arrival is what told Ola he
		-- had reached a Dragonspiral stairwell a tile before he was on it.
		if rgn[7] then return inx and inz end
		-- ⛔ A STAND-ON TYPE WHOSE REGION IS SOLID IS A WALK-INTO DOORWAY, and only types 0/5/6 skip the
		-- direction test there. The stand-on launch is a different function — CheckWarpMapChangeEvent
		-- FUN_0218ACF0, called from the same pipeline at 0x02189C76, which fires types 0/5/6 from the
		-- completed step; the front-tile branch of FUN_0218AEF4 does NOT exempt type 1 (Codex verify
		-- 2026-08-26). So zone 136 warp 0 — type 1, nine solid cells — opens only from its north side
		-- pressing down, and calling all four sides "arrived" there was the same mistake one level down.
		if t == 0 or t == 5 or t == 6 then
			return (inx and inz)
			    or (inx and (z == rgn[2] - 1 or z == rgn[2] + rgn[4]))
			    or (inz and (x == rgn[1] - 1 or x == rgn[1] + rgn[3]))
		end
		if d == 1 then return inx and z == rgn[2] - 1 end                -- press DOWN from the north
		if d == 2 then return inx and z == rgn[2] + rgn[4] end           -- press UP    from the south
		if d == 3 then return inz and x == rgn[1] - 1 end                -- press RIGHT from the west
		if d == 4 then return inz and x == rgn[1] + rgn[3] end           -- press LEFT  from the east
		-- No usable code: the same permissive answer ow.reg_tiles enumerates, region included. The two
		-- encode one rule twice — a predicate and an enumeration — and a divergence would have the
		-- router aim at a tile arrival never accepts. No warp in the game currently reaches this
		-- branch (measured), but they must agree by construction, not by luck.
		return (inx and inz)
		    or (inx and (z == rgn[2] - 1 or z == rgn[2] + rgn[4]))
		    or (inz and (x == rgn[1] - 1 or x == rgn[1] + rgn[3]))
	end
	-- Every tile ow.at_reg would call "arrived" for this doorway — the engine's demanded side, or all four
	-- straight neighbours plus the region itself where the record bypasses the direction test.
	function ow.reg_tiles(rgn)
		local x0, z0, nx, nz, d, t = rgn[1], rgn[2], rgn[3], rgn[4], rgn[5], rgn[6]
		local out = {}
		local function add(x, z) out[#out + 1] = { x, z } end
		if rgn[7] then                                                  -- stand ON it: the region, nothing else
			for a = 0, nx - 1 do for b = 0, nz - 1 do add(x0 + a, z0 + b) end end
			return out
		end
		if t == 0 or t == 5 or t == 6 or not d or d < 1 or d > 4 then    -- (type 1 solid: direction-gated, below)
			for a = 0, nx - 1 do for b = 0, nz - 1 do add(x0 + a, z0 + b) end end
			for a = 0, nx - 1 do add(x0 + a, z0 - 1); add(x0 + a, z0 + nz) end
			for b = 0, nz - 1 do add(x0 - 1, z0 + b); add(x0 + nx, z0 + b) end
		elseif d == 1 then for a = 0, nx - 1 do add(x0 + a, z0 - 1) end
		elseif d == 2 then for a = 0, nx - 1 do add(x0 + a, z0 + nz) end
		elseif d == 3 then for b = 0, nz - 1 do add(x0 - 1, z0 + b) end
		else                for b = 0, nz - 1 do add(x0 + nx, z0 + b) end end
		return out
	end
	-- The one of those the player can actually WALK to, nearest by real path length. A flood fill rather
	-- than a distance guess, because the whole point is that the nearest tile in a straight line can be
	-- on the far side of a wall — which is precisely how this doorway work went wrong twice.
	function ow.reg_aim(cs, rgn, sx, sz)
		local want, n, list = {}, 0, {}
		for _, t in ipairs(ow.reg_tiles(rgn)) do
			if pf_walk(pf_tile(cs, t[1], t[2])) then
				want[t[1] * 100000 + t[2]] = t; n = n + 1; list[n] = t
			end
		end
		if n == 0 then return nil end
		-- ⚠ CAPPED, like pf_astar's own 15000-node limit. This runs on every re-plan, and a re-plan happens
		-- whenever the player steps off route — so an unbounded fill over a big outdoor zone's streamed
		-- chunks would be a lag source in exactly the places routes are longest. Hitting the cap returns
		-- nil, which leaves the caller's existing aim alone: fail soft, never fail loud.
		local seen, q, head, n2 = { [sx * 100000 + sz] = true }, { { sx, sz } }, 1, 0
		local aimhard = ow.aocc(nil, true)                              -- nil when unreadable: no restriction
		while head <= #q do
			local c = q[head]; head = head + 1
			n2 = n2 + 1; if n2 > 15000 then return nil, "cap" end
			local k = c[1] * 100000 + c[2]
			if want[k] then return want[k] end
			-- ⛔⛔ THE SAME STEP RULE A* AND THE REACHABILITY FILTER USE — fences, height AND one-way LEDGE
			-- HOPS. Ola, 2026-08-25, in Twist Mountain: "it showed me 4, but I could not go to the other 3."
			-- Zone 201 is six pieces joined only by ledge drops; the filter credits those (it hops ledges) so
			-- it listed four exits, and this flood did NOT (it was plain pf_walk), so it could not reach a
			-- single approach tile across a ledge, answered "none", and pf_start refused all three. Listed
			-- but unroutable — the worst of both answers.
			-- ⚠ THE CELESTIAL REVIEW NAMED THIS IN SO MANY WORDS — "reg_aim uses pf_walk only, not A*'s
			-- sealed edges / height / ledges / occupancy" — and this morning I fixed the reachability half of
			-- that sentence and left the ledge half sitting there. Read a deferred finding as a list of
			-- hazards, not as history. (Occupancy is deliberately still out: A* retries without it, and a
			-- person standing in a doorway must not make the door unroutable.)
			for _, g in ipairs(ow.sink(cs, c[1], c[2]) and ow.NODIRS or PF_DIRS) do   -- a pit centre is a leaf
				local dir, dx, dz = g[1], g[2], g[3]
				local nx2, nz2 = c[1] + dx, c[2] + dz
				local p = pf_tile(cs, nx2, nz2)
				if not ow.edge_ok(pf_tile(cs, c[1], c[2]), p, dir)
				   or not ow.hstep(cs, c[1], c[2], nx2, nz2) then p = nil end
				local hopped = false
				if p and not pf_walk(p) and PF_LEDGE_HOP[p % 0x10000] == dir then
					nx2, nz2 = nx2 + dx, nz2 + dz                       -- one-way hop, landing 2 tiles over
					p = pf_tile(cs, nx2, nz2)
					hopped = true                                       -- no height gate: see the note in pf_astar
				end
				-- ⛔ AND ICE CARRIES YOU HERE TOO. This search claimed to use "the same step rule as A*" and
				-- did not: it still queued the adjacent tile on ice, recreating the exact passed-over-tiles-
				-- are-standable error that had just been fixed in reach.for_ (codex-review 2026-08-25,
				-- MEDIUM). Four rooms in the game have ice and doors: Cold Storage, its back room, the
				-- Route 10 gate and Icirrus. Occupancy stays deliberately out — A* retries without it, and a
				-- person standing in a doorway must not make the door unroutable — but the slide, the fence
				-- and the height rule all belong.
				if p and pf_walk(p) and ow.ice(p) and not hopped then
					-- `nil` occupancy, `aimhard` blockers: the same split ow.slide's header sets out. A
					-- person in a doorway must not make the door unroutable, but a boulder in the lane
					-- changes where the ice puts you down and this flood has to agree with A* about that.
					local ex, ez = ow.slide(cs, c[1], c[2], dir, dx, dz, nil, aimhard)
					if ex then nx2, nz2, p = ex, ez, pf_tile(cs, ex, ez) else p = nil end
				end
				local k2 = nx2 * 100000 + nz2
				if not seen[k2] and pf_walk(p) then
					seen[k2] = true; q[#q + 1] = { nx2, nz2 }
				end
			end
		end
		-- ⛔⛔ ...UNLESS WALKING IS NOT THE ONLY WAY TO GET SOMEWHERE HERE. Ola, 2026-08-25, standing on
		-- Skyla's platform with the badge in hand: "it says that I can't reach the entrance from the
		-- leader, so it can't route me back." This flood is a WALK-ONLY reachability test, and in a gym
		-- built out of disconnected islands it is right that the door cannot be walked to and wrong to
		-- conclude there is no route — the cannon (or coaster, or lift) is what gets you there. It is
		-- EXACTLY the mistake ow.NOREACH exists to name, and this function had the same premise buried in
		-- it, one layer below where the fix was applied: pf_start calls reg_aim BEFORE pf_build, so the
		-- refusal fired before the cannon planner was ever asked.
		-- ⚠ THE SIDE IS STILL THE ENGINE'S. Every tile in `list` already passed reg_tiles' approach-side
		-- rule, so handing back the nearest of them cannot walk him to a side the door does not open from
		-- — which is the one thing the fail-closed branch below exists to prevent. We give up only the
		-- CHOICE BETWEEN accepted tiles by real path length, which is meaningless here anyway: from
		-- another island every one of them is equally unwalkable, and the leg planner re-plans on landing,
		-- at which point this flood works normally and picks properly.
		-- ⛔ THE PRECONDITION A NEW NOREACH ZONE MUST MEET, because nearest-by-distance is NOT a generally
		-- safe multimodal tie-break (codex-review 2026-08-25, MEDIUM): every walkable candidate the door
		-- can produce must be ONE TARGET as far as that zone's leg planner is concerned. Otherwise the
		-- choice made here changes the route, and worse, a candidate the planner cannot classify makes its
		-- leg() return plain nil — which is "nothing to plan", so pf_build would fall through to ordinary
		-- A* and walk him confidently toward a tile on another island. Both current doors were MEASURED
		-- against that and both hold: zone 108's six walkable candidates are all on the entrance island and
		-- provably yield the identical cannon leg, and zone 63's six all classify to platform 1 under
		-- ow.rc.at. Both are pinned in research/re/z108_legtest.py — add the equivalent check before
		-- putting a fourth zone in ow.NOREACH.
		-- ⛔ ITS OWN TABLE, NOT ow.NOREACH. Those two exemptions answer different questions: NOREACH says
		-- "the walk-only nav filter must not run here", while this says "a nearest-by-distance tie-break
		-- among this door's accepted tiles is safe here". A codex-review (2026-08-25, MEDIUM) was right that
		-- inferring the second from the first hands the fallback to any zone that ever earns the first —
		-- zone 97 got it silently, and zone 114 got it the moment its planner landed.
		-- The precondition is the one stated below: every walkable candidate a door can produce must be ONE
		-- TARGET to that zone's leg planner. It is MEASURED for each entry, not assumed:
		--   63  Nimbasa   — all 6 candidates classify to entrance platform 1 (ow.rc.at).
		--   108 Mistralton — all 6 are on the entrance island and yield the identical cannon leg.
		--   114 Icirrus   — all 6 are mutually rest-reachable in ALL EIGHT mechanism states, so the
		--                   tie-break cannot change which leg ow.ig picks.
		--   97  Driftveil — deliberately ABSENT: it has no warp records at all, so no door can ever ask.
		-- All three are pinned in research/re/z108_legtest.py. Prove the same before adding a fourth.
		if ow.REGNEAR[reg.zid()] then
			local best, bd
			for _, t in ipairs(list) do
				local d = math.abs(t[1] - sx) + math.abs(t[2] - sz)
				if not bd or d < bd then best, bd = t, d end
			end
			return best
		end
		return nil, "none"                                              -- none of them reachable
	end
	-- "Is this the entry the live route is already going to?" — compared BY VALUE, never by table identity.
	-- The static list is rebuilt when a zone's rail data arrives late, which makes fresh tables for the same
	-- doorway, and p would then start a second route instead of toggling off (codex-review 2026-08-18).
	function ow.same_goal(p, e)
		if not p then return false end
		if p.gx == e.x and p.gz == e.z then return true end
		local a, b = p.reg, e.reg
		if a and b and a[1] == b[1] and a[2] == b[2] and a[3] == b[3] and a[4] == b[4] then return true end
		a, b = p.warp, e.warp
		if a and b and a.l == b.l and a.f0 == b.f0 and a.s0 == b.s0 and a.fn == b.fn and a.sn == b.sn then
			return true
		end
		return false
	end

	-- ── GETTING ONTO THE RAILS: THE HANDOFF TILES (Celestial Tower, 2026-08-25) ──────────────────────
	-- Ola, in Celestial Tower: "I tried going up to the second floor and that was fine, but beyond that
	-- the path finder was not working." He was right that something about the building is different, and
	-- it is the same class of mistake as the ice work: WE NEVER READ THE Y.
	--   pf_start aimed the GRID leg of a hybrid route at rail.line_near(the goal warp's own line) — the
	--   tile of that line nearest the player. rail.line_near measures in X/Z ONLY (rail.world returns a
	--   tile pair; the height is thrown away). In Castelia that is harmless because the street rails run
	--   at ground level, so the nearest tile of a street's line really is its mouth.
	--   The tower's rails are SPIRAL STAIRCASES that climb up to 14 tiles into the air over the middle of
	--   the room. Their x/z projection lies right on top of the floor you are standing on, so "the nearest
	--   tile of the goal's line" is a point in MID-AIR above the room, nowhere near the stairs.
	--   Measured over every standable tile of every hybrid zone (research/re/cel_rule.py, which runs THIS
	--   file's rail code against the real rail data under lua54.dll):
	--       floor 1F  the old aim landed you somewhere you could get on the rails from  86 of 245 tiles
	--                 — the stair foot is at the bottom of line 0, so from near it the projection is the
	--                 foot itself and it worked BY GEOMETRIC LUCK, which is exactly what Ola saw
	--       floors 2F/3F/4F                                                              0 of 158/81/83
	--                 — never, from anywhere. On 2F it aims at (23,16), a perfectly ordinary floor tile
	--                 UNDER the suspended staircase, so the route succeeded and walked him to the wrong
	--                 place with no error at all.
	--   ⛔ THE ENGINE'S OWN RULE: you can only be handed from grid to rail on an authored class-0xA3 tile,
	--   and only when the step you attempt from it is grid-blocked. So aim at the HANDOFF, never at the
	--   goal's line. ow.HANDOFF is that set, baked (research/re/gen_handoff.py); a whole-ROM sweep finds
	--   class 0xA3 in exactly the nine hybrid zones and nowhere else, which is the collision data agreeing
	--   with the area-config vtable classification from the other direction.
	--   Baked and not read live because the engine streams only the chunks around the player: Village
	--   Bridge is 96 tiles wide and its far handoff is simply not in RAM to find.
	--   Same measurement with the new rule: 413 of 413 on all four Castelia streets (up from 31..310),
	--   174/174 at the League, 245/245 on 1F and 147/158, 81/81, 79/83 above it — the shortfalls being
	--   tiles in a different grid component, which genuinely cannot reach the stairs at all.
	-- Doorways whose authored approach side has no standable tile, so the engine's side rule cannot be
	-- honoured and we keep the old permissive arrival instead. Exactly one in the game; re-derive with
	-- research/re/cel_approach.py. Keyed zone -> x0*1000 + z0 of the warp region.
	ow.WARP_ANYSIDE = { [0] = { [11037] = true } }        -- Black City zone 0 -> 2, region (11,37) 1x1
	-- The only two stand-on doorways in either game whose region has no walkable tile (zone 136, warps 0
	-- and 1; identical in White and Black — research/re/ds_warprule_black.py). Every other type-0/1/5/6
	-- warp in the game is standable, so these two keep the old permissive arrival instead of becoming
	-- unreachable. Keyed the same way as WARP_ANYSIDE: zone -> x0 * 1000 + z0.
	ow.WARP_STANDON_SOLID = { [136] = { [28058] = true, [31045] = true } }
	ow.HANDOFF = {
	  [30] = { 3,19, 4,19, 5,19, 6,19, 7,19, 8,19, 11,22, 12,22, 13,22, 14,22, 18,22, 19,22, 20,22, 21,22, 25,19, 26,19, 27,19, 28,19, 29,19, 30,19 },
	  [114] = { 9,22, 25,37, 25,38, 25,39, 29,47, 29,48, 29,49, 41,37, 41,38, 41,39, 45,47, 45,48, 45,49 },
	  -- ⛔ ZONE 121 IS THE ONLY HYBRID ZONE WITH TWO PERMISSION LAYERS, and this table was generated
	  -- from layer 0 alone, so it held TWELVE of its twenty-one handoffs. Every upper-gallery one
	  -- was missing — including (42,1), (28,27) and (38,17), which the Opelucid Gym route runs
	  -- straight through — and ow.hand.offdir could not name the step off the rails at any of them.
	  -- Pre-existing, found while building ow.dg (research/re/gen_handoff2.py measures all nine
	  -- hybrid zones: the other eight have ONE layer, so their tables were right and are untouched).
	  -- ⚠ (9,22) and (46,29) are NOT handoffs. Both are class 0xA3 in layer 1 and a WALL in layer 0
	  -- at exactly the same height, so the tie keeps layer 0 and they stay solid — the same rule
	  -- this file already documents for Dragonspiral 2F's seven tiles. Twenty-one, not twenty-three.
	  [121] = { 1,39, 10,7, 11,29, 12,50, 13,29, 16,17, 23,39, 24,41, 26,27, 27,6, 28,27,
	            29,49, 31,12, 31,34, 38,17, 41,22, 42,1, 43,22, 44,44, 48,29, 53,12 },
	  [137] = { 23,45, 25,36, 37,36, 39,45 },
	  [255] = { 7,29, 7,30, 7,31, 7,32, 7,33, 7,34, 88,29, 88,30, 88,31, 88,32, 88,33, 88,34 },
	  [338] = { 14,25 },
	  [339] = { 15,8 },
	  [340] = { 16,22 },
	  [341] = { 14,10 },
	}
	-- Which world directions have a usable GRID step from each handoff above, in the same order:
	-- bit 1 up, 2 down, 4 left, 8 right. This is only the static-terrain half of getting off: the live
	-- rail attempt must also hit a blocked/absent rail cell (ow.hand.offdir intersects the two). Every Celestial Tower
	-- handoff has exactly one, and it is the opposite of the way on (1F right, 2F left,
	-- 3F right, 4F left), which is why getting off could never be defaulted either.
	ow.HANDOFF_OFF = {
	  [30] = { 9, 13, 13, 13, 13, 13, 13, 13, 13, 13, 13, 13, 13, 5, 13, 13, 13, 13, 13, 5 },
	  [114] = { 8, 6, 7, 5, 6, 7, 5, 10, 11, 9, 10, 11, 9 },
	  -- ⚠ 21 entries, not 23: (9,22) and (46,29) are class 0xA3 in layer 1 but a WALL in layer 0 at the
	  -- same height, and an equal-height tie keeps layer 0, so the wall wins and they are not handoffs
	  -- at all. (The earlier note here called (9,22) an authored orphan — right that nothing reaches it,
	  -- wrong about why. Same rule as Dragonspiral 2F's seven tiles, above.)
	  [121] = { 7, 8, 4, 14, 8, 7, 11, 8, 4, 13, 8, 4, 7, 4, 11, 4, 13, 8, 4, 8, 11 },
	  [137] = { 2, 2, 2, 2 },
	  [255] = { 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 11, 11, 11, 11, 11, 11 },
	  [338] = { 8 },
	  [339] = { 4 },
	  [340] = { 8 },
	  [341] = { 4 },
	}
	ow.hand = {}
	-- One integer per rail tile, the same packing rail.warp_goals uses. A field, not a local: the chunk
	-- is at the 200-local ceiling.
	function ow.hand.k(l, f, s) return ((l * 512) + f) * 512 + (s + 128) end
	-- ⛔ DISTANCE **TO** THE WARP, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS DISTANCE FROM IT. The first cut searched
	-- OUTWARD from the warp with rail.step and read "the search reached this tile" as "this tile reaches
	-- the warp". That is only true if every move is reversible, and the only support for that was a
	-- sentence in a docstring. Measured (research/re/cel_reversible.py: every walkable tile x every button
	-- x all 17 areas) the general claim is FALSE — **174 of the game's 22,716 rail moves are ONE-WAY**, in
	-- areas 1, 3, 5, 7 and 8, and area 3 is OPELUCID GYM, itself a hybrid zone, excluded from this code
	-- only because it has no rail warp to route to. One row from being wrong.
	-- So build the REVERSE graph first — for every walkable tile, every successor rail.step admits — and
	-- BFS backward from the warp along it. That is a genuine distance-to-goal for BOTH consumers: `pick`
	-- learns which handoffs really reach the warp (no false negatives from a one-way prefilter either),
	-- and `cross` can order presses by real progress rather than by a mirror image of it.
	-- Cost: one pass over the area's walkable tiles x 4 buttons, cached per graph generation — the same
	-- order as rail.nearest's own one-off tile map, and per plan, never per frame.
	function ow.hand.pred(rc)
		local c = ow.hand.pc
		if c and c.gen == rc.gen and c.sig == ow.hand.dsig(rc) then return c.p end
		local p = {}
		for lid = 0, rc.nl - 1 do
			local w, h = rail.block(rc, lid)
			if w then
				local half = math.floor(w / 2)
				for f = 0, h - 1 do
					for s = -half, half do
						if rail.walk(rc, lid, f, s) then
							local from = ow.hand.k(lid, f, s)
							for b = 1, 4 do
								local nl, nf, ns = rail.move(rc, lid, f, s, b)   -- ramps join components here too
								if nl then
									local to = ow.hand.k(nl, nf, ns)
									if to ~= from then
										local t = p[to]; if not t then t = {}; p[to] = t end
										t[#t + 1] = from
									end
								end
							end
						end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		ow.hand.pc = { gen = rc.gen, sig = ow.hand.dsig(rc), p = p }
		return p
	end
	-- The disabled-line list is part of every rail identity here: rail.step honours FieldRailSystem's
	-- temporarily disabled lines, which rail.sys re-reads on every call, so a map computed before a story
	-- event disabled a line would quietly outlive the graph it was measured on.
	function ow.hand.dsig(rc)
		local sig = ""
		for i = 0, rc.nl - 1 do if rc.dis[i] then sig = sig .. i .. "," end end
		return sig
	end
	-- Every rail tile from which SOME press opens this warp, with the press. The single source of
	-- truth for the exact-trigger work: built once per (graph generation, disabled lines, warp) and
	-- reused, because pf_update's retry spells call ow.replan on every frame for up to half a second
	-- and an uncached full-area scan there would be a per-frame scan in all but name (codex-review
	-- 2026-08-25, HIGH). Returns nil, never a guess, if nothing triggers.
	function ow.hand.trigset(rc, warp)
		local sig = ow.hand.dsig(rc)
		local c = ow.hand.tc
		if c and c.gen == rc.gen and c.sig == sig and c.l == warp.l and c.f == warp.f0
		   and c.s == warp.s0 and c.fn == warp.fn and c.sn == warp.sn then return c.set, c.press end
		local set, press, any = {}, {}, false
		for lid = 0, rc.nl - 1 do
			local bw, bh = rail.block(rc, lid)
			if bw then
				local half = math.floor(bw / 2)
				for f = 0, bh - 1 do
					for sd = -half, half do
						if rail.walk(rc, lid, f, sd) then
							local d = ow.hand.trigger(rc, warp, lid, f, sd)
							if d then
								local k = ow.hand.k(lid, f, sd)
								set[k] = true; press[k] = d; any = true
							end
						end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		if not any then return nil end
		ow.hand.tc = { gen = rc.gen, sig = sig, l = warp.l, f = warp.f0, s = warp.s0,
		               fn = warp.fn, sn = warp.sn, set = set, press = press }
		return set, press
	end
	function ow.hand.dist(rc, warp)
		-- ⛔ KEY ON THE GRAPH'S GENERATION, NOT ON rc.tm — the tower proved that pointer gets reused for
		-- the next floor's area, which is precisely how 2F's graph got served to a player on 3F. And on the
		-- warp's SIZE as well as its origin: rail.warp_goals reads fn/sn, so two regions sharing an origin
		-- but differing in extent would otherwise share a map (codex-review 2026-08-25, MEDIUM).
		local sig = ow.hand.dsig(rc)
		local c = ow.hand.c
		if c and c.gen == rc.gen and c.l == warp.l and c.f == warp.f0 and c.s == warp.s0
		   and c.fn == warp.fn and c.sn == warp.sn and c.sig == sig then
			return c.d
		end
		-- ⛔ SEED FROM THE EXACT TRIGGERS, NOT rail.warp_goals. warp_goals is the (front,side)
		-- adjacency guess; seeding the reverse search from it meant handoff choice and crossing presses
		-- were still measured to a guessed approach rather than to tiles a press provably opens the
		-- warp from (codex-review 2026-08-25, HIGH). One exact set, used by dist, pick, cross and the
		-- rail search alike, cached with the same key so a retry spell cannot rebuild it per frame.
		local goals = ow.hand.trigset(rc, warp); if not goals then return nil end
		local pred = ow.hand.pred(rc)
		local d, q, head = {}, {}, 1
		for k in pairs(goals) do d[k] = 0; q[#q + 1] = k end
		if #q == 0 then return nil end
		while head <= #q do                                             -- backward along the reverse edges:
			local cur = q[head]; head = head + 1                        -- d[x] = real steps from x TO the warp
			local dd = d[cur]
			local ps = pred[cur]
			if ps then
				for i = 1, #ps do
					if not d[ps[i]] then d[ps[i]] = dd + 1; q[#q + 1] = ps[i] end
				end
			end
		end
		ow.hand.c = { gen = rc.gen, l = warp.l, f = warp.f0, s = warp.s0,
		              fn = warp.fn, sn = warp.sn, sig = sig, d = d }
		return d
	end
	-- The rail tile UNDER a handoff tile. Required to be an EXACT match, not merely the nearest: all 65
	-- handoff tiles in the game sit exactly on a walkable rail tile (measured), so anything less means we
	-- are reading the wrong area and the caller must fall back rather than aim at a guess.
	function ow.hand.rail_at(rc, x, z)
		local l, f, s, d = rail.nearest(rc, x, z)
		if l and d == 0 then return l, f, s end
		return nil
	end
	-- The handoff to walk to for this goal: the one nearest the player among those that GENUINELY reach
	-- it. `d` is now a real distance-TO-the-warp map (see ow.hand.dist), so membership in it IS the
	-- reachability test — no separate forward search, and no one-way prefilter to produce false negatives.
	-- Manhattan distance is enough to choose between them: scored against a full grid flood fill over every
	-- hybrid zone it sometimes picks a different tile of the same opening, never a worse one (identical
	-- success counts on all 15 rail warps). Ties broken by coordinate so the answer is deterministic —
	-- table.sort is not stable, and an arbitrary winner would make a bug here irreproducible.
	-- GETTING OFF THE RAIL, which is the mirror of ow.hand.pick. A goal that lives on the GRID cannot be
	-- reached along a rail at all, so the rail half of the route ends at a handoff and the grid half takes
	-- over from there (the crossing check in pf_update re-plans on the space change). Which handoff: the
	-- one nearest the GOAL, because that is the one whose grid side is closest to where we are heading —
	-- for the tower there is exactly one per floor, so this is exact there. Nil outside the nine hybrid
	-- zones, which keeps every pure-rail zone on its old path.
	-- ⛔ RETURN EVERY HANDOFF, NOT THE ONE NEAREST THE GOAL IN A STRAIGHT LINE. Icirrus and Opelucid have
	-- rail graphs in 11 and 3 DISCONNECTED components, so the handoff closest to where we are heading can
	-- sit on a component we cannot reach at all while another is perfectly fine — the same "nearest in a
	-- straight line is not nearest" trap as the doorway aim (codex-review 2026-08-25, HIGH). Hand the whole
	-- set to the rail search and let it pick one it can actually walk to.
	function ow.hand.exit(rc)
		local H = rc and ow.HANDOFF[reg.zid()]; if not H then return nil end
		local set, n = {}, 0
		for i = 1, #H - 1, 2 do
			local l, f, sd = ow.hand.rail_at(rc, H[i], H[i + 1])
			if l then set[ow.hand.k(l, f, sd)] = true; n = n + 1 end
		end
		if n == 0 then return nil end
		return set
	end
	-- ⛔ ARRIVING AT A RAIL WARP IS ONE PRESS SHORT OF GOING THROUGH IT, AND THE PRESS IS NOT GUESSABLE.
	-- Ola, on the tower: "it isn't finding the correct spot for the path finder to call the destination,
	-- this only seems to be a thing for rail warps." He was right, and the reason is that a rail route
	-- ENDS on a step-in tile — correctly, because the warp's own tiles cannot be stood on — and then said
	-- "Arrived" with nothing about the one move left. On the grid you are beside a doorway you can feel
	-- your way into; on a rail you are on a stair with no cue at all.
	-- Derived exactly, per warp, by asking rail.attempt what each press TRIES to do (research/re/
	-- cel_trigger.py). The tower's answer: 1F press LEFT, 2F RIGHT, 3F LEFT, 4F RIGHT — **it alternates
	-- with the spiral's direction**, so it could never have been guessed or defaulted.
	function ow.hand.trigger(rc, warp, l, f, s)
		if not (rc and warp and l) then return nil end
		local fn, sn = math.max(warp.fn or 1, 1), math.max(warp.sn or 1, 1)
		for _, g in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
			local nl, nf, ns = rail.attempt(rc, l, f, s, ow.RAIL_BTN[g[1]])
			if nl == warp.l and nf >= warp.f0 and nf < warp.f0 + fn
			   and ns >= warp.s0 and ns < warp.s0 + sn then
				return g[1]
			end
		end
		return nil
	end
	-- Getting OFF at a handoff: the usable grid neighbour that heads toward the goal. HANDOFF_OFF is baked
	-- because at this moment the RAIL controller is driving, so ow.grid() is nil. But that mask alone is not
	-- an exit: 021A7030 switches controllers only when the SAME press's raw rail attempt hits a blocked or
	-- absent rail cell. Disabled lines can clamp that attempt onto another live cell — pose-dependent in
	-- Opelucid — so intersect the baked grid half with rail.attempt live. Exhaustive zone-121 oracle:
	-- 1,008 static-mask disagreements across 5,376 handoff/pose/direction queries; this intersection: zero.
	function ow.hand.offdir(hx, hz, gx, gz)
		local H, M = ow.HANDOFF[reg.zid()], ow.HANDOFF_OFF[reg.zid()]
		if not (H and M) then return nil end
		local rc = rail.sys(); if not rc then return nil end
		local l, f, s = ow.hand.rail_at(rc, hx, hz); if not l then return nil end
		local here = rail.tileat(rc, l, f, s)
		if not here or here % 0x10000 ~= 0xA3 then return nil end           -- controller's own handoff gate
		for i = 1, #H - 1, 2 do
			if H[i] == hx and H[i + 1] == hz then
				local m, best, dir = M[(i + 1) // 2], nil, nil
				for b, g in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do                            -- PF_DIRS order IS the bit order
					if m and (m // (2 ^ (b - 1))) % 2 >= 1 then
						local al, af, as = rail.attempt(rc, l, f, s, ow.RAIL_BTN[g[1]])
						local t = al and rail.tileat(rc, al, af, as)
						if al and (not t or t % 0x10000 == 0xFF or (t // 0x10000) % 2 == 1) then
							local d = math.abs(hx + g[2] - gx) + math.abs(hz + g[3] - gz)
							if not best or d < best then best, dir = d, g[1] end
						end
					end
				end
				return dir
			end
		end
		return nil
	end
	function ow.hand.pick(rc, warp, sx, sz)
		local H = ow.HANDOFF[reg.zid()]; if not (H and rc and warp) then return nil end
		local d = ow.hand.dist(rc, warp); if not d then return nil end
		local best
		for i = 1, #H - 1, 2 do
			local hx, hz = H[i], H[i + 1]
			local l, f, s = ow.hand.rail_at(rc, hx, hz)
			if l and d[ow.hand.k(l, f, s)] then
				local m = math.abs(hx - sx) + math.abs(hz - sz)
				if not best or m < best.m or (m == best.m and (hx < best.x or (hx == best.x and hz < best.z)))
				then best = { x = hx, z = hz, m = m } end
			end
		end
		if best then return best.x, best.z end
		return nil
	end
	-- Standing ON a handoff tile: which way to press to be handed onto the rails TOWARD the goal.
	-- ⛔ NOT "whichever blocked side points at the goal tile" — that was the old test, and it only ever
	-- worked because the old aim was a tile down the street beyond the mouth. On a staircase the goal is
	-- above you, so a grid bearing means nothing. Ask the rail instead: step from the tile we are standing
	-- on and keep the press whose successor is genuinely CLOSER TO the warp. Both of the engine's own
	-- conditions still have to hold — the grid step must be blocked, and there must be a rail under us.
	-- ⛔ FAILS CLOSED WITHOUT A GRID. `cs` nil used to reach pf_tile and crash on cs.cache — and the one
	-- caller that can pass nil is the N-dump, i.e. it crashed the diagnostic exactly when the grid reader
	-- was the thing that had failed (codex-review 2026-08-25, HIGH).
	-- Returns a direction word, or nil — and a nil here must NOT be turned into a guess by the caller.
	function ow.hand.cross(rc, warp, cs, tx, tz)
		if not cs then return nil end
		local d = rc and warp and ow.hand.dist(rc, warp); if not d then return nil end
		local l, f, s = ow.hand.rail_at(rc, tx, tz); if not l then return nil end
		if rc.dis[l] then return nil end                                  -- 021B9528 never binds a disabled line
		local here = d[ow.hand.k(l, f, s)]
		local best, dir
		for _, g in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do                                   -- PF_DIRS is up/down/left/right...
			local btn = ow.RAIL_BTN[g[1]]                                  -- ...and rail.step wants a BUTTON code
			if not pf_walk(pf_tile(cs, tx + g[2], tz + g[3])) then       -- the engine hands over only on a
				local nl, nf, ns = rail.step(rc, l, f, s, btn)           -- BLOCKED attempted step
				local nd = nl and d[ow.hand.k(nl, nf, ns)]
				if nd and (not here or nd < here) and (not best or nd < best) then best, dir = nd, g[1] end
			end
		end
		return dir
	end

	-- ── RAIL AREAS (Castelia City, Skyarrow Bridge, the other bridges) ────────────────────────────────
	-- BW has TWO collision systems. Everything above is the GRID one. Castelia and the bridges are RAIL:
	-- you walk along LINES joined at POINTS, and a tile is addressed by (which line, how far along it, how
	-- far off its centre) instead of by (x, z). pf_chunks() deliberately fails closed there; this is the
	-- rail half. Full RE + evidence: accessibility-docs/17, the two 2026-08-07 sections. Everything here
	-- was validated OFFLINE against the real ROM data first (research/re/rail_succ.py): 100% move
	-- reversibility across Castelia and the bridge, and A* button trails that survive line handoffs.
	--
	-- ONE LOCAL ON PURPOSE. This do-block runs at Lua's 200-local cap, so the whole rail subsystem lives
	-- in this table (same reason `reach` above is one table).
	--
	-- NO SCAN. It hangs off the fixed global the grid path already trusts:
	--   GameSystem = u32(0x02146268) -> Field = u32(GS+0x20) -> NoGridMapper = u32(Field+0x3c)
	--   NoGridMapper: +0x00 RailSystem, +0x04 RailLoader, +0x08 FieldRailTilemap
	-- RailDataHandle (0x30) is INLINE at RailLoader+0x08 (and copied to RailSystem+0x20). We read the
	-- loader copy, the one the loader itself fills in.
	-- ⛔ STREETS ARE SEPARATE MAPS (Ola 2026-08-07: "some streets are their own maps, as there are map
	-- transitions between them, and yes that main street is one line"). One rail file covers several
	-- zones, so its graph is legitimately disconnected — Castelia splits into 18 pieces and the main
	-- street is a single line standing alone. Confirmed from the other side too: the engine-exact
	-- successor rule and a naive one produce the SAME components, so this is the data, not the rule.
	-- ⇒ no zone->line map is ever needed: the live ComponentID says which line you are on, a search
	-- outward stays inside your street, and streets are joined by WARPS, which the nav list already offers.
	rail = {}
	rail.OPP = { [1]=3, [2]=4, [3]=1, [4]=2 }        -- direction codes are BUTTONS: 1 Up 2 Right 3 Down 4 Left
	rail.CW  = { [1]=2, [2]=3, [3]=4, [4]=1 }
	rail.CCW = { [1]=4, [2]=1, [3]=2, [4]=3 }
	-- Is the cached graph still the graph that lives at these addresses? A content fingerprint, not a
	-- guess: the FIRST and LAST line's endpoints and length, and point 0's world x/z. Celestial Tower's
	-- areas 14 and 15 differ at line 0's Point2 alone, which is what made this necessary; taking the last
	-- line and a point as well costs three more reads and makes an accidental match vanishingly unlikely.
	-- Cheap enough to run on every cache HIT, which is the point — the alternative was trusting a pointer.
	function rail.same(c, lns, pts, nl)
		local a, b = lns, lns + (nl - 1) * 0x48
		if not (c.L[0] and c.L[nl - 1] and c.P[0]) then return false end
		return c.L[0].p1 == u32(a) and c.L[0].p2 == u32(a + 0x04) and c.L[0].len == u32(a + 0x14)
		   and c.L[nl - 1].p1 == u32(b) and c.L[nl - 1].p2 == u32(b + 0x04)
		   and c.L[nl - 1].len == u32(b + 0x14)
		   and c.P[0].x == u32(pts + 0x30) and c.P[0].z == u32(pts + 0x38)
	end
	-- The live system, validated and cached by CONTENT identity (rebuilt only when the graph really changes).
	function rail.sys()
		local gs = u32(0x02146268 + DELTA); if not inram(gs) then return nil end
		local fld = u32(gs + 0x20); if not inram(fld) then return nil end
		-- certify the Field root before trusting anything hanging off it (Field+0x04 = GameSystem,
		-- Field+0x08 = GameData) — the same cross-check walk-probe.lua prints
		if u32(fld + 0x04) ~= gs or u32(fld + 0x08) ~= u32(gs + 0x1C) then return nil end
		local mapper = u32(fld + 0x3c); if not inram(mapper) then return nil end   -- absent in grid areas
		local tm = u32(mapper + 0x08); if not inram(tm) then return nil end
		local loader = u32(mapper + 0x04); if not inram(loader) then return nil end
		local sysp = u32(mapper + 0x00); if not inram(sysp) then return nil end
		local h = loader + 0x08                                                    -- RailDataHandle, inline
		-- ⛔ VALIDATE THE HANDLE, NOT JUST THE POINTER (CLAUDE.md: readability is not a type check).
		-- TileWidth is 0x1000 in all 34 retail files across BOTH games, so it is a free magic; counts must
		-- be sane; both arrays readable. A wrong chain dies here and every rail reader stays silent.
		if u32(h + 0x2C) ~= 0x1000 then return nil end
		local np, nl = u16(h + 0x00), u16(h + 0x02)
		if np == 0 or nl == 0 or np > 512 or nl > 512 then return nil end
		-- ⛔ VALIDATE THE WHOLE RANGE A CONSUMER WILL DEREFERENCE, NOT ITS FIRST BYTE. inram(base) alone
		-- lets a torn or wrong handle near the top of RAM become a plausible-looking graph, because the
		-- readers return 0 past the end instead of failing — which is the project's fail-closed rule
		-- inverted (codex-review 2026-08-25, MEDIUM). Check the last byte each loop below will touch.
		local pts, lns = u32(h + 0x10), u32(h + 0x14)
		if not inram(pts) or not inram(lns) then return nil end
		if not inram(pts + np * 0x70 - 1) or not inram(lns + nl * 0x48 - 1) then return nil end
		-- CROSS-CHECK graph against tilemap: exactly one block per line. True for all 374 lines in both
		-- ROMs, so a disagreement means we are reading the wrong object.
		local nblk, blocks = u32(tm + 0x08), u32(tm + 0x0c)
		if nblk ~= nl or not inram(blocks) then return nil end
		if not inram(blocks + nl * 4 - 1) then return nil end            -- the whole pointer array, not its head
		-- ⛔⛔ THE POINTERS ARE NOT AN IDENTITY. Ola, on Celestial Tower 3F: "I am trying to reach 4F and
		-- I can't reach it, it doesn't find a path." His N-dump said the handoff at (16,22) had NO RAIL
		-- UNDER IT and that line 0 front 8 mapped to world (19,22). Zone 340's area is 15, and area 15
		-- puts line 0 front 8 at (13,10) with a PERFECT match at (16,22) — but **area 14, which belongs to
		-- the floor below**, puts it at (19,23) and has no match at (16,22) at all. We were serving 2F's
		-- graph to a player standing on 3F.
		-- Cause: this cache was keyed on the tilemap/points/lines POINTERS alone. Celestial Tower's floors
		-- have near-identical rail areas (13 is 3 points/2 lines; 14 and 15 are both 4 points/3 lines), so
		-- climbing a floor frees those objects and the allocator hands the next area back AT THE SAME
		-- ADDRESSES. The key matched, and every cached derivative — c.L, c.P, c.half, and the lazily built
		-- c.wmap rail.nearest depends on — stayed the previous floor's. 1F->2F was safe only because area
		-- 13 is a different SHAPE and so allocates differently; that is exactly why 2F worked and 4F did not.
		-- Pre-existing, and nothing to do with the handoff work: the old line_near aim was reading the same
		-- stale graph. It could not bite before because the tower is the only place in the game where you
		-- walk between two rail areas of matching shape.
		-- TWO guards, because one of them is an assumption and the other is a measurement:
		--   ZONE  — a rail area is authored per zone (GetRailIDForZone), so it cannot change without the
		--           zone changing. Cheap primary key.
		--   CONTENT — re-read line 0's own fields and compare. This catches "same addresses, different
		--           data" whatever the reason, without trusting the zone rule, and it is two reads.
		-- ⛔ EVERY POINTER AND COUNT THE CACHED OBJECT WILL LATER USE BELONGS IN THE IDENTITY, not just the
		-- three it was built from. blocks / sysp / curves / ncur / np / nl all steer later answers, and the
		-- allocator reuse proved above can relocate one of them while leaving tm/pts/lns and the graph
		-- content alone — re-entering the SAME zone would then keep a freed disabled-line list or block
		-- array (codex-review 2026-08-25, MEDIUM). Comparing them costs six reads on a cache hit.
		-- ⛔⛔ AND **NOT** ON THE ZONE. The first cut of this fix keyed on reg.zid() too, reasoning that
		-- "a rail area is authored per zone, so it cannot change without the zone changing". That is true of
		-- the LOOKUP and false as a cache invariant, and Ola found the difference immediately: Castelia is a
		-- seamless city whose mappos_id flips between the avenue, the plaza and the ten streets **while one
		-- rail area stays loaded**, so the key missed on every crossing and the cache churned — rebuilding
		-- the graph and throwing away the lazily built c.wmap (1041 tiles in that area) over and over.
		-- His report: "it now only gives me 1 path find turn when going in the city... I go 20 left and it
		-- doesn't update". A ONE-WAY FACT USED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is the exact trap CLAUDE.md names.
		-- So identity is the DATA, not where the player happens to be standing: every pointer and count the
		-- cached object will later use, plus a content fingerprint of the graph itself. That is what caught
		-- Celestial Tower (areas 14 and 15 differ at line 0's Point2) and it needs no zone at all.
		local curves, ncur = u32(h + 0x1C), u16(h + 0x06)
		local c = rail.cache
		if c and c.tm == tm and c.pts == pts and c.lns == lns
		   and c.blocks == blocks and c.sysp == sysp and c.curves == curves and c.ncur == ncur
		   and c.np == np and c.nl == nl and rail.same(c, lns, pts, nl) then
			rail.disabled(c); return c
		end
		-- A generation counter, so anything that caches its own derivative of this graph (ow.hand.dist)
		-- can key on the GRAPH rather than on a pointer that just proved it can be reused.
		-- ⚠ It doubles as the CHURN counter the N-dump prints. A tightened identity can only ever cause
		-- MORE rebuilds, never a wrong answer — but more rebuilds throw away c.wmap (1041 tiles in
		-- Castelia's area) every time, so "how many generations in this session" is the one number that
		-- separates "the cache is thrashing" from "the cache is fine and the fault is elsewhere".
		rail.gen = (rail.gen or 0) + 1
		c = { tm = tm, blocks = blocks, np = np, nl = nl, pts = pts, lns = lns, sysp = sysp,
		      gen = rail.gen, curves = curves, ncur = ncur, L = {}, P = {}, half = {} }
		for i = 0, nl - 1 do                                            -- lines, read once per area
			local r = lns + i * 0x48
			c.L[i] = { p1 = u32(r), p2 = u32(r + 0x04), dir = u32(r + 0x08), curve = u32(r + 0x0C),
			           len = u32(r + 0x14) }
		end
		for i = 0, np - 1 do                                            -- points + their half-widths
			local r = pts + i * 0x70
			local slots = {}
			for k = 0, 3 do
				local id = u32(r + k * 4)
				slots[k] = { line = id, dir = u32(r + 0x10 + k * 4), half = u32(r + 0x20 + k * 4) }
				if id ~= 0xFFFF and id < nl then c.half[i * 1024 + id] = slots[k].half end
			end
			c.P[i] = { slots = slots, x = u32(r + 0x30), y = u32(r + 0x34), z = u32(r + 0x38) }
		end
		rail.islands(c)
		rail.cache = c
		rail.disabled(c)
		return c
	end
	-- ⭐ THE WALKABLE ISLANDS OF A RAIL AREA — connected components of the line graph, two lines joined
	-- when they share a point. Built once per graph generation (65 lines in the biggest area, so this is
	-- nothing) and cached on `c`, because it answers a question the GRID reachability BFS structurally
	-- cannot: `reach.for_` floods `pf_chunks()`, and a pure rail zone has no chunks at all, so it returns
	-- nil there and `reach.filter` then keeps every entry. That is why Victory Road offered all eighteen
	-- of its entrances at once from anywhere on the mountain.
	-- ⛔ THIS IS THE STATIC SHAPE OF THE MAP, NOT LIVE REACHABILITY, and the two must not be confused.
	-- The live disabled-line list (rail.disabled) is deliberately NOT consulted: a disabled line is a
	-- story event temporarily closing a route, which changes what you can reach WITHOUT changing which
	-- island a tile belongs to. Anything asking "can I get there right now" wants the disabled list on
	-- top of this, never this alone.
	-- ⚠ A SENTINEL ENDPOINT WOULD MERGE EVERY ISLAND, so it was checked rather than assumed: across all
	-- 374 lines of all 17 rail areas in the game, NO line has an endpoint of 0xFFFF or one past the
	-- point count (research/re/vr_recon.py). Point SLOTS use 0xFFFF for "no line here"; line endpoints
	-- never do. If that ever changed, every line carrying the sentinel would union into one island and
	-- the filter would silently stop filtering — which is the fail-open direction, but wrong.
	function rail.islands(c)
		local up = {}                                                   -- union-find over line ids
		for i = 0, c.nl - 1 do up[i] = i end
		local function find(a) while up[a] ~= a do up[a] = up[up[a]]; a = up[a] end return a end
		local at = {}                                                   -- point id -> a line meeting it
		for i = 0, c.nl - 1 do
			local L = c.L[i]
			for _, p in ipairs({ L.p1, L.p2 }) do
				if at[p] then
					local ra, rb = find(at[p]), find(i)
					if ra ~= rb then up[ra] = rb end
				else at[p] = i end
			end
		end
		local seen, n = {}, 0
		c.isle = {}
		for i = 0, c.nl - 1 do
			local r = find(i)
			if not seen[r] then seen[r] = n; n = n + 1 end
			c.isle[i] = seen[r]
		end
		c.nisle = n
	end
	-- Which island is the player standing on? nil when we cannot say — not line-form, mid-junction, a
	-- stale position that fails rail.onrail's world cross-check, or no rail area at all. Every caller
	-- must treat nil as "do not filter", never as "filter everything out": going quiet is the one
	-- failure mode this project refuses (a door never listed is worse than a door you cannot walk to).
	function rail.isle_now(c)
		if not (c and c.isle) then return nil end
		local p = rail.onrail(c); if not p then return nil end
		return c.isle[p.l]
	end
	-- ⭐⭐ AN ISLAND IS NOT A POCKET. The line graph says which TERRACE a tile belongs to; it says nothing
	-- about whether you can WALK there, and on this mountain those differ sharply. Ola, standing on the
	-- second terrace: "I am in a place where I can't route to anything shown in the nav list."
	-- He was right, and the map explains it. Line 15's tilemap has two SOLID ROWS across it (fronts 3 and
	-- 6), cutting the line into three pockets; he had come out of zone 219's door into the middle one,
	-- which is 8 tiles. The terrace as a whole is 143 tiles in 3 pockets, and the whole area is 1419
	-- tiles in 18 pockets against 11 terraces. So a list filtered by TERRACE still offered him a door
	-- (zone 216) and a slide that were on his terrace and behind a wall.
	-- ⛔ AND THE POCKETS ARE REAL LEVEL DESIGN, NOT A HOLE IN OUR MODEL -- checked before building on it,
	-- because building a filter on a movement rule that under-reports would HIDE doors. Every one of
	-- zone 214's 18 warps opens into a pocket that contains at least one other warp or slide
	-- (research/re/vr_reach_from.py): cave -> ledge -> slide down is exactly how this mountain works.
	-- His own pocket has the door back into 219 and two slide cells, which is the intended way on.
	-- ⚠ WEAK connectivity, deliberately. A* needs FORWARD reachability, but 174 of the game's rail moves
	-- are one-way, and computing per-start forward sets would cost a flood per press. Weak connectivity
	-- is a SUPERSET, so the filter can at worst offer something the router then cannot quite reach --
	-- never hide something it could. That is the right direction for the one rule this file will not
	-- break: a door you cannot walk to beats a door that was never listed.
	-- Built once per (graph generation, disabled-line signature) and cached -- the same one-off cost
	-- ow.hand.trigset already pays to sweep an area.
	function rail.pieces(c)
		local sig = ow.hand.dsig(c)
		if c.pc and c.pcsig == sig then return c.pc, c.npc end
		local pc, n = {}, 0
		local function key(l, f, s) return ((l * 512) + f) * 512 + (s + 128) end
		local adj = {}                                                  -- undirected: record both ends
		for lid = 0, c.nl - 1 do
			local w, h = rail.block(c, lid)
			if w then
				local half = math.floor(w / 2)
				for f = 0, h - 1 do
					for s = -half, half do
						if rail.walk(c, lid, f, s) then
							local k = key(lid, f, s)
							if adj[k] == nil then adj[k] = {} end
							for b = 1, 4 do
								local nl, nf, ns = rail.move(c, lid, f, s, b)
								if nl then
									local nk = key(nl, nf, ns)
									adj[k][#adj[k] + 1] = nk
									if adj[nk] == nil then adj[nk] = {} end
									adj[nk][#adj[nk] + 1] = k
								end
							end
						end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		for k in pairs(adj) do
			if not pc[k] then
				n = n + 1
				pc[k] = n
				local stack = { k }
				while #stack > 0 do
					local cur = table.remove(stack)
					for _, nb in ipairs(adj[cur] or {}) do
						if not pc[nb] then pc[nb] = n; stack[#stack + 1] = nb end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		c.pc, c.pcsig, c.npc = pc, sig, n
		return pc, n
	end
	-- Which pocket is the player standing in? nil whenever we cannot say -- every caller must treat that
	-- as "do not filter", never as "filter everything out".
	function rail.piece_now(c)
		if not c then return nil end
		local p = rail.onrail(c); if not p then return nil end
		local pc = rail.pieces(c)
		return pc[((p.l * 512) + p.f) * 512 + (p.s + 128)]
	end
	function rail.pkey(l, f, s) return ((l * 512) + f) * 512 + (s + 128) end
	-- ⛔ READ THE DISABLED LIST LIVE, NEVER ASSUME IT IS EMPTY (Ola 2026-08-07: "yes read that, that is
	-- important"). FieldRailSystem+0x50 is an 18-entry u16 list of temporarily disabled line ids, 0xFFFF
	-- = empty, initialised by FUN_021B947C and edited by FUN_021B9490 / FUN_021B94B4. It sits exactly
	-- after the 0x30-byte handle copy at +0x20, which is a nice consistency check on the offset.
	-- A disabled line is NOT wholly unusable: the engine keeps the small region where it overlaps enabled
	-- lines at its Point1, which is what rail.overlap computes. Which story events populate the list is
	-- still unidentified — irrelevant to the formula, and precisely why we read it instead of guessing.
	function rail.disabled(c)
		local d, any = {}, false
		for i = 0, 17 do
			local v = u16(c.sysp + 0x50 + i * 2)
			if v ~= 0xFFFF and v < c.nl then d[v] = true; any = true end
		end
		c.dis, c.any_dis = d, any
	end
	function rail.block(c, lid)                                         -- Width, Height of a line's tile grid
		local b = u32(c.blocks + lid * 4); if not inram(b) then return nil end
		return u16(b), u16(b + 2)
	end
	-- Can the player STAND at (line, front, side)? The game's own FieldRailTilemap_GetTileAtPos in Lua:
	-- localX = side + Width/2, localZ = front, then the same TileType decode the grid side uses.
	function rail.walk(c, lid, front, side)
		if lid < 0 or lid >= c.nl then return false end
		local b = u32(c.blocks + lid * 4); if not inram(b) then return false end
		local w, hgt = u16(b), u16(b + 2)
		local lx = side + math.floor(w / 2)
		if lx < 0 or front < 0 or lx >= w or front >= hgt then return false end
		local t = u32(b + 4 + (front * w + lx) * 4)
		if t % 0x10000 == 0xFF then return false end                    -- invalid tile Class
		return (math.floor(t / 0x10000) % 2) == 0                       -- Flags bit0 (collision) clear
	end
	-- The raw TileType at a rail cell (Class | Flags<<16), or nil off the block. rail.walk answers the
	-- yes/no; the ramps below need the Class itself.
	function rail.tileat(c, lid, front, side)
		if lid < 0 or lid >= c.nl then return nil end
		local b = u32(c.blocks + lid * 4); if not inram(b) then return nil end
		local w, hgt = u16(b), u16(b + 2)
		local lx = side + math.floor(w / 2)
		if lx < 0 or front < 0 or lx >= w or front >= hgt then return nil end
		return u32(b + 4 + (front * w + lx) * 4)
	end
	-- ⭐⭐ THE LAUNCH RAMPS. Dragonspiral 3F is "covered with one-way launch ramps" (Ola's walkthrough),
	-- and they are LEDGES — the same four classes the overworld uses, authored into the RAIL tilemap.
	-- 21 of them on that one floor, 3 on 5F, 8 in area 9 and 3 in area 3: 35 in the game, and without them
	-- the engine's own successor splits 3F into NINETEEN disconnected pieces (research/re/ds_railjump.py).
	-- So they are not scenery; they are that floor's only connectivity, and the router could not use one.
	--
	-- THE RULE (Codex read it out of White; Claude derived the same shape from the maps independently, and
	-- the two agree — research/re/report_rail_jump.md + ds_railjump3.py):
	--   • The rail evaluators FUN_021A5D24 / FUN_021A60FC call the SAME shared predicates the grid uses
	--     (MapTile_IsLedgeU/D/L/R at 0x021AB178/184/190/19C) on the cell the press attempts, and return
	--     the jump result 5 only when the ledge's direction EQUALS the pressed direction.
	--   • ⚠ That comparison happens in the engine's INTERNAL direction enum (0 Up, 1 Down, 2 Left,
	--     3 Right), not in rail button space; the translation to buttons happens later through the table
	--     at ARM9 0x0209E068 = {1,3,4,2}. Composing the two gives class -> BUTTON as below. The two spaces
	--     agreeing here is luck, not identity — exactly the trap CLAUDE.md names.
	--   • Result 5 dispatches to actor action 0x38, which is TWO tile-widths (0x2000 per tick for 16
	--     ticks) driven by the RailUnit, so the landing is the ordinary rail successor applied TWICE in
	--     the same direction: over the ramp, then one more. Either step can cross a graph point onto
	--     another line — (L17,5,0) + Right lands on (L19,0,0) — which is why a front+/-2 formula fits only
	--     19 of the 35 and this one fits 34.
	--   • One-way falls out of the same test: the ramp's Flags bit 0 makes it a wall, and only its own
	--     direction lifts that. Pressing back gives a different direction and it stays a wall.
	--   • NO height check runs on this path (the trace reaches no height core at all), and the engine
	--     does not look at the landing cell either — so we validate the landing ourselves and fail closed,
	--     rather than copy an omission the retail maps happen to make safe.
	-- ⚠ ONE CELL IN THE GAME DOES NOT FIT: area 7's L16 (front 1, side -1). No standable cell reaches it
	-- as a first step under the discrete model, so it is either decorative or a sub-tile lateral case the
	-- integer model cannot express. Both fronts found it independently; it is left alone, not smoothed over.
	rail.JUMP = { [0x74] = 1, [0x72] = 2, [0x75] = 3, [0x73] = 4 }      -- ledge Class -> rail BUTTON
	function rail.jump(c, lid, front, side, pressed)
		local rl, rf, rs = rail.attempt(c, lid, front, side, pressed)   -- the cell this press ATTEMPTS
		if not rl then return nil end
		local t = rail.tileat(c, rl, rf, rs); if not t then return nil end
		if (math.floor(t / 0x10000) % 2) == 0 then return nil end       -- not blocked: an ordinary step, not a ramp
		if rail.JUMP[t % 0x10000] ~= pressed then return nil end        -- a wall that this press does not open
		return rail.step(c, rl, rf, rs, pressed)                        -- one more, and it must be standable
	end
	-- ⛔ ONE SUCCESSOR FOR EVERY SEARCH, AND FOR THE REPLAY. The route is built by A* and then REPLAYED
	-- move by move to produce its waypoints, deliberately through the same function the player's own input
	-- goes through. Teaching only A* about the ramps would make every route that used one die at the
	-- replay with "replay" — a route found and then thrown away, which is the worst of both. So the ramp
	-- lives here, and everything that walks the rails uses this.
	function rail.move(c, lid, front, side, pressed)
		local nl, nf, ns = rail.step(c, lid, front, side, pressed)
		if nl then return nl, nf, ns end
		return rail.jump(c, lid, front, side, pressed)
	end
	-- The player's live RailPosition (PlayerState + 0x10). nil while standing on a POINT: points are
	-- junctions the engine passes THROUGH, not cells, and the tilemap getter refuses them.
	function rail.pos()
		local mp = A.map_x - 0x06                                       -- mappos base (map_x is mappos+0x06)
		if u8(mp + 0x12) ~= 1 then return nil end                        -- ComponentIsLine
		local side = u16(mp + 0x14)                                      -- PosSide is SIGNED
		if side >= 0x8000 then side = side - 0x10000 end
		return u16(mp + 0x10), u16(mp + 0x16), side                      -- line, front, side
	end
	-- ⛔ "A RAIL AREA IS LOADED" IS NOT "THE PLAYER IS ON A RAIL" (2026-08-18, Ola's Castelia report).
	-- Central Plaza runs both systems at once: you cross the plaza itself on the ordinary tile grid and
	-- its four street mouths on rails, and the engine picks per position. So every choice between the two
	-- has to be made from the PLAYER'S state — and that state must be VALIDATED, not trusted: the
	-- serialized RailPosition at mappos+0x10 simply keeps whatever it last held, so on the grid it can
	-- still read line-form and point at a street you walked out of minutes ago.
	-- The check that cannot be fooled is the one thing both systems must agree on: the world tile. The
	-- engine maintains mappos +0x06/+0x0E in either mode, and our rail->world conversion is exact to the
	-- integer tile (proven against Ola's Skyarrow capture), so a rail position that does not convert to
	-- where the player actually stands is not the position driving them. Two tiles of slack for the arc
	-- math, which is far tighter than any stale position: those land in another street entirely.
	-- Returns the validated position (handy as a route goal), or nil for "we are not on a rail".
	function rail.onrail(c)
		local l, f, s = rail.pos(); if not l then return nil end          -- not line-form, or crossing a junction
		if l >= c.nl or not rail.walk(c, l, f, s) then return nil end      -- not a standable tile of THIS area
		local wx, wz = rail.world(c, l, f, s); if not wx then return nil end
		if math.abs(wx - ow.s16(A.map_x)) + math.abs(wz - ow.s16(A.map_y)) > 2 then return nil end
		return { l = l, f = f, s = s }
	end
	-- W(line, F): the half-width allowed at internal position F (tiles*16), interpolated between the two
	-- endpoints' half-widths (FUN_021B9298). This is a RAIL-GEOMETRY limit and is NOT the same constraint
	-- as tile collision — a move must satisfy both.
	function rail.W(c, lid, F)
		local L = c.L[lid]; if not L then return 0 end
		local N = L.len * 16
		local h1, h2 = c.half[L.p1 * 1024 + lid], c.half[L.p2 * 1024 + lid]
		h1 = h1 or h2; h2 = h2 or h1
		if not h1 then return 0 end
		if N == 16 then return h1 end
		local x = F; if x < 0 then x = 0 elseif x > N - 16 then x = N - 16 end
		local num = (h2 - h1) * x
		local q = math.floor(math.abs(num) / (N - 16))                   -- signed division TOWARD ZERO
		if num < 0 then q = -q end
		return h1 + q
	end
	-- FUN_021B852C: EXACT equality against the point's DepartureDirections, in SLOT ORDER, first match
	-- only. A duplicate direction in a later slot is unreachable — verified against the retail data:
	-- exactly 3 such points exist across all 17 areas.
	function rail.first_line(c, pid, dir, skip_disabled)
		local P = c.P[pid]; if not P then return nil end
		for k = 0, 3 do
			if P.slots[k].dir == dir then
				local id = P.slots[k].line
				if id == 0xFFFF or id >= c.nl then return nil end
				if skip_disabled and c.dis[id] then return nil end       -- do NOT fall through to a duplicate
				return id
			end
		end
		return nil
	end
	function rail.ekind(c, lid, pid)                                    -- FUN_021B868C / FUN_021B86A4
		local L = c.L[lid]; if not L then return 0 end
		if L.p1 == pid then return 1 end
		if L.p2 == pid then return 2 end
		return 0
	end
	-- FUN_021B86BC: the region where a DISABLED line still works, because it overlaps enabled lines at
	-- its Point1. Only consulted when the candidate is actually in the live disabled list.
	function rail.overlap(c, lid)
		local L = c.L[lid]
		local straight = rail.first_line(c, L.p1, rail.OPP[L.dir], true)
		local sl = rail.W(c, lid, 0)
		if straight then
			local w = rail.W(c, straight, c.L[straight].len * 16)
			if w > sl then sl = w end
		end
		local fl = 0
		for _, d in ipairs({ rail.CCW[L.dir], rail.CW[L.dir] }) do
			local lat = rail.first_line(c, L.p1, d, true)
			if lat then
				local w
				if rail.ekind(c, lat, L.p1) == 1 then w = rail.W(c, lat, 0)
				else w = rail.W(c, lat, c.L[lat].len * 16) end
				if w > fl then fl = w end
			end
		end
		return fl, sl
	end
	function rail.emit(c, lid, front, side)                             -- a real, standable tile
		local L = c.L[lid]
		if not L or front < 0 or front >= L.len then return nil end
		if not rail.walk(c, lid, front, side) then return nil end
		return lid, front, side
	end
	-- ⛔ THE ENGINE TESTS THE POSITION YOU ATTEMPT, NOT THE ONE YOU REACH. A warp region's own tiles are
	-- collision-BLOCKED (measured: every one of the game's rail warps, 0 walkable tiles in the region), so
	-- rail.step — which models MOVEMENT and refuses a blocked tile at rail.emit — can never return one.
	-- Brute-forcing every tile and press against every rail warp in the game found exactly ZERO triggers,
	-- which is the proof: the question "what does this press attempt" is a different question from "where
	-- does this press leave me", and only the first one decides a warp.
	-- `raw=true` is FUN_0219E684's exact output shape: keep Point-2 line-boundary / out-of-width attempts
	-- for the tilemap collision test, and keep the current position when a Point-1 attempt fails (021B8DA2
	-- restores the subtracted increment). Ordinary rail.step still rejects both, as movement must.
	function rail.attempt(c, lid, front, side, pressed)
		return rail.step(c, lid, front, side, pressed, true)
	end
	local function trunc16(v)                                           -- serialize: divide by 16 toward zero
		local q = math.floor(math.abs(v) / 16)
		return v < 0 and -q or q
	end
	-- FUN_021B8564: accept a candidate, with one extra collinear handoff if the front overshoots an end.
	function rail.accept(c, lid, front, side)
		local F, S = front * 16, side * 16
		local N = c.L[lid].len * 16
		if c.any_dis and c.dis[lid] then                                 -- disabled: only the overlap region
			local fl, sl = rail.overlap(c, lid)
			if F > fl or math.abs(S) > sl then return nil end
		end
		if math.abs(S) > rail.W(c, lid, F) then return nil end
		if F < 0 or F >= N then
			local L = c.L[lid]
			local pid, cd
			if F >= N then pid, cd = L.p2, L.dir else pid, cd = L.p1, rail.OPP[L.dir] end
			local c2 = rail.first_line(c, pid, cd, true)
			if not c2 then return nil end
			if F >= N then F = F - N else F = F + c.L[c2].len * 16 end
			lid = c2; N = c.L[lid].len * 16
			if F < 0 or F >= N or math.abs(S) > rail.W(c, lid, F) then return nil end
		end
		return lid, trunc16(F), trunc16(S)
	end
	function rail.try(c, lid, front, side)
		local a, b, d = rail.accept(c, lid, front, side)
		if not a then return nil end
		return rail.emit(c, a, b, d)
	end
	-- FUN_021B88F4 — one pressed BUTTON from (lid, front, side) -> the single tile you end on, or nil.
	-- The lateral reframe (turning onto a perpendicular line swaps front and side, signed by which end
	-- of the new line meets the junction) is the piece a "natural" guess lacks; offline, adding it took
	-- move reversibility to 100% on both Castelia and the bridge.
	function rail.step(c, lid, front, side, pressed, raw)
		local L = c.L[lid]; if not L then return nil end
		local T, d = L.len, L.dir
		local of, os = front, side
		-- 021B9548: movement from a disabled CURRENT line is first pulled back into the enabled
		-- Point-1 overlap. Work in the engine's sixteenths until the final RailPosition serialization;
		-- truncating the overlap limits before the junction tests changes which line wins.
		local function apply(df, ds)
			local F, S = front * 16 + df, side * 16 + ds
			if c.any_dis and c.dis[lid] then
				local fl, sl = rail.overlap(c, lid)
				if F > fl then F = fl end
				if not rail.first_line(c, L.p1, pressed, false) and math.abs(S) > sl then
					S = S > 0 and sl or -sl
				end
			end
			return F / 16, S / 16
		end
		local function emit(cl, cf, cs)
			cf, cs = trunc16(cf * 16), trunc16(cs * 16)
			if raw then return cl, cf, cs end                                -- attempted position, even off the line
			if cl == lid and cf == of and cs == os then return nil end       -- unchanged serialization = failure
			return rail.emit(c, cl, cf, cs)
		end
		local function try(cl, cf, cs)
			local a, b, e
			if raw then a, b, e = rail.accept(c, cl, cf, cs)
			else a, b, e = rail.try(c, cl, cf, cs) end
			if not raw and a == lid and b == of and e == os then return nil end
			return a, b, e
		end
		local q, wbad, pid, cand, k, tf, ts
		if pressed == d then                                            -- + front
			q, side = apply(16, 0)
			if q < T and math.abs(side * 16) <= rail.W(c, lid, q * 16) then return emit(lid, q, side) end
			wbad = (q < T)
			pid = L.p2
			cand = rail.first_line(c, pid, pressed, false)
			if cand and not wbad then
				local a, b, e = try(cand, q - T, side); if a then return a, b, e end
			end
			cand = rail.first_line(c, pid, rail.CCW[pressed], false)
			if cand and side <= 0 then
				k = rail.ekind(c, cand, pid)
				if k == 1 then tf, ts = -side, q - T elseif k == 2 then tf, ts = c.L[cand].len + side, T - q end
				if k ~= 0 then local a, b, e = try(cand, tf, ts); if a then return a, b, e end end
			end
			cand = rail.first_line(c, pid, rail.CW[pressed], false)
			if cand and side >= 0 then
				k = rail.ekind(c, cand, pid)
				if k == 1 then tf, ts = side, T - q elseif k == 2 then tf, ts = c.L[cand].len - side, q - T end
				if k ~= 0 then local a, b, e = try(cand, tf, ts); if a then return a, b, e end end
			end
			if wbad then
				local cs = trunc16(rail.W(c, lid, q * 16))
				return emit(lid, q, side > 0 and cs or -cs)
			end
			if raw then return emit(lid, q, side) end                      -- Point 2 keeps the boundary attempt
			return nil                                                   -- q == T is the point boundary, not a tile
		elseif pressed == rail.OPP[d] then                               -- - front
			q, side = apply(-16, 0)
			if q >= 0 and math.abs(side * 16) <= rail.W(c, lid, q * 16) then return emit(lid, q, side) end
			wbad = (q >= 0)
			pid = L.p1
			cand = rail.first_line(c, pid, pressed, false)
			if cand and not wbad then
				local a, b, e = try(cand, c.L[cand].len - math.abs(q), side); if a then return a, b, e end
			end
			cand = rail.first_line(c, pid, rail.CW[pressed], false)
			if cand and side <= 0 then
				k = rail.ekind(c, cand, pid)
				if k == 1 then tf, ts = -side, q elseif k == 2 then tf, ts = c.L[cand].len + side, -q end
				if k ~= 0 then local a, b, e = try(cand, tf, ts); if a then return a, b, e end end
			end
			cand = rail.first_line(c, pid, rail.CCW[pressed], false)
			if cand and side >= 0 then
				k = rail.ekind(c, cand, pid)
				if k == 1 then tf, ts = side, -q elseif k == 2 then tf, ts = c.L[cand].len - side, q end
				if k ~= 0 then local a, b, e = try(cand, tf, ts); if a then return a, b, e end end
			end
			if wbad then
				local cs = trunc16(rail.W(c, lid, q * 16))
				return emit(lid, q, side > 0 and cs or -cs)
			end
			if raw then return lid, of, os end                            -- Point 1 restores the failed decrement
			return nil
		elseif pressed == rail.CW[d] then                                -- + side
			front, q = apply(0, 16)
			if q > 0 then
				for _, pp in ipairs({ L.p1, L.p2 }) do                   -- Point1 has priority over Point2
					cand = rail.first_line(c, pp, pressed, false)
					if cand then
						k = rail.ekind(c, cand, pp)
						if pp == L.p1 then
							if k == 1 then tf, ts = q, -front elseif k == 2 then tf, ts = c.L[cand].len - q, front end
						else
							if k == 1 then tf, ts = q, T - front elseif k == 2 then tf, ts = c.L[cand].len - q, front - T end
						end
						if k ~= 0 then local a, b, e = try(cand, tf, ts); if a then return a, b, e end end
					end
				end
			end
			return emit(lid, front, q)
		elseif pressed == rail.CCW[d] then                               -- - side
			front, q = apply(0, -16)
			if q < 0 then
				local a2 = math.abs(q)
				for _, pp in ipairs({ L.p1, L.p2 }) do
					cand = rail.first_line(c, pp, pressed, false)
					if cand then
						k = rail.ekind(c, cand, pp)
						if pp == L.p1 then
							if k == 1 then tf, ts = a2, front elseif k == 2 then tf, ts = c.L[cand].len - a2, -front end
						else
							if k == 1 then tf, ts = a2, front - T elseif k == 2 then tf, ts = c.L[cand].len - a2, T - front end
						end
						if k ~= 0 then local a, b, e = try(cand, tf, ts); if a then return a, b, e end end
					end
				end
			end
			return emit(lid, front, q)
		end
		return nil
	end
	-- A* in rail space. Returns {{dir=button, span=1}, ...} the same shape the grid pathfinder produces,
	-- so pf_build's segment merging and pf_update's turn-by-turn work unchanged.
	-- `goals` is a SET of node keys, not one node: a warp is a REGION whose own tiles are collision
	-- BLOCKED, so the destination is "any walkable tile you can step into it from", which is several.
	-- `pen` (optional): key -> EXTRA cost for entering that tile. Used for Victory Road's slides, which
	-- are ordinary walkable tiles the engine happens to launch you off (see ow.PF_SLIDE). Costs stay
	-- finite on purpose — a penalty must never be able to make somewhere unroutable, only unattractive,
	-- which is the same rule ow.PF_SAND follows for Relic Castle's quicksand.
	function rail.astar(c, s0, s1, s2, goals, occ, pen)
		local function key(l, f, sd) return ((l * 512) + f) * 512 + (sd + 128) end
		local sk = key(s0, s1, s2)
		local heap, hn = {}, 0
		local function push(fv, l, f, sd) hn = hn + 1; heap[hn] = { f = fv, l = l, p = f, s = sd }; local i = hn
			while i > 1 do local pa = math.floor(i / 2); if heap[pa].f <= heap[i].f then break end
				heap[pa], heap[i] = heap[i], heap[pa]; i = pa end end
		local function pop() local top = heap[1]; heap[1] = heap[hn]; heap[hn] = nil; hn = hn - 1; local i = 1
			while true do local l, r, sm = i * 2, i * 2 + 1, i
				if l <= hn and heap[l].f < heap[sm].f then sm = l end
				if r <= hn and heap[r].f < heap[sm].f then sm = r end
				if sm == i then break end
				heap[sm], heap[i] = heap[i], heap[sm]; i = sm end
			return top end
		-- ⚠ NO GEOMETRIC HEURISTIC. Rail space is not metric — a lateral step can teleport you onto a
		-- perpendicular line — so any world-distance estimate can overestimate and break admissibility.
		-- Plain Dijkstra (h = 0) is correct, and these graphs are small (Castelia's biggest street is a
		-- few hundred tiles), so it costs nothing worth optimising.
		local g, came = { [sk] = 0 }, {}
		push(0, s0, s1, s2)
		local n = 0
		while hn > 0 and n < 20000 do
			local cur = pop(); n = n + 1
			local ck = key(cur.l, cur.p, cur.s)
			if goals[ck] then
				local moves, k = {}, ck
				while came[k] do local m = came[k]; moves[#moves + 1] = { dir = m.dir, span = 1 }; k = m.pk end
				local rev = {}; for i = #moves, 1, -1 do rev[#rev + 1] = moves[i] end
				return rev, { l = cur.l, f = cur.p, s = cur.s }
			end
			local cg = g[ck]
			if cg then
				for pressed = 1, 4 do
					local nl, nf, ns = rail.move(c, cur.l, cur.p, cur.s, pressed)
					if nl then
						local nk = key(nl, nf, ns)
						if occ and occ[nk] then nl = nil end             -- someone is standing there
						local step = 1 + ((pen and pen[nk]) or 0)
						if nl and nk ~= ck and (not g[nk] or cg + step < g[nk]) then
							g[nk] = cg + step; came[nk] = { dir = pressed, pk = ck }
							push(cg + step, nl, nf, ns)
						end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		return nil
	end
	-- ── RAIL -> WORLD (Codex Q3; needed to place nav entries that only have world coordinates) ────────
	-- Data only, no game call. fx32: 0x1000 = one world unit, 0x10000 = one TILE. Serialized front/side
	-- are tiles. Positive side is to your RIGHT travelling Point1 -> Point2.
	--   type 0 straight: base = lerp(P1, P2, t); right = normalize(cross(P2-P1, up)); world = base + side*right
	--   type 1 XZ slerp / type 2 XYZ slerp: radial slerp about the curve centre, with the radius LERPed
	--     between the two endpoint radii (there is no stored radius or angular span — the span IS the
	--     angle between the endpoint radial vectors). Retail's largest span is 153.6°, so no arc needs
	--     the >180° choice. Expected error well under half a tile, which is inside what we need to pick
	--     the nearest tile to a target.
	-- We work in DOUBLES here (Lua numbers) rather than the game's fixed point: this only ever chooses a
	-- goal tile and prints distances, never feeds the engine, so the game's rounding is not required.
	local function fx(v) if v >= 0x80000000 then v = v - 0x100000000 end; return v / 4096.0 end
	local function vsub(a, b) return { a[1]-b[1], a[2]-b[2], a[3]-b[3] } end
	local function vlen(a) return math.sqrt(a[1]*a[1] + a[2]*a[2] + a[3]*a[3]) end
	local function vnorm(a) local L = vlen(a); if L < 1e-9 then return { 0, 0, 0 } end
		return { a[1]/L, a[2]/L, a[3]/L } end
	local function slerp_r(a, b, t)                                     -- FUN_021B9A68 + FUN_021B9B3C
		local ua, ub = vnorm(a), vnorm(b)
		local d = ua[1]*ub[1] + ua[2]*ub[2] + ua[3]*ub[3]
		if d > 1 then d = 1 elseif d < -1 then d = -1 end
		local th = math.acos(d)
		local st = math.sin(th)
		local dir
		if math.abs(st) < 1e-9 then dir = ub                            -- the game's sin(theta)==0 fallback
		else
			local w1, w2 = math.sin((1-t)*th)/st, math.sin(t*th)/st
			dir = vnorm({ w1*ua[1] + w2*ub[1], w1*ua[2] + w2*ub[2], w1*ua[3] + w2*ub[3] })
		end
		local r = vlen(a) + (vlen(b) - vlen(a)) * t
		return { dir[1]*r, dir[2]*r, dir[3]*r }
	end
	-- world position of a rail tile, in TILES (the space the nav list and A.map_x/map_y use)
	function rail.world(c, lid, front, side)
		local L = c.L[lid]; if not L then return nil end
		local P1, P2 = c.P[L.p1], c.P[L.p2]; if not (P1 and P2) then return nil end
		local a = { fx(P1.x), fx(P1.y), fx(P1.z) }
		local b = { fx(P2.x), fx(P2.y), fx(P2.z) }
		local t = (L.len > 0) and (front / L.len) or 0
		-- ⛔⛔ THE LATERAL DIRECTION WAS INVERTED, GAME-WIDE, AND IT HID BEHIND SIDE 0 (2026-08-28).
		-- The perpendicular built below is `normalize(cross(delta, up))` = (dz, 0, -dx). The ENGINE's own
		-- +Side basis is the OTHER one: ov063's ray setup takes `(-tangent.z, 0, tangent.x)` from the
		-- normalized tangent (Codex's read of the retail vector path). Those are exact negations, so every
		-- off-centre rail position we converted came out mirrored across the centreline.
		-- ⭐ WHY IT SURVIVED THIS LONG, and why the one live check that existed could not see it: the error
		-- is EXACTLY ZERO at side 0, and grows with |side|. Walking a rail lane you are at side 0 almost
		-- always, and the Skyarrow capture this function was validated against (line 13, front 1, **side
		-- 0**) is precisely the case that cannot distinguish the two. rail.onrail's +-2 tolerance then
		-- absorbed |side| = 1, so the remaining error only ever showed up as things being "a bit off".
		-- MEASURED against the engine's own conversion over all 138 launch/landing positions of Victory
		-- Road's slide census (research/re/vr_world_check.py, comparing against numbers Codex produced by
		-- running White's real routines under Unicorn):
		--        as shipped   33/138 exact, mean error 3.86 tiles, max 13, 95 past onrail's limit
		--        negated     117/138 exact, mean error 0.77 tiles, max  8, 21 past onrail's limit
		-- mean error by |side| before the fix: 0.00 at side 0, 2.40 at 1, 4.23 at 2, 7.00 at 3, 13.00 at 5.
		-- ⭐ THIS IS THE BUG OLA REPORTED: after a slide the terrace filter showed all 18 entrances again.
		-- Slide landings sit at |side| up to 5, so rail.onrail's world cross-check failed there and
		-- rail.isle_now returned nil -- which fails OPEN, i.e. "show everything". His earlier dump was the
		-- tell and I explained it away: `rail->world=(31,55)` while he stood at `(31,57)`, sitting exactly
		-- ON the tolerance at side 1. A constant offset is arithmetic, not noise -- this file's own
		-- Opelucid lesson, and I made the same mistake again one session later.
		-- ⛔ AND THE FIX IS IN THE **STRAIGHT BRANCH ONLY** -- the curved one was already right. Negating
		-- `sd` globally (the first attempt) fixed all 23 straight lines and broke the curved ones, and the
		-- residue named itself: every one of the 10 lines still wrong was curve type 1, every type-0 line
		-- was exact. Measured three ways over the same 138 positions:
		--        original            33/138 exact, mean 3.86, max 13, 95 past onrail's limit
		--        negate globally    117/138 exact, mean 0.77, max  8, 21 past onrail's limit
		--        negate straight    **138/138 exact, mean 0.00, max 0, 0 past the limit**
		-- No caller compensates for the old sign (every call site passes the engine's own PosSide
		-- straight through), so this is a fix, not a convention swap.
		-- ⚠ GAME-WIDE: rail.world underpins rail.onrail, rail.nearest, rail.line_near and rail.warp_pos,
		-- so this changes every rail and hybrid zone -- Castelia, Skyarrow, the towers, Opelucid, Icirrus.
		-- It can only move answers TOWARD the engine's, and side 0 (almost all ordinary walking) is
		-- unchanged, but it wants testing beyond Victory Road.
		local sd = side * 16.0                                          -- side is tiles; TileWidth 0x1000 -> 16 units
		local ctype, cen = 0, nil
		if inram(c.curves) and L.curve < c.ncur then
			local r = c.curves + L.curve * 0x24
			ctype = u32(r)
			cen = { fx(u32(r + 0x04)), fx(u32(r + 0x08)), fx(u32(r + 0x0C)) }
		end
		local base, lateral
		if ctype == 1 or ctype == 2 then
			local ca, cb
			if ctype == 1 then                                          -- XZ arc: centre Y ignored, Y lerps
				cen = { cen[1], a[2] + (b[2] - a[2]) * t, cen[3] }
				ca = { a[1]-cen[1], 0, a[3]-cen[3] }
				cb = { b[1]-cen[1], 0, b[3]-cen[3] }
			else
				ca, cb = vsub(a, cen), vsub(b, cen)
			end
			local rad = slerp_r(ca, cb, t)
			base = { cen[1]+rad[1], cen[2]+rad[2], cen[3]+rad[3] }
			local lb
			if ctype == 1 then lb = vnorm({ rad[1], 0, rad[3] })
			elseif rad[1] ~= 0 and rad[3] ~= 0 then lb = vnorm({ rad[1], 0, rad[3] })
			else local d = vsub(b, a); lb = vnorm({ d[3], 0, -d[1] }) end
			local chord = vnorm(vsub(cb, ca))
			local crossy = lb[3]*chord[1] - lb[1]*chord[3]              -- (lb x chord).y
			if crossy < 0 then lb = { -lb[1], 0, -lb[3] } end
			lateral = lb
		else
			local d = vsub(b, a)
			base = { a[1] + d[1]*t, a[2] + d[2]*t, a[3] + d[3]*t }
			-- ⛔ THE ENGINE'S +Side BASIS, not the other perpendicular. ov063's ray setup builds it as
			-- `(-tangent.z, 0, tangent.x)` from the normalized tangent; this used to be the negation of
			-- that, `(dz, 0, -dx)`, which mirrored every off-centre position across the centreline. See
			-- the long note on `sd` above for the measurement -- with this line the conversion matches the
			-- engine on all 138 positions of the Victory Road census exactly.
			lateral = vnorm({ -d[3], 0, d[1] })                          -- engine's normalize(cross(up, delta))
		end
		local wx = base[1] + sd*lateral[1]
		local wz = base[3] + sd*lateral[3]
		-- ⛔ INTEGER PART, NOT ROUNDED. Measured against the live game (Ola's Skyarrow capture 2026-08-08):
		-- standing at line 13, front 1, side 0 the game reports world tile (90, 275); rounding gave
		-- (90, 276). The engine's own tile pair (mappos +0x6/+0xE) is the integer part of the position,
		-- so match that exactly or every nearest-tile match is liable to be one tile out.
		return math.floor(wx / 16), math.floor(wz / 16)                  -- world units -> TILES
	end
	-- The walkable rail tile closest to a world tile — how a nav entry that only has world coordinates
	-- (a warp, a sign) becomes a pathfinding goal. Built once per area and cached; Castelia's whole
	-- area is ~1400 tiles, so this is a one-off, never per frame.
	function rail.nearest(c, wx, wz)
		if not c.wmap then
			local m = {}
			for lid = 0, c.nl - 1 do
				local w, h = rail.block(c, lid)
				if w then
					local half = math.floor(w / 2)
					for f = 0, h - 1 do
						for sd = -half, half do
							if rail.walk(c, lid, f, sd) then
								local x, z = rail.world(c, lid, f, sd)
								if x then m[#m + 1] = { l = lid, f = f, s = sd, x = x, z = z } end
							end
						end
					end
				end
			end
			c.wmap = m
		end
		local best, bd
		for _, e in ipairs(c.wmap) do
			local d = math.abs(e.x - wx) + math.abs(e.z - wz)
			if not bd or d < bd then best, bd = e, d end
		end
		if not best then return nil end
		return best.l, best.f, best.s, bd
	end
	-- The tile of ONE line nearest a world tile, in world coordinates. This is what the GRID half of a
	-- hybrid zone needs when its goal is on the rails. Aiming at the warp's own position picks whatever
	-- is closest in a straight line, which in Castelia's plaza can be a different street's mouth than the
	-- one you want (measured: routing to Mode Street from the east side ended on a tile that was not a
	-- handoff at all, so the crossing could not be named). Aiming at the nearest tile OF THE TARGET'S OWN
	-- LINE walks you to the handoff that leads into it — 12 of 12 plaza routes then end on a class-0xA3
	-- tile. One line's tile block only, and only when a route is planned, never per frame.
	function rail.line_near(c, lid, wx, wz)
		local w, h = rail.block(c, lid); if not w then return nil end
		local half = math.floor(w / 2)
		local bx, bz, bd
		for f = 0, h - 1 do
			for sd = -half, half do
				if rail.walk(c, lid, f, sd) then
					local x, z = rail.world(c, lid, f, sd)
					if x then
						local d = math.abs(x - wx) + math.abs(z - wz)
						if not bd or d < bd then bx, bz, bd = x, z, d end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		return bx, bz
	end
	-- ⛔⛔ THE TILE YOU ARE IN, NOT THE NEAREST TILE BOUNDARY. This rounded — `floor(v / 65536 + 0.5)` —
	-- and that is wrong for a position, because an actor stands at the CENTRE of its tile: tile 27 is
	-- world 27.5, and rounding sends it to 28. Every actor in a rail or hybrid zone therefore read
	-- exactly +1 in BOTH axes, which is precisely the "one up and one left" Ola reported for Iris and,
	-- before her, for Brycen in Icirrus — another hybrid zone. Her real tile is (27,2); the nav list
	-- announced (28,3); the N-dump agreed with the game because it reads the cached grid tile instead.
	-- ⚠ AND IT IS WHY MY FIRST TWO EXPLANATIONS WERE WRONG. I diagnosed a stale cached position and then
	-- a stale snapshot, and neither was the cause: a constant +1,+1 in two different sessions is not a
	-- race, it is arithmetic. The identity re-read I added for the route did fix the ROUTE — but only by
	-- accident of reading +0x3C/+0x40, the cached grid tile, which was right all along. The LIST kept
	-- using this conversion and kept being one tile out.
	-- Both callers convert an actor's world position to the tile it occupies, so both want truncation.
	function rail.wtile(v)                                              -- fx32 world coord -> TILE index
		if v >= 0x80000000 then v = v - 0x100000000 end
		return math.floor(v / 65536)                                    -- 0x1000 per unit * 16 units per tile
	end
	-- An ACTOR'S own rail position (actor+0x98, reached via the ActorPositionRail at +0x94), same shape as
	-- the player's. This is what makes an NPC an EXACT pathfinding goal with no world->rail conversion.
	-- ⛔ VALIDATED, never trusted: the offset came from a report, so require IsLine, a line id inside this
	-- area's graph, and a tile the player could actually stand on. If any of that fails we return nil and
	-- the caller falls back to matching by world position — wrong-but-plausible coordinates would send the
	-- pathfinder somewhere confidently wrong, which is the failure mode this project cares most about.
	function rail.actor_pos(c, o)
		local r = o + 0x98
		if u8(r + 0x02) ~= 1 then return nil end                         -- ComponentIsLine
		local lid, front = u16(r + 0x00), u16(r + 0x06)
		local side = u16(r + 0x04); if side >= 0x8000 then side = side - 0x10000 end
		if lid >= c.nl then return nil end
		if not rail.walk(c, lid, front, side) then return nil end
		return { l = lid, f = front, s = side }
	end
	-- ── RAIL WARPS ARE RECTANGULAR REGIONS, AND THEIR OWN TILES ARE BLOCKED ──────────────────────────
	-- RE'd by Codex from the engine's own matcher (FUN_02162E20, the White CheckWarpPositionMatchRail)
	-- after Ola found both Skyarrow transitions announced as "3000 south". A warp record in a RAIL zone
	-- is not a point in world units at all; it is a region in rail space:
	--   +0x06 kind   — the engine's OWN discriminator (the grid matcher rejects nonzero, the rail
	--                  matcher rejects zero), so this is how we tell which reading applies
	--   +0x08 line   +0x0A front origin   +0x0C side origin (SIGNED)   +0x0E front count   +0x10 side count
	-- matched lower-inclusive / upper-exclusive against the player's serialized RailPosition.
	-- ⛔ THE REGION'S TILES ARE COLLISION-BLOCKED — verified on Skyarrow: all 7 tiles of warp 0 and all
	-- 5 of warp 1 read class 1, flags 0x0081, the standard solid obstacle. That is why fitting the field
	-- layout against WALKABLE tiles could never converge: the predicate itself was wrong. The engine
	-- tests the ATTEMPTED position, so you trigger a warp by walking INTO it, which means the route must
	-- end on a walkable tile beside the region, not on it.
	function rail.warp_pos(c, w)                                        -- a representative point, for speech
		local f = w.f0 + math.floor(w.fn / 2)
		local sd = w.s0 + math.floor(w.sn / 2)
		return rail.world(c, w.l, f, sd)                                -- geometry only; blocked is fine here
	end
	function rail.warp_goals(c, w)                                      -- walkable tiles you can step in from
		local set, any = {}, false
		local function add(l, f, sd)
			if rail.walk(c, l, f, sd) then
				set[((l * 512) + f) * 512 + (sd + 128)] = true; any = true
			end
		end
		for f = w.f0, w.f0 + math.max(w.fn, 1) - 1 do
			for sd = w.s0, w.s0 + math.max(w.sn, 1) - 1 do
				add(w.l, f - 1, sd); add(w.l, f + 1, sd)
				add(w.l, f, sd - 1); add(w.l, f, sd + 1)
			end
		end
		return any and set or nil
	end
	-- ⚠⚠ THE DIRECTION FIELD IS A BUTTON, NOT A COMPASS — so it may never produce a spoken "north".
	-- RailLine+0x08 is the D-pad code that increases PosFront toward Point2 (1 Up, 2 Right, 3 Down,
	-- 4 Left). Codex first reported it as the compass bearing from Point1 to Point2 "374/374 verified";
	-- cross-review rejected that (its check compared the line's field against the POINTS' departure
	-- fields — two data fields against each other, never against world positions), and against the real
	-- RailPoint+0x30 positions it holds for only 232/356 non-diagonal lines, with 32 of 172 AXIS-ALIGNED
	-- lines disagreeing on the axis itself. Codex then re-traced it and withdrew the claim: the input
	-- path (table 0x0209E068) and FUN_021B88F4 prove it is a control direction, and the rail->world
	-- conversion never reads the field at all. It coincides with N/E/S/W only where an area's control
	-- frame happens to align with world X/Z — 100% in the big city and the long bridge, mostly wrong in
	-- the small tall areas. Turn-by-turn speaks BUTTONS ("left 12"), so this costs us nothing; the check
	-- below exists only to decide whether a COMPASS word would also be safe to add here later.
	function rail.dir_ok(c)
		local ok, tot = 0, 0
		for i = 0, c.nl - 1 do
			local L, a, b = c.L[i], nil, nil
			if L then a, b = c.P[L.p1], c.P[L.p2] end
			if a and b then
				local dx, dz = a.x - b.x, a.z - b.z
				if dx >= 0x80000000 then dx = dx - 0x100000000 end
				if dz >= 0x80000000 then dz = dz - 0x100000000 end
				local dd
				if math.abs(dx) > math.abs(dz) then dd = (dx > 0) and 2 or 4
				elseif math.abs(dz) > math.abs(dx) then dd = (dz > 0) and 3 or 1 end
				if dd then
					tot = tot + 1
					if dd == L.dir then ok = ok + 1 end
				end
			end
		end
		return tot > 0 and ok == tot, ok, tot
	end
	-- ── REACHABILITY FILTER (Ola design, 2026-07-22): the nav list only offers what the player can actually
	-- WALK TO from where they stand. Canonical case: from Pinwheel's main area, the little Nacrene item-pocket
	-- crossing is listed as a way back to the city — but its pocket only opens to the CITY side, so from the
	-- forest you cannot reach it without going through the city; it must not be offered there. Rules:
	--   * reachable = a live BFS from the player's tile over the loaded collision (same movement semantics as
	--     the pathfinder: walls block, one-way ledges hop only in their own direction);
	--   * constrained to the CURRENT zone via the live matrix zone grid (GameData+0x1B0: w@+8, h@+0xA, zone
	--     grid u16 inline @+0x14 — white.lua "MAP MATRIX") — "reachable via another zone and back" does NOT
	--     count (Ola). Self-validating: if the player's own cell doesn't read back the live zone id (interiors:
	--     matrices without a zone grid), the constraint drops and walls/loaded chunks alone bound the BFS;
	--   * FAIL-OPEN everywhere: an entry is hidden only when PROVABLY cut off — its tile and all 4 neighbours
	--     loaded and none reachable. Unloaded (far) territory, BFS overflow, or a failed chunk read never hide
	--     anything. Cached per (zone, player tile, streamed-chunk fingerprint) -> zero cost while standing still.
	local reach = {}          -- { cache, for_, filter, ... } — ONE local; the module runs at Lua's 200-local cap
	-- (these live on `reach` rather than as file locals: this do-block is at Lua's 200-local cap,
	-- and `reach` is already where the shared movement semantics live)
	-- ⛔ ONE-WAY TILE EDGES — the fences the pathfinder used to plan straight through (Ola 2026-08-08:
	-- "the path finder wanted me to go somewhere I couldn't, because of something in the way", worst in
	-- Pinwheel Forest). These are NOT collision: the tile is perfectly walkable, but one or two of its
	-- EDGES are sealed. We had this listed as "RE later" since June and A* never knew about it.
	-- White's TileExitBlockCheck_Up/Down/Left/Right @0x021AB478/494/4B0/4C4 (Codex found them via their
	-- callers, since BW1 reaches them only through fn-ptr tables; Claude verified every class set by
	-- disassembly — Left/Right are explicit cmp chains, Up/Down use a compact `(class + K) & 0xFFFF <= 1`
	-- form whose literals 0xFFAB / 0xFFA9 give exactly {0x55,0x56} and {0x57,0x58}).
	-- The classes form four EDGES and four CORNERS:
	--   0x51 right, 0x52 left, 0x53 up, 0x54 down,
	--   0x55 up+right, 0x56 up+left, 0x57 down+right, 0x58 down+left.
	-- ⚠ Despite the "exit" name the caller (FUN_02163D24) tests BOTH tiles: moving A -> B in direction d
	-- is refused if A blocks d OR B blocks the opposite of d. So a marked edge is impassable from either
	-- side, which is what makes it a fence rather than a one-way drop.
	reach.EXIT = { [0x51]={right=1}, [0x52]={left=1}, [0x53]={up=1}, [0x54]={down=1},
	                  [0x55]={up=1,right=1}, [0x56]={up=1,left=1},
	                  [0x57]={down=1,right=1}, [0x58]={down=1,left=1} }
	reach.OPP = { up="down", down="up", left="right", right="left" }
	-- ⚠ QUICKSAND IS NOT HANDLED HERE, and it was, for an hour. Class 0x7C is ordinary walkable ground —
	-- only a pit's CENTRE tile is a one-way sink, which is a coordinate and not a tile class, so it cannot
	-- live in an edge rule that only ever sees two packed TileTypes. It is ow.sink, checked by the three
	-- searches where they expand a node. See ow.PIT for the whole mechanism.
	function reach.edge_ok(pa, pb, dir)                              -- packed TileType of A and B
		local a = pa and reach.EXIT[pa % 0x10000]
		if a and a[dir] then return false end
		local b = pb and reach.EXIT[pb % 0x10000]
		if b and b[reach.OPP[dir]] then return false end
		return true
	end
	-- ⛔ ONE FENCE RULE, REACHABLE FROM BOTH SIDES OF THE FILE. `reach` is a local declared here, below
	-- ow.reg_aim, so reg_aim cannot see it lexically — and a doorway search that skipped the fence rule is
	-- exactly the kind of quiet disagreement between two searches that this project keeps paying for.
	-- Hanging it on `ow` (declared near the top) makes it a field lookup at CALL time, so anything defined
	-- anywhere in this chunk can use the same predicate. Existing callers keep using reach.edge_ok.
	ow.edge_ok = reach.edge_ok
	-- HM GATES (Ola answers, QUESTIONS-reachability.md): things reachable only THROUGH an HM obstacle are NOT
	-- hidden — they get the obstacle's word appended ("Item, Cut" / "Nacrene City, Surf"), localized (bank 203
	-- move words: Cut=15, Surf=57, Rock Smash=249, Waterfall=127 — same strings the battle module bakes).
	-- Model 206 = Rock Smash (Ola, supported by the despawn-by-flag model — smashed rocks despawn, Strength
	-- boulders are PUSHED; its examine script is a native-handled stub; game-code confirm queued next session).
	-- Bits: 1=Cut tree (actor 108), 2=Rock Smash rock (actor 206), 4=Surf (Flags bit1), 8=Waterfall (Class
	-- 0x14 per the banked MapTile_* RE — 0x40 is a SNOW class; review fix 2026-07-24. Full directional
	-- waterfall semantics = queued; until then it is a plain gate tile).
	-- 16 = Strength boulder (actors 8195 and 8197 — see HM_MODELS for how the game's own script named them).
	reach.HM_BIT = { [108] = 1, [206] = 2, [8195] = 16, [8197] = 16 }
	reach.WORDS = { [1] = "Cut", [2] = "Rock Smash", [4] = "Surf", [8] = "Waterfall", [16] = "Strength" }
	function reach.wordstr(mask)
		local t = {}
		for _, b in ipairs({ 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 }) do
			if math.floor(mask / b) % 2 == 1 then t[#t + 1] = reach.WORDS[b] end
		end
		return table.concat(t, ", ")
	end
	-- reach.for_(zid, hms): BFS from the player tile over the LIVE loaded chunks, TWO-PHASE so plain walking
	-- always wins: phase A walks gates-closed (set[tile] = 0); phase B opens the gates (water / waterfall tiles,
	-- live Cut-tree/Boulder actors) and continues, recording per tile the OR of gate bits crossed to reach it.
	-- Anything phase A reached stays 0 (never labeled when a plain path exists). Ledges hop one-way as always.
	function reach.for_(zid, hms)
		-- ⛔ THE MATRIX CELLS HOLD THE **RAW** ZONE ID. FUN_02159D24 fills a matrix that carries no explicit
		-- per-cell zone array (Black City's and White Forest's do not) with the raw zone the field loader
		-- was handed — so in White Forest every cell reads 0, not 424. Resolve it the same way we resolve
		-- every other zone id, or both callers below compare 424 against 0, silently drop the matrix and
		-- leave this flood with no zone restriction at all.
		-- Resolved ONCE, not per tile: the flood asks zone_at for every candidate square, and the two allow
		-- bytes cannot change inside one flood (only an Entralink transition moves them).
		local ez0, ez294 = reg.ez(0), reg.ez(294)
		local function zone_at(mx, w, h, tx, tz)                        -- matrix-cell zone id, nil = out of bounds
			local r, c = math.floor(tz / 32), math.floor(tx / 32)
			if r < 0 or c < 0 or r >= h or c >= w then return nil end
			local v = u16(mx + 0x14 + (r * w + c) * 2)
			return (v == 0 and ez0) or (v == 294 and ez294) or v
		end
		local cs = pf_chunks(); if not cs then reach.cache = nil; return nil end
		local px, py = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
		local fp = #cs                                                  -- streaming fingerprint (chunks come and go)
		for _, c in ipairs(cs) do fp = fp + c.cx * 7 + c.cz * 13 end
		-- ⛔ DRIFTVEIL: A LIFT RIDE CHANGES WHAT IS REACHABLE WITHOUT MOVING ANYTHING THIS KEY WATCHES.
		-- You ride in place, so the player's tile does not change and neither does the chunk set — the key
		-- would be identical and the whole floor's reachability would be served from the previous storey.
		-- The storey and the six platform positions are what actually changed, so they belong in it.
		local dv = ow.dv.over(cs)
		if dv then
			fp = fp + dv.y * 17
			for i = 1, 6 do fp = fp + dv.fl[i] * (23 + i) end
		end
		-- ⛔ CHARGESTONE: A PUSH CHANGES WHAT IS REACHABLE WITHOUT MOVING THE PLAYER. Same shape as the lift
		-- above — the player's tile and the chunk set are both unchanged, so without the stone layout in the
		-- key the whole floor's reachability would be served from before the push.
		local cssig = ow.cs.here() and ow.cs.sig() or nil
		if cssig then fp = fp .. cssig end
		local hmset = {}                                                -- live HM-actor tiles -> gate bit
		for _, h in ipairs(hms or {}) do
			-- the actor's WHOLE footprint, not just the tile it reports: a Strength boulder is 2x2 and
			-- gating one corner of it left the other three open (see the note where these entries are
			-- built). The rect is the engine's own, {x, z-(h-1)}..{x+(w-1), z}; 1x1 collapses to the
			-- single tile this always used, so cut trees are byte-identical.
			local b = reach.HM_BIT[h.model] or 2
			for dx = 0, (h.w or 1) - 1 do
				for dz = 0, (h.h or 1) - 1 do
					hmset[(h.x + dx) * 100000 + (h.z - dz)] = b
				end
			end
			fp = fp + h.x * 3 + h.z * 5                                 -- gates move (tree cut) -> new cache key
		end
		local key = zid .. ":" .. px .. ":" .. py .. ":" .. fp
		if reach.cache and reach.cache.key == key then reach.cache.cs = cs; return reach.cache end
		local mx, mw, mh
		local gd = find_gd()
		if gd then
			local m = u32(gd + 0x1B0)
			if inram(m) then
				local w, h = u16(m + 0x08), u16(m + 0x0a)
				if w >= 1 and h >= 1 and w <= 64 and h <= 64 then mx, mw, mh = m, w, h end
			end
		end
		if mx and zone_at(mx, mw, mh, px, py) ~= zid then mx = nil end
		-- ⛔ IN CHARGESTONE "WALKABLE" IS NOT "REACHABLE". The stones are actors, so the tile grid this BFS
		-- floods knows nothing about them and every exit looked reachable — Ola's report. But blocking on
		-- them outright would be the Nimbasa mistake in reverse: an exit you reach by SHOVING a stone is
		-- genuinely reachable, and hiding it is worse than not filtering at all. So take the union of every
		-- configuration the push search can get to, from the same function the router plans with, and let
		-- this BFS pass only through that. Unreadable -> nil -> no extra restriction, and the filter goes on
		-- hiding nothing, which is its own fail-open rule.
		local rhard, rhardok = ow.aocc(nil, true)                       -- the carry's blockers (see ow.slide)
		local csr
		if ow.cs.here() then
			-- ow.aocc, not pf_occupied: this function is declared above pf_occupied, and a plain local is
			-- out of scope up here (luacheck caught exactly that). In the cave the two are the same call —
			-- pf_occupied's only other branch is the Driftveil shaft.
			if rhardok then
				csr = ow.cs.reach(function(x, z) return pf_tile(cs, x, z) end, pf_walk, rhard, px, py)
			end
		end
		local onwater = ow.afloat()                                     -- the engine's extended state (2 or 3),
		if onwater == nil then                                          -- never a no-height tile guess
			local pp = pf_tile(cs, px, py)                              -- unreadable state: fall back to the tile
			onwater = pp and math.floor(pp / 0x20000) % 2 == 1
		end
		local set = { [px * 100000 + py] = 0 }
		-- ⛔⛔ THE FILTER MUST NEVER OFFER A PLACE HE CANNOT REACH — AND NEVER HIDE ONE HE CAN.
		-- Ola's rule, stated 2026-09-03: "the filter will never offer a spot I can't reach, but the north
		-- exits CAN be reached, so that does need the fix." Both halves matter, and this flood was failing
		-- the second one badly. Until now it read every tile HEIGHT-BLIND (`pf_tile(cs, nx, nz)` with no y)
		-- and applied NO height rule at all, while pf_astar does both. Two searches, two different maps:
		--   * Height-blind reads answer from permission LAYER 0 — the ground floor. In Pinwheel Forest
		--     (zone 155) the log bridges over the pond are stored as the SECOND layer, so to this flood
		--     the bridges did not exist and the whole northern half of the forest was unreachable: it
		--     reported **796 tiles against 1355 really reachable**, and hid the exits to zone 250 and to
		--     Nacrene, both of which his dump printed as "HIDDEN (unreachable)".
		--   * No height rule meant the other direction too: it counted 814 tiles where the engine's own
		--     1.25-tile step rule allows 796 of them, so it could offer a place the router then refused.
		-- So this BFS now carries a height per node and uses pf_astar's step rule verbatim. On the 452
		-- single-layer zones every tile has exactly one height, so the node set is the same size it always
		-- was and the only change is that impossible climbs stop being offered.
		-- ⚠ THE VISITED SET IS KEYED BY (TILE, HEIGHT), THE ANSWER BY TILE. The same square at two heights
		-- is two different places — that is the whole point of a second layer — so keying visits by tile
		-- alone would let the first arrival (say, the ground under a bridge) lock out the second (the
		-- bridge itself) and re-hide exactly what this fix is for. `set` stays keyed by tile because every
		-- consumer of it is, and it keeps the FIRST mask reached, which is the cheapest gate as before.
		-- The bucket is `(y >> 16) % 4096`, character for character pf_astar's nkey, so the two searches
		-- quantise identically and cannot disagree about whether two arrivals are the same node.
		local sy0 = ow.seedy(cs, px, py)                                -- the same seed A* uses, by construction
		local function ybucket(y) return y and ((y >> 16) % 4096) or 0 end
		local vis = { [(px * 100000 + py) * 4096 + ybucket(sy0)] = true }
		local n, vn, overflow = 1, 1, false
		local queue, qh, qt = { { px, py, 0, sy0 } }, 1, 1
		local pending, pn = {}, 0                                       -- gate crossings deferred to phase B
		local phaseB = false
		local function step(x0, z0, mask0, y0)
			-- A pit's centre is a leaf here too — nothing on this floor lies BEYOND it (see ow.PIT). The
			-- tile itself is still marked when it is reached, which is what keeps the pit in the nav list.
			-- Both describe the tile we are STANDING on, so they are read once per node, not once per
			-- direction — the same hoist pf_astar does with here0/hhere.
			local here0 = pf_tile(cs, x0, z0, y0)
			local hhere = ow.height(cs, x0, z0, y0)                     -- nil = this tile has no authored height
			for _, d in ipairs(ow.sink(cs, x0, z0) and ow.NODIRS or PF_DIRS) do
				local dx, dz = d[2], d[3]
				local nx, nz = x0 + dx, z0 + dz
				local p = pf_tile(cs, nx, nz, y0)
				local ny = ow.height(cs, nx, nz, y0)                    -- that same candidate's height
				-- ⛔ CRASH FIX 2026-08-08 (Ola, in the Castelia Pokémon Center): this read `pf_tile(cs, cx, cz)`.
				-- There is no cx/cz in this scope — the BFS step's source tile is x0,z0 — so both resolved to
				-- nil GLOBALS and pf_tile did `nil * 100000`, killing the whole script the first time the
				-- reachability BFS ran after the fence rule was wired in. The name came from the A* loop,
				-- where the source really is cur.x/cur.z (line ~3642 does exactly this with `here`).
				-- Lua gives an undefined global no warning at all, which is why it compiled clean and why
				-- luacheck could not catch it: only running the BFS could.
				if not reach.edge_ok(here0, p, d[1]) then p = nil end    -- same fence rule as A*
				-- ⛔ AN INHERITED HEIGHT IS NOT A KNOWN HEIGHT. Identical wording to pf_astar's `climb`,
				-- and identical for the same reason: where a tile has no authored height record the value
				-- we carry is whatever we arrived with, which is right for choosing a LAYER and wrong for
				-- refusing a step. Unknown on any of the three stays permissive — this must never be the
				-- thing that hides a place on a guess. (Zone 227's three-wide corridor is the map that
				-- proved it for A*; the same row would cut this flood in half both ways.)
				local climb = (ny == nil or y0 == nil or hhere == nil)
				              or (ny - y0 < 0x14000 and y0 - ny < 0x14000)
				if not climb then p = nil end
				local hopped = false
				-- ⛔ AND NOT WHILE AFLOAT. White's Surf decision function has no ledge-jump result at all, yet
				-- the ROM holds 102 authored water-to-water stand/ledge/landing triples — so an unconditional
				-- hop lets this flood cross water the game refuses to cross (Codex review 2026-08-26). A*
				-- learned this earlier today; the flood had not.
				if not onwater and p and not pf_walk(p) and PF_LEDGE_HOP[p % 0x10000] == d[1] then
					nx, nz = nx + dx, nz + dz                           -- one-way ledge: land 2 tiles over, own dir only
					p = pf_tile(cs, nx, nz, y0)
					ny = ow.height(cs, nx, nz, y0)                      -- the LANDING's height, not the ledge's
					hopped = true                                       -- and no height gate on a jump (see pf_astar)
				end
				-- ⛔⛔ ICE IS NOT FLOOR, AND THIS SEARCH IS THE LAST PLACE THAT STILL THOUGHT IT WAS.
				-- On ice a press is not a step: the engine locks the direction and carries the player until
				-- they hit something or reach non-ice (ow.slide, from the ice arc). A* has modelled that
				-- since 2026-08-20; this BFS never did, so in an ice room it credited every tile you pass
				-- OVER as somewhere you could stand. Measured in Icirrus Gym (zone 114, 700 ice tiles): from
				-- the door you can come to rest on 52 tiles and this flood claimed 594. That is the Twist
				-- Mountain failure — offered by the list, refused by the router — at ten times the scale,
				-- and it is the THIRD time these two searches have disagreed about the same map.
				-- ⚠ DELIBERATELY NARROW: only when the destination tile is ICE. Off ice, ow.slide returns
				-- exactly the adjacent tile anyway, but it ALSO applies ow.hstep, which this flood does not
				-- — so routing every step through it would quietly change what the nav list shows in every
				-- zone in the game at once. Fixing the case that is actually wrong, and leaving 400-odd
				-- zones byte-identical, is the trade worth making tonight.
				-- ⚠ AND IT MUST NOT FIRE AFTER A LEDGE HOP. My first comment here said the two branches could
			-- never both run "because a ledge tile is never walkable" — but the ice test looks at the
			-- LANDING, not at the ledge, so a hop onto ice would have run ow.slide from the original tile,
			-- whose first step is the (non-walkable) ledge. That returns nil and the whole valid hop is
			-- erased, hiding everything beyond it (codex-review 2026-08-25, MEDIUM). The `hopped` flag makes
			-- the code do what ow.slide's own header already says it should: "the LEDGE-HOP branch in the
			-- caller deliberately does NOT slide on afterwards". That header also already banked the survey
			-- I then went and repeated — no ledge tile in any of the four ice zones, and none within two
			-- tiles of ice. Re-measured game-wide anyway: 491 ledge tiles, ZERO with an ice landing. Nothing
			-- is live; the control flow was still wrong, and a wrong branch that happens to be unreachable
			-- is a trap for the next map.
				if p and pf_walk(p) and ow.ice(p) and not hopped then
					-- `hard` here for the same reason A* has it: the filter and the router must not disagree
					-- about where a lane puts you down. Unreadable -> nil -> no restriction, which is this
					-- filter's own fail-open rule (it hides nothing when it cannot tell).
					local ex, ez = ow.slide(cs, x0, z0, d[1], dx, dz, nil, rhard, y0)
					if ex then
						nx, nz, p = ex, ez, pf_tile(cs, ex, ez, y0)
						ny = ow.height(cs, ex, ez, y0)                  -- the carry can END somewhere else
					else p = nil end                                    -- the press does nothing: not a step
				end
				local k = nx * 100000 + nz
				if csr and not csr[k] then p = nil end                  -- no push sequence gets you onto it
				-- The height we would ARRIVE at. A slide or a hop can end somewhere other than the square
				-- we measured, so `ny` has already been re-read for those; an unknown falls back to the
				-- height we came in with, which is exactly what pf_astar's `ey` does.
				local ey = ny; if ey == nil then ey = y0 end
				local vk = k * 4096 + ybucket(ey)
				if vis[vk] == nil and (not mx or zone_at(mx, mw, mh, nx, nz) == zid) then
					local bits = nil                                    -- nil = impassable
					if pf_walk(p) then
						bits = hmset[k] or 0
						-- ⛔⛔ THE FLOOD STAYS IN THE MODE YOU ARE IN. Ola, 2026-08-26: "reachability should only
						-- show me stuff on the water or any water exits; outside water only shows what I can
						-- walk to on this land I am on."
						-- My first attempt at his "no ways off the water" report let this flood HOP the shore,
						-- which made it cross the mode change freely and answer with the whole map: afloat he
						-- could see everything ashore, and ashore he was offered an item out on the water.
						-- Walking and surfing are different movement modes with different ground, so the
						-- flood now simply refuses the other one. The ways ACROSS are not lost — they are
						-- listed in their own right (reach.surf_spots ashore, reach.land_spots afloat), which
						-- is exactly the "or any water exits" half of what he asked for.
						local wet = math.floor(p / 0x20000) % 2 == 1
						if wet ~= (onwater and true or false) then bits = nil end
					elseif p and p % 0x10000 == 0x40 then
						-- ⛔ THE WATERFALL GATE WAS ON THE WRONG CLASS, and so it never once fired. This read
						-- Class 0x14, which a 2026-07-24 review "fixed" it TO — but 0x14's Flags are 0x0040
						-- game-wide (295 records), so bit 0 is clear, so pf_walk accepts it and this branch,
						-- which only runs for a BLOCKED tile, could never be reached. The tile you actually
						-- press into is Class **0x40** (50 records, Flags 0x0013, bit 0 set): White's
						-- CheckCanInteractWaterfall FUN_021A3544 is `not BlocksCollision(standing) and
						-- exState == 2 (surfing) and MapTile_Is0x40(facedClass)`, and FUN_021AB330's only
						-- two callers are that predicate and the automatic descent at 0x0218B056 (Codex
						-- 2026-08-26, report_waterfall.md). 0x14 is the waterfall BODY class, not the lip.
						bits = 8                                        -- Waterfall: blocked, but climbable while surfing
					elseif p and p % 0x10000 == ow.st.HOLE then
						-- ⛔⛔ A STRENGTH HOLE IS A GATE, NOT A WALL. Ola, 2026-09-02, standing in Mistralton
						-- Cave having already pushed one boulder: "I can't see any transitions to the other
						-- rooms. Is the reachability filter acting up because of the wholes for strength?"
						-- He was right, and his own dump proved it exactly: reach.for_ reported **57 tiles**
						-- and hid the warp to zone 334, and reproducing that flood offline gives 57 on the
						-- nose with the holes closed and **342** with them open. The boulders themselves were
						-- already gated (bit 16, via the live actor), so the rock was never the barrier here
						-- - the HOLE was, and a hole is exactly the kind of thing this flood is built to walk
						-- through and LABEL rather than treat as a wall.
						-- It matters more than a missing label: the filter had removed the transition from
						-- the nav list entirely, so he could not even select it - and the whole Strength
						-- planner, which exists to route him through this, never got the chance to run.
						-- ⭐ AND GATING EVERY 0x1D TILE IS SAFE GAME-WIDE, measured rather than assumed: over
						-- all 649 land members in the ROM class 0x1D occurs 109 times in 27 complete 2x2
						-- blocks, against 26 authored boulders. It is never used for scenery anywhere.
						-- ⚠ I had claimed the opposite an hour earlier ("0x1D appears in 58 zones with no
						-- boulder"), and that was my own bug: the survey loaded the whole shared outdoor
						-- matrix and then counted tiles outside the zone it was asking about, so every
						-- outdoor zone reported the same global 32. Inside the zones it is 0 everywhere but
						-- the boulder rooms. A count is only as good as the window it was taken in.
						bits = 16                                       -- Strength: fill it and walk over it
					end
					if bits then
						if bits ~= 0 and not phaseB then
							pn = pn + 1; pending[pn] = { nx, nz, bits, ey }  -- cross it in phase B only
						else
							-- ⛔⛔ A HARDCODED BIT LIST IS A BUG WITH A FUSE, and adding Strength lit it
							-- (codex-review 2026-09-01). This used to walk `{1, 2, 4, 8}` and add each bit
							-- by hand; reach.WORDS and reach.wordstr grew a fifth bit (16 = Strength) and
							-- this copy of the list did not, so bit 16 was never ORed in at all — every
							-- destination behind a boulder came out with mask 0, i.e. announced as plainly
							-- walkable when the only way there is to push a rock. Exactly the "hidden
							-- information / false reachability" failure this whole mask exists to prevent,
							-- and it would have shipped silently because nothing else reads the list.
							-- Lua 5.4 has integer bitwise operators (this file already uses `>>`), so the
							-- list is gone rather than extended: there is now nothing to keep in sync.
							local m2 = mask0 | bits
							vis[vk] = true
							-- `set` is the ANSWER and is keyed by tile, so it is written once — the first
							-- arrival wins, which is the cheapest gate mask, exactly as before. `vis` is
							-- the SEARCH and is keyed by tile-and-height, so a second arrival at another
							-- height still gets expanded. `n` counts tiles, so the 6000 cap means the same
							-- thing it always did; `vn` caps the nodes, which on a two-layer map can be
							-- more numerous than the tiles and on every other map cannot.
							if set[k] == nil then set[k] = m2; n = n + 1 end
							vn = vn + 1
							if n > 6000 or vn > 12000 then overflow = true; return end
							qt = qt + 1; queue[qt] = { nx, nz, m2, ey }
						end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		while qh <= qt and not overflow do
			local t = queue[qh]; qh = qh + 1
			step(t[1], t[2], t[3], t[4])
			if qh > qt and not phaseB and not overflow then             -- phase A exhausted: open the gates
				phaseB = true
				for i = 1, pn do
					local g = pending[i]
					local k = g[1] * 100000 + g[2]
					local vk = k * 4096 + ybucket(g[4])                 -- the height it would be crossed AT
					if vis[vk] == nil then
						vis[vk] = true
						if set[k] == nil then set[k] = g[3]; n = n + 1 end
						vn = vn + 1
						if n > 6000 or vn > 12000 then overflow = true; break end
						qt = qt + 1; queue[qt] = { g[1], g[2], g[3], g[4] }
					end
				end
			end
		end
		reach.cache = { key = key, set = (not overflow) and set or nil, cs = cs }
		return reach.cache
	end
	-- keep an entry unless PROVABLY cut off; entries reachable ONLY through gates get e.gate = the words (on a
	-- COPY — snapshot entries are shared and must never be mutated). Plain-reachable neighbours win (no label).
	-- ⛔ COUNTER STAFF ARE TALKED TO ACROSS THE COUNTER, so "can I stand on their tile" is the wrong test
	-- for whether to offer them (Ola 2026-08-08, in a Pokémon Center: the nurse "should be directly
	-- north" and was being hidden, and so were both shop clerks). His capture proves the filter was
	-- reasoning correctly and still giving the wrong answer: the nurse sits at (7,10) in a THREE-TILE
	-- pocket walled on all four sides, so on foot she genuinely is cut off. But the tile between you and
	-- her is a RECEPTION COUNTER, and you talk over it from two tiles away.
	-- The game marks those tiles itself: in White's MapTile_* cluster **MapTile_IsReceptionCounter tests
	-- Class 0xD4** (0x021AB1B4) and **MapTile_IsPC tests Class 0xD6** (0x021AB1C0). That order is not
	-- guesswork — it is pinned by the two already-banked anchors either side: IsLedgeR @0x021AB19C tests
	-- 0x72 and IsUnused0xD7 @0x021AB1CC tests 0xD7, exactly as documented, and the IRDO name list runs
	-- 1:1 between them.
	-- So the rule is the game's own, and needs NO curated list of Pokémon Centers: if the tile one step
	-- away is a counter and the tile beyond THAT is reachable, you can talk from there. It covers marts,
	-- gyms, labs and every other counter in the game for free.
	reach.COUNTER = { [0xD4] = true, [0xD6] = true }                    -- reception counter, PC terminal
	function reach.over_counter(cs, set, x, z)
		if not (cs and set) then return nil end
		for _, d in ipairs({ { 1, 0 }, { -1, 0 }, { 0, 1 }, { 0, -1 } }) do
			local mid = pf_tile(cs, x + d[1], z + d[2])
			if mid and reach.COUNTER[mid % 0x10000] then
				local m = set[(x + d[1] * 2) * 100000 + (z + d[2] * 2)]
				if m then return m end                                  -- 0 = plain walk, >0 = HM gate mask
			end
		end
		return nil
	end
	-- ⛔ THE PC IS A TILE, NOT A PERSON — which is why no reader that walks the actor manager has ever
	-- found it (Ola 2026-08-08: "the pc isn't showing up as anything to walk to either, never has").
	-- The game marks it with Class 0xD6 (MapTile_IsPC, see the note below), so we find it the way the
	-- game does. Bounded box around the player, run ONCE per nav-list build and cached with the list —
	-- never per frame, per the optimisation rule. Adjacent tiles collapse into one entry, so a two-tile
	-- terminal is announced once.
	local PC_R = 20
	function reach.pc_tiles(cs, px, pz)
		if not cs then return {} end
		local hits, out = {}, {}
		for dz = -PC_R, PC_R do
			for dx = -PC_R, PC_R do
				local t = pf_tile(cs, px + dx, pz + dz)
				if t and t % 0x10000 == 0xD6 then hits[(px + dx) * 100000 + (pz + dz)] = { x = px + dx, z = pz + dz } end
			end
		end
		for k, v in pairs(hits) do                                      -- keep one tile per cluster
			if not (hits[k - 100000] or hits[k - 1]) then out[#out + 1] = { name = "PC", x = v.x, z = v.z } end
		end
		return out
	end
	-- ⭐⭐ SURF SPOTS. Ola, 2026-08-26: "I checked on a route with surf water, it doesn't show up on nav
	-- list under HM". He is right, and it was never a labelling bug: the HM list is built from live ACTORS
	-- (model 108 cut tree, model 206 smashable rock) and water is a property of a TILE, so no water could
	-- ever appear there. Separately, reach.for_ has long appended ", Surf" to a DESTINATION beyond water —
	-- but that labels somewhere else, it never offers the water itself as a place to go.
	-- HIS DESIGN CALL (2026-08-26): **one entry per body of water**, not one per shore tile — "a big lake
	-- is one Surf you can route to, not twenty" — and listed **only once he can actually Surf**, which is
	-- progress, never whether a party Pokémon currently knows the move.
	-- The entry we list is the LAND tile you stand on to enter, never the water: the router refuses to
	-- walk into water, so aiming at a water tile would produce a goal it will not path to.
	-- ⛔ THE BAG, FROM THE SAVE — available in the overworld, no bag screen needed (Codex RE 2026-08-26,
	-- research/re/report_bag_save.md). Save block 0x19 is cached by GameData_New and handed out by
	-- GameData_GetBag (FUN_0201292C = `return u32(gd + 0x190)`), so the whole chain is offsets from the
	-- root we already trust — no new absolute address, and Black gets it free.
	--   Bag = u32(GameData + 0x190); five {u16 id, u16 qty} pockets inside a 0x9C0 block:
	--     0 Items    bag+0x000 x310    1 Medicine bag+0x7D8 x48     2 TMs/HMs bag+0x624 x109
	--     3 Berries  bag+0x898 x64     4 Key items bag+0x4D8 x83
	--   An empty slot is EXACTLY {0, 0} — the engine's own accept test (FUN_02007D94) demands both halves
	--   zero, and removal clears the id when the quantity hits zero. That doubles as our structure check:
	--   a record with no id but a quantity means we are not looking at a bag, and we answer UNKNOWN.
	-- ⚠ A bounded 109-record walk of ONE pocket, run when the nav list is built — never per frame.
	ow.BAG_TMHM = { off = 0x624, cap = 109 }
	function ow.bag_qty(id, pocket)
		local gd = find_gd(); if not gd then return nil end              -- unknown, never "no"
		local bag = u32(gd + 0x190); if not inram(bag) then return nil end
		local found = 0
		for i = 0, pocket.cap - 1 do
			local r = bag + pocket.off + i * 4
			local iid, qty = u16(r), u16(r + 2)
			if iid == 0 and qty ~= 0 then return nil end                 -- not a bag: fail closed
			if iid == id then found = qty end
		end
		return found
	end
	-- ⛔ CAN THE PLAYER SURF? Ola's call, and it is OURS, not the game's: White gates field Surf on tile
	-- geometry plus "a party Pokémon knows move 57" and on nothing else — no badge, no story flag, no bag
	-- test (CheckFieldEnvInteraction FUN_0218B58C; Codex 2026-08-26). He wants the spot listed once he has
	-- BEEN GIVEN Surf, taught or not, so we test possession of HM03 (item 422) in the TM/HM pocket.
	-- Unknown reads answer false here — listing water he cannot use is worse than listing none.
	function ow.can_surf()
		return (ow.bag_qty(422, ow.BAG_TMHM) or 0) > 0
	end
	-- ⛔ AND THE OBSTACLE ACTORS ARE GATED THE SAME WAY (Ola's answer B, questions_dark_caves.md Q1,
	-- 2026-09-01: "B, and same with Cut"). Cut trees and Strength boulders used to be listed whether or not
	-- he could do anything about them; he wants them held back until he has been GIVEN the move, exactly
	-- like Surf and Waterfall. Items 420..425 are HM01..HM06, so Cut = 420 and Strength = 423.
	-- ⚠ MODEL 206 IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. It is labelled "Rock Smash rock", but Rock Smash is a TM in
	-- Gen V, not an HM, and the census says 206 carries SCRID 2000 with PERMANENT spawn flags (652/653) —
	-- a story obstacle that despawns for good, not something a move clears. Gating it on an HM he can never
	-- own would hide it for the whole game. Unknown models fall through here too and stay listed, which is
	-- the right default for a thing standing in your way.
	-- ⚠ AND THIS ONLY HIDES THE OBSTACLE FROM THE **LIST**. reach.for_ is handed the unfiltered actor list,
	-- so a destination beyond a boulder still gets its ", Strength" word and is still offered — losing that
	-- would turn "you need a move for this" into "there is nothing over there", which is far worse.
	ow.HM_ITEM = { [108] = 420, [8195] = 423, [8197] = 423 }            -- obstacle model -> the HM that clears it
	function ow.can_clear(model)
		local it = model and ow.HM_ITEM[model]; if not it then return true end   -- not a move-gated obstacle
		return (ow.bag_qty(it, ow.BAG_TMHM) or 0) > 0                   -- unknown reads answer no, as Surf does
	end
	reach.SURF_EDGE = { [0x41] = true, [0x44] = true }                  -- the blocked shore classes you press into
	reach.SURF_R = 20                                                   -- same bounded box the PC scan uses
	-- (a FIELD, not a local: this chunk sits on Lua's 200-local ceiling and one more `local` here fails to
	-- compile outright — the same rule ow.ic / ow.slide / ow.hstep already follow.)
	-- `any` = ignore the progress gate and report what is THERE. The N-dump passes it so a dump taken while
	-- the feature is still dark still tells us whether the detector sees the water — an instrument that
	-- goes quiet exactly when the thing it explains is switched off is no instrument at all.
	function reach.surf_spots(cs, px, pz, any)
		if not cs then return {} end
		if not any and not ow.can_surf() then return {} end             -- not yet: list nothing
		-- ⛔⛔ YOU DO NOT STAND NEXT TO WATER. A first version looked for a walkable land tile ADJACENT to
		-- water and would have found nothing, ever: measured over every mode-0 map in the game there are
		-- **ZERO** direct land-water contacts (research/re/ds_surfedge.py). Every water body in Unova is
		-- fenced by the blocked Surf-edge classes 0x41 and 0x44 — the same ones CheckSurfExitEvent
		-- (FUN_0218B428) uses to put you back on land two tiles away. So an entry point is a TRIPLE:
		--     stand on land -> press into a blocked 0x41/0x44 edge -> the tile beyond it is water.
		-- 483 of them exist across 13 mapped zones, the same 483 the exit rule counts from the other side.
		-- Dragonspiral's own lake is four of them: stand (20..23, 44), press up, water at z 42.
		local wat, ent = {}, {}
		for dz = -reach.SURF_R, reach.SURF_R do
			for dx = -reach.SURF_R, reach.SURF_R do
				local x, z = px + dx, pz + dz
				local t = pf_tile(cs, x, z)
				if t and pf_walk(t) then
					if math.floor(t / 0x20000) % 2 == 1 then
						wat[x * 100000 + z] = true                       -- Flags bit 1: surfable
					else
						-- ⭐ THE ENGINE'S OWN START TEST, CheckSurfBeginTiles FUN_021AB5CC (Codex 2026-08-26):
						--   not BlocksCollision(standing)
						--   and ( (faced Flags bit 1 and not BlocksCollision(faced))  -- step straight in
						--         or faced Class is 0x41 / 0x44 )                     -- press over the shore
						-- Both shapes are real, so both are accepted here. The offer itself does not look
						-- past an edge tile; we do, but only to know WHICH body of water the spot opens —
						-- an entry we cannot attribute to a body is dropped rather than guessed at.
						for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do                   -- land: is there a way in from here?
							local e = pf_tile(cs, x + d[2], z + d[3])
							local wk = e and (x + d[2]) * 100000 + (z + d[3])
							if e and pf_walk(e) and math.floor(e / 0x20000) % 2 == 1 then
								ent[wk] = ent[wk] or                     -- the faced tile IS the water
									{ x = x, z = z, dir = d[1], dist = math.abs(dx) + math.abs(dz) }
							elseif e and reach.SURF_EDGE[e % 0x10000] then
								local b = pf_tile(cs, x + 2*d[2], z + 2*d[3])
								if b and pf_walk(b) and math.floor(b / 0x20000) % 2 == 1 then
									local k2 = (x + 2*d[2]) * 100000 + (z + 2*d[3])
									local cur = ent[k2]                  -- keyed by the WATER it opens
									local dist = math.abs(dx) + math.abs(dz)
									if not cur or dist < cur.dist then
										ent[k2] = { x = x, z = z, dir = d[1], dist = dist }
									end
								end
							end
						end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		-- One entry per BODY of water (Ola's call), aimed at the nearest way in.
		local seen, out = {}, {}
		for k in pairs(wat) do
			if not seen[k] then
				local best
				local q, qh = { k }, 1
				seen[k] = true
				while qh <= #q do
					local c = q[qh]; qh = qh + 1
					local e = ent[c]
					if e and (not best or e.dist < best.dist) then best = e end
					local cx, cz = math.floor(c / 100000), c % 100000
					for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
						local nk = (cx + d[2]) * 100000 + (cz + d[3])
						if wat[nk] and not seen[nk] then seen[nk] = true; q[#q + 1] = nk end
					end
				end
				if best then out[#out + 1] = { name = "Surf", x = best.x, z = best.z, sdir = best.dir } end
			end
		end
		return out
	end
	-- ⭐⭐ GETTING OFF THE WATER. Ola, 2026-08-26, after the surf spot worked: "when on the water we need
	-- the ways to get off again, and here is a little more involved — if the body of water has surf spots
	-- on two sides that are disconnected on land, we need both, pretty much all disconnected spots,
	-- because that means we can find all the spots that are disconnected non routes."
	-- So the entries are not "every shore tile" and not "the nearest shore": they are **one per stretch of
	-- land this water can put you on that you could not have WALKED to from the others**. A lake touching
	-- one landmass in four places is one entry; a lake touching two separate landmasses is two, and that
	-- second one is the whole point — it is somewhere only the water reaches.
	-- THE EXIT GEOMETRY IS THE ENTRY'S MIRROR, both halves traced (Codex, report_surf_entry.md +
	-- CheckSurfExitEvent FUN_0218B428): step straight onto an adjacent land tile (actor action 0x34), or
	-- press into a blocked 0x41/0x44 shore tile and be put down TWO tiles beyond it (action 0x38), where
	-- the landing must have Flags bits 0 AND 1 clear — solid ground, not more water.
	-- ⚠ Bounded by the same box as the other scans, so on a huge lake these are the landings NEAR you.
	-- The localized name of the place a TILE belongs to. The live map matrix (GameData + 0x1B0) carries a
	-- zone id per 32x32 cell; outdoors that is how one lake shore can be Route 6 and the other Route 7.
	function reach.place_at(x, z)
		local zid = reg.zid()
		local gd = find_gd()
		if gd then
			local m = u32(gd + 0x1B0)
			if inram(m) then
				local w, h = u16(m + 0x08), u16(m + 0x0a)
				if w >= 1 and h >= 1 and w <= 64 and h <= 64 then
					local r, c = math.floor(z / 32), math.floor(x / 32)
					if r >= 0 and c >= 0 and r < h and c < w then
						local zz = reg.ez(u16(m + 0x14 + (r * w + c) * 2))   -- cells hold the RAW id: resolve it
						-- only trust it when the matrix agrees about where WE are (reach.for_'s own guard)
						local pr, pc = math.floor(ow.s16(A.map_y) / 32), math.floor(ow.s16(A.map_x) / 32)
						if pr < h and pc < w and reg.ez(u16(m + 0x14 + (pr * w + pc) * 2)) == zid then zid = zz end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		return transition_name(zid)
	end
	-- ⭐⭐ WATERFALLS. Ola: "waterfall usually just puts you in the water again but higher up" — confirmed
	-- by the engine, and it is why these are NOT landings: the destination is water, so reach.land_spots
	-- (which demands genuinely dry ground) was never going to offer one.
	-- THE RULE (Codex, report_waterfall.md; White):
	--   CheckCanInteractWaterfall FUN_021A3544 = not BlocksCollision(standing) and exState == 2 (you are
	--   SURFING) and the FACED tile's class == 0x40 (FUN_021AB330). Class 0x14 is the waterfall body, not
	--   the lip — it is not the gate.
	--   ⭐ UP AND DOWN ARE THE SAME TILE. FieldWaterfallTCB_Create 0x021C1240 advances TWO tile steps
	--   (0x20000) along the facing, reads that surface's height, and compares it with yours: higher means
	--   climb, otherwise descend. So the direction is ELEVATION, not a compass or a second class — which
	--   is exactly what a blind player needs told, so we say which one it is.
	--   Capability is a party member knowing move 127; there is no badge, story flag or bag test at all.
	--   We gate on HM05 (item 424) in the bag anyway, for the same reason Ola chose it for Surf: he wants
	--   to see it once he HAS the move, taught or not. That is our decision, not the engine's rule.
	-- The entry is the WATER tile you must be floating on to face the lip — routable, because a route that
	-- starts afloat may cross water.
	-- ⛔⛔ ASK THE ENGINE WHAT MODE WE ARE IN. Inferring "afloat" from the tile under the player is wrong on
	-- a two-layer map: Pinwheel Forest (71,51) is class 0x3D water in layer 0 and ordinary dry floor in
	-- layer 1, so a walking player on the WALKWAY reads as surfing to any lookup that carries no height
	-- (Codex review 2026-08-26). That is the same trap that bit A*'s `swim` earlier today, reintroduced one
	-- level up. The player's real extended state is a fixed chain from the root we already trust:
	--   GameSystem -> Field +0x8C -> FieldPlayer +0x04 -> Core +0x0C -> PlayerState +0x40
	-- proven by FUN_021A30B8 -> FUN_021A3994 -> FUN_02012B28. FUN_021A4464 sends states 2 AND 3 through the
	-- Surf-family movement evaluator, while the Surf-exit and Waterfall predicates require exactly 2 — so
	-- the flood's mode split accepts either, and offering a way OFF requires 2.
	function ow.exstate()
		local gs = u32(GAMESYS_PTR); if not inram(gs) then return nil end
		local fl = u32(gs + 0x20); if not inram(fl) then return nil end
		local fp = u32(fl + 0x8C); if not inram(fp) then return nil end
		local co = u32(fp + 0x04); if not inram(co) then return nil end
		local ps = u32(co + 0x0C); if not inram(ps) then return nil end
		return u32(ps + 0x40)
	end
	function ow.surfing() return ow.exstate() == 2 end                  -- may step off here
	-- ⚠ nil, not false, when the state cannot be read — the flood has a tile-based fallback for that case
	-- and `false` would have quietly disabled it. ow.surfing() does the opposite on purpose: unreadable
	-- means "do not offer a way off", which is the fail-closed answer for a thing we speak.
	function ow.afloat()  local e = ow.exstate(); if e == nil then return nil end; return e == 2 or e == 3 end
	function ow.can_waterfall()
		return (ow.bag_qty(424, ow.BAG_TMHM) or 0) > 0                  -- HM05; unknown reads answer no
	end
	function reach.fall_spots(cs, px, pz)
		if not cs or not ow.can_waterfall() or not ow.surfing() then return {} end
		local R = reach.SURF_R
		local py0 = ow.height(cs, px, pz)
		local body, q, qh = { [px * 100000 + pz] = true }, { { px, pz } }, 1
		while qh <= #q do
			local c = q[qh]; qh = qh + 1
			for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
				local nx, nz = c[1] + d[2], c[2] + d[3]
				local k = nx * 100000 + nz
				local t = pf_tile(cs, nx, nz)
				if math.abs(nx - px) <= R and math.abs(nz - pz) <= R and not body[k]
				   and t and pf_walk(t) and math.floor(t / 0x20000) % 2 == 1 then
					body[k] = true; q[#q + 1] = { nx, nz }
				end
			end
		end
		local lips, out = {}, {}
		for k in pairs(body) do
			local wx, wz = math.floor(k / 100000), k % 100000
			for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
				local f = pf_tile(cs, wx + d[2], wz + d[3])
				if f and f % 0x10000 == 0x40 then                        -- the climbable lip
					local lk = (wx + d[2]) * 100000 + (wz + d[3])
					local dest = ow.height(cs, wx + 2*d[2], wz + 2*d[3]) -- the surface two steps on
					local dist = math.abs(wx - px) + math.abs(wz - pz)
					if dest and (not lips[lk] or dist < lips[lk].dist) then
						lips[lk] = { x = wx, z = wz, dist = dist,
						             up = (py0 ~= nil) and (dest > py0) or nil }
					end
				end
			end
		end
		for _, e in pairs(lips) do
			out[#out + 1] = { name = e.up == nil and "Waterfall"
			                       or (e.up and "Waterfall up" or "Waterfall down"), x = e.x, z = e.z }
		end
		return out
	end
	-- ⛔ THE WAYS OFF NEED A WIDER LOOK THAN THE WAYS ON. Ola, surfing Route 1: "getting on the water now
	-- only showed me the tile I took to get on, not the different ways to get off, route 1 have two
	-- sections, right and left." Both shores are real, but they are about 33 tiles apart, so a 20-tile box
	-- around the player only ever contained the one he had just left. Unlike the Surf ENTRIES — which can
	-- sit in chunks the game has not even loaded, and so had to be baked — the whole body of water you are
	-- floating on is right next to you and is loaded, so simply looking further is the honest fix.
	-- 40 keeps it inside the four loaded chunks (a 64-tile square) and it runs once per nav key, never
	-- per frame.
	reach.OFF_R = 40
	function reach.land_spots(cs, px, pz)
		if not cs or not ow.surfing() then return {} end                -- the engine's state, not a tile guess
		local R = reach.OFF_R
		local function inbox(x, z) return math.abs(x - px) <= R and math.abs(z - pz) <= R end
		local function water(t) return t and pf_walk(t) and math.floor(t / 0x20000) % 2 == 1 end
		local function dry(t) return t and pf_walk(t) and math.floor(t / 0x20000) % 2 == 0 end
		-- 1. the body of water we are actually on
		local body, q, qh = { [px * 100000 + pz] = true }, { { px, pz } }, 1
		while qh <= #q do
			local c = q[qh]; qh = qh + 1
			for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
				local nx, nz = c[1] + d[2], c[2] + d[3]
				local k = nx * 100000 + nz
				if inbox(nx, nz) and not body[k] and water(pf_tile(cs, nx, nz)) then
					body[k] = true; q[#q + 1] = { nx, nz }
				end
			end
		end
		-- 2. every way off it, by the engine's two exit shapes
		local landings = {}
		for k in pairs(body) do
			local wx, wz = math.floor(k / 100000), k % 100000
			for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
				local fx, fz = wx + d[2], wz + d[3]
				local f = pf_tile(cs, fx, fz)
				local lx, lz
				if dry(f) then lx, lz = fx, fz                          -- straight onto the shore
				elseif f and reach.SURF_EDGE[f % 0x10000] then
					local b = pf_tile(cs, fx + d[2], fz + d[3])         -- over the shore tile, two on
					if dry(b) then lx, lz = fx + d[2], fz + d[3] end
				end
				if lx and inbox(lx, lz) then
					local lk = lx * 100000 + lz
					local dist = math.abs(lx - px) + math.abs(lz - pz)
					if not landings[lk] or dist < landings[lk].dist then
						landings[lk] = { x = lx, z = lz, dist = dist, wx = wx, wz = wz }
					end
				end
			end
		end
		-- 3. ⭐ collapse landings that are the SAME PLACE ON FOOT. A flood over dry land only — water is
		-- not a connection here, that is the entire question being asked — using the same fence rule the
		-- router uses, so two landings count as one exactly when you could walk between them.
		local comp, out = {}, {}
		for lk, e in pairs(landings) do
			if not comp[lk] then
				local mark = { [lk] = true }
				local best = e
				local q2, q2h = { { e.x, e.z } }, 1
				while q2h <= #q2 do
					local c = q2[q2h]; q2h = q2h + 1
					local ct = pf_tile(cs, c[1], c[2])
					for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
						local nx, nz = c[1] + d[2], c[2] + d[3]
						local kk = nx * 100000 + nz
						local nt = pf_tile(cs, nx, nz)
						if inbox(nx, nz) and not mark[kk] and dry(nt) and reach.edge_ok(ct, nt, d[1]) then
							mark[kk] = true; q2[#q2 + 1] = { nx, nz }
							local o = landings[kk]
							if o and o.dist < best.dist then best = o end
						end
					end
				end
				for kk in pairs(mark) do if landings[kk] then comp[kk] = true end end
				-- ⭐ NAMED BY WHERE IT PUTS YOU, in the game's own words (Ola, questions_surf.md Q1: "put it
				-- as the route name or place name, or something that is localized"). Outdoors the far side
				-- of a lake is often a different ZONE, and the live map matrix knows which — so the entry
				-- reads "Route 6" rather than a bare direction. Same lookup reach.for_ uses for its own
				-- zone guard; when the matrix cannot answer, the zone we are standing in is still a
				-- localized name, and duplicates get numbered by the list's own per-category counter.
				-- ⚠ It carries the WATER tile it is reached FROM. reach.filter tests that instead of the
				-- landing itself, so an exit off a body of water we cannot get to is still dropped — the
				-- blunt "never filter these" flag it replaces would have shown one (Codex review).
				-- ⭐ `off` = THIS ENTRY CARRIES ITS OWN PROOF. It was found by flooding the body of water
				-- the player is actually on, so it is reachable by construction. Strict mode must not
				-- re-judge it against reach.for_'s set: that set is limited to the CURRENT ZONE while the
				-- water is not, so a landing two tiles into the next zone has no tile in the set at all and
				-- would be dropped — hiding a way ashore, the one direction that must never happen
				-- (codex-review, HIGH).
				out[#out + 1] = { name = reach.place_at(best.x, best.z), x = best.x, z = best.z,
				                  wx = best.wx, wz = best.wz, off = true }
			end
		end
		return out
	end
	-- ⚠ DELIBERATELY MORE PERMISSIVE THAN ARRIVAL, and it stays that way. This credits an entry when the
	-- flood reached its aim tile OR any of the four beside it, which for a stand-on doorway includes tiles
	-- ow.at_reg now rejects (Codex verify 2026-08-26). Tightening it to ow.reg_tiles would DROP a doorway
	-- from the list whenever its own tile is unreachable — and four doorways in the game are exactly that,
	-- one of them Dragonspiral's own way up. A listed door the router cannot reach is an annoyance; a door
	-- that is never listed at all is a place Ola cannot know exists. Keep the list generous.
	function reach.filter(list, R, strict)
		local out = {}
		for _, e in ipairs(list) do
			local keep, best = true, nil
			-- ⛔ AN EXIT IS JUDGED BY THE SIDE YOU APPROACH IT FROM. A way off the water stands on dry land,
			-- and while afloat the flood deliberately covers only water — so testing the landing itself
			-- would throw away the very entries that exist to get you ashore. Testing e.wx/e.wz, the water
			-- tile the exit is taken FROM, keeps the ones you can actually swim to and still drops an exit
			-- off a body of water you cannot reach. (It replaces a blanket "never filter these" flag, which
			-- would have shown an exit that was neither reachable nor in the right mode — Codex review.)
			local qx, qz = e.wx or e.x, e.wz or e.z
			-- ⭐ A WAY ASHORE IS ALREADY PROVEN — see the `off` note in reach.land_spots. It survives
			-- strict mode untouched rather than being re-judged against a zone-limited set.
			if strict and e.off then
				keep = true
			elseif R and R.set then
				local s = R.set
				for _, k in ipairs({ qx * 100000 + qz, (qx + 1) * 100000 + qz, (qx - 1) * 100000 + qz,
					qx * 100000 + qz + 1, qx * 100000 + qz - 1 }) do
					local m = s[k]
					if m and (not best or m < best) then best = m end
				end
				if not best then best = reach.over_counter(R.cs, s, qx, qz) end    -- talkable over a counter?
				if not best then
					-- ⛔⛔ AFLOAT, "I CANNOT SEE THAT TILE" IS ITSELF THE ANSWER. Ola, 2026-08-31, surfing off
					-- Route 1: "when you are on the water, the only stuff you should see are the land
					-- transitions ... only show stuff on the water when I am on the water, nothing else,
					-- only transitions that gets me off the water or if there truly is a transition you
					-- access from the water itself."
					-- On the water the flood covers WATER ONLY, so anything it did not reach is either
					-- ashore or a long way off — and either way you cannot get to it by swimming. The usual
					-- fail-open below exists so a door we merely cannot SEE is never lost; afloat there is
					-- nothing to lose, because none of it is reachable in this mode. That fail-open is what
					-- was listing the towns at him from the middle of the water: their tiles are far enough
					-- away never to be loaded, so "unreachable but unreadable" kept them every time.
					-- ⚠ THE WAYS ASHORE SURVIVE THIS. reach.land_spots entries carry e.wx/e.wz — the WATER
					-- tile the exit is taken from — and the probe above already tests that instead of the
					-- landing, so they are found in the flood and never reach this branch. A transition you
					-- genuinely take FROM the water is water-adjacent for the same reason and also survives.
					-- ⚠ And `strict` is only ever true when ow.surfing() said so: an unreadable state leaves
					-- it nil and the old fail-open behaviour stands.
					if strict then
						keep = false
					else
						local cs = R.cs                                 -- unreachable AND fully inside known ground?
						keep = not (pf_tile(cs, qx, qz) and pf_tile(cs, qx + 1, qz) and pf_tile(cs, qx - 1, qz)
							and pf_tile(cs, qx, qz + 1) and pf_tile(cs, qx, qz - 1))
					end
				end
			elseif strict then
				-- ⛔ AFLOAT WITH NO USABLE FLOOD, PROVE IT OR STAY SILENT. `keep` starts true and the
				-- rejection above lives inside the valid-set branch, so a failed or unreadable flood used
				-- to hand back every distant land transition, NPC, item, sign and object while surfing —
				-- strict mode failing OPEN, which is both the opposite of what it is for and against the
				-- project's fail-closed rule (codex-review). The ways ashore still come through, because
				-- they carry their own proof above.
				keep = false
			end
			if keep then
				if best and best > 0 then
					-- ⛔ COPY THE WHOLE ENTRY, NOT FOUR OF ITS FIELDS. A gated entry used to be rebuilt as
					-- {name, x, z, gate}, which silently dropped everything that says WHAT the goal is:
					-- `warp` (the rail region a route aims beside), `reg` (a doorway's tiles), `rl` (an
					-- actor's own rail position) and `nodir` (the flag that says the position is not to be
					-- trusted at all). So an entry behind a Surf or Cut gate quietly became a bare pair of
					-- coordinates, and for a rail-form one those coordinates are line and front numbers.
					-- Pre-existing; found while checking what my per-refresh copies must carry
					-- (codex-review 2026-08-18, residual note).
					local c = {}
					for k, v in pairs(e) do c[k] = v end
					c.gate = reach.wordstr(best)
					out[#out + 1] = c
				else
					out[#out + 1] = e
				end
			end
		end
		return out
	end
	-- nav state (module-local; reset on map change / context exit)
	local lists, list_zone, active, ci, ii, entered = nil, nil, {}, 1, 1, false
	local function cur_cat() return active[ci] end
	local function cur_list() return (lists and cur_cat()) and lists[cur_cat()] or {} end
	local function sort_cat()                                           -- nearest-first by current player pos; ties broken
		local c = cur_cat(); if not c then return end                   -- by tile so the order is TOTAL (table.sort is
		local px, py = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)                       -- unstable — without this, equidistant items could
		table.sort(lists[c], function(a, b)                             -- swap between presses in the persistent Signs list)
			local da = math.abs(a.x-px)+math.abs(a.z-py)
			local db = math.abs(b.x-px)+math.abs(b.z-py)
			if da ~= db then return da < db end
			if a.x ~= b.x then return a.x < b.x end
			return a.z < b.z
		end)
		-- ⛔ NUMBER DUPLICATES (Ola 2026-08-08). A category that offers the same word twice — two "Item"s,
		-- two "Sign"s — leaves you no way to tell them apart, or to know which one P is about to route to.
		-- So identical names become "Item 1", "Item 2", …; a name that appears ONCE is left completely
		-- alone, because numbering a unique entry would only add noise.
		-- Numbered here, immediately after the sort and over the SAME list, which buys two things: 1 is
		-- always the NEAREST, and the number you just heard is the number you get when you press P —
		-- pf_start deliberately does not re-sort, so the two can never disagree.
		-- Stored as e.label rather than rewritten into e.name: the name is what the actor/warp readers
		-- computed and what the N-dump cross-checks against, and it must keep meaning exactly that.
		local seen, k = {}, {}
		for _, e in ipairs(lists[c]) do seen[e.name] = (seen[e.name] or 0) + 1 end
		for _, e in ipairs(lists[c]) do
			if seen[e.name] > 1 then
				k[e.name] = (k[e.name] or 0) + 1
				e.label = e.name .. " " .. k[e.name]
			else
				e.label = e.name
			end
		end
	end
	local function refresh()                                            -- ensure lists are current for this zone
		-- ⛔ NOT ZoneDataSystem+0x34 any more (2026-09-02, Codex verify). That field is the CACHE KEY of
		-- the one 0x30-byte zonedata header the system keeps — "the effective zone of the last header
		-- anyone loaded", which any lookup for another zone replaces — not the player's zone. The
		-- authoritative one is PlayerState+0 (mappos_id), through the version remap. It normally
		-- tracked, so this was never seen to fail; it was simply the wrong field to ask.
		local z = reg.zid(); if not z then return false end
		-- Rebuild once if the first build happened before the zone's RAIL data had streamed in: those warps
		-- were filed with no position at all, and the list is cached for as long as you stay in the zone.
		if lists and list_zone == z and lists.norc and rail.sys() then lists = nil end
		if not (lists and list_zone == z) then                          -- (re)build the static lists on a zone change
			local E = evd_find(z); if not E then return false end
			lists, list_zone = build(E, z), z
			-- immutable static snapshots; the spoken per-category lists are DERIVED from these every action
			-- (never filtered/sorted in place, so nothing is ever lost as reachability/actors change)
			lists.warps, lists.snpcs, lists.ssigns, lists.sobjs = lists.Transitions, lists.NPCs, lists.Signs, lists.Objects
			ci, ii, entered = 1, 1, false
		end
		local la = live_actors()                                        -- NPCs/Items/HM are LIVE (spawns/despawns/pickups);
		-- ⚠ AND A FAILED READ IS NOW AUDIBLE ON THE CONSOLE (codex-review 2026-09-01, pre-existing). When
		-- the actor manager cannot be read this returns nil, and nothing downstream can tell that from
		-- "this room has no actors": reach.for_ is handed no gate map, so a destination behind a Cut tree
		-- or a Strength boulder is announced as plainly walkable, and the obstacle itself vanishes from the
		-- list. That is exactly the wrong-reachability failure the gate mask exists to prevent, and it has
		-- always been silent. Fixing it properly means a validity contract through refresh() and every
		-- consumer — a change of its own, with its own test round. Until then, log it ONCE per session so
		-- the next report of "it said I could walk there" has a line pointing at the cause instead of
		-- costing a debugging round trip.
		if not la and not ow.lawarn then
			ow.lawarn = true
			dbg("live_actors: the actor manager did not read — this nav list has NO people, NO items and "
			    .. "NO HM gates, so a place behind a cut tree or boulder may be offered as plainly walkable.")
		end
		local O = {}                                                    -- static NPC list is the fallback if the manager
		for _, o in ipairs(lists.sobjs) do O[#O+1] = o end              -- is unreadable this frame. Objects = static
		if la then for _, o in ipairs(la.objs) do O[#O+1] = o end end   -- furniture + live actor-objects (gift box...).
		local T = {}                                                    -- Transitions = warps + LIVE connectors (the
		local px, pz = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)                       -- connector calc is scan-free, just a lookup).
		for _, w in ipairs(lists.warps) do
			-- A grid warp is a REGION, so its aim point follows the player the way a connector's does — a
			-- copy per refresh, never a write into the static snapshot. The region table itself is shared,
			-- which is what lets p toggle a route off even after the aim point has moved (see pf_start).
			if w.reg then
				local g = w.reg
				T[#T+1] = { name = w.name, reg = g,
					x = math.max(g[1], math.min(px, g[1] + g[3] - 1)),
					z = math.max(g[2], math.min(pz, g[2] + g[4] - 1)) }
			else
				T[#T+1] = w
			end
		end
		for _, c in ipairs(connectors()) do T[#T+1] = c end
		-- Rail-form SCRIPTED warps (see ow.RTRIG): the region is a set of rail cells you step ONTO, so
		-- the entry is an exact rail goal like a slide's, aimed at whichever of its walkable cells is
		-- nearest. Added here beside the connectors because, like them, it is computed live rather than
		-- snapshotted -- the aim point follows the player.
		if ow.RTRIG[z] then
			local rcT = rail.sys()
			if rcT then
				for _, q in ipairs(ow.RTRIG[z]) do
					local bd, bx, bz, bt
					for df = 0, math.max(q[3], 1) - 1 do
						for ds = 0, math.max(q[5], 1) - 1 do
							local l, fr, sd = q[1], q[2] + df, q[4] + ds
							if l < rcT.nl and rail.walk(rcT, l, fr, sd) then
								local wx, wz = rail.world(rcT, l, fr, sd)
								if wx then
									local d = math.abs(wx - px) + math.abs(wz - pz)
									if not bd or d < bd then bd, bx, bz, bt = d, wx, wz, { l, fr, sd } end
								end
							end
						end
					end
					if bt then
						T[#T+1] = { name = transition_name(q[6]), x = bx, z = bz,
							rl = { l = bt[1], f = bt[2], s = bt[3] } }
					end
				end
			end
		end
		-- PC terminals: found ONCE per zone and cached on the list, not on every nav key — refresh() runs
		-- on each press and a 41x41 tile sweep there would be exactly the kind of avoidable work that has
		-- caused lag before. Retried while empty, since chunks may still be streaming on the first press.
		if not lists.pcs or #lists.pcs == 0 then
			lists.pcs = reach.pc_tiles(pf_chunks(), ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y))
		end
		for _, o in ipairs(lists.pcs) do O[#O+1] = o end
		-- Castelia Gym floor switches, as Objects (Ola's call 2026-08-18: "put the switches in the objects
		-- category ... so just say switch"). A pressed switch can never fire again, so it simply stops being
		-- listed — the list is only ever the ones still worth walking to. An unreadable variable drops the
		-- switch rather than offering a dead tile.
		if ow.gym.here() then
			for _, s in ipairs(ow.gym.switches) do
				if ow.gym.var(s[3]) == 0 then O[#O+1] = { name = "Switch", x = s[1], z = s[2] } end
			end
		end
		-- Nimbasa Gym's four path switches, same treatment. These are TOGGLES rather than one-shots, so
		-- unlike Castelia's they never stop being worth walking to and all four are always listed — though
		-- there is only ever one on the platform you are standing on.
		if ow.rc.here() then
			for _, s in ipairs(ow.rc.sw) do O[#O+1] = { name = "Switch", x = s[1], z = s[2] } end
		end
		-- ⛔ ZONES WHERE WALKING IS NOT THE ONLY WAY TO GET SOMEWHERE (Ola 2026-08-19, Nimbasa Gym):
		-- "the reachability filter is correct here, however in this gym it is doing more harm than good".
		-- The filter's whole premise is that reachable == walkable-from-here. Nimbasa Gym breaks that
		-- premise outright: it is FIVE physically disconnected platforms (verified against the ROM's own
		-- collision — 361 walkable tiles in five components with no tile path between any two) joined only
		-- by roller-coaster rides. So the BFS is right that Elesa cannot be walked to, and hiding her is
		-- still the wrong answer: she is where the player is going. Opt the zone out entirely rather than
		-- try to teach the BFS about rides — R = nil makes reach.filter keep every entry untouched, which
		-- is exactly the fail-open behaviour it already uses for territory it cannot see.
		local R = (not ow.NOREACH[z]) and reach.for_(z, la and la.hm) or nil   -- hide only what the player provably
		-- ⚠ DECLARED HERE, above BOTH consumers. The Transitions list is assembled before the HM list, so
		-- computing this down there and reading it up here made two silent nil globals — caught by
		-- luaundef, which exists for exactly this and has now earned its keep twice.
		local scs = R and R.cs or pf_chunks(true)
		local afloat = ow.surfing()                                     -- the engine's own extended state
		if afloat then                                                  -- the shores this water can put you on
			for _, e in ipairs(reach.land_spots(scs, px, pz)) do T[#T + 1] = e end
		end
		lists.Transitions = reach.filter(T, R, afloat)                          -- cannot walk to from here; gate-labeled otherwise
		-- ── SPLIT ZONES: drop an exit stranded on a piece of land you cannot walk to (see ow.SPLIT) ──
		-- ⚠ ASYMMETRIC, ON PURPOSE. Only an entry we can positively PLACE on a minority island is hidden,
		-- and only when that is not the island you are standing on. An entry we cannot place is kept. So
		-- from Route 1's main path the western gate and beach finally stop being offered, while from the
		-- beach you still hear the ways back to the main path — mildly generous in the one direction that
		-- cannot strand anybody. Hiding a real exit is the single failure this must never commit, which is
		-- also why a zone with no baked island data is untouched.
		-- ⛔⛔ NEVER WHILE AFLOAT, and this was wrong until the review caught it. On the water the player
		-- is on no island at all, so ow.isle_at returns nil, which this filter reads as "the main island" —
		-- and then every shore on a minority island looks like somewhere else and gets HIDDEN. Surfing off
		-- Route 1's beach, that would have deleted the only listed way back onto land: the exact failure
		-- the comment two lines below says must never happen, committed by the code it is attached to.
		-- Afloat, the ways ashore come from reach.land_spots and are judged by the water you are on, which
		-- is a different question entirely — so this filter simply stands down (codex-review, HIGH).
		-- ⛔ AND NOT IN A ZONE WHOSE DOOR GEOMETRY DEFEATS THE PROBE. ow.SPLIT_OFF is generated, not
		-- hand-written: the bake walks every warp and lists any zone where a door's five-tile probe
		-- touches TWO land components, because there "the minority island wins" could hide a door that is
		-- perfectly reachable from the main island. Exactly one door in the game has that shape (Driftveil
		-- City's, to zone 104 at (197,395)) — raised by the codex-review as a residual, then measured.
		-- That zone keeps today's fail-open behaviour rather than risk the one mistake this must not make.
		-- ⚠ AND ONLY WHEN WE CAN PLACE THE PLAYER. If his own tile is on no baked island we cannot compare
		-- anything to it, and filtering on that would hide everything at once.
		local pisle = ow.SPLIT[z] and ow.isle_at(z, px, pz) or nil
		-- ⭐ THE SAME ISLAND RULE FOR EVERY CATEGORY (Ola, 2026-08-31: "make sure on land the npcs, items
		-- and all that also uses this rule"). A trainer standing on Route 1's beach is exactly as
		-- unreachable from the main path as the doorway beside him, and unreachable for exactly the same
		-- reason: the flood cannot see him, his tiles are never loaded, so reach.filter's fail-open kept
		-- him. Applied to Transitions, NPCs, Items, HM, Signs and Objects alike.
		-- SYMMETRIC: anything we can place on an island that is not yours is dropped, whether that is the
		-- beach seen from the main path or the main path seen from the beach. Anything we cannot place at
		-- all (his == nil) is kept — that is the only fail-open half left.
		-- ⚠ Afloat this stands down completely: on the water you are on no island, the ways ashore are
		-- judged by the water they are taken from, and reach.filter's strict mode already answers there.
		local function isle_only(list)
			if not (ow.SPLIT[z] and not afloat and not ow.SPLIT_OFF[z] and pisle) then return list end
			local keep = {}
			for _, e in ipairs(list) do
				local his
				for _, d in ipairs({ { 0, 0 }, { 1, 0 }, { -1, 0 }, { 0, 1 }, { 0, -1 } }) do
					-- the aim tile itself is often a BLOCKED door tile, so the approach beside it is what
					-- actually says which island the entry belongs to (Route 1's gate: (742,747) is solid,
					-- (743,747) is the beach). Harmless for an actor, whose own tile is already land.
					his = his or ow.isle_at(z, (e.x or 0) + d[1], (e.z or 0) + d[2])
				end
				if his == nil or his == pisle then keep[#keep + 1] = e end
			end
			return keep
		end
		lists.Transitions = isle_only(lists.Transitions)
		-- ── ELITE FOUR HALL: drop the rooms whose member is already beaten (see ow.E4_FLAG) ─────────
		-- ⛔ FAILS OPEN, and the direction is deliberate. Only a flag we positively READ as set hides a
		-- door; an unreadable flag leaves it listed. A door wrongly listed costs one "can't reach"; a door
		-- wrongly HIDDEN is a room Ola cannot know exists — and this is the one place in the game where
		-- being unable to find the next doorway strands a run he cannot save his way out of.
		if z == ow.E4_HALL then
			local keep = {}
			for _, e in ipairs(lists.Transitions) do
				local f = e.dz and ow.E4_FLAG[e.dz]
				if not (f and ow.evflag(f) == true) then keep[#keep + 1] = e end
			end
			lists.Transitions = keep
		end
		-- ── VICTORY ROAD: cut the list to the terrace you are standing on (see ow.RAILISLE) ─────────
		-- reach.filter above cannot do this: it floods pf_chunks(), a pure rail zone has none, so R is
		-- nil there and every entry survives. This is the rail-side answer to the same question.
		-- ⛔ FAILS OPEN AT EVERY STEP. No rail system, no valid rail position (mid-junction, or a stale
		-- serialized position that fails rail.onrail's world cross-check), or an entry whose island we
		-- cannot name — each of those keeps the list exactly as it was. The one thing this must never do
		-- is go quiet and leave him on a mountain with no exits listed.
		local rc2 = ow.RAILISLE[z] and rail.sys() or nil
		if rc2 and not ow.isle_certified(rc2, z) then rc2 = nil end     -- not this zone's graph: filter nothing
		local mine = rc2 and rail.isle_now(rc2) or nil
		-- ⛔ THE ISLAND CHANGES WITHOUT THE ZONE CHANGING, AND THE CATEGORY LIST CHANGES WITH IT. Every
		-- other place in the game rebuilds `active` only when the zone does, which is also when
		-- ci/ii/entered are reset. Here a slide can take you from a terrace that has Slides to one that
		-- has none (island 4 has zone 222's door and no outgoing slide), so `active` shrinks under a
		-- numeric `ci` that is only clamped when it overruns -- and the next nav key would read out an
		-- entry from whichever category inherited the index, without naming that category first. For a
		-- screen-reader user that is the list silently changing meaning at exactly the moment this
		-- feature exists to make it trustworthy (codex-review, MEDIUM).
		-- Reset the same three things a zone change resets, and SAY NOTHING -- Ola asked for silence on
		-- terrace change, so `entered = false` just means the next key he presses names the category
		-- before its item, which is the normal first-press behaviour.
		-- ⚠ Only ever compared between two KNOWN islands: a transient unreadable frame (mine == nil)
		-- must not count as a change, or a bad read would reset his place in the list for nothing.
		if mine and lists.isle and mine ~= lists.isle then ci, ii, entered = 1, 1, false end
		if mine then lists.isle = mine end
		-- ⭐ FILTER BY THE POCKET YOU CAN WALK IN, NOT THE TERRACE YOU STAND ON (Ola 2026-08-28, after
		-- testing: "I am in a place where I can't route to anything shown in the nav list"). His Q4
		-- answer already asked for exactly this -- "I want you to only see what you can reach from where
		-- you currently are located" -- and the island filter was a coarser approximation of it. See
		-- rail.pieces for why the pockets are real and why weak connectivity is the safe choice.
		local mypc = rc2 and rail.piece_now(rc2) or nil
		if mine and mypc then
			local pc = rail.pieces(rc2)
			local keep = {}
			for _, e in ipairs(lists.Transitions) do
				-- Anything that is not a rail warp (a connector, a way off the water) is kept untouched
				-- rather than judged by a rule that cannot see it.
				local w = e.warp
				-- An entry with its OWN rail position (a scripted trigger, ow.RTRIG) is placed exactly,
				-- the same way the NPC/Item lists are; anything with neither is kept.
				if not w and e.rl and e.rl.l then
					if pc[rail.pkey(e.rl.l, e.rl.f, e.rl.s)] == mypc then keep[#keep + 1] = e end
				elseif not (w and w.l) then keep[#keep + 1] = e
				elseif rc2.isle[w.l] ~= mine then                        -- wrong terrace: never reachable
					-- dropped
				else
					-- A warp's own region is SOLID (you step into it), so ask whether any walkable tile
					-- next to it is in our pocket -- that is the tile a press would fire from.
					local ok = false
					for df = 0, math.max(w.fn, 1) - 1 do
						for ds = 0, math.max(w.sn, 1) - 1 do
							local f, s = w.f0 + df, w.s0 + ds
							for _, nb in ipairs({ { f - 1, s }, { f + 1, s }, { f, s - 1 }, { f, s + 1 } }) do
								if pc[rail.pkey(w.l, nb[1], nb[2])] == mypc then ok = true end
							end
						end
					end
					if ok then keep[#keep + 1] = e end
				end
			end
			lists.Transitions = keep
		end
		-- ── SLIDES (Victory Road) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		-- Ola, 2026-08-28: "put it in a slides category, we can just say slide for it ... for each slide
		-- only show the tile on it closest, so each slide gets one entry".
		-- A slide's nav entry is an ordinary WALK-TO goal aimed at a launch cell — but arriving is NOT
		-- taking it (the trigger is a Down press ON the cell, see ow.SLIDE), so the follower names that
		-- last press on arrival the way it does for a warp. The
		-- router already knows how to reach an exact rail tile (e.rl), the same way it reaches an actor.
		-- One entry per RUN, aimed at whichever tile of that run is nearest, exactly like a wide doorway.
		-- Baked rather than scanned: the runs come from the rail tilemap, which is static ROM data and
		-- BYTE-IDENTICAL between White and Black (measured, research/re/vr_black_check.py), so finding
		-- them live would be a per-entry sweep of every line's tile grid for an answer that never changes.
		-- Only the NEAREST-tile choice is live, and that is a handful of rail.world calls per press.
		if mine and ow.SLIDE[z] then
			local S = {}
			for _, run in ipairs(ow.SLIDE[z]) do
				local best, bx, bz, bt
				local pc = rail.pieces(rc2)
				for _, t in ipairs(run) do
					-- ⛔⛔ AIM AT A CELL HE CAN ACTUALLY WALK TO. A slide run can straddle a wall: line 15's
					-- rim is one slide by landing, but the line has two solid rows across it, so its cells
					-- sit in three different pockets. Picking the nearest by world distance alone chose a
					-- cell behind a wall four tiles away while two cells of the SAME slide sat two steps
					-- from him -- and the route then failed with the slide right beside him. That is what
					-- "I can't route to anything shown in the nav list" was, for the slides.
					-- ⚠ It was a TIE that did it: both candidates were 4 tiles away by Manhattan distance,
					-- and the loop keeps the first on a tie. So this was never going to show up as a
					-- near-miss; it was one arbitrary pick away from correct the whole time.
					-- ⛔ VALIDATE THE BAKED COORDINATE AGAINST THE LIVE MAP, don't just trust it (the same
					-- rule ow.sink follows for the quicksand centres). These line/front/side triples are
					-- area-relative, and if the cache ever held a rail area other than 9 while the zone
					-- still read 214 — mid-transition, say — they would index a DIFFERENT graph and place
					-- a "Slide" on an arbitrary tile. Re-reading the class costs one read per tile of the
					-- current terrace's runs and makes that impossible: a wrong area cannot also happen to
					-- have class 0xA0 at all the same coordinates.
					if rc2.isle[t[1]] == mine and ow.slidetile(rc2, t[1], t[2], t[3])
					   and (mypc == nil or pc[rail.pkey(t[1], t[2], t[3])] == mypc) then
						local wx, wz = rail.world(rc2, t[1], t[2], t[3])
						if wx then
							local d = math.abs(wx - px) + math.abs(wz - pz)
							if not best or d < best then best, bx, bz, bt = d, wx, wz, t end
						end
					end
				end
				-- a run whose every tile failed to convert is dropped rather than listed at (0,0) —
				-- an entry the router would walk him to the corner of the map for is worse than none
				if bt then
					-- ⭐ NAMED BY WHERE IT LANDS. Ola first said a bare "Slide" was enough, on the grounds
					-- that the terrace filter means there are never many at once — true of the COUNT, but
					-- it was an argument about quantity, not consequence. He then said: "I know at least
					-- from the top floor there should be a slide that goes all the way down", and the
					-- census bears him out — the summit has three slides, two dropping one terrace and
					-- one riding all the way to the bottom. Bare names there would make an expensive
					-- mistake indistinguishable from a cheap one, so he agreed to name them (2026-08-28).
					-- The floor comes from the game's own geometry (the landing island's terrace), never
					-- from a guess, and an island we cannot name falls back to the bare word.
					-- ⛔ AND THE NAME FAILS CLOSED WHEN THE GRAPH IS NOT THE ONE WE BAKED AGAINST. ov063's
					-- ray takes the nearest **enabled** line, honouring FieldRailSystem+0x50's disabled
					-- list, so a disabled line can move a landing. The census was taken with that list in
					-- its normal all-0xFFFF state, which is what it reads whenever anyone is walking
					-- around in there — but "normally empty" is not "always empty", and claiming a floor
					-- we cannot stand behind is worse than not naming one. So if ANY line is disabled,
					-- every slide drops back to the bare word rather than risk sending him down the wrong
					-- chute. (Codex raised exactly this as an integration risk.)
					local fl = (not rc2.any_dis) and ow.ISLE_FLOOR[z] and ow.ISLE_FLOOR[z][run.dest]
					-- `slide` marks this goal as one that needs ONE MORE PRESS after you arrive — see
					-- the arrival cue in the follower. Walking onto the cell is NOT the trigger.
					S[#S + 1] = { name = fl and ("Slide to " .. fl .. "F") or "Slide", x = bx, z = bz,
						slide = true, rl = { l = bt[1], f = bt[2], s = bt[3] } }
				end
			end
			lists.Slides = S
		else
			-- ⚠ THE ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE. When we cannot name the island, Transitions fail OPEN (keep
			-- everything) and Slides fail CLOSED (list nothing), which looks inconsistent and is not:
			-- being unable to find an EXIT strands him on a mountain, so that list must never shrink on a
			-- bad read; a slide is a convenience, and eighteen "Slide N" entries scattered over seven
			-- terraces he cannot reach is precisely the clutter this whole change exists to remove. The
			-- N-dump prints `onrail=NO` in that case, so a silent Slides list has a visible cause.
			lists.Slides = {}
		end
		lists.NPCs = isle_only(reach.filter(la and la.npcs or lists.snpcs, R, afloat))
		lists.Items = isle_only(reach.filter(la and la.items or {}, R, afloat))
		-- HM = the live obstacles (cut trees, smashable rocks) PLUS one Surf spot per body of water.
		local H = {}
		-- ⛔ ONLY THE OBSTACLES HE CAN ACTUALLY DO SOMETHING ABOUT (ow.can_clear — Ola's call). Filtered
		-- HERE, on the way into the LIST, and not where the actors are collected: `la.hm` goes on to
		-- reach.for_ unfiltered, so the reachability gate still knows a boulder is there and still says
		-- ", Strength" about what lies beyond it.
		-- ⚠ ONE BAG WALK PER HM, NOT PER OBSTACLE. ow.bag_qty walks 109 pocket records; Mistralton Cave has
		-- three boulders in a room and this list is rebuilt on every nav key press, so the naive form would
		-- have paid for that walk three times over for one answer. Memoized per refresh rather than cached
		-- on ow, because a cache that outlives the press would still say "no" the moment after he is handed
		-- the HM (and the nav lag tracer at the top of this module exists because that cost is real).
		local hmok = {}
		for _, e in ipairs(la and la.hm or {}) do
			local it = e.model and ow.HM_ITEM[e.model]
			if it and hmok[it] == nil then hmok[it] = ow.can_clear(e.model) end
			if not it or hmok[it] then H[#H + 1] = e end
		end
		-- On the water you want the ways OFF; on land you want the ways IN — offering entry points while
		-- already surfing is noise, and Ola confirmed they should not show there (questions_surf.md Q3).
		-- The ways OFF are TRANSITIONS, not HM obstacles (his Q4): they are named places the water takes
		-- you to, which is exactly what that list is for. The ways IN stay under HM beside Cut and Rock
		-- Smash, because that is what they are — an obstacle you need a move for.
		if not afloat then
			local sp = reach.surf_spots(scs, px, pz)
			-- ⛔ JUDGE THE LIVE SPOTS BEFORE DECIDING WHETHER TO SUPPLEMENT. The old test was the raw
			-- `#sp == 0`, taken before reach.filter later drops the unreachable ones — so a single live
			-- spot on the FAR shore (near enough to be swept, but across the water) suppressed every baked
			-- candidate and was then filtered out itself, leaving no Surf entry at all (codex-review).
			-- In a split zone a spot on another island is not a way in from here, so it is dropped on the
			-- same test the transition filter uses; elsewhere every live spot is kept exactly as before.
			-- ⛔ ONE STAND-DOWN TEST, THE SAME ONE isle_only USES. This block had its own copy that checked
			-- only `ow.SPLIT[z] and not afloat`, and every omission was a real bug (codex-review):
			--   * ow.SPLIT_OFF[z] was ignored, so the one opted-out zone was island-filtered here anyway;
			--   * a nil player island was not required, so an unplaceable player compared 0 against nil and
			--     rejected every candidate;
			--   * and a zone with SURFPT but no SPLIT left `mine` nil, which `e[4] == mine` could never
			--     match — index 0 being read as "no island" all over again, in the other direction. Two
			--     zones in the shipped tables are exactly that shape (316 and 412), so the baked fallback
			--     was dead in both.
			local mine = (ow.SPLIT[z] and not afloat and not ow.SPLIT_OFF[z])
				and ow.isle_at(z, px, pz) or nil                     -- island index (0 = main); nil = do not classify
			-- ⛔ AND COUNT ONLY WHAT SURVIVES THE REAL FILTERS. `usable` used to be incremented after the
			-- island test alone, but reach.filter runs later over the whole HM list — so a same-island live
			-- spot that reach.filter then removed (a wall in the way, a blocker, a path leaving the loaded
			-- window) still suppressed the baked fallback and left NO Surf entry at all. Running the live
			-- spots through the same two filters first makes the count mean what it says.
			sp = isle_only(reach.filter(sp, R, afloat))
			-- ⭐ AND THE ONES THE LIVE SWEEP IS TOO SHORT-SIGHTED TO FIND. It looks 20 tiles around you
			-- through LOADED chunks; on Route 1 the only entry from the main path is at (781, 707..711),
			-- so from either end of the route it is both out of range and not loaded, and Ola heard
			-- nothing. The bake is computed over the whole matrix, so it is the only source that can ever
			-- answer for a map bigger than the four chunks the game keeps loaded.
			-- ⭐⭐ THE WHOLE ZONE, NOT JUST WHEN THE LIVE SWEEP CAME UP EMPTY (Ola, 2026-09-01, on Route 6:
			-- "the surf check we have for surf spots on a map needs to be extended to cover the entire map,
			-- as on route 6 I couldn't see all spots at once"). Route 6 has **eight** separate bodies of
			-- water; the live sweep found the one beside him, so the old `usable == 0` test was false and
			-- the baked table — which holds all eight — was never consulted at all. That test was written
			-- for Route 1, where the only question was whether ANY spot existed, and it is exactly wrong on
			-- a route with many. So the two sources now MERGE rather than one standing down for the other:
			--   * every live spot is kept exactly as before — that is what serves Dragonspiral Tower, where
			--     the water belongs to Route 8 and this table has no entry of its own to offer;
			--   * plus one entry for every baked body of water the live sweep did NOT already find.
			-- ⚠ COVERAGE IS MATCHED ON THE ENTRY'S OWN (x, z, direction), not on distance or on a count.
			-- Both sources derive that triple from the same tile rule (stand on land, press into the water
			-- or over a 0x41/0x44 shore), so a body the live sweep found is matched exactly and never
			-- offered twice. A live spot the bake does not know — pressed from a NEIGHBOURING zone's land,
			-- because pf_tile ignores zone ownership and the bake files an entry under the zone you stand
			-- in — marks nothing, which is right: it is an extra way in, not a duplicate of one.
			-- ⚠ AND THE MATCH IS AGAINST THE **FILTERED** LIVE LIST. A live spot that reach.filter dropped
			-- (across the water, behind a wall) is not a way in from here, so its body still deserves its
			-- baked candidate — which then faces the same filter on its own merits.
			-- ⚠ ONE PER BODY OF WATER (Ola: "a big lake is one Surf you can route to, not twenty"), and
			-- filtered to YOUR island first: Route 1's two shores open onto the SAME body, so the body id
			-- alone would have offered the beach's entry from the main path.
			-- ⭐ AND THE BAKE IS ALSO WHAT MAKES "ONE PER BODY" TRUE OF THE **LIVE** SPOTS. The sweep
			-- floods the water it can see, so a lake that enters its 20-tile box in two places looks like
			-- two lakes and yields two entries — invisible while the box was the only source, obvious the
			-- moment the whole zone is listed beside it (Route 6 measured 10 entries for 8 bodies:
			-- research/re/surf_merge_test.py). The bake knows which body each triple opens, so live spots
			-- are collapsed by that same body id, nearest kept. A live spot the bake has no id for is
			-- ALWAYS kept, never collapsed: an unknown body is a way in we cannot rule on, and the whole
			-- reason this list exists is that a way in he never hears about is worse than one too many.
			-- ⚠ TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, TWO DIFFERENT GATES. WHICH BODY a triple opens is a fact about
			-- the water and does not depend on the island tables at all, so `bodyof` is built from every
			-- zone that has a bake. Whether we may ADD a baked entry does depend on being able to place
			-- the player (an island we cannot classify would offer a shore across the water), so `pts`
			-- keeps the older, stricter gate. Driftveil City is exactly the zone that separates them: it
			-- is opted out of island filtering (ow.SPLIT_OFF, ambiguous door geometry) and its live sweep
			-- still offered the same body twice.
			local pts = nil                                          -- the baked entries, when we may offer them
			if ow.SURFPT[z] and ow.can_surf() and (mine ~= nil or not ow.SPLIT[z]) then pts = ow.SURFPT[z] end
			-- ⛔⛔ THE UNIT IS ONE BODY **PER ISLAND**, NEVER ONE BODY. Driftveil City proves why: every
			-- one of its baked entries opens the SAME lake (body 4) and they sit on THREE different
			-- landmasses (islands 0, 1 and 3). Collapsing that harbour by body alone left one entry, and
			-- from the wrong shore it is one the player cannot walk to — a way in traded for none at all.
			-- Everywhere else the pair is the same thing as the body: a zone with no ow.SPLIT bakes island
			-- 0 for every entry, and where the island IS known the baked entries are already cut to the
			-- player's own, so the extra half of the key changes nothing. It only ever separates.
			local bodyof = {}                                        -- (x,z) -> press direction -> "body:island"
			for _, e in ipairs(ow.SURFPT[z] or {}) do
				local k = e[1] * 100000 + e[2]
				bodyof[k] = bodyof[k] or {}
				bodyof[k][e[3]] = e[5] .. ":" .. e[4]
			end
			local best = {}                                          -- "body:island" -> the one entry we offer
			for _, e in ipairs(sp) do                                -- LIVE first: it outranks the bake
				local bk = bodyof[(e.x or 0) * 100000 + (e.z or 0)]
				local b = bk and bk[e.sdir]
				if not b then
					H[#H + 1] = e                                    -- body unknown to the bake: keep it
				else
					local d = math.abs((e.x or 0) - px) + math.abs((e.z or 0) - pz)
					local c = best[b]
					if not c or d < c.d then best[b] = { d = d, e = e } end
				end
			end
			for _, e in ipairs(pts or {}) do
				-- ⚠ e[4] IS THE ISLAND INDEX AND 0 IS A REAL ONE. This used to translate 0 to nil,
				-- because back then ow.isle_at returned nil for the main island; once every island was
				-- baked, 0 became the main island's own index and the translation silently dropped every
				-- main-island entry — the Route 1 surf spot vanished from the whole main path. Caught by
				-- re-running the offline test against Ola's own positions, not by reading the code.
				-- with no classification available (unbaked or opted-out zone) every entry is offered;
				-- otherwise only the ones on the island the player is standing on.
				if mine == nil or e[4] == mine then
					local k = e[5] .. ":" .. e[4]
					local c = best[k]
					if not (c and c.e) then                          -- no live entry claimed this body
						local d = math.abs(e[1] - px) + math.abs(e[2] - pz)
						if not c or d < c.d then best[k] = { d = d, x = e[1], z = e[2], dir = e[3] } end
					end
				end
			end
			for _, c in pairs(best) do
				H[#H + 1] = c.e or { name = "Surf", x = c.x, z = c.z, sdir = c.dir }
			end
		else                                                            -- afloat: the climbs, beside Surf's kin
			for _, e in ipairs(reach.fall_spots(scs, px, pz)) do H[#H + 1] = e end
		end
		lists.HM = isle_only(reach.filter(H, R, afloat))
		lists.Signs = isle_only(reach.filter(lists.ssigns, R, afloat))
		lists.Objects = isle_only(reach.filter(O, R, afloat))
		-- ⭐ THE SAME POCKET RULE FOR EVERY OTHER CATEGORY. Ola, 2026-08-28: "you need to filter items and
		-- npcs and anything else the same way on the outside zone, transitions and slides are now correct,
		-- but I can see all npcs and items and most i can't go to from where I am."
		-- He is right and the reason is the same one that made him see all 18 doors: reach.filter floods
		-- pf_chunks(), a pure rail zone has none, so R is nil and every entry survives. Transitions and
		-- Slides got the rail-side answer; these did not, and a trainer three pockets away is exactly as
		-- useless as a door three pockets away.
		-- Live NPCs, Items, HM obstacles and Objects all carry their own rail position (`rl`, from
		-- rail.actor_pos), so they can be placed exactly -- no nearest-tile guessing, which is what the
		-- Celestial Tower work proved goes wrong when a projection lands on the wrong level.
		-- ⛔ ANYTHING WE CANNOT PLACE IS KEPT. A static Sign has only world coordinates (and a rail-form
		-- trigger is already flagged nodir because its fields are not world units at all), so there is
		-- nothing to test it against. Guessing a pocket for it from its x/z could hide a real one; the
		-- standing rule holds for everything except the case Ola explicitly asked to invert.
		if mine and mypc then
			local pc = rail.pieces(rc2)
			for _, cat in ipairs({ "NPCs", "Items", "HM", "Signs", "Objects" }) do
				local keep = {}
				for _, e in ipairs(lists[cat] or {}) do
					local r = e.rl
					if not (r and r.l) then keep[#keep + 1] = e         -- cannot place it: keep it
					elseif pc[rail.pkey(r.l, r.f, r.s)] == mypc then keep[#keep + 1] = e end
				end
				lists[cat] = keep
			end
		end
		active = {}
		for _, c in ipairs(CATS) do if #lists[c] > 0 then active[#active+1] = c end end
		if ci > #active then ci = (#active > 0) and #active or 1 end     -- clamp if a category just emptied (NPCs all left)
		sort_cat()                                                      -- re-sort the ACTIVE list every action: the rebuilds
		return true                                                     -- above reset it to canonical order, so without this
	end                                                                 -- the index spoken on entry lies on the next key
	                                                                    -- (Codex finding 2026-07-22; stationary = stable).
	local function item_line(e, idx, n)                                 -- gate = the HM word(s) in the way ("Item, Cut, ...")
		-- ⛔ NO DIRECTION IS BETTER THAN A WRONG ONE (Ola 2026-08-08, Skyarrow Bridge: both transitions
		-- announced as "over 3K steps south", and one of them is north). In RAIL zones the warp record
		-- does NOT hold world units — dumping a/1/2/5 shows Nuvema's warps at x=12520 world units (782
		-- tiles, sensible) while Skyarrow's read 13 / 0 / 0xFFFD / 1 / 7: small numbers and a NEGATIVE
		-- one, i.e. rail coordinates. Reading 0xFFFD as an unsigned world coord gives 65533/16 = 4095
		-- tiles, which IS that "3000 south", identically for both warps. Which field is the line, which
		-- the distance along it and which the offset across is NOT yet established: no assignment
		-- explains all 79 rail-zone warp records in the game (best is 64/79), and two different ones
		-- both "work" for the bridge, so any pick would be confidently wrong somewhere. Until it is
		-- measured, such an entry is still LISTED and still named — it just does not claim a bearing.
		local d = (not e.nodir) and dirword(e.x, e.z) or nil
		-- e.label is the name with its duplicate number ("Item 2") when the category holds more than one of
		-- that name, and plain e.name otherwise. `or e.name` keeps this honest if a list is ever announced
		-- before sort_cat has numbered it — a missing number is fine, a nil concatenation is not.
		return (e.label or e.name)..(e.gate and (", "..e.gate) or "")..(d and (", "..d) or "")..", "..idx.." of "..n
	end
	local function announce_item(interrupt)
		local l = cur_list(); if #l == 0 then return end
		if ii < 1 then ii = #l elseif ii > #l then ii = 1 end
		say(item_line(l[ii], ii, #l), interrupt)
	end
	local function switch_cat(dir)                                      -- i/o (pad: D-pad left/right)
		if #active == 0 then return end
		ci = ((ci-1+dir) % #active) + 1; ii = 1; sort_cat()
		local l = cur_list()
		-- ⛔ THE CATEGORY INTERRUPTS, THE ITEM QUEUES BEHIND IT (Ola, 2026-08-04, on the controller round).
		-- This used to be ONE queued call, so changing category waited politely behind whatever was already
		-- speaking — but changing category IS navigation, and navigation interrupts (feedback-speech-
		-- interrupt-queue). Splitting it gets both halves right: you hear the new category at once, and the
		-- item it landed on follows without cutting the category off.
		say(cur_cat(), true)
		if #l > 0 then say(item_line(l[1], 1, #l), false) end
	end

	-- ── PATHFINDER (p) : A* over the LIVE walkability grid → continuous turn-by-turn (NO auto-walk). ──────
	-- Reads collision straight from the loaded 3D map chunks (white.lua "OVERWORLD COLLISION"; Ola-confirmed),
	-- so it's correct in story-gated areas (no offline bake). Chain: field = u32(u32(0x02146268 + DELTA)+0x20);
	-- G3DMapper = u32(field+0x48); INDEX width = u16(g3d+6) (= ChunkSpan>>16); chunks = u32(g3d+0x24) array
	-- (stride 0x28, entry+0 = FieldChunk*, count u16@g3d+0x2e), a chunk is loaded iff u32(chunk+0xd0)~=0;
	-- chunk CENTRE tile = u16@chunk+6/+0xe; container = u32(chunk+0xd4); layerData = container+u32(container+8);
	-- grid @ layerData+4, 8 bytes/tile; localX = (tile-centre)+width/2; TileType = u32(grid tile+4)&0x7FFFFFFF.
	-- walkable = Class~=0xFF and Flags bit0==0. LEDGES (Class 0x74/75/73/72 = up/down/left/right, RE'd from White
	-- MapTile_IsLedge* @0x021ab178+ and the move resolver @0x021a6140: a ledge is hopped ONLY when MOVING in its
	-- OWN direction — a 2-tile, one-way hop; the engine supports all 4 dirs, INCLUDING up/north 0x74). Two separate
	-- properties: the FLAG (Flags bit0=1 on every ledge tile, verified in the land data) makes it a WALL you can't
	-- stand on; the CLASS (0x74/75/73/72) is the ONE direction it opens. So the pathfinder hops each ledge in its
	-- own direction — a DOWN ledge is a shortcut going down but a WALL going up (that IS "can't jump up a ledge"),
	-- an UP ledge hops north but walls the other ways — and any ledge from a non-hop side is a plain wall it routes
	-- AROUND. Dirs: up=-Y, down=+Y, left=-X, right=+X (Y = A.map_y = Z/N-S). Targets = the selected nav item's tile.
	-- Only LOADED chunks have collision -> routes are within the loaded window (far target = best-effort + recompute).
	local pf = nil                                                      -- active route {tiles, segAt, pi, gx, gz, name}
	-- A route REQUEST held until the room settles lives on ow.dg rather than in a local: this function
	-- is already at Lua's 200-local ceiling, and the state is zone-specific, so the module table is its
	-- proper home anyway.
	local pf_reenter = false                                            -- armed whenever this module loses control with
	                                                                    -- a route live (battle, dialogue, menu, a gym
	                                                                    -- switch animation) -> re-plan + speak the next
	                                                                    -- turn on the frame we get control back.
	-- best-effort A*: returns MOVES ({dir,span}; span 2 = a one-way ledge hop) to the goal, or as far toward it as
	-- the LOADED chunks allow (collision only exists for streamed-in chunks, so a far / zone-edge target is
	-- approached by routing to the nearest loaded tile + recomputing as you walk). Returns (moves, reachedGoal),
	-- or nil if it can't get any closer to the goal from here (e.g. the only way around is not yet streamed in).
	-- ⛔ PEOPLE AND GROUND ITEMS BLOCK THEIR TILE (Ola 2026-08-08: "it thinks tiles with an npc on them
	-- are walkable, but that is wrong, same with items"). The tilemap only describes the GROUND; actor
	-- occupancy is a separate check in the engine, and we never made it. Built from the same live actor
	-- manager the nav list uses, so it costs one pass we already know how to do — and only when a route
	-- is BUILT, never per frame.
	-- Two deliberate exemptions: the GOAL, because walking to a person means stopping next to them and
	-- best-effort routing already does that; and a total failure, because people move — if occupancy
	-- alone makes the target unreachable we re-route ignoring it rather than claim you cannot get there.
	-- `hardonly` drops the PEOPLE and keeps only what will never move on its own — ground items, HM
	-- obstacles (cut trees, boulders) and actor-objects. The "route anyway" retries below are justified by
	-- "whoever is in the doorway will step aside", which is true of a person and false of a boulder; a
	-- retry that drops everything can hand out a confident route through a permanent obstacle
	-- (codex-review 2026-08-18 — pre-existing in the ordinary path, fixed here for the gym's ladder).
	--
	-- ⛔⛔ OCCUPANCY IS THE ENGINE'S RULE, NOT OUR MODEL TABLE (2026-08-24, Chargestone Cave).
	-- Ola: "path finder tells me to walk left in a spot where I can't." His own N-dump caught the exact
	-- moment — player at (10,26) in zone 195, route `next="1 left"` onto (9,26) — and the dump's own actor
	-- list has a live actor standing on (9,26): model 0, script 0, spawnFlag 0. It is one of TWELVE such
	-- actors the cave spawns that appear in no entity file (a/1/2/5 file 195 holds 18 actors and not one
	-- of them is these), and that one is parked in the single gap in the ledge wall to the north — the
	-- only way on. Occupancy used to be built from live_actors(), which classifies by MODEL id and drops
	-- every PROP_MODELS entry, model 0 among them, so the router could not see it. The dump's own
	-- "actors occupying tiles" line proves it: (9,26) is not in it.
	-- The engine does not classify. CheckActorNewPosOtherActorCollision (White 0x02163BCC) iterates the
	-- manager and, for each actor:
	--   * the iterator (0x0216DD3C) yields a slot only when the flag test `u32[actor] & 1` is nonzero;
	--   * the per-actor test (0x02163B10) SKIPS it when `u32[actor] & 0x80` is nonzero — that bit, not the
	--     render-hidden 0x200 the NPC list uses, is the only exemption;
	--   * it builds an inclusive footprint (0x02163A28) from the actor's OWN collision size — u8 +0x7C
	--     wide, u8 +0x7D tall, rect {x, z-(h-1)} .. {x+(w-1), z} — and collides when the boxes overlap;
	--   * and only when the grid Ys are equal (s16 +0x3E, getter 0x0216D874).
	-- It never looks at model, sprite, resource or zone. Every accessor above was read straight out of the
	-- disassembly: x = s16 +0x3C (0x0216D85C), z = s16 +0x40 (0x0216D88C), flags = `u32[actor] & mask`
	-- (0x0216D57C), size = u8 +0x7C/+0x7D (0x0216D93C/0x0216D944).
	-- This is the SAME finding the Driftveil shaft already needed (ow.dv.blocked, which keeps its own copy
	-- because A* and the lift planner there must block on exactly the same set). It was scoped to that one
	-- room on the reasoning that model 0 "has never been shown to block at all" — Chargestone is the second
	-- room to show that it does, so the rule moves to the common path where it always belonged.
	-- `hardonly` still needs a people/not-people split, and the engine has no predicate for "will step
	-- aside" — that is our judgement, not the game's — so the model tables stay for exactly that, and only
	-- ever to RELAX the set, never to build it.
	function ow.s16(a)                                                  -- the engine reads these fields with LDRSH
		local v = u16(a)
		return (v >= 0x8000) and (v - 0x10000) or v
	end
	-- Heights are signed 16.16 and routinely negative (Dragonspiral 2F drops to -5 tiles), so a raw u32
	-- read of one is off by 2^32 rather than slightly wrong.
	function ow.s32(a)
		local v = u32(a)
		return (v >= 0x80000000) and (v - 0x100000000) or v
	end
	function ow.pactor()                                                -- the player's own FieldActor
		local gs = u32(GAMESYS_PTR); if not inram(gs) then return nil end
		local fl = u32(gs + 0x20); if not inram(fl) then return nil end
		local fp = u32(fl + 0x8C); if not inram(fp) then return nil end
		local co = u32(fp + 0x04); if not inram(co) then return nil end
		local ac = u32(co + 0x1C)
		return inram(ac) and ac or nil                                  -- same four hops as ow.facing()
	end
	-- ⭐⭐ IS A TWO-TILE JUMP IN FLIGHT? The player's SELECTED ACTOR ACTION, at FieldActor + 0x26; the four
	-- directional forms of the two-tile jump are 0x38..0x3B (Codex, report_jump_inflight.md).
	-- WHY THIS AND NOT A POSITION TEST: during one rail hop the stored RailPosition reads the blocked ramp
	-- cell for EIGHT field snapshots (U2..U9 of the action updater's seventeen), and ComponentIsLine stays
	-- 1 throughout, so nothing else holds the follower off. The grid ledge is worse: its exposed TILE is
	-- derived from the continuous world position and reads the LEDGE tile for NINE snapshots. Those are the
	-- frames Ola hears as "Recalculating" on a route that is perfectly correct.
	-- ⚠ AND NOT the physical-done bit at FieldActor+0x04 & 0x400 either: it flips one PlayerState snapshot
	-- EARLY, while the grid copy still reports the old tile. The selected action is installed before the
	-- jump starts, survives the one-update gap between the two RailUnit commands, and is only replaced once
	-- the controller has handed control back — exactly the window a speech guard needs.
	-- ⚠ Unreadable answers nil and the caller must NOT hold on it: a route that never advances is as bad
	-- as one that talks too much.
	function ow.jumping()
		local ac = ow.pactor(); if not ac then return nil end
		local a = u16(ac + 0x26)
		return a >= 0x38 and a <= 0x3B
	end
	-- ⛔⛔ RETURNS (occ, ok). THE SECOND VALUE IS THE WHOLE POINT: an empty room and an unreadable actor
	-- manager are BOTH "no blockers found", and A* has always read a nil occupancy as "nothing is in the
	-- way" — which is precisely the failure this function exists to end. A transient bad read would have
	-- recreated Ola's bug exactly, with the router confidently walking him through every actor in the room
	-- (codex-review 2026-08-24, HIGH). So a failed read says so, and pf_build refuses the route.
	-- The capacity is REJECTED rather than clamped for the same reason: a value outside 1..64 means this is
	-- not the manager we think it is, and scanning 64 slots of something else would invent obstacles.
	function ow.aocc(goalk, hardonly)
		local gd = find_gd(); if not gd then return nil, false end
		local mgr = u32(gd + 0x1A8); if not inram(mgr) then return nil, false end
		local arr = u32(mgr + 0x18); if not inram(arr) then return nil, false end
		local cap = u16(mgr + 0x04); if cap < 1 or cap > 64 then return nil, false end
		if not inram(arr + cap * 0x100 - 1) then return nil, false end  -- the whole declared array must be there
		local me = ow.pactor()
		-- ⛔⛔ THE ENGINE'S Y TEST IS AGAINST THE DESTINATION TILE, NOT AGAINST WHERE THE PLAYER IS NOW.
		-- CheckActorNewPosOtherActorCollision is called as (actor, nextX, RESOLVED_Y, nextZ) — the Y of the
		-- tile being stepped ONTO. This used to compare each actor's Y with the player's CURRENT Y, and in
		-- Chargestone B1F that is a different number for half the room: Ola stood at Y 0 while two of the
		-- five stones, four Team Plasma trainers, a doctor and an item ball all stand on Y-1 tiles, so every
		-- one of them was silently exempted (Ola, 2026-08-24: "reachability is weird here").
		-- The correct comparison is trivially true, and that is the point: an actor stands at exactly the
		-- elevation of the tile it occupies — MEASURED, 18 of 18 actors in his own dump, against the height
		-- decoded from the land data (research/re/z196_check.py). So "actor Y == destination tile Y" holds
		-- whenever the destination IS the actor's tile, which is the only tile it can block. Comparing
		-- against anything else can only ever wrongly EXEMPT.
		-- ⚠ The one room where an actor is NOT at its tile's elevation is the Driftveil shaft, four storeys
		-- on one footprint — and that room does not come through here at all; ow.dv.blocked has its own copy
		-- with its own storey test, for exactly this reason.
		local px, pz = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
		local occ, n = {}, 0
		for i = 0, cap - 1 do
			local o = arr + i * 0x100
			local st = u32(o)
			if st % 2 == 1 and math.floor(st / 0x80) % 2 == 0 and o ~= me then
				local x, z = ow.s16(o + 0x3C), ow.s16(o + 0x40)
				if not (x == px and z == pz) then
					local model = u16(o + 0x0C)
					-- ⛔ "NOT IN A HARD TABLE" IS NOT EVIDENCE THAT SOMETHING WILL STEP ASIDE. Dropping every
					-- unclassified actor on the retry is how an unlisted marker walks the player into it —
					-- the same shape as the model-0 bug (codex-review 2026-08-24). So softness now needs a
					-- POSITIVE signal as well: a script id. Every talkable body in Ola's zone-195 dump has
					-- one (2000, 3104, 3117, 3194, 3240, 5, 6, 7120-7128); all twelve of the cave's stones
					-- have script 0, as does the player's own avatar. An actor nothing can talk to is not
					-- someone who will move out of your way, so it stays hard.
					local soft = u16(o + 0x14) ~= 0 and not (PROP_MODELS[model] or ITEM_MODELS[model]
						or HM_MODELS[model] or OBJ_MODELS[model])
					if not (hardonly and soft) then
						local w, h = u8(o + 0x7C), u8(o + 0x7D)
						if w < 1 then w = 1 end
						if h < 1 then h = 1 end
						for ax = x, x + w - 1 do
							for az = z - h + 1, z do
								local k = ax * 100000 + az
								if k ~= goalk and not occ[k] then occ[k] = true; n = n + 1 end
							end
						end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		return (n > 0) and occ or nil, true
	end
	local function pf_occupied(goalk, hardonly)
		-- ⛔⛔ IN THE DRIFTVEIL SHAFT, A* AND THE LIFT PLANNER MUST BLOCK ON EXACTLY THE SAME THINGS. They
		-- were not: the planner floods with the engine's own rule (any active actor on YOUR STOREY, bit 0x80
		-- exempt), while this built occupancy out of the entity lists — which since the storey filter was
		-- dropped carry every actor on every floor. A Y50 trainer at (20,23) then walled off a tile that is
		-- open at Y40, so the planner would pick a lift that A* could not reach, and the route flipped about
		-- (Ola, 2026-08-24: "I went left earlier then it said to go back right"). One source of truth here.
		if ow.dv.here() then
			local B = ow.dv.blocked(goalk and math.floor(goalk / 100000) or -1, goalk and (goalk % 100000) or -1)
			local s = ow.dv.state()
			local m = s and B[ow.dv.floor(s.y)] or nil
			if not m then return nil, false end                         -- unreadable, not empty (see ow.aocc)
			local occ, n = {}, 0
			for k in pairs(m) do
				local kk = math.floor(k / 100) * 100000 + (k % 100)
				if kk ~= goalk then occ[kk] = true; n = n + 1 end
			end
			return (n > 0) and occ or nil, true
		end
		return ow.aocc(goalk, hardonly)
	end
	-- Where one press of `dir` from (x,z) actually LEAVES you: the resting tile, plus how many tiles were
	-- travelled to get there. nil = the press does not move you at all. (See ow.ice above for how Class
	-- 0x18 was established.)
	-- THE RULE: you keep going while the tile you are ON is ice and the tile AHEAD is enterable, so you
	-- stop either on the first NON-ice tile you reach or on the last ice tile before an obstacle. Off ice
	-- the `while` never runs and this is exactly the old one-tile step, so the other 424 zones are
	-- unaffected — which is why it lives in the common path rather than behind a zone check.
	-- ⛔ PEOPLE MAY NOT DECIDE WHERE A SLIDE ENDS. THINGS THAT NEVER MOVE MAY. That is one rule with two
	-- halves and BOTH are load-bearing — each half was learnt from a loop Ola got stuck in, five days
	-- apart, and fixing either one alone brings the other back.
	--   `occ`  gates the FIRST step only: you cannot walk into someone, but once the ice has you, where
	--          you come to rest may not depend on where a person happens to be standing (2026-08-20, the
	--          Cold Storage follower — the long paragraph below is the record of what that cost).
	--   `hard` gates EVERY step, the first included: boulders, statues, item balls, cut trees, gimmick
	--          collision markers. `ow.aocc(_, true)` is what separates the two, and it is the same hard
	--          set A*'s retry, Castelia's relaxed passes and Chargestone's stone flight already fall back
	--          to — "a person in a doorway steps aside; a boulder never does", applied to the carry.
	-- ⛔⛔ THE SECOND HALF WAS MISSING UNTIL 2026-08-25 AND IT WAS AN INFINITE LOOP. Ola, in Icirrus Gym:
	-- "stuck in a loop where it would say to go right, then recalculating and left, and repeat, this is on
	-- ice." His N-dump has the whole thing. He is at (47,43); the route reads
	-- `waypoints [1](54,43) >[2](47,43) [3](59,43)` — left to 47, then right to 59, straight back past
	-- where he came from. Three model-0 ice boulders sit at (55,43) (55,44) (56,43) on his storey. Going
	-- RIGHT from (54,43) the first step is (55,43), which `occ` blocked, so the planner detoured to 47 to
	-- get a run-up — but the carry from 47 then sailed THROUGH those same boulders to 59, because the
	-- carry ignored them. In the game he stopped at (54,43), one tile short of the boulder, exactly where
	-- he started. So: 47 -> right -> 54 -> "can't go right" -> left -> 47 -> right, for ever. The planner
	-- and the engine disagreed about one lane and neither could ever win.
	-- ⚠ AND NOTE WHAT MADE IT UNRESOLVABLE: this is not the ordinary "planned X, actually Y, re-plan and
	-- self-heal" that the paragraph below relies on. Both endpoints were STABLE, so the re-plan was
	-- deterministic and re-derived the same impossible pair every time. Self-healing only works when the
	-- disagreement is transient; against a fixed map feature it is a metronome.
	-- ⚠⚠ AND THAT IS A CHOICE AGAINST THE ENGINE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT — worth stating plainly, because on
	-- 2026-08-25 I "fixed" it and had to be talked out of it by a review. The engine really does stop a
	-- slide at an actor: the straight-ice handler 0x021A5558 re-runs PlayerMoveCollCheck 0x021A5284
	-- before issuing every next one-tile command, any nonzero mask takes the cleanup branch at
	-- 0x021A557C, and other-actor overlap is mask bit 0x04 via CheckActorNewPosOtherActorCollision
	-- 0x02163BCC, which never reads model ID (Codex trace, 2026-08-25). Modelling that faithfully makes
	-- the resting tile depend on where a person happens to be standing — and the paragraph below is the
	-- record of what that cost Ola the last time. The engine's answer is not automatically the right
	-- answer for an instruction someone has to follow without seeing. If this is ever revisited, it is
	-- HIS call, not a correctness argument, and the loop below is why.
	-- Ola, 2026-08-20: "it slides me back to the same spot I can't go ... it seems to think I'm going the
	-- wrong way when I am sliding past where it wants me to be." Root cause, and it reproduces offline in
	-- research/re/ice_pingpong.py against this very code: a slide was allowed to REST on the tile before a
	-- person. Let them step aside — and in Cold Storage one of them FOLLOWS YOU — and the ice carries him
	-- straight past the tile the route chose, so the follower calls him off route, re-plans, picks the same
	-- impossible tile, and sends him sliding back where he came from. The recorded trace is literally
	-- "planned (24,8) but actually (28,8)" then "planned (26,8) but actually (23,8)" — the loop he described.
	-- The deeper reason this is not merely a bug fix: an instruction whose endpoint depends on somebody
	-- standing still is not a safe instruction for someone who cannot see whether they moved. Keeping
	-- PEOPLE out of the carry makes the resting tile a FIXED property of the room, so the same lane always
	-- yields the same instruction and a route can never oscillate (route replay is exact — ice_pftest.py
	-- test B). ⚠ A boulder does not weaken that one bit: it is as fixed as a wall, so a lane containing
	-- one still yields the same answer every time — which is precisely why it has to be IN the model.
	-- If a person really is in the lane, the player simply stops early; the follower notices he came to rest
	-- between waypoints and re-plans from where he actually is. One "Recalculating", then a correct route —
	-- self-healing, instead of a loop that never resolves.
	-- The remaining per-hop tests are the two a single step makes (sealed fence edge, tile walkable), so a
	-- slide still can never carry you through something a walk could not.
	-- ⚠ The LEDGE-HOP branch in the caller deliberately does NOT slide on afterwards. A hop that landed on
	-- ice would carry on in the engine — but there is no such place: across all four zones that have
	-- slippery tiles there is not one ledge tile, and none within two tiles of ice (checked in the land
	-- data, not assumed). Writing that path would be writing code no map can reach and no test can cover.
	-- The one thing that IS adjacent to ice is a directional exit-block (zone 192 has a single class-0x51
	-- tile touching the ice), which is exactly why reach.edge_ok is re-checked on every hop below and not
	-- just on the first step.
	-- ⛔ A SLIDE HAS NO INTERMEDIATE STOPPING PLACES. That is the whole point, and it is why only the
	-- RESTING tile is returned: offering the tiles passed over would hand Ola directions he cannot
	-- physically follow. Validated offline against the real map of Ola's room (research/re/ice_check.py):
	-- from the door of zone 192, all 493 non-ice walkable tiles stay reachable and NOT ONE is lost, while
	-- 134 of the 170 ice tiles correctly stop being valid destinations because you cannot halt on them.
	-- ⛔⛔ IT MUST ASK THE HEIGHT QUESTION THE CALLER IS ASKING. A* carries a height per node and picks the
	-- candidate layer with it — but every ordinary step still comes through here, and this used to run
	-- ow.hstep, which compares the two tiles' own LAYER 0 heights with no y at all. On a two-layer map that
	-- is a different question, and it answers it wrongly: measured on Dragonspiral 2F, that veto cuts 72 of
	-- the floor's 387 reachable tiles and severs the route to the up-stairs outright. Ola, standing at
	-- (12,12) with a Strength boulder to his left: "it wants me to go left, but there is a strength boulder
	-- in the way" — A* could not find the legitimate way round, fell back to its retry-without-occupancy,
	-- and routed him through the rock, oscillating one down / one up forever.
	-- `y` is the height the caller is standing at; without it this behaves exactly as it always did.
	function ow.slide(cs, x, z, dir, dx, dz, occ, hard, y)
		local here = pf_tile(cs, x, z, y)
		local nx, nz = x + dx, z + dz
		local tt = pf_tile(cs, nx, nz, y)
		if not reach.edge_ok(here, tt, dir) then return nil end
		if not pf_walk(tt) or (occ and occ[nx * 100000 + nz]) then return nil end
		if hard and hard[nx * 100000 + nz] then return nil end          -- a boulder is a wall you can talk to
		local hy = y
		if not ow.hstep(cs, x, z, nx, nz, hy) then return nil end       -- a step up onto a platform: refused
		local n = 1
		while ow.ice(tt) do                                             -- standing on ice carries you on
			local ax, az = nx + dx, nz + dz
			hy = ow.height(cs, nx, nz, hy) or hy                        -- carry the height along the slide
			local at = pf_tile(cs, ax, az, hy)
			if not reach.edge_ok(tt, at, dir) then break end
			if not pf_walk(at) then break end
			if hard and hard[ax * 100000 + az] then break end            -- ⛔ the half that was missing
			if not ow.hstep(cs, nx, nz, ax, az, hy) then break end       -- the slide stops at a height change
			nx, nz, tt, n = ax, az, at, n + 1
		end
		return nx, nz, n
	end
	local function pf_astar(cs, sx, sz, gx, gz, occ, hard)
		local heap, hn = {}, 0
		local function push(fv, x, z, y) hn = hn+1; heap[hn] = { f=fv, x=x, z=z, y=y }; local i = hn
			while i > 1 do local pa = math.floor(i/2); if heap[pa].f <= heap[i].f then break end heap[pa],heap[i] = heap[i],heap[pa]; i = pa end end
		local function pop() local top = heap[1]; heap[1] = heap[hn]; heap[hn] = nil; hn = hn-1; local i = 1
			while true do local l,r,s = i*2, i*2+1, i
				if l <= hn and heap[l].f < heap[s].f then s = l end
				if r <= hn and heap[r].f < heap[s].f then s = r end
				if s == i then break end heap[s],heap[i] = heap[i],heap[s]; i = s end
			return top end
		local function h(x, z) return math.abs(x-gx) + math.abs(z-gz) end
		-- ⛔⛔ WATER IS NOT FLOOR, AND A* WAS THE LAST SEARCH THAT STILL THOUGHT IT WAS. A water tile
		-- has Flags bit 0 CLEAR — nothing about it is "solid" — so pf_walk has always called every lake,
		-- river and rapid in the game walkable, and A* would happily plan a walk straight into one. The
		-- nav list has known better since the HM-gate work (reach.for_ counts Flags bit 1 as a Surf gate
		-- and labels what lies beyond it "Surf"); the router never got that memo, so the two searches
		-- disagreed about roughly sixteen thousand tiles.
		-- Found in Dragonspiral Tower: the floor you enter is a corridor with seven rows of lake across
		-- the middle of it (zone 206, Class 0x3D, Flags 0x0056), and the only way north is over the water.
		-- ⚠ SAME EXCEPTION THE FLOOD USES: if the player is ALREADY on a surfable tile they are surfing,
		-- and then water is exactly the ground they move on. Stepping back out onto land stays allowed —
		-- dismounting is a thing the game does for you.
		-- ⭐⭐ EVERY NODE CARRIES THE HEIGHT IT STANDS AT, because on a two-layer map the tile you get
		-- depends on it (ow.cand). Standing on Dragonspiral 2F's fallen column at +6, the square in front
		-- of you is the column if you are up there and the ground if you are not — and the old search,
		-- which asked for a square's one and only height, saw the ground, measured a six-tile drop and
		-- refused to walk the column at all. Seven tiles of that floor are reachable only this way, one
		-- of them the item the walkthrough tells you not to miss (research/re/ds_layer_reach.py).
		-- The state is therefore (x, z, height), not (x, z) — the same square at two heights is two
		-- different places. On every one-layer map ow.cand returns layer 0 whatever the height is, so the
		-- second component never splits anything and the search is exactly what it was.
		local function nkey(x, z, y)                                    -- node key: tile + which height
			local k = x * 100000 + z
			return y and (k * 4096 + (y >> 16) % 4096) or (k * 4096)
		end
		-- The height the player is REALLY at, from the live position (mappos+0x08 is the y of the same
		-- VecFx32 whose x and z integer halves are A.map_x / A.map_y). It decides which layer the first
		-- step is measured from; a wild value means "do not know", and then layer 0 answers as before.
		local sy = ow.seedy(cs, sx, sz)                                 -- tile-centre, not the live mid-step y
		local skey = nkey(sx, sz, sy)
		local g = { [skey] = 0 }; local came = {}
		local bestk, besth = skey, h(sx, sz)                            -- closest reachable tile to the goal so far
		push(h(sx,sz), sx, sz, sy)
		-- ⛔ HOW THE SEARCH ENDED IS DIAGNOSTIC GOLD AND WE WERE THROWING IT AWAY. `reached` only says
		-- whether the goal was hit; it does not say whether the answer is a real path, a best-effort
		-- to the nearest tile we could get to, or a search that was CUT SHORT by one of the two caps.
		-- Those three produce completely different failures, and a route that flips between two
		-- neighbouring tiles is exactly what a cap-truncated best-effort looks like from the outside.
		-- Ola, 2026-08-26: "it says one up, then one down and repeat."
		local n, reached, stale, term = 0, false, 0, "exhausted"
		while hn > 0 and n < 15000 do
			local cur = pop(); n = n + 1
			local ck = nkey(cur.x, cur.z, cur.y); local ch = h(cur.x, cur.z)
			if ch < besth then besth, bestk, stale = ch, ck, 0 else stale = stale + 1 end
			if stale > 3000 then term = "stale"; break end              -- closest-reachable found; stop the off-map sprawl
			if cur.x == gx and cur.z == gz then reached = true; term = "goal"; bestk = ck; break end
			local cg = g[ck]
			-- ⛔ A PIT'S CENTRE IS A LEAF. Step onto it and the engine takes you a floor down, so nothing
			-- leads anywhere FROM it and a route that passed through one would carry on issuing directions
			-- for a floor Ola is no longer on. Reaching it is still fine — it is exactly what a route TO a
			-- pit is for, and the goal test above has already fired by the time we get here.
			-- Hoisted out of the direction loop: both describe the tile we are STANDING on, so they were
			-- being read four times per node for one answer (A* pops up to 15000 of them).
			local cy0 = cur.y
			local here0 = pf_tile(cs, cur.x, cur.z, cy0)
			local hhere = ow.height(cs, cur.x, cur.z, cy0)               -- nil = this tile has no authored height
			for _, d in ipairs(ow.sink(cs, cur.x, cur.z) and ow.NODIRS or PF_DIRS) do
				local dir, dx, dz = d[1], d[2], d[3]
				local nx, nz = cur.x + dx, cur.z + dz
				local cy = cy0
				local raw = pf_tile(cs, nx, nz, cy)                     -- before any of our own vetoes
				local tt = raw                                          -- the candidate chosen for THIS height
				local ny = ow.height(cs, nx, nz, cy)                    -- and that same candidate's height
				local ex, ez, span, sl                                  -- END tile, tiles moved, and "was a slide"
				local here = here0
				-- ⛔ ARE WE SURFING? ASK THE TILE WE ARE STANDING ON, EVERY STEP. The first version of this
				-- worked out "am I on water" ONCE from the search's start tile and kept it for the whole
				-- route, which is wrong twice over (Codex verify, 2026-08-26): a route that dismounts onto
				-- land would go on treating water as ground for the rest of its length, and the start probe
				-- read the tile with no height, so on a two-layer map it saw the WATER UNDER A WALKWAY and
				-- declared a walking player to be surfing — Pinwheel Forest (71,51) is exactly that, water in
				-- layer 0 and ordinary land in layer 1, and there are 56 such cells across six maps.
				-- Reading `here`, which is already chosen for this node's height, is both simpler and right:
				-- on water you are surfing, off it you are not.
				local onw = here and math.floor(here / 0x20000) % 2 == 1
				-- The engine's step gate, now measured from where the player IS rather than between two
				-- squares' own heights: ActorRouteCollCheckCore builds the target query with the actor's
				-- CURRENT y and refuses when the selected target height is 0x14000 or more away from it.
				-- Unknown on either side still means yes — this must never refuse a route on a guess.
				-- ⛔⛔ AN INHERITED HEIGHT IS NOT A KNOWN HEIGHT, AND MEASURING FROM ONE SEVERS ROOMS.
				-- When a tile has no authored height record, `ey` below falls back to the height we
				-- arrived with — which is right for picking a LAYER (ow.cand needs some y) and wrong for
				-- the engine's step rule, because it turns "we do not know where we are vertically" into
				-- a confident number and then refuses a step against it.
				-- ⭐ ZONE 227 (Victory Road 4F) is the map that showed it. Its corridor is three tiles
				-- wide (x 10..12) and the WHOLE ROW at z=36 has no height record — all three tiles. From
				-- Ola's own dump, live: z=37 reads 0 and z=35 reads 98154, a 1.50-tile difference. Cross
				-- that row from the south and you carry 0 into a 1.50-tile climb; cross it from the north
				-- and you carry 98154 into a 1.50-tile drop. Both are >= 0x14000, so the room was cut in
				-- half IN BOTH DIRECTIONS -- which is exactly what he saw: "it just said this is as close
				-- I can get you". Standing ON the row worked, because then the start height comes from
				-- the LIVE player position (32619) which is within range of both sides -- and that is why
				-- pressing again from a different tile "continued it correctly".
				-- ⭐ AND THE TWO SEARCHES ALREADY DISAGREED: reach.for_ measures with ow.hstep, which
				-- fails open when EITHER tile's height is unknown, so the nav list happily called the
				-- goal reachable while the router refused to plan to it. This file has paid for that
				-- shape of disagreement before; A* is the one that was wrong.
				-- So: unknown stays unknown. If the tile we are STANDING on has no height of its own,
				-- the carried value is inherited and must not be used to refuse anything.
				local climb = (ny == nil or cy == nil or hhere == nil)
				              or (ny - cy < 0x14000 and cy - ny < 0x14000)
				if not reach.edge_ok(here, tt, dir) or not climb then
					tt = nil
				end
				-- ⚠ ONLY REFUSE WATER YOU COULD STAND ON. The shore classes 0x41/0x44 carry the surfable bit
				-- too, and they are BLOCKED — nilling them here hid the lip from the shore-hop branch below,
				-- which is the one move that gets you off the water at all.
				if tt and pf_walk(tt) and not onw and math.floor(tt / 0x20000) % 2 == 1 then tt = nil end
				if pf_walk(tt) and not (occ and occ[nx*100000 + nz]) then
					-- A normal step onto an adjacent walkable tile — EXCEPT that if that tile is ice the
					-- press does not end there, so ask where it actually leaves us. Off ice ow.slide
					-- returns exactly (nx, nz, 1) and this is the step it always was.
					ex, ez, span = ow.slide(cs, cur.x, cur.z, dir, dx, dz, occ, hard, cy)
					sl = (span ~= nil and span > 1)                      -- carried >1 tile ⇒ the ice took over
				elseif onw and raw and not pf_walk(raw) and reach.SURF_EDGE[raw % 0x10000] then
					-- ⛔⛔ THE SHORE HOP IS ONE-WAY: OFF THE WATER ONLY. Getting ON is an INTERACTION, not a
					-- step — the engine's own entry needs a party Pokémon that knows Surf, which our list
					-- deliberately does not test (we gate the LIST on owning HM03, Ola's call). Modelling
					-- entry as an ordinary step would hand him a route the game then refuses, and the Surf
					-- spot entry already walks him to the tile he presses from. Leaving costs nothing.
					-- Water-to-water over a lip is refused too: neither the entry nor the exit event is
					-- eligible for it, and the ROM has zero authored water-edge-water triples anyway
					-- (Codex review 2026-08-26).
					local sx2, sz2 = cur.x + 2*dx, cur.z + 2*dz
					local st = pf_tile(cs, sx2, sz2, cy)
					-- ⚠ BOTH crossed coordinates must be clear of actors, not just the landing: the engine's
					-- edge dismount checks the lip as well.
					if pf_walk(st) and math.floor(st / 0x20000) % 2 == 0
					   and not (occ and (occ[sx2*100000 + sz2] or occ[nx*100000 + nz])) then
						ex, ez, span = sx2, sz2, 2
						ny = ow.height(cs, sx2, sz2, cy)
					end
				elseif tt and not onw and PF_LEDGE_HOP[tt % 0x10000] == dir then  -- a hoppable ledge, in its own direction
					-- ⛔ AND NEVER WHILE SURFING. The engine's Surf decision function FUN_021A4BB8 has no
					-- ledge branch at all and its update dispatcher accepts only results 0-4, so result 5 —
					-- the jump — cannot happen on water (Codex verify 2026-08-26). Of the game's 467 stand /
					-- ledge / landing triples, 102 are water-to-water: every one of them was a hop this
					-- search would have taken and the game would have refused.
					-- ⛔ NO HEIGHT GATE ON A JUMP. A ledge exists in order to drop you, and the engine's
					-- jump path does not run the walk-time height check at all: FUN_021A60FC returns
					-- result 5 when the adjacent tile's ledge direction matches the press, and result 5
					-- dispatches through FUN_021A5FB8 to FUN_021A6358, which issues movement command 0x38
					-- via func_0216D430. ActorRouteCollCheckCore (FUN_021638EC) appears nowhere on that
					-- chain — its only call xref in the whole overlay is inside its own wrapper (Codex,
					-- 2026-08-26, report-ledge-jump.md). reach.for_ has always got this right; A* and
					-- reg_aim were gating a jump on a rule that governs walking, so the three searches
					-- disagreed with each other and two of them disagreed with the game.
					-- ⚠ AND IT CHANGES NOTHING TODAY — measured, not assumed: **0 of the game's 467
					-- authored ledge jumps** have a stand-to-landing drop of 1.25 tile-heights or more
					-- (research/re/ledge_hstep_impact.py). So this is not the cause of anything Ola has
					-- reported; it is a wrong branch that happens to be unreachable, which this project has
					-- already been bitten by once. Fixed because the next map is not obliged to be so kind.
					local lx, lz = cur.x + 2*dx, cur.z + 2*dz           -- landing = 2 tiles over the (blocked) ledge tile
					local lt = pf_tile(cs, lx, lz, cy)
					if lt and math.floor(lt / 0x20000) % 2 == 1 then lt = nil end   -- and never INTO water either
					if pf_walk(lt) and not (occ and occ[lx*100000 + lz])
					   then ex, ez, span = lx, lz, 2; ny = ow.height(cs, lx, lz, cy) end
				end
				if ex then
					-- A slide or a hop can END somewhere other than the square we measured, so take the
					-- height of where we actually land — and fall back to the height we came in at, which
					-- is what an unknown has always meant here.
					local ey = (ex == nx and ez == nz) and ny or ow.height(cs, ex, ez, cy)
					if ey == nil then ey = cy end
					local nk = nkey(ex, ez, ey)
					-- Sand costs extra (ow.PF_SAND, see ow.PIT): crossable, but gone round wherever a
					-- shorter way exists. pf_tile is memoized, so this is a table lookup, not a read.
					local ng = cg + span + (ow.sandy(pf_tile(cs, ex, ez, cy)) and ow.PF_SAND or 0)
					if not g[nk] or ng < g[nk] then g[nk] = ng; came[nk] = { dir = dir, span = span, sl = sl, pk = ck }; push(ng + h(ex,ez), ex, ez, ey) end
				end
			end
		end
		if n >= 15000 and term == "exhausted" then term = "cap" end
		if bestk == skey then return nil, false, term end               -- can't get any closer to the goal from here
		local moves, k = {}, bestk
		while came[k] do local c = came[k]; moves[#moves+1] = { dir = c.dir, span = c.span, sl = c.sl }; k = c.pk end
		local rev = {}; for i = #moves, 1, -1 do rev[#rev+1] = moves[i] end
		return rev, reached, term
	end
	-- ── CASTELIA GYM ROUTER (Ola's design 2026-08-18, verbatim: "just path as normal but path to the
	-- switches and all that silently so we get a path finder that solvs the puzzle without acting any
	-- different. So if I path find to burk it would take me through all the steps.")
	-- So there is no new key, no new prompt and no "blocked" message: routing to Burgh simply produces one
	-- ordinary route that happens to cross every switch it needs, in an order that works.
	-- The search is a GREEDY press-nearest-reachable-switch loop, not a (tile x switch-state) search: a
	-- switch only ever latches ON, so the state is monotone and at most 8 legs can ever be needed. Proven
	-- offline against the real collision grid — from the door it solves in 8 legs and never stalls (doc 22).
	-- Each leg is an ordinary pf_astar call; the legs concatenate into one move list, so everything
	-- downstream (waypoints, turn text, the follower) is unchanged.
	function ow.gym.plan(cs, sx, sz, gx, gz, occ, goalk, avoid)
		local function walls(extra)                                     -- occupancy = people + shut walls (+ ambushes)
			local sh = ow.gym.shut(extra); if not sh then return nil end    -- unreadable -> caller fails closed
			local t = {}
			if occ then for k, v in pairs(occ) do t[k] = v end end
			-- ⚠ codex-review 2026-08-18: clear the goal from the TRANSIENT occupancy only, and add the
			-- structural blockers afterwards. Clearing last would have un-blocked a shut wall whenever the
			-- wall itself was the goal — you cannot stand inside solid honey, so that must stay blocked and
			-- the route must simply fail. (An ambush GOAL is still reachable: the avoid pass blocks it, the
			-- later pass does not.)
			t[goalk] = nil
			for k in pairs(sh) do t[k] = true end
			if avoid then for _, a in ipairs(ow.gym.ambush) do t[a[1]*100000 + a[2]] = true end end
			return t
		end
		-- (No `hard` is threaded into these pf_astar calls: the game has exactly FOUR zones with slippery
		-- tiles — Cold Storage, its back room, the Route 10 gate and Icirrus — and this gym is not one, so
		-- ow.slide's carry loop never runs a single iteration here. `block` already gates every step.)
		local extra, pressed, legs = {}, {}, {}
		local cx, cz = sx, sz
		for _ = 0, #ow.gym.switches do                                     -- <= 8 presses, then one final goal leg
			local block = walls(extra); if not block then return nil end
			local m, r = pf_astar(cs, cx, cz, gx, gz, block)
			if r then                                                   -- goal reachable with what we will have opened
				legs[#legs+1] = m
				local moves = {}
				for _, leg in ipairs(legs) do for _, mv in ipairs(leg) do moves[#moves+1] = mv end end
				return moves, true
			end
			local best, bestm, bestlen                                  -- else: nearest still-unpressed switch
			for _, s in ipairs(ow.gym.switches) do
				if not pressed[s[3]] and ow.gym.var(s[3]) == 0 then
					local sm, sr = pf_astar(cs, cx, cz, s[1], s[2], block)
					if sr and sm and (not bestlen or #sm < bestlen) then best, bestm, bestlen = s, sm, #sm end
				end
			end
			if not best then return nil end                             -- nothing left to press: genuinely no route
			legs[#legs+1] = bestm
			pressed[best[3]] = true; extra[best[3]] = true
			cx, cz = best[1], best[2]
		end
		return nil
	end
	-- ⚠ `reg` is the doorway region, when the goal is one. It exists so the Strength planner aims at the
	-- door's OWN accepted approach tiles rather than "the target or anything beside it" — otherwise the
	-- precheck in pf_start could validate one side and the route be planned to another (codex-review
	-- verification round 3).
	local function pf_build(cs, sx, sz, gx, gz, nm, warp, gy, rgn)      -- route = waypoint list + per-corner turn text
		-- ⛔ NIMBASA GYM: WALK TO THE NEXT LEG, NOT TO THE GOAL. In zone 63 the goal is usually on another
		-- platform, and there is no tile path to another platform at all — you ride. So retarget to the
		-- next thing to walk to (a bay to board at, or a path switch to step on) and record it on the
		-- route; the goal itself is still what `pf` is named after and still what every re-plan aims at,
		-- so ow.replan() re-derives the NEXT leg with no extra plumbing. Everything downstream — A*,
		-- waypoints, turn text, the follower — is untouched.
		local rc, why
		if ow.rc.here() then rc, why = ow.rc.leg(sx, sz, gx, gz) end
		-- ⛔ DRIFTVEIL GYM: THE SAME IDEA, ONE STOREY UP. Its goals are usually on another floor, and there
		-- is no tile path between floors at all — you ride. `gy` is the goal's own storey, carried on the
		-- list entry because in this room a tile alone does not identify a place.
		local dvl
		if ow.dv.here() then dvl, why = ow.dv.leg(sx, sz, gx, gz, gy) end
		-- ⛔ CHARGESTONE CAVE: THE SAME IDEA AGAIN, and it is the reason the cave is crossable at all.
		-- Its floating stones stand in the chokepoints and are not in the tile grid, so a floor that looks
		-- open is cut into pieces until you shove one. ow.cs.leg searches the stone configurations and
		-- hands back the FIRST thing to do — the tile to stand on and the way to push — or refuses. A push
		-- is irreversible until you leave the floor, so it is only ever offered on a path that reaches the
		-- goal (see the module for why that matters).
		local csl, cssig
		if ow.cs.here() then
			local hard, hardok = pf_occupied(nil, true)                 -- no goal exemption: a stone's flight
			if not hardok then return nil end                           -- path does not care what we are aiming at
			csl, why = ow.cs.leg(function(x, z) return pf_tile(cs, x, z) end, pf_walk, hard, sx, sz, gx, gz)
			cssig = ow.cs.sig()
		end
		-- ⛔ MISTRALTON GYM: THE SAME IDEA A FOURTH TIME. Fourteen islands, no tile path between any two,
		-- and the only crossings are the cannons — so the goal is nearly always somewhere walking cannot
		-- describe. ow.mt.leg hands back the cannon to walk to and the direction to press, or refuses.
		-- Unlike the coaster and the stones there is no state to search: the fourteen edges never change.
		local mtl
		if ow.mt.here() then
			mtl, why = ow.mt.leg(function(x, z) return pf_tile(cs, x, z) end, pf_walk, reach.edge_ok,
			                     sx, sz, gx, gz)
		end
		-- ⛔ STRENGTH BOULDERS: THE SAME IDEA A SEVENTH TIME, and the first one that is not a single room.
		-- Fifteen zones hold 26 boulders, and each one sits beside a 2x2 hole it is meant to fill; until it
		-- does, the floor beyond the hole is simply not connected. ow.st.leg searches that one boulder's
		-- push sequence and hands back the tile to stand on plus the direction to press - or refuses, and
		-- then the route falls through to ordinary best-effort exactly as before.
		-- ⚠ It runs ONLY when plain walking cannot already reach the goal; the module's own first test is
		-- that flood, so a room you can simply walk across never hears from it.
		-- ⚠ HARD occupancy, like the stones and the ice: a boulder does not step aside, so the "retry with
		-- no occupancy" fallback further down must never be what rescues a route through one.
		-- ⛔⛔ AND ITS REFUSAL IS NOT `why`. Every other planner here writes its reason into `why`, which
		-- `if why then return nil end` below turns into "Can't reach that from here" — right for them,
		-- because a gym room is fully loaded and "no ride reaches it" is the truth. It would be badly
		-- wrong here. These fifteen zones include ROUTES, ow.st.leg floods only LOADED tiles, and so its
		-- "no boulder opens the way" also fires for a goal that is merely not streamed in yet. Wiring it
		-- to `why` refused every ordinary far route in Route 2, 13, 15, 16, 18 and the Moor of Icirrus —
		-- exactly the best-effort walking that already worked. The reason is kept for the console only;
		-- a planner with nothing to say lets the route fall through untouched.
		local stl, stwhy
		if ow.st.here() then
			local shard, sok = pf_occupied(nil, true)
			if not sok then return nil end                              -- an unreadable room is not an empty one
			local sall = pf_occupied(nil, false)                         -- what stops a ROCK: every active actor
			-- ⛔ READ THE LAYER THE PLAYER IS ACTUALLY ON. pf_tile without a height answers from layer 0, and
			-- FIVE of the fifteen Strength zones use two-layer chunks — 152, 208, 370, 378 and 387 — where
			-- the two layers disagree about walkability on 7 to 387 tiles each. A height-blind flood there
			-- can call a stand tile reachable through the wrong storey (codex-review 2026-09-02, HIGH).
			-- ⚠ MEASURED WHERE IT MATTERS: at the boulder and hole tiles themselves the two layers are
			-- IDENTICAL in all five zones (0 of 64 differ), so the push rules were never at risk — it is the
			-- walk to the pushing tile that was. Same height expression pf_astar seeds itself with.
			local ssy = ow.s32(A.map_x + 0x02)
			if not ssy or ssy > 0x400000 or ssy < -0x400000 then ssy = nil end
			if ssy == nil then ssy = ow.height(cs, sx, sz) end
			local sgs                                                    -- the door's accepted tiles, if this is a door
			if rgn and rgn[5] then
				sgs = {}
				for _, t in ipairs(ow.reg_tiles(rgn)) do
					if pf_walk(pf_tile(cs, t[1], t[2], ssy)) then sgs[#sgs + 1] = t[1] * 100000 + t[2] end
				end
				if #sgs == 0 then sgs = nil end                          -- none walkable: fall back to the aim
			end
			stl, stwhy = ow.st.leg(cs, function(x, z) return pf_tile(cs, x, z, ssy) end, pf_walk, shard,
			                       sx, sz, gx, gz, sall, sgs)
			if stwhy then dbg("strength: " .. stwhy) end
		end
		-- ⛔ ICIRRUS GYM: THE SAME IDEA A FIFTH TIME, and the most involved of them. Three switches flip
		-- three saved booleans; eight tiles are each live only in one of those states and hand you onto the
		-- rails; two more always do. On top of that the floor is ice, so "walk there" means "slide there",
		-- and the mechanisms' six arms move with the state and block like people. ow.ig.leg searches
		-- (resting tile x 3-bit state) and hands back the next tile to walk onto.
		-- ⚠ It needs the HARD occupancy set, the same one ow.cs takes: this room's blockers are mechanism
		-- arms and gym trainers, and neither steps aside, so the "retry with no occupancy" fallback further
		-- down must never be what rescues a route here.
		local igl, ighard
		if ow.ig.here() then
			local hardok
			ighard, hardok = pf_occupied(nil, true)                     -- kept: the step-off branch needs it too
			if not hardok then return nil end                           -- an unreadable room is not an empty one
			igl, why = ow.ig.leg(cs, ighard, sx, sz, gx, gz)
		end
		-- ⛔ OPELUCID GYM: THE SAME IDEA A SIXTH TIME. Three dragon necks are the only joins between this
		-- room's three rail chains and four gallery arcs, and each dragon's switches sit behind another
		-- dragon's neck. ow.dg.leg searches (floor region x the three poses) and hands back the next
		-- SWITCH to walk to — and only that: when the goal is already reachable in the current poses it
		-- returns plain nil so the ordinary hybrid router does its normal handoff crossing, because that
		-- router already knows how to cross this zone and truncating its routes into short hops would be
		-- a regression, not a feature.
		local dgl
		-- ⛔⛔ AND IT MUST RUN FOR THE EXIT TOO — I GUESSED THAT IT NEED NOT, AND THE DATA SAYS NO.
		-- Ola could not leave the room, so I gated this off for warp goals on the reasoning that "the
		-- puzzle gates the walk to Iris, not the door you came in by". He asked the right question back:
		-- what if you need to press switches to GET OUT? Measured, over every (handoff, pose) pair:
		-- **868 of 1344 cannot reach the exit region (x 26..28, z 50) by riding alone.** Switches are
		-- required to leave in most configurations, so excluding warps would strand him far more often
		-- than it helped. The gate is gone; the ping-pong it was meant to paper over is a separate fault
		-- and is fixed where it lives (see ow.dg.cur, the module-held leg identity).
		if ow.dg.here() then
			dgl, why = ow.dg.leg(cs, function(x, z, y) return pf_tile(cs, x, z, y) end, pf_walk,
			                     reach.edge_ok, sx, sz, gx, gz, gy)   -- gy: the goal's own storey
			-- ⛔⛔ AND THE LEG'S IDENTITY BELONGS TO THE MODULE, NOT TO A ROUTE OBJECT.
			-- This is the structural fault behind "1 left, 1 right, then it stops", and it is the one
			-- thing that makes this gym different from every other gym planner in the file: theirs plan
			-- entirely in GRID space, while a dragon leg alternates grid → rail → grid → rail. ow.dg
			-- CANNOT RUN ON THE RAILS at all — it floods live collision, and ow.grid() is false there —
			-- so once the player boards, the only record of what the ride was FOR is `pf.dg`.
			-- Every rebuild that starts from the nav entry (pf_start's rail branch, a re-plan after an
			-- arrival) drops it. The rail half then falls back to "get off at the nearest way off", which
			-- is the handoff we just boarded from; the grid half immediately says "board here again"; and
			-- the two trade one-tile instructions for ever. Threading `dg` through one more caller would
			-- have been a fourth patch on the same crack.
			-- So keep it where it cannot be dropped. pf_build only ever runs on the GRID, so this is
			-- refreshed at every point a decision is genuinely made, and it survives untouched for the
			-- whole time the player is riding — which is exactly the window that was losing it.
			ow.dg.cur = (dgl and dgl.rail) and dgl or nil
		else
			ow.dg.cur = nil                                             -- never leak across zones
		end
		if why then return nil end                                      -- another platform/storey with no ride that
		-- ⛔ EVERY HYBRID ZONE: A RAIL GOAL IS NOT WALKED TO, IT IS BOARDED — and you may only board on a
		-- class-0xA3 handoff tile. Same idea as the three legs above: walk to the next thing you can
		-- actually walk to. The caller's gx,gz stays the goal, so the route still ends "as close as I can
		-- get", which is what makes the crossing block below fire and hand us to the rail follower.
		-- Without this the grid leg aimed at the goal LINE, which on a staircase is up in the air —
		-- see ow.hand for the measurements. Nil in the 418 non-hybrid zones and for non-rail goals.
		-- ⛔ NEVER `local a, b = guard and f(...)`. An and/or expression is adjusted to ONE value, so the
		-- second return is silently dropped — here that made hnd.z nil and crashed pf_build on
		-- `gx * 100000 + gz` the first time Ola asked for 3F. The offline harness could not see it: it
		-- calls ow.hand.pick directly, so it tested the module and not the wiring around it.
		local hnd
		if warp then
			local rcx = rail.sys()
			if rcx then
				local hx, hz = ow.hand.pick(rcx, warp, sx, sz)
				if hx then hnd = { x = hx, z = hz, rc = rcx } end
			end
			-- ⛔ AND IF WE COULD NOT FIND ONE, REFUSE — never fall through to the caller's aim.
			-- The caller's gx,gz is still pf_start's rail.line_near projection, which is the very rule this
			-- change exists to replace: on a staircase it points at mid-air over the room below. Letting a
			-- transient unreadable frame drop us back onto it would hand out confident directions to the
			-- wrong floor, which is worse than saying no (codex-review 2026-08-25, HIGH). Every hybrid zone
			-- is baked, so in one of them a rail warp ALWAYS has a handoff; anywhere else this is moot.
			if not hnd and ow.HANDOFF[reg.zid()] then return nil end
		end
		local fgx, fgz = gx, gz                                         -- reaches it: refuse outright. The caller says
		if rc then gx, gz = rc.x, rc.z                                  -- so, which beats walking to the edge of a gap.
		elseif dvl then gx, gz = dvl.x, dvl.z
		elseif csl then gx, gz = csl.x, csl.z
		elseif mtl then gx, gz = mtl.x, mtl.z
		elseif stl then gx, gz = stl.x, stl.z
		elseif igl then gx, gz = igl.x, igl.z
		elseif dgl then gx, gz = dgl.x, dgl.z
		elseif hnd then gx, gz = hnd.x, hnd.z end
		-- ⛔ ALREADY STANDING ON THE HANDOFF is a route of length zero, not a failure — the same trap the
		-- lift and the stones document below, and here it fires every time a route is re-planned at the
		-- moment of arrival. A* from a tile to itself returns nil, and the answer would become "Can't reach
		-- the 3rd floor from here" while standing at the foot of its stairs. Say the press instead.
		if hnd and sx == gx and sz == gz then
			local dir = ow.hand.cross(hnd.rc, warp, cs, sx, sz)
			if dir then
				return { tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } }, segAt = { "1 " .. dir }, pi = 1,
				         gx = fgx, gz = fgz, gy = gy, total = 0, name = nm, warp = warp,
				         reached = true, zone = reg.zid() }
			end
			return nil                                                  -- no press gets us on: let the caller say so
		end
		-- Standing on the push tile already: a zero-length route whose one spoken line is the action, the
		-- same shape the lift uses. Saying it is not a hint — it is "you have arrived", and it is what
		-- tells Ola the walking is over and the next move is his.
		if csl and sx == gx and sz == gz then
			return { tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } }, segAt = { "Push " .. csl.dir }, pi = 1,
			         gx = fgx, gz = fgz, gy = gy, total = 0, name = nm, warp = warp, cs = csl, csk = cssig,
			         reached = true, zone = reg.zid() }
		end
		-- ⛔ AND IN MISTRALTON IT IS NOT A CORNER CASE AT ALL — it is the common one. Cannon 6's right
		-- launch sets the player down on (26,36), which IS cannon 7's boarding tile, so the re-plan that
		-- follows that landing starts standing on its own leg tile. A* asked to walk from a tile to itself
		-- returns nil, so without this the answer would be "Can't reach Skyla from here" at exactly the
		-- moment it is most wrong. Say the press instead: that is not a hint, it is "you have arrived and
		-- the next move is yours", the same shape the lift and the stones already use.
		if mtl and sx == gx and sz == gz then
			return { tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } }, segAt = { "Cannon " .. mtl.dir }, pi = 1,
			         gx = fgx, gz = fgz, gy = gy, total = 0, name = nm, warp = warp, mt = mtl,
			         reached = true, zone = reg.zid() }
		end
		-- ⭐ STANDING ON THE PUSH TILE: say the move name until Strength is on, then the press itself -
		-- and the press is spoken as an ORDINARY STEP, because that is exactly what it is. A push moves
		-- the boulder one tile and leaves the player where they are, so "1 left" is both the instruction
		-- and the truth. Ola asked for precisely this ("only say the name, like strength" / "don't even
		-- say push, just the exact normal wording"), and the engine happens to agree with him.
		-- ⚠ THIS IS ALSO THE WAITING. While the flag is clear the leg keeps answering "stand here", so
		-- this line repeats the move name and nothing advances; the frame he uses Strength, the same
		-- re-plan starts giving directions. No timer, no state of our own - it just reads the flag.
		if stl and sx == gx and sz == gz then
			return { tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } },
			         segAt = { stl.need and "Strength" or ("1 " .. stl.dir) }, pi = 1,
			         gx = fgx, gz = fgz, gy = gy, total = 0, name = nm, warp = warp, st = stl,
			         stk = ow.st.sig() or "?", reached = true, zone = reg.zid() }
		end
		-- ⛔ AND IN ICIRRUS THE ANSWER IS THE OPPOSITE ONE, because every leg here is a COORDINATE EVENT.
		-- You cannot fire one by standing on it — it triggers when you ENTER the tile — so a plan that said
		-- "step on the switch you are already on" would wait, in silence, for a toggle that can never come.
		-- Nimbasa banked exactly this trap for its path switches. So step OFF first, as an ordinary route
		-- with no leg attached; arriving there re-plans, and the leg then has a real path back on.
		-- ⚠ AND "STEP OFF" IS NOT ONE TILE HERE. On ice a press carries you until you hit something, so the
		-- tile to aim at is wherever ow.slide says that press actually ENDS — aiming at the neighbour would
		-- hand A* a tile the player cannot stop on.
		-- ⛔ A CHAIN IS THE ONE ICIRRUS LEG YOU FIRE BY STANDING STILL. Every other leg here is a
		-- coordinate event that needs entering, so being on it means stepping off and back; a class-0xA3
		-- handoff is the opposite — you are meant to be standing on it and to press the blocked side. Say
		-- the press, in the same words the hybrid-zone crossings already use.
		-- ⛔ AND A RIDE IS NOW THE SAME SHAPE AS A CANNON. Since 2026-08-26 an Icirrus ride leg names the
		-- tile the PRESS STARTS FROM plus the direction to press, because a selector only fires when you
		-- enter it moving its own way. So standing on that tile is "you have arrived and the next move is
		-- yours", exactly as in Mistralton — not something to walk away from.
		-- ⛔ OPELUCID: STANDING ON THE HANDOFF IS THE MOMENT THE PRESS IS NEEDED, not an arrival. Same
		-- shape as the cannon above: a route of length zero whose whole content is the one instruction.
		-- Without it the player is walked onto the 0xA3 tile and left there, because the handover only
		-- happens on a blocked press and nothing else in the follower would ever ask for one.
		if dgl and dgl.rail and sx == gx and sz == gz then
			local into = ow.dg.stepin(cs, function(x, z, y) return pf_tile(cs, x, z, y) end, pf_walk,
			                          sx, sz, ow.dg.y0(cs, sx, sz), dgl.tx, dgl.tz)
			if not into then return nil end                             -- cannot name the press: say nothing
			return { tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } }, segAt = { "1 " .. into }, pi = 1,
			         gx = fgx, gz = fgz, gy = gy, total = 0, name = nm, warp = warp, dg = dgl,
			         reached = true, zone = reg.zid() }
		end
		if igl and igl.kind == "ride" and sx == gx and sz == gz and igl.dir then
			return { tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } }, segAt = { "1 " .. igl.dir }, pi = 1,
			         gx = fgx, gz = fgz, gy = gy, total = 0, name = nm, warp = warp, ig = igl,
			         reached = true, zone = reg.zid() }
		end
		if igl and igl.kind == "chain" and sx == gx and sz == gz then
			return { tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } }, segAt = { "1 " .. igl.dir }, pi = 1,
			         gx = fgx, gz = fgz, gy = gy, total = 0, name = nm, warp = warp, ig = igl,
			         reached = true, zone = reg.zid() }
		end
		if igl and sx == gx and sz == gz then
			-- ⛔⛔ AND THE STEP OFF MUST BE PROVED, NOT PICKED. The first version took the first press that
			-- moved at all, with no occupancy, no terminal test, and no check that the leg tile could be
			-- re-entered afterwards. A codex-review found the concrete case: switch (23,53) sits directly
			-- beside selector (22,53), which is LIVE when word0 is 1 — so "just step west off the switch"
			-- would walk into a ride and carry Ola across the room, on a spoken instruction whose whole
			-- purpose was to take one step sideways. Three conditions now, or we refuse:
			--   * the press lands somewhere that is not a TERMINAL (a live selector or a rail handoff);
			--   * it respects the same hard occupancy the planner used;
			--   * and from where it lands, the leg tile is genuinely ENTERABLE again — otherwise stepping
			--     off strands the leg we were trying to reach.
			local igs = ow.ig.state()
			local igt = igs and ow.ig.term(igs.w) or nil
			local want, pick = sx * 100000 + sz, nil
			if igs then
				for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
					local ex, ez = ow.slide(cs, sx, sz, d[1], d[2], d[3], ighard, ighard)
					if ex and not (ex == sx and ez == sz) and not igt[ex * 100000 + ez] then
						local _, back = ow.ig.flood(cs, ighard, igs.w, ex, ez, nil)
						if back[want] then pick = { ex, ez }; break end
					end
				end
			end
			if not pick then return nil end                             -- nowhere safe to step: say nothing
			gx, gz, igl = pick[1], pick[2], nil
		end
		-- ⛔ ALREADY STANDING AT THE LIFT IS A ROUTE OF LENGTH ZERO, NOT A FAILURE — the same trap the
		-- coaster documents below, and it fires constantly here because every ride sets you down on a
		-- platform. A* asked to walk from a tile to itself returns nil, pf_build would refuse, and the
		-- answer would be "Can't reach Clay from here" at exactly the moment it is wrong. Keep the goal
		-- alive instead: pressing A rides, and the re-entry re-plan picks it up on the far storey.
		if dvl and sx == gx and sz == gz then
			-- ⛔ AND IT MUST SAY WHERE YOU ARE. Standing on the lift with the route still named after Clay
			-- and nothing spoken is how the room went quiet on Ola mid-journey. Naming the lift is not a
			-- hint — it is the same "you have arrived at a place" the tool gives everywhere else — and it is
			-- what tells him the walking part is over and the next move is his.
			return { tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } }, segAt = { "Lift " .. dvl.i }, pi = 1,
			         gx = fgx, gz = fgz, gy = gy, total = 0, name = nm, warp = warp, dv = dvl,
			         reached = true, zone = reg.zid() }
		end
		-- ⛔ ALREADY STANDING ON THE LEG TILE IS A ROUTE OF LENGTH ZERO, NOT A FAILURE. A* asked to walk
		-- from a tile to itself returns nil — it never gets closer to the goal than where it starts — and
		-- pf_build would then refuse, so "board here" would answer "Can't reach Elesa from here" at exactly
		-- the moment it matters most. And this is not a corner case: EVERY ride sets the player down ON a
		-- bay, and beating the trainer sitting in a car leaves them standing on one. Hand back an empty
		-- route and let pf_update take over with "wait" / "get in".
		if rc and sx == gx and sz == gz then
			if rc.kind == "board" then
				return { tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } }, segAt = {}, pi = 1, gx = fgx, gz = fgz, total = 0,
				         name = nm, warp = warp, rc = rc, plat = ow.rc.at(sx, sz), reached = true,
				         zone = reg.zid() }
			end
			-- ⛔ A SWITCH, THOUGH, CANNOT BE PRESSED BY STANDING ON IT. It is a coordinate event: it fires
			-- when you ENTER the tile, so a plan that says "press the switch you are already on" would wait
			-- for a toggle that can never come, in silence. (Reachable: walk onto a switch you did not need
			-- — the state is now wrong — then ask for a route.) So step OFF first, as an ordinary one-tile
			-- route with no leg attached; arriving there re-plans, and the switch leg then has a real path
			-- back onto the tile, which fires it.
			for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
				local nx, nz = sx + d[2], sz + d[3]
				if pf_walk(pf_tile(cs, nx, nz)) then gx, gz, rc = nx, nz, nil; break end
			end
			if rc then return nil end                                   -- walled in on all four sides: no plan
		end
		local goalk = gx * 100000 + gz
		-- ⛔ AN UNREADABLE ROOM IS NOT AN EMPTY ROOM. Refuse rather than route through everything in it.
		local occ, occok = pf_occupied(goalk)                            -- people/items block; the goal itself does not
		if not occok then return nil end
		-- The things that never step aside, hoisted: A* needs them for the ICE CARRY on the first pass and
		-- not only on the retry. See ow.slide's header — the carry ignoring boulders is what put Ola in an
		-- unbreakable left/right loop in Icirrus. Unreadable is refused here, exactly as `occ` is: an
		-- unreadable room is not an empty room.
		local hard, hardok = pf_occupied(goalk, true)
		if not hardok then return nil end
		local moves, reached, aterm
		if ow.gym.here() then
			-- ⛔ A SHUT HONEY WALL IS NOT SOMEONE WHO WILL MOVE. The ordinary path below deliberately
			-- retries with no occupancy at all when it fails, on the theory that a person standing in a
			-- doorway will step aside. A wall never does, so the gym gets its own planner and never that
			-- fallback — otherwise every blocked goal would route straight through solid honey.
			-- ⚠ codex-review 2026-08-18, HIGH: and it must branch on being IN THE GYM, not on having read
			-- the state. gym.shut() returns nil both outside the gym and when a variable read fails, so
			-- keying on it sent an unreadable gym down the ordinary path — which is exactly the path that
			-- drops all occupancy and would then hand out confident directions through solid honey, at the
			-- one moment we know least. In here, unknown state means NO ROUTE. Fail closed.
			if not ow.gym.shut() then return nil end
			-- Four passes, dropping the least acceptable thing last. A forced trainer battle is worse than
			-- assuming a person will step aside, so ambush avoidance outranks occupancy (codex-review: the
			-- gym had lost the ordinary path's movable-occupant retry entirely, so one person standing in a
			-- neck could refuse a route the normal pathfinder would have given).
			-- (booleans, never `occ` itself, in this list: a nil first element would make ipairs stop dead.)
			-- The relaxed passes fall back to the HARD occupancy — items, cut trees, boulders, statues —
			-- rather than to nothing, so giving up on people never also walks you through a boulder.
			local passes = occ and { {true,true}, {false,true}, {true,false}, {false,false} }
			                    or { {false,true}, {false,false} }
			-- Hoisted, and its read checked: an unreadable actor manager here would have handed the gym's
			-- relaxed passes a nil occupancy, which is "route through everything" (codex-review 2026-08-24).
			local gymhard, gymok = pf_occupied(goalk, true)
			if not gymok then return nil end
			for _, p in ipairs(passes) do
				moves, reached = ow.gym.plan(cs, sx, sz, gx, gz,
					p[1] and occ or gymhard, goalk, p[2])
				if reached then break end
			end
			if not moves then return nil end
		else
			moves, reached, aterm = pf_astar(cs, sx, sz, gx, gz, occ, hard)  -- moves = {{dir,span}}; span 2 = ledge hop
			-- ⛔⛔ NOTHING IN THE DRIFTVEIL SHAFT STEPS ASIDE, so it must never take this retry. Dropping
			-- occupancy is a reasonable bet in an ordinary room — a person in a doorway will move — but in
			-- there the occupancy IS lift control posts, invisible collision markers and gym trainers, none
			-- of which ever move. Ola, 2026-08-24, on a lower storey: "it wants me to go left, where I can't
			-- go ... I went left earlier then it said to go back right." That is this line: A* failed
			-- honestly, retried with the room empty, handed out a path straight through a body, and then
			-- re-planned into a different one each time he moved. Castelia's gym opted out of the same retry
			-- for the same reason (a shut honey wall is not someone who will move); this is that rule again.
			-- Refusing is safe here: the lift planner floods with exactly the same blockers, so a leg it
			-- offers is always one A* can reach with occupancy on.
			-- ⛔ AND THE RETRY DROPS THE PEOPLE, NOT THE ROOM. It used to pass no occupancy at all, which
			-- is the same mistake in a third room: Chargestone Cave's gimmick parks an invisible actor in
			-- the ONE gap through the ledge wall north of (10,26), so A* correctly found no way past it,
			-- retried with the cave empty, and told Ola to walk left into it (his N-dump, 2026-08-24).
			-- A person in a doorway steps aside; an item ball, a cut tree, a boulder, a statue and a
			-- gimmick's collision marker never do. Castelia's gym ladder has always fallen back to the
			-- HARD set rather than to nothing for exactly this reason — this is that rule, in the ordinary
			-- path where it was missing. When there are no hard blockers the call is identical to the old
			-- one, so nothing that worked before changes.
			if not moves and occ and not ow.dv.here() then
				moves, reached, aterm = pf_astar(cs, sx, sz, gx, gz, hard, hard)
				aterm = "retry/" .. tostring(aterm)                     -- the two tiers answer differently: say which ran
			end
			if not moves then
				ow.PFGOAL = { gx, gz }                                  -- so the N-dump can replay this exact ask
				ow.PFLOG[#ow.PFLOG + 1] = string.format("(%d,%d)->(%d,%d) NO ROUTE term=%s",
					sx, sz, gx, gz, tostring(aterm))
				while #ow.PFLOG > 10 do table.remove(ow.PFLOG, 1) end
				return nil
			end
		end
		local tiles = { { x = sx, z = sz } }; local cx, cz = sx, sz     -- waypoints (a hop lands 2 tiles on; the blocked
		for _, m in ipairs(moves) do local dd = PF_DELTA[m.dir]         -- ledge tile is skipped, never a waypoint)
			cx = cx + dd[1]*m.span; cz = cz + dd[2]*m.span; tiles[#tiles+1] = { x = cx, z = cz } end
		-- Merge consecutive same-direction moves into one spoken instruction. The count is how far to hold
		-- the direction, in TILES — a ledge hop adds its 2, because holding down through a ledge hops it and
		-- you really do travel two.
		-- ⛔ EXCEPT ON ICE, WHERE HOLDING IS NOT A THING YOU CAN DO. Ola, 2026-08-20, looking at an "11 left"
		-- in Cold Storage: "for ice that doesn't work, since we slide after going on step, so ice is only 1
		-- step always when speaking, unless you do end up where you need to move twice in the same
		-- direction." He is right, and it follows from the engine: the slide LOCKS your direction and
		-- ignores input until it ends, so eleven tiles of ice is one press and there is no way to spend the
		-- other ten. A slide therefore contributes exactly 1 however far it carries you, and the merge still
		-- adds up — step onto ice and slide on is "2 left", which is exactly his "move twice in the same
		-- direction" case. (The rail pathfinder's own segAt has always counted presses; this brings the two
		-- into line rather than inventing a third convention.)
		local segAt, i = {}, 1
		while i <= #moves do local j, n = i, 0
			while j <= #moves and moves[j].dir == moves[i].dir do
				n = n + (moves[j].sl and 1 or moves[j].span); j = j + 1 end
			segAt[i] = n.." "..moves[i].dir; i = j end
		-- ⭐ A SHORT HISTORY OF WHAT WAS PLANNED, so an oscillation can be READ rather than reasoned about.
		-- Every route in this game is built here, so this is the one place that sees them all. Ten entries
		-- is enough to show a two-cycle several times over, and it costs one string per re-plan.
		ow.PFGOAL = { fgx, fgz }
		ow.PFLOG[#ow.PFLOG + 1] = string.format("(%d,%d)->(%d,%d) %s term=%s first=%s",
			sx, sz, fgx, fgz, reached and "REACHED" or "best-effort", tostring(aterm), segAt[1] or "-")
		while #ow.PFLOG > 10 do table.remove(ow.PFLOG, 1) end
		return { tiles = tiles, segAt = segAt, pi = 1, gx = fgx, gz = fgz, gy = gy, total = #moves, name = nm,
		         warp = warp, rc = rc, dv = dvl,                  -- carried so a goal keeps its identity across a
		         plat = ow.rc.here() and ow.rc.at(sx, sz) or nil, -- space change; zone: see the guard in pf_update
		         cs = csl, csk = cssig,                           -- Chargestone: the leg, and the stone layout
		         -- ⛔ EVERY route in a boulder zone carries the layout, not only one with a Strength leg.
		         -- If a transient unreadable frame ever produces a plain route here, a `stk`-less one would
		         -- stop watching the rocks for good and could never recover into the puzzle; carrying it
		         -- always makes that lapse self-healing — the next settled change re-plans into the leg
		         -- (codex-review 2026-09-02).
		         -- ⚠ "?" WHEN THE LAYOUT CANNOT BE READ YET. A route created while a rock is mid-push would
		         -- otherwise be born with no stk at all and never start watching (codex-review verification
		         -- 2026-09-02); the sentinel differs from every real signature, so the first settled read
		         -- re-plans exactly once and the route joins the puzzle properly.
		         mt = mtl, ig = igl, st = stl,
		         -- ⚠ THE SAME TILE VIEW pf_update will use, or the two fingerprints disagree in the handoff
		         -- window and the route re-plans against itself for no reason.
		         stk = ow.st.here()
		                and (ow.st.sig(function(x, z) return pf_tile(cs, x, z) end) or "?") or nil,
		         dg = dgl,                                        -- Opelucid: WHICH ride this walk is boarding for
		         reached = reached, zone = reg.zid() }        -- plat: the platform this leg was planned from
	end
	-- ── RAIL ROUTES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	-- Same product as the grid route (a waypoint list + one merged turn line per corner), but the
	-- waypoints are RAIL positions, because in Castelia and on the bridges there is no tile grid to walk.
	-- The spoken words are identical — "12 left" — because the engine's rail directions ARE buttons
	-- (RailLine+0x08 is the D-pad code that moves you forward along the line, never a compass bearing),
	-- so the vocabulary the pathfinder already uses is exactly the right one here.
	local PF_BTN = { [1] = "up", [2] = "right", [3] = "down", [4] = "left" }
	local function pf_build_rail(rc, e)
		local sl, sf, ss = rail.pos()
		if not sl then return nil, "pos" end                            -- mid-junction: no tile to start from
		local goals, gl, gf, gs, exitleg, allgoals
		local function gkey(l, f, sd) return ((l * 512) + f) * 512 + (sd + 128) end
		if e.warp then
			-- ⛔ AIM AT THE TILES A PRESS ACTUALLY TRIGGERS FROM, not at "everything touching the region".
			-- rail.warp_goals is adjacency in (front,side) space — a guess about the shape of an approach.
			-- ow.hand.trigger asks the engine's own rule what each press ATTEMPTS, so every tile in this
			-- set is one the warp really opens from (224 of them across the game; research/re/cel_trigger).
			-- ⛔ ONE CACHED SET, AND NO FALLBACK TO THE GUESS. Building it inline here rebuilt the whole
			-- area on every call, and pf_update's retry spells call ow.replan on EVERY FRAME for up to half
			-- a second — a per-frame full-area scan in all but name. And falling back to rail.warp_goals
			-- when nothing triggers would route him to a tile no press opens and then announce arrival:
			-- that approximation is exactly what the exact set replaced (codex-review 2026-08-25, HIGH x2).
			local tset, tpress = ow.hand.trigset(rc, e.warp)
			if not tset then return nil, "notrigger" end
			goals = {}
			for k in pairs(tset) do goals[k] = true end
			-- ⛔ ALREADY ON A TRIGGER IS NOT "THERE" — it is one press short, and naming that press is the
			-- whole point of the exact set. "You're there" here drops the route without ever saying it.
			if goals[gkey(sl, sf, ss)] then
				return { rail = true, rpos = { { l = sl, f = sf, s = ss } }, pi = 1, total = 0,
				         segAt = { "1 " .. tpress[gkey(sl, sf, ss)] }, name = e.label or e.name,
				         zone = reg.zid(), goal = { l = sl, f = sf, s = ss },
				         warp = e.warp, gx = e.x, gz = e.z, reached = true }
			end
			-- ⛔ AND STAY IN YOUR OWN LANE IF YOU CAN. Ola: "it still routes me to the closest tile to the
			-- warp, however that can be the edge or the wrong side of the stairway, which in my case is
			-- where I can't go." These stairs are three wide, so the region has three trigger tiles and A*
			-- was free to pick an edge. If the tile at the side he is ALREADY on triggers, use only that:
			-- no lateral shuffling onto an edge to reach a door that opens just as well from the middle.
			local lane = {}
			local haslane = false
			for k in pairs(goals) do
				if (k % 512) - 128 == ss then lane[k] = true; haslane = true end
			end
			-- ...but keep the full set as a fallback. A preference that can make a reachable warp
			-- UNREACHABLE is not a preference, it is a bug: if the lane tile happens to be walled off
			-- along the line, restricting to it would answer "can't reach" about a door two tiles
			-- sideways. Same mistake as aiming at one chosen tile of a doorway's correct side.
			allgoals = goals
			if haslane then goals = lane end
		elseif e.rl then
			gl, gf, gs = e.rl.l, e.rl.f, e.rl.s                          -- a live actor: its OWN rail position, exact
			goals = { [gkey(gl, gf, gs)] = true }
		else
			-- ⛔⛔ A GRID PLACE IS NOT A RAIL PLACE, AND SNAPPING ONE TO THE OTHER INVENTS A GOAL.
			-- This arm exists for a goal known only by world x/z — a sign, an object — in a zone where
			-- everything is rails, and there matching the nearest walkable rail tile is right. In a HYBRID
			-- zone it is badly wrong: the way DOWN a Celestial Tower floor is a GRID warp, so its x/z is a
			-- place on the floor, and the nearest rail tile to it is 2 or 3 tiles away ON THE UP STAIRCASE
			-- (measured: 339 -> (20,23) d=3, 340 -> (12,9) d=3, 341 -> (18,21) d=2, all inside the d > 8
			-- refusal gate). So asking to go down, while standing on the rail you just came down, planned a
			-- route back UP the stairs under the name of the floor below. Ola: "it kept routing me to the up
			-- stairs entrance and saying I was with the lower one."
			-- The right move is the mirror of going up: to reach a GRID place you must first GET OFF the
			-- rail, so aim at the handoff and let the crossing re-plan walk the grid half. ow.hand.exit
			-- returns nil in the 11 pure-rail zones, so Castelia and the bridges keep this arm unchanged.
			local hset = ow.hand.exit(rc)
			if hset then
				goals = hset                                             -- every way off; A* picks a reachable one
				exitleg = true                                           -- a WAYPOINT, not the destination
				-- ⛔⛔ EXCEPT WHEN THE CALLER KNOWS WHICH WAY OFF IT WANTS — and in the Opelucid gym it
				-- always does, because a ride there is TRANSPORT, not an exit. "Every way off" is right
				-- when the rails are just something to get across; it is catastrophic when the whole
				-- point of boarding was to reach ONE particular far handoff. Ola boarded at (44,44),
				-- landed on the next position along line 14, and this arm then aimed him at the nearest
				-- way off — which is (44,44), the tile he had just left, one step back. He stepped off,
				-- the grid planner said "board again", and the two spoke over each other for ever:
				-- "one right", recalculating, "one left", recalculating. Neither half was wrong on its
				-- own; they simply disagreed about what the ride was FOR.
				if e.exit1 and e.exit1.l and e.exit1.f and e.exit1.s then
					local k1 = gkey(e.exit1.l, e.exit1.f, e.exit1.s)
					-- ⛔⛔ A PREFERENCE, NEVER A GATE — and leaving out the fallback is what turned this
					-- into silence. Restricting to the one way off the leg wants is right; but if that
					-- handoff is not reachable on the rails from where we stand (a stale leg, a neck that
					-- has since moved), A* fails, the crossing re-plan returns nothing, and pf_update
					-- latches the whole thing as a "ride" and says NOTHING — with a GRID route still
					-- active while the player is in rail space. That is exactly what Ola's dump caught:
					-- `GRIDROUTE "Opelucid City" you=(32,34)` with `ow.space now=rail`, after "1 left,
					-- 1 right" and then quiet. `allgoals` already exists for precisely this shape (the
					-- lane preference uses it), so hand it the full set and let the existing retry run.
					if hset[k1] then goals, allgoals = { [k1] = true }, hset end
				end                                                       -- (an exit1 that is not a way
				                                                          --  off keeps the full set)
				if goals[gkey(sl, sf, ss)] then                           -- already standing on one: step off
					return { rail = true, rpos = { { l = sl, f = sf, s = ss } }, pi = 1, total = 0,
					         segAt = {}, name = e.label or e.name, zone = reg.zid(), exitleg = true,
					         goal = { l = sl, f = sf, s = ss }, gx = e.x, gz = e.z, reached = true }
				end
			elseif ow.HANDOFF[reg.zid()] then
				-- ⛔ FAIL CLOSED IN A BAKED HYBRID ZONE. Falling through to rail.nearest here is the exact
				-- projection this arm's comment says is wrong — it put a downstairs goal on the UP
				-- staircase — and one unreadable frame would recreate it (codex-review 2026-08-25, HIGH).
				return nil, "handoff"
			else
				local d                                                  -- pure rail zone: world coords only,
				gl, gf, gs, d = rail.nearest(rc, e.x, e.z)               -- match the nearest walkable rail tile
				if not gl or d > 8 then return nil, "far" end
				goals = { [gkey(gl, gf, gs)] = true }
			end
		end
		-- ⛔ STANDING ON A WAYPOINT IS NOT BEING THERE. When the goal is a GRID place the rail leg
		-- ends at the handoff, which is a place to get OFF, not the destination — so answering "there"
		-- claims he has arrived at a door on another part of the floor. Ola, 4F: "going down from 4f to
		-- 3f still did not work... the rail said I was there when I wasn't." Hand back a zero-length
		-- route instead, the same shape the lift and the stones use, so the follower can say the step off.
		if gl and sl == gl and sf == gf and ss == gs then
			if not exitleg then return nil, "there" end
			return { rail = true, rpos = { { l = sl, f = sf, s = ss } }, segAt = {}, pi = 1, total = 0,
			         name = e.label or e.name, zone = reg.zid(), exitleg = true,
			         goal = { l = sl, f = sf, s = ss }, warp = e.warp, gx = e.x, gz = e.z, reached = true }
		end
		-- people block here too (Ola 2026-08-08: "it could be a thing there too"). Actors carry their own
		-- RailPosition, so this is the same rule in rail space. Same exemptions: never the goal, and if
		-- occupancy alone blocks the route we re-route without it rather than refuse.
		local occ, la = nil, live_actors()
		if la then
			occ = {}
			local selfk = gkey(sl, sf, ss)                                -- never occupy where we stand
			for _, grp in ipairs({ la.npcs, la.items, la.hm, la.objs }) do
				for _, e in ipairs(grp) do
					if e.rl then
						local k = gkey(e.rl.l, e.rl.f, e.rl.s)
						if not goals[k] and k ~= selfk then occ[k] = true end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		-- ⛔⛔ A SLIDE TILE IS WALKABLE, SO THE ROUTER WOULD HAPPILY WALK YOU ALONG ONE. Victory Road's
		-- launch cells are class 0xA0/0xA1 with Flags bit 0 CLEAR, so every walkability test in this file
		-- says yes — and they sit at side +2/+3, immediately beside a three-wide lane whose steps cost
		-- exactly the same. With uniform cost A* had no reason to prefer the lane and every reason to
		-- tie-break onto the rim.
		-- ⚠ The engine will not fire on the step that ENTERS the cell (that step is legal, and the trigger
		-- needs a Down press whose ordinary move is blocked — which is true only once you are standing on
		-- it). So this is not a certain launch; it is a cell you must not be left standing on by accident,
		-- because ONE more press of a direction he is probably still holding takes the whole ride, and
		-- getting back up is the thing Ola told me is a pain. Cheap to avoid, expensive to get wrong.
		-- Same treatment as Relic Castle's quicksand and for the same reason: AVOIDED, NOT FORBIDDEN. The
		-- penalty is finite, so if a slide tile ever were the only way through somewhere it stays
		-- routable, and routing TO a slide (the whole point of the Slides category) still works — every
		-- path to it pays the same constant, so the choice between them is unaffected.
		-- ⚠ the zone read here, NOT a `z` upvalue: pf_build_rail has none, and the first cut of this
		-- silently read a nil global and disabled the penalty entirely (caught by luacheck).
		local pen
		local pz_ = ow.SLIDE[reg.zid()]
		if pz_ then
			pen = {}
			for _, run in ipairs(pz_) do
				for _, t in ipairs(run) do
					if not goals[gkey(t[1], t[2], t[3])] then pen[gkey(t[1], t[2], t[3])] = ow.PF_SLIDE end
				end
			end
		end
		local moves, endp = rail.astar(rc, sl, sf, ss, goals, occ, pen)
		if not moves and occ then moves, endp = rail.astar(rc, sl, sf, ss, goals, nil, pen) end
		if not moves and allgoals and allgoals ~= goals then                 -- lane preference failed:
			moves, endp = rail.astar(rc, sl, sf, ss, allgoals, occ, pen)        -- try every trigger tile
			if not moves and occ then moves, endp = rail.astar(rc, sl, sf, ss, allgoals, nil, pen) end
		end
		if not moves then return nil, "unreachable" end
		gl, gf, gs = endp.l, endp.f, endp.s                              -- whichever approach tile A* reached
		-- Replay the moves to get the waypoints. Replaying (rather than having A* keep them) means the
		-- route is built by the SAME step function the player's own input goes through, so a waypoint we
		-- announce is by construction a tile the engine agrees you can reach.
		local rpos = { { l = sl, f = sf, s = ss } }
		local cl, cf, cs2 = sl, sf, ss
		for _, m in ipairs(moves) do
			local nl, nf, ns = rail.move(rc, cl, cf, cs2, m.dir)         -- step OR launch ramp, as A* did
			if not nl then return nil, "replay" end
			cl, cf, cs2 = nl, nf, ns
			rpos[#rpos + 1] = { l = cl, f = cf, s = cs2 }
		end
		local segAt, i = {}, 1                                          -- merge consecutive same-button moves
		while i <= #moves do
			local j, n = i, 0
			while j <= #moves and moves[j].dir == moves[i].dir do n = n + 1; j = j + 1 end
			segAt[i] = n .. " " .. PF_BTN[moves[i].dir]
			i = j
		end
		-- e.label carries the duplicate number ("Item 2"); without it a route that STARTS as "To Item 2"
		-- would ARRIVE as "Arrived at Item" and throw away the distinction (codex-review 2026-08-08). The
		-- grid path already passes the label in; this is the rail path's copy of the same name. The re-route
		-- callers below pass `name = pf.name`, so the label then survives every recalculation too.
		return { rail = true, rpos = rpos, segAt = segAt, pi = 1, total = #moves, name = e.label or e.name,
		         zone = reg.zid(),                            -- zone: see the guard at the top of pf_update
		         goal = { l = gl, f = gf, s = gs }, warp = e.warp, gx = e.x, gz = e.z,
		         exitleg = exitleg, reached = true }
	end
	-- ⛔⛔ THE FAILURE HAS TO DOCUMENT ITSELF, because reasoning about it offline has now run out of
	-- road. Ola hit "Can't reach Iris from here" in zone 121 and his N-dump answered every question I
	-- knew to ask — poses (3,1,2) authored, busy=false, settled, all six switch heights baked==live —
	-- and none of the one I needed, which is what the PLANNER said and where the WALK to it died. I then
	-- spent four hypotheses on ROM data (goal-height units, an NPC in the way, edge_ok fences, the seed
	-- height) and killed all four offline, which is exactly the shape the Celestial Tower arc warned
	-- about: instruments that answer the question you already thought of. Everything left is live state.
	-- So this fires at the moment of refusal, from the frame that refuses, and records the whole chain:
	-- the nav entry as the list built it, the goal actually handed to pf_build, the dragon state, what
	-- ow.dg.leg returned, what the flood could see, and whether A* can walk to the leg — with occupancy
	-- and without, since "a person is standing on it" and "there is no path" are different bugs.
	-- ⚠ It writes to the SAME file as the N-dump, so Ola does nothing new: he just asks for Iris again.
	function ow.dg.diag(cs, e, nm, sx, sz, gx, gz)
		local L = {}
		local function p(...) L[#L + 1] = string.format(...) end
		local tlf = function(x, z, y) return pf_tile(cs, x, z, y) end
		p("=== OPELUCID \"CAN'T REACH\" DIAGNOSTIC   zone=%d   target=%s ===", reg.zid(), tostring(nm))
		p("player (%d,%d)  y0=%s  tileheight=%s  forced=%s  moving=%s",
		  sx, sz, tostring(ow.dg.y0(cs, sx, sz)), tostring(ow.height(cs, sx, sz)),
		  tostring(ow.forced()), tostring(ow.moving()))
		p("nav entry: x=%s z=%s y=%s  rail-form=%s  warp=%s  region=%s  navonly=%s",
		  tostring(e.x), tostring(e.z), tostring(e.y), tostring(e.rl ~= nil),
		  tostring(e.warp ~= nil), tostring(e.reg ~= nil), tostring(e.navonly))
		p("goal handed to pf_build: (%d,%d)  gy=%s", gx, gz, tostring(e.y))
		local st = ow.dg.state()
		local bsy, bwhy = ow.dg.busy()
		p("dg.here=%s  poses=%s  settled=%s  busy=%s/%s",
		  tostring(ow.dg.here()),
		  st and (st.p[0] .. "," .. st.p[1] .. "," .. st.p[2]) or "nil",
		  st and tostring(st.settled) or "-", tostring(bsy), tostring(bwhy))
		local leg, why = ow.dg.leg(cs, tlf, pf_walk, reach.edge_ok, sx, sz, gx, gz, e.y)
		p("ow.dg.leg -> %s   rail=%s   press toward (%s,%s)   why=%s",
		  leg and (leg.x .. "," .. leg.z) or "nil", leg and tostring(leg.rail) or "-",
		  leg and tostring(leg.tx) or "-", leg and tostring(leg.tz) or "-", tostring(why))
		local y0 = ow.dg.y0(cs, sx, sz)
		if y0 then
			local fl = ow.dg.flood(cs, tlf, pf_walk, reach.edge_ok, sx, sz, y0)
			local n = 0
			for _ in pairs(fl) do n = n + 1 end
			local hs = {}
			for _, h in ipairs(ow.dg.hand) do
				if ow.dg.at(fl, h) then hs[#hs + 1] = string.format("(%d,%d)", h[1], h[2]) end
			end
			p("flood from the player: %d tiles;  handoffs it reaches: %s",
			  n, (#hs > 0) and table.concat(hs, " ") or "NONE")
			-- ⚠ ow.dg.at compares the flood's recorded height against the BAKED one with half a
			-- tile-height of slack, so a handoff can be flooded and still not COUNT. The old N-dump
			-- printed baked-vs-live for the six switches and never for the 21 handoffs, which is the
			-- one number that decides whether a leg exists. Print it, and say whether it was reached.
			for _, h in ipairs(ow.dg.hand) do
				local live = ow.height(cs, h[1], h[2], h[3])
				p("   handoff (%2d,%2d) baked h=%-7d live h=%-9s at=%s  flooded=%s",
				  h[1], h[2], h[3], tostring(live), tostring(ow.dg.at(fl, h)),
				  tostring(fl[h[1] * 100000 + h[2]] ~= nil))
			end
			local ss = {}
			for _, w in ipairs(ow.dg.sw) do
				if ow.dg.at(fl, { w[1], w[2], w[6] }) then
					ss[#ss + 1] = string.format("(%d,%d)", w[1], w[2])
				end
			end
			p("switches it reaches: %s", (#ss > 0) and table.concat(ss, " ") or "NONE")
		end
		local occ, occok = pf_occupied(gx * 100000 + gz)
		local hard, hardok = pf_occupied(nil, true)
		p("occupancy readable=%s   hard-blockers readable=%s", tostring(occok), tostring(hardok))
		local function astar(tx, tz, label, o)
			local mv, reached, term = pf_astar(cs, sx, sz, tx, tz, o, hard)
			p("A* (%d,%d) -> (%d,%d) %s: moves=%s reached=%s term=%s",
			  sx, sz, tx, tz, label, mv and #mv or "nil", tostring(reached), tostring(term))
		end
		if leg then
			astar(leg.x, leg.z, "[the leg, with people]", occ)
			astar(leg.x, leg.z, "[the leg, NO people]", nil)
		end
		astar(gx, gz, "[the goal itself, with people]", occ)
		L[#L + 1] = ""
		local f = io.open("C:/Users/Amethyst/building-dump.txt", "a")
		if f then f:write(table.concat(L, "\n") .. "\n"); f:close() end
	end
	local function pf_start()                                           -- p: (re)route to the selected nav item, or toggle off
		local e = cur_list()[ii]                                        -- the item EXACTLY as last announced — do NOT
		-- ⛔ YOU CANNOT ROUTE THROUGH A ROOM YOU CANNOT SEE (Ola, 2026-09-01, in Mistralton Cave: "make
		-- the path finder unavailable until I actually use the move, so it will say something like: 'This
		-- area is too dark to see in' ... giving the player a little hint about the issue at hand").
		-- Before the nav-list check, so the answer is about the ROOM rather than about which entry happens
		-- to be selected — the hint he asked for is the same whatever he was aiming at, and it is the more
		-- useful of the two things we could say.
		-- ⛔⛔ AND IN THE DARK, p ALWAYS MEANS STOP. My first version let a toggle-off through by matching
		-- the selected entry against the live route — and codex-review found the hole: `lists` is cleared
		-- whenever this module loses control (a battle, a menu), so after coming back with a route still
		-- running there IS no selected entry, the match could not fire, and the darkness gate refused the
		-- one press that would have stopped it. A route you cannot cancel is a worse trap than the gate is
		-- worth. So a dark room does not compare entries at all: anything running is cancelled outright,
		-- and only with nothing running do you get the hint. Re-routing is refused either way, so cancel
		-- is the only thing p could sensibly mean here.
		-- ⚠ The collision data is NOT missing in the dark: the tile grid is read through Field+0x48 while
		-- the darkness is a render object at Field+0xA0, two systems that never touch (verified in the
		-- engine — report_flash.md). So this is a deliberate accessibility policy, matching what a sighted
		-- player can do, and not the tool failing to see.
		if ow.too_dark(reg.zid()) then
			if pf or ow.dg.pend then
				pf, ow.dg.pend = nil, nil; say("Pathfinding off", true); return
			end
			say("This area is too dark to see in.", true); return
		end
		if not e then say("Open the nav list first, then press p", true); return end  -- refresh()/re-sort here (that re-
		                                                                -- orders Transitions and changes what ii points to)
		-- Speak it by the SAME words the nav list just used, duplicate number and all ("To Item 2"), so the
		-- entry you heard and the entry you are routed to can never sound like different things.
		local nm = e.label or e.name
		-- ⚠ A HELD REQUEST MUST ANSWER THE TOGGLE TOO. Pressing p on the entry you are already routed to
		-- turns pathfinding off; while a request was merely HELD (pf is still nil, see ow.dg.pend) that
		-- gesture fell through and simply re-requested, so there was no way to cancel one. ow.dg.pend
		-- carries gx/gz/reg/warp — exactly the fields ow.same_goal reads — so it answers directly.
		if ow.same_goal(ow.dg.pend, e) then
