			local into
			if pf.reg then
				for _, g in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
					local nx, nz = tx + g[2], tz + g[3]
					if nx >= pf.reg[1] and nx <= pf.reg[1] + pf.reg[3] - 1
					   and nz >= pf.reg[2] and nz <= pf.reg[2] + pf.reg[4] - 1 then into = g[1] end
				end
			end
			-- ⛔ AND IT IS NOT SPOKEN. Ola, 2026-08-25: "one up is wrong, but also don't speak that, we
			-- don't need it." On the grid he can feel his way into a doorway; a step he has to distrust is
			-- worse than no step at all. It is still COMPUTED, because the N-dump wants it — a wrong answer
			-- kept where only I can see it is a diagnostic, and spoken it is noise.
			ow.lastroute = { name = pf.name, gx = pf.gx, gz = pf.gz, reg = pf.reg, ex = tx, ez = tz,
			                 into = into, zone = reg.zid() }      -- kept for the N-dump: arrival used to
			-- ⛔⛔ "ARRIVED" ON ITS OWN IS NOT ENOUGH WHEN THE TARGET IS A PERSON. Ola, 2026-08-26, standing
			-- one tile from Brycen: "It says I arrived but I am not at him." You cannot stand ON someone, so
			-- a route to a person ALWAYS ends beside them — and a sighted player can see which side. He
			-- cannot, and we were telling him nothing, which makes a correct arrival indistinguishable from
			-- a wrong one. Say where the target is, in the same words the route itself uses ("1 left"), so
			-- it reads as the press that faces them.
			-- ⚠ AND IT DOUBLES AS THE INSTRUMENT FOR THE REAL BUG UNDER THIS. His route was aiming at
			-- (18,15) while Brycen's actor sat at (17,14) — DIAGONAL, and you cannot talk to anyone
			-- diagonally. Nothing authored in the zone sits at (18,15) (research/re/z114_brycen.py checks
			-- every furniture, actor, sign and event record), and neither NPC reader can produce it: both
			-- take the actor's live tile from +0x3C/+0x40. So it was his live tile WHEN THE ROUTE WAS
			-- BUILT — pf.gx/gz are frozen at creation and ow.replan reuses them, so a target that moves
			-- leaves the route pointing at where it used to be. A person with moveCode 0 does not walk, but
			-- opcode 0x6D relocates an actor without touching its behaviour (banked in white.lua), which is
			-- exactly what a gym script does with its leader. Saying the offset makes that visible the
			-- instant it happens instead of sounding like a successful arrival.
			local far = ""
			if pf.gx and pf.gz and not (pf.gx == tx and pf.gz == tz) then
				local parts = {}
				local dx, dz = pf.gx - tx, pf.gz - tz
				if dz < 0 then parts[#parts+1] = (-dz) .. " up" elseif dz > 0 then parts[#parts+1] = dz .. " down" end
				if dx < 0 then parts[#parts+1] = (-dx) .. " left" elseif dx > 0 then parts[#parts+1] = dx .. " right" end
				if #parts > 0 then far = ", " .. table.concat(parts, " and ") end
			end
			say("Arrived at " .. (pf.name or "target") .. far, true)  --   destroy the only record of itself
			pf = nil; return
		end
		if nxt and tx == nxt.x and tz == nxt.z then                     -- advanced one tile along the route
			pf.pi = pf.pi + 1
			-- ⛔⛔ ARRIVING ON A DRAGON SWITCH IS NOT THE END OF A ROUTE, IT IS THE MIDDLE OF ONE.
			-- A switch is a COORDINATE EVENT: stepping onto the tile IS the action, so the leg ends
			-- exactly where the game takes over — it hops the player sideways onto the arm and swings a
			-- neck. The generic end-of-route code re-planned on that very frame, and every part of that
			-- answer is unusable: the room is mid-transaction, and ow.dg.leg is being asked from the one
			-- tile it deliberately never offers (the switch under the player's feet, because aiming a
			-- route at where you already stand is an infinite loop). It came back with nothing, and the
			-- caller reads "no route" as "this is as close as I can get" — so the route was DESTROYED at
			-- the first switch, one step into an eleven-step journey. Ola: "at the first switch it said
			-- As close as I can get to Iris."
			-- Arm the swing hold instead and say nothing. pf_update's dragon block already knows how to
			-- wait out the animation and re-plan the moment the neck lands ("Recalculating"), and the
			-- forced-move guard at the top of this function covers the hop itself. pf.dgat records WHICH
			-- switch, so the hold cannot release while the player is still standing on it — a swing that
			-- has not started yet reads as settled, and releasing there would walk straight back into the
			-- same bad re-plan.
			if pf.dg and not pf.dg.rail and tx == pf.dg.x and tz == pf.dg.z then
				pf.dgsw, pf.dgat, pf.dghold = true, { x = tx, z = tz }, nil
				pf.dgpress = true                                       -- WE asked for this one: see the
				return                                                  -- release, which then skips the word
			end
			if pf.pi >= #pf.tiles then                                  -- end of a PARTIAL route (real target wasn't
				local r = ow.replan()                                   -- loaded yet): we've moved + more chunks streamed
				if r then pf = r; if r.segAt[1] then say(r.segAt[1], true) end
				else
					-- A RAIL goal walked to across the GRID ends where the grid does: on the HANDOFF tile at
					-- the street mouth, one step short. That step is not blocked-and-hopeless, it IS the
					-- crossing — the engine hands you to the rails when you press into the blocked side of a
					-- class-0xA3 tile — so name it in the route's own words ("1 down") and keep the route
					-- alive. Stepping across flips the controller, and the crossing check at the top of
					-- pf_update then re-plans the rest of the way in rail space.
					-- THE ENGINE'S OWN CONDITIONS FOR THE HANDOVER, as far as we can check them (Codex's
					-- Ghidra trace): the tile UNDER US is the authored class 0xA3, the attempted step is
					-- BLOCKED on the grid, and the world-to-rail projection succeeds. That projection is of
					-- the CURRENT position — not of the tile we are stepping into — so the stand-in for it
					-- is a walkable rail tile near where we STAND. Three tiles of slack, measured rather
					-- than guessed: across all 65 handoff tiles in all nine hybrid zones the nearest
					-- walkable rail tile is 0 to 3 away (41 of them 0).
					-- Choose the best direction outright: several sides of a corner tile can qualify, and
					-- "whichever came last in the list" would be arbitrary (codex-review verification).
					local rcx = (pf.warp or pf.rl0) and rail.sys()                -- any goal that lives on the rails
					local cs = rcx and ow.grid()
					local t = cs and pf_tile(cs, tx, tz)
					local dir
					if t and t % 0x10000 == 0xA3 then
						-- ⛔ ASK THE RAIL, NOT THE GRID BEARING. The test below this one picks the blocked
						-- side that points nearest pf.gx/gz, which is only ever right because the old aim
						-- was a tile down the street BEYOND the mouth. In Celestial Tower the goal is up a
						-- staircase directly above you, so a grid bearing is meaningless and it would pick
						-- an arbitrary wall. ow.hand.cross steps the rail from the tile we stand on and
						-- keeps the press whose successor is genuinely closer to the warp along the rails —
						-- the engine's own model, and it needs no bearing at all. The old test stays as the
						-- fallback for a goal that is on the rails but is not a WARP (an NPC or an item in
						-- one of the two gyms), where there is no rail region to measure a distance to.
						-- ⛔ AND THE FALLBACK IS FOR NON-WARP GOALS ONLY — the comment above said so but the
						-- code did not, so when hand.cross failed closed on a staircase the bearing test
						-- ran anyway and could name an arbitrary wall (codex-review 2026-08-25, HIGH).
						dir = pf.warp and ow.hand.cross(rcx, pf.warp, cs, tx, tz) or nil
						local _, _, _, rd = rail.nearest(rcx, tx, tz)
						if not dir and not pf.warp and rd and rd <= 3 then
							local best
							for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
								local nx, nz = tx + d[2], tz + d[3]
								local gain = math.abs(nx - pf.gx) + math.abs(nz - pf.gz)
								if not pf_walk(pf_tile(cs, nx, nz)) and (not best or gain < best) then
									best, dir = gain, d[1]
								end
							end
							if best and best >= math.abs(tx - pf.gx) + math.abs(tz - pf.gz) then dir = nil end
						end                                                       -- (no side takes us any closer)
					end
					if dir then say("1 " .. dir, true)
					else say("As close as I can get to "..(pf.name or "target"), true); pf = nil end
				end
			-- ⛔ ONE LINE PER EVENT. Ola 2026-08-18: some gym switches announced TWICE — "where to go when I
			-- step on it as well as when it's done". It was never the switch being slow. Stepping onto a tile
			-- that happens to be a CORNER speaks the next turn here; then the switch script takes control and
			-- pf_reenter re-plans and speaks again on the way back. Switches mid-straight-run had no corner
			-- line, which is exactly why "not all switches do this".
			-- The re-planned line is the authoritative one (a wall has just opened, so the route changed), so
			-- the corner line is the redundant one — suppress it when we can PROVE a script is about to run:
			-- in the gym, on a switch tile, with its variable still 0. Not a delay, and not a guess: if the
			-- script had already fired we would have been gated off and never reached this branch at all, and
			-- if that switch were already pressed its variable would read 1 and we would speak normally.
			-- ⚠ The one way this could bite is a switch that does NOT fire when stepped on, which would
			-- swallow a turn with nothing to replace it. Checked: the coordinate-event engine does apply a
			-- direction condition, but ALL ELEVEN of this zone's trigger records carry 0 in both candidate
			-- fields (+0x06 and +0x08), so no gym switch is restricted to one approach — stepping on it from
			-- any side runs the script. Its only other condition is var == 0, which is what we just tested.
			else
				-- ⛔ REVALIDATE THE ROUTE AHEAD AGAINST WHATEVER DATA EXISTS NOW. The interior bake is
				-- immutable ROM data, so a tile that is walkable in the ROM but gets BLOCKED at runtime
				-- (a puzzle dropping a barrier, say) would still read walkable for as long as its chunk is
				-- unloaded, and a route could be planned straight through it. Rather than hope, re-check the
				-- remaining path every time we advance a tile: the engine streams a chunk in once you are
				-- within about K=16 tiles of it, and the window below is wider than that, so every tile gets
				-- checked against LIVE data before you can reach it. Live always wins in pf_tile, so this
				-- converges on the truth as you walk. (codex-review 2026-08-18 raised this as an
				-- unresolved uncertainty; Ola: "we should probably fix it properly now anyways", and she is
				-- right — Dragonspiral Tower is late-game, so a fault there would hide for months.)
				-- Bounded to a window so the cost stays flat on a long route; tiles beyond it are validated
				-- on later advances, always well before they are stepped on.
				-- ⚠ ONLY IN BAKED ZONES. Outdoors and in one-chunk rooms pf_tile never substitutes ROM
				-- data: a tile is either live or nil, and nil counts as blocked, so A* can never have
				-- routed through a tile we guessed at. Confining the check to the 67 baked interiors keeps
				-- this off the outdoor hot path, where Ola has reported pathfinder lag before, and where
				-- the pre-existing "off route" recovery already handles a genuine runtime change.
				local cs = ow.ic.ZONE[reg.zid()] and pf_chunks(true) or nil
				local stale = false
				if cs then
					-- ⛔⛔ AT THE PLAYER'S HEIGHT. This re-checks the route ahead on EVERY advance, and
					-- height-blind it answers from permission layer 0 — the ground floor. On Opelucid's
					-- upper gallery every tile of a perfectly good route reads as the WALL underneath it,
					-- so the route was declared stale at every single step: re-plan, "Recalculating",
					-- correct path, repeat. Ola: "each step would say recalculating". It is the same
					-- fault as ow.grid's, one screen further on, and it only surfaced once ow.grid was
					-- fixed — before that this branch was never reached up there at all.
					-- ⛔⛔ AND THE HEIGHT MUST TRAVEL ALONG THE ROUTE, NOT BE THE PLAYER'S THROUGHOUT.
					-- This used to validate every tile ahead at the player's CURRENT height, on the
					-- reasoning written above it — "a route between two handoffs stays on one storey".
					-- A RAMP is precisely a route that does not: Pinwheel Forest climbs z=53 eastward in
					-- half-tile steps, 0 -> 16384 -> 49152 -> 81920 -> 114688 -> 147456 -> 180224, and
					-- from x=18 on those tiles exist only in permission layer 1. Checked at the player's
					-- own height of 0 they all read as the WALL underneath, so a perfectly good route was
					-- declared stale on every advance: re-plan, "Recalculating", same route, repeat.
					-- Ola, 2026-09-04: "it says recalculating when I am going the right way and following
					-- the route?" — and he was.
					-- So carry the height forward the way pf_astar carried it when it PLANNED these tiles:
					-- each tile answers at the height we arrive with, and an unknown keeps the last known
					-- one (A*'s own `ey = ny or cy`). A nil start still falls through to the old blind
					-- call, and on the 452 single-layer zones every tile has one height, so nothing moves.
					local y = ow.py()
					local last = math.min(#pf.tiles, pf.pi + 48)
					for i = pf.pi + 1, last do
						local t = pf.tiles[i]
						if not pf_walk(pf_tile(cs, t.x, t.z, y)) then stale = true; break end
						y = ow.height(cs, t.x, t.z, y) or y
					end
				end
				if stale then
					local r = ow.replan()
					if r then pf = r; say("Recalculating. " .. (r.segAt[1] or ""), true)
					else say("Can't reach " .. (pf.name or "target") .. " from here", true); pf = nil end
					return
				end
				if pf.segAt[pf.pi] and not ow.gym.arming(tx, tz) then
					say(pf.segAt[pf.pi], true)                           -- reached a corner → next turn
				end
			end
			return
		end
		if tx == pf.tiles[pf.pi].x and tz == pf.tiles[pf.pi].z then return end   -- hasn't moved
		-- (A hop's middle is not tested here. The follower is held for the WHOLE flight by the jump
		--  latch at the top of pf_update — the engine's own selected action — so no in-flight frame
		--  ever reaches this branch. An unreadable actor chain holds it even earlier, at ow.forced().)
		if not ow.grid() then return end                                -- off route → recompute from here
		local r = ow.replan()
		if r then pf = r; say("Recalculating. "..(r.segAt[1] or ""), true) else pf = nil end
	end
	-- ── BUILDING CURATION DUMP (dev tool) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	-- The game has no zone->building-name link (RE'd exhaustively), so we curate zone -> bank-185 building
	-- names by hand. Press N inside a building to APPEND a context record to a FILE (NOT the speech console,
	-- so play stays clean). It captures the zone id, our place name, the parent town, position, the raw
	-- zonedata header, and the zone's warps (dest zones) + NPC model ids — enough for Claude to map most
	-- buildings (and to learn NPC-model patterns, e.g. a nurse model => Pokemon Center). Walk into each
	-- building, press N; send Claude the file afterward + which ones are which where it's ambiguous.
	-- File: C:/Users/Amethyst/building-dump.txt  (appends across the session).
	local DUMP_PATH = "C:/Users/Amethyst/building-dump.txt"
	local dump_seq = 0
	local function building_dump(m)
		dump_seq = dump_seq + 1
		local out = { string.format("=== dump #%d   zone %d (raw %d)   our_name=%q ===",
			dump_seq, m, u16(A.map_id), zone_name(m)),
			string.format("player pos: x=%d  y=%d", ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)) }
		-- ⛔ THE LAST FINISHED ROUTE, BECAUSE ARRIVAL DESTROYS THE ONLY RECORD OF ITSELF. I asked Ola to
		-- press N "when it says you have arrived" and got a dump with nothing in it — arrival sets pf = nil,
		-- so the live-route section could not fire at the one moment it was wanted. Design a diagnostic for
		-- the instant it has to run in, not the instant you happen to be thinking about.
		if ow.lastroute and ow.lastroute.zone == m then
			local L = ow.lastroute
			out[#out+1] = string.format("LAST ROUTE %q: ended at (%d,%d), aim was (%s,%s), step in = %s",
				tostring(L.name), L.ex, L.ez, tostring(L.gx), tostring(L.gz), tostring(L.into))
			if L.reg then
				local r = L.reg
				-- ⚠ "you ended EAST of it" was a lie for a stand-on doorway, where ending ON the region is
				-- the correct arrival (Codex verify 2026-08-26). An instrument that reports the one case it
				-- exists to explain as its opposite is worse than no instrument.
				local side
				if L.ex >= r[1] and L.ex <= r[1] + r[3] - 1
				   and L.ez >= r[2] and L.ez <= r[2] + r[4] - 1 then side = "ON"
				else side = (L.ez < r[2] and "NORTH") or (L.ez >= r[2] + r[4] and "SOUTH")
				            or (L.ex < r[1] and "WEST") or "EAST" end
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  its doorway region = (%d,%d) %dx%d %s; you ended %s of it",
					r[1], r[2], r[3], r[4], r[7] and "(stand ON it) " or "(walk INTO it) ", side)
				local cs2 = ow.grid()
				local rows = {}
				for zz = r[2] - 2, r[2] + r[4] + 1 do
					local row = ""
					for xx = r[1] - 3, r[1] + r[3] + 2 do
						local inreg = xx >= r[1] and xx <= r[1] + r[3] - 1
						               and zz >= r[2] and zz <= r[2] + r[4] - 1
						if xx == L.ex and zz == L.ez then row = row .. "X"
						elseif inreg then row = row .. "D"
						elseif not cs2 then row = row .. "?"
						else row = row .. (pf_walk(pf_tile(cs2, xx, zz)) and "." or "#") end
					end
					rows[#rows+1] = string.format("z=%d %s", zz, row)
				end
				out[#out+1] = "  map around the doorway (D door, X where the route left you): "
					.. table.concat(rows, " | ")
			end
		end
		-- A LIVE GRID ROUTE GETS ITS OWN LINES TOO. Ola on Celestial Tower 5F: "it didn't path find me
		-- correctly to the staircase, I had to walk a little around to find it." I could NOT reproduce that
		-- offline — modelling A*'s fallback over every standable tile of 342 says the route ends on a tile
		-- ow.at_reg accepts from all 130 of them — so rather than guess a third time, print what the route
		-- actually did: where it ends, what it was aiming at, and which tiles beside the doorway count as
		-- arrival. If the end tile is an accepted one, the fault is that ARRIVING is not the same as being
		-- where he can see the stairs, and that is a different fix from the routing.
		do                                                              -- SURF: what the detector sees right now
			local cs3 = ow.grid()
			local sp = cs3 and reach.surf_spots(cs3, ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y), true) or {}
			local w = {}
			for _, e in ipairs(sp) do w[#w+1] = string.format("(%d,%d) press %s", e.x, e.z, tostring(e.sdir)) end
			out[#out+1] = string.format("SURF: can_surf=%s  spots near you: %s",
				tostring(ow.can_surf()), #w > 0 and table.concat(w, " | ") or "none")
			-- and the other direction: are we afloat, what would the ways OFF be, and did the nav list
			-- actually keep them? Ola's report was "no ways off the water" and the cause turned out to be
			-- the FILTER, not the detector, so print both halves side by side.
			local hx, hz = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
			local ht2 = cs3 and pf_tile(cs3, hx, hz)
			local af = ht2 and math.floor(ht2 / 0x20000) % 2 == 1
			local lo = (cs3 and af) and reach.land_spots(cs3, hx, hz) or {}
			local l2 = {}
			for _, e in ipairs(lo) do l2[#l2+1] = string.format("%s (%d,%d)", tostring(e.name), e.x, e.z) end
			local kept = 0
			for _, e in ipairs(lists and lists.Transitions or {}) do kept = kept + 1 end
			out[#out+1] = string.format("WATER: afloat=%s  ways off: %s  |  Transitions in the list: %d",
				tostring(af), #l2 > 0 and table.concat(l2, " | ") or "none", kept)
		end
		-- ⭐⭐ THE STRENGTH PLANNER, ASKED OUT LOUD. Ola, 2026-09-02: the router got him to push one boulder,
		-- then said "as close as I can get" halfway through the puzzle. I reproduced that exact frame SIX
		-- ways offline — his position, his rock layout, the door's real approach tile, the dump's own
		-- occupancy set, and the live-vs-ROM tiles compared 289 for 289 — and the planner solved it every
		-- single time. Past that point more reading is guessing, and this file's own rule is to log the
		-- speaker instead of theorising about it.
		-- So the dump now asks ow.st exactly what pf_build asks it, from where he is standing, for every
		-- transition in the list: what the live read sees, and what the planner answers and WHY. Whatever
		-- is different in the running game, this prints it.
		do
			local zs = reg.zid()
			if ow.st.here() then
				out[#out+1] = "STRENGTH: boulder zone | flag 0x0964 (Strength used here) = "
					.. tostring(ow.st.on()) .. " | sig = " .. tostring(ow.st.sig())
				local rs = ow.st.raw()
				if not rs then
					out[#out+1] = "  raw(): NIL  <-- the actor manager did not read; the planner is blind"
				else
					for _, r in ipairs(rs) do
						-- ⚠ PRINT THE FIELDS THE TEST ACTUALLY READS. The first version of this line printed
						-- `origin` from r.ox/r.oz, which raw() never stored, so it showed (-1,-1) for every
						-- rock in every state while the real origin was the thing being compared. The one
						-- field it did print truthfully, cmd, is what gave the answer — by luck, not design.
						out[#out+1] = string.format(
							"  rock uid=%d slot=%s cur=(%d,%d,%d) org=(%d,%d,%d) sta=0x%X cmd=%d "
							.. "moving=%s saved=%s@(%s,%s) busy=%s down=%s",
							r.uid, tostring(r.slot), r.x, r.y, r.z, r.ox, r.oy, r.oz, r.sta, r.cmd,
							tostring(r.moving), tostring(r.saved), tostring(r.sx), tostring(r.sz),
							tostring(ow.st.busy(r)), tostring(ow.st.down(r)))
					end
				end
				local cq = ow.grid() or pf_chunks(true)
				if not cq then
					out[#out+1] = "  no usable chunk set here, so the planner cannot be asked"
				else
					local qx, qz = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
					local qy = ow.s32(A.map_x + 0x02)
					if not qy or qy > 0x400000 or qy < -0x400000 then qy = nil end
					if qy == nil then qy = ow.height(cq, qx, qz) end
					local qh, qok = pf_occupied(nil, true)
					local ql, qlf = ow.st.live(function(x, z) return pf_tile(cq, x, z) end)
					out[#out+1] = string.format("  live(): %s pushable, %s fallen | occupancy readable=%s "
						.. "| startY=%s | stfill=%s", ql and #ql or "NIL", qlf and #qlf or "NIL",
						tostring(qok), tostring(qy), tostring(cq.stfill ~= nil))
					local nt = 0
					for _, e in ipairs((lists and lists.Transitions) or {}) do
						nt = nt + 1
						local gs
						if e.reg and e.reg[5] then
							gs = {}
							for _, t in ipairs(ow.reg_tiles(e.reg)) do
								if pf_walk(pf_tile(cq, t[1], t[2], qy)) then
									gs[#gs+1] = t[1] * 100000 + t[2]
								end
							end
							if #gs == 0 then gs = nil end
						end
						local lg, lw = ow.st.leg(cq,
							function(x, z) return pf_tile(cq, x, z, qy) end, pf_walk, qh,
							qx, qz, e.x or 0, e.z or 0, pf_occupied(nil, false), gs)
						out[#out+1] = string.format("  -> %-20s (%d,%d) reg=%s accepted=%s : %s",
							tostring(e.label or e.name), e.x or -1, e.z or -1,
							e.reg and ("side " .. tostring(e.reg[5])) or "none",
							gs and #gs or "n/a",
							lg and string.format("PLAN stand (%d,%d) press %s%s", lg.x, lg.z, lg.dir,
								lg.need and " (Strength first)" or "")
							    or ("NO PLAN: " .. tostring(lw)))
					end
					if nt == 0 then
						out[#out+1] = "  (no transitions in the list yet - press a nav key first, then N)"
					end
				end
			elseif ow.st.UID[zs] then
				out[#out+1] = "STRENGTH: ow.st.here() is FALSE in a zone the table knows - look here first"
			end
		end
		-- ⭐ THE DARKNESS GATE, ALWAYS PRINTED — including in a lit room, where it says so. A line that
		-- only appears when the feature has already decided something is no use for finding out WHY it
		-- decided it, which is the instrument lesson this file keeps relearning. Three numbers is all it
		-- takes: whether the zone is in the baked table, the live IsFlashUsed byte, and what the two
		-- together make pf_start do. `flash=nil` means the FieldStatus chain did not read — that answers
		-- false and the pathfinder stays available, so a broken chain looks like a lit room here.
		do
			local zdk = reg.zid()
			out[#out+1] = string.format("DARK: zone %d in the flash table = %s  |  flash used now = %s  |  "
				.. "pathfinder %s", zdk, tostring(ow.DARK[zdk] or false), tostring(ow.flash_on()),
				ow.too_dark(zdk) and "REFUSED (too dark)" or "available")
		end
		if pf and not pf.rail and pf.reg then
			local tx2, tz2 = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
			local last = pf.tiles and pf.tiles[#pf.tiles]
			local r4 = pf.reg
			-- ⚠ ASK THE ARRIVAL RULE, do not re-enumerate its neighbours here. This listed all four sides
			-- whatever the record said, so for a stand-on doorway it printed the exact set of tiles that do
			-- NOT count (Codex verify 2026-08-26). ow.reg_tiles IS the rule, in list form.
			local csg, ns = ow.grid(), {}
			for _, t in ipairs(ow.reg_tiles(r4)) do
				if csg and pf_walk(pf_tile(csg, t[1], t[2])) then ns[#ns+1] = string.format("(%d,%d)", t[1], t[2]) end
			end
			out[#out+1] = string.format("GRIDROUTE %q  you=(%d,%d)  aim=(%s,%s)  region=(%d,%d) %dx%d",
				tostring(pf.name), tx2, tz2, tostring(pf.gx), tostring(pf.gz), r4[1], r4[2], r4[3], r4[4])
			out[#out+1] = string.format("  route ends at %s of %d tiles  pi=%d  at_reg(you)=%s",
				last and string.format("(%d,%d)", last.x, last.z) or "-",
				pf.tiles and #pf.tiles or 0, pf.pi, tostring(ow.at_reg(r4, tx2, tz2)))
			out[#out+1] = string.format("  %s that count as arrival: %s",
				r4[7] and "its own tiles" or "tiles beside the doorway", table.concat(ns, " "))
		end
		-- ⛔ A LIVE RAIL ROUTE GETS ITS OWN LINES. Ola in Castelia: "it only gives me first path finder
		-- directions, nothing else" — the follower advances only on an EXACT (line, front, side) match with
		-- the next planned tile, and area 5 is NINETEEN tiles wide, so there is a lot of room for the
		-- player's real path to differ from the planned one. Reading the code could not tell me whether the
		-- match fails, the position goes non-line-form, or the re-plan refuses; this prints all three at
		-- once, plus what pf_build_rail would say right now. Same principle as the Chargestone dump: one
		-- keypress instead of a test round.
		if pf and pf.rail then
			local rcx = rail.sys()
			local l, f, sd = rail.pos()
			local cp, nx = pf.rpos[pf.pi], pf.rpos[pf.pi + 1]
			local now, static = ow.space()
			-- ⚠ THE CHURN NUMBER FIRST. If rail.gen is in the thousands the rail cache is thrashing and
			-- c.wmap is being rebuilt over and over; if it is a handful, the cache is fine and the fault
			-- is in the follower. One number, two hypotheses, one keypress.
			out[#out+1] = string.format("RAIL CACHE: generation %s (rebuilds this session)", tostring(rail.gen))
			-- the guard at the very top of pf_update: anything but `false` here and the follower never runs
			local gs2 = u32(GAMESYS_PTR)
			local fl2 = inram(gs2) and u32(gs2 + 0x20) or 0
			local fp2 = inram(fl2) and u32(fl2 + 0x8C) or 0
			out[#out+1] = string.format("FOLLOWER GATE: ow.forced()=%s (must be false)  moving=%s"
				.. "  FieldPlayer=%s grid=%s rail=%s",
				tostring(ow.forced()), tostring(ow.moving()),
				inram(fp2) and string.format("%08X", fp2) or "nil",
				inram(fp2) and string.format("%08X", u32(fp2 + 0x08)) or "-",
				inram(fp2) and string.format("%08X", u32(fp2 + 0x0C)) or "-")
			out[#out+1] = string.format("RAILROUTE %q  pi=%d of %d  live=%s  space=%s/%s  onrail=%s  rtry=%s",
				tostring(pf.name), pf.pi, #pf.rpos,
				l and string.format("line %d front %d side %d", l, f, sd) or "NOT LINE-FORM",
				tostring(now), tostring(static),
				tostring(rcx and rail.onrail(rcx) ~= nil), tostring(pf.rtry))
			out[#out+1] = string.format("  at pi   = %s   %s",
				cp and string.format("line %d front %d side %d", cp.l, cp.f, cp.s) or "-",
				(cp and l and cp.l == l and cp.f == f and cp.s == sd) and "MATCHES live" or "does not match")
			out[#out+1] = string.format("  next    = %s   %s",
				nx and string.format("line %d front %d side %d", nx.l, nx.f, nx.s) or "- (end of route)",
				(nx and l and nx.l == l and nx.f == f and nx.s == sd) and "MATCHES live" or "does not match")
			out[#out+1] = string.format("  goal    = line %d front %d side %d   segAt[pi]=%s",
				pf.goal and pf.goal.l or -1, pf.goal and pf.goal.f or -1, pf.goal and pf.goal.s or -1,
				tostring(pf.segAt[pf.pi]))
			if rcx then
				local dgx2
				local dgl2 = pf.dg or (ow.dg.here() and ow.dg.cur) or nil
				if dgl2 and dgl2.tx then
					local el, ef, es = ow.hand.rail_at(rcx, dgl2.tx, dgl2.tz)
					if el then dgx2 = { l = el, f = ef, s = es } end
				end
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  dg leg: %s  -> exit1 %s",
					dgl2 and (dgl2.x .. "," .. dgl2.z .. " ride to " .. tostring(dgl2.tx) .. ","
					           .. tostring(dgl2.tz) .. (pf.dg and "" or "  [module-held]")) or "none",
					dgx2 and (dgx2.l .. "/" .. dgx2.f .. "/" .. dgx2.s) or "-")
				local r, why = pf_build_rail(rcx, { x = pf.gx, z = pf.gz, name = pf.name, warp = pf.warp,
				                                   exit1 = dgx2,
				                                   rl = (not pf.warp) and (pf.goal or pf.rl0) or nil })
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  a re-plan RIGHT NOW would: %s",
					r and ("succeed, first turn " .. tostring(r.segAt[1])) or ("REFUSE, why=" .. tostring(why)))
			else
				out[#out+1] = "  rail.sys() is NIL right now — the follower would return every frame"
			end
			-- the next few planned tiles, so a desync by SIDE on a wide rail is visible at a glance
			local nxt = {}
			for i = pf.pi, math.min(pf.pi + 5, #pf.rpos) do
				local t = pf.rpos[i]
				nxt[#nxt+1] = string.format("%d/%d/%d", t.l, t.f, t.s)
			end
			out[#out+1] = "  planned next: " .. table.concat(nxt, "  ")
			-- ⛔ AND THE SAME POSITIONS AS WORLD TILES, WITH WHOEVER IS STANDING ON OR BESIDE THEM.
			-- Ola was stopped mid-ride by "someone in the way", and neither the rail positions nor the
			-- actor list could answer WHO on their own: the actor list is in grid tiles, the route is in
			-- rail positions, and the nearest person turned out to be on neither of the route's tiles.
			-- Comparing them by hand across two sections of a dump is exactly the kind of step that costs
			-- a whole round, so do it here, in the frame that matters.
			do
				local la2 = live_actors()
				local who = {}
				if la2 then
					for _, grp in ipairs({ la2.npcs, la2.items, la2.hm, la2.objs }) do
						for _, a in ipairs(grp) do
							if a.x and a.z then who[a.x * 100000 + a.z] = a.name or "?" end
						end
					end
				end
				local rows = {}
				for i = pf.pi, math.min(#pf.rpos, pf.pi + 14) do
					local q = pf.rpos[i]
					local wx, wz = rail.world(rcx, q.l, q.f, q.s)
					local tag = ""
					if wx then
						local onit = who[wx * 100000 + wz]
						if onit then tag = "  <-- " .. onit .. " IS ON THIS TILE" end
						if tag == "" then
							for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
								local nb = who[(wx + d[2]) * 100000 + (wz + d[3])]
								if nb then tag = tag .. "  (" .. nb .. " " .. d[1] .. " of it)" end
							end
						end
					end
					rows[#rows + 1] = string.format("    %2d: %s/%s/%s -> %s,%s%s", i,
						tostring(q.l), tostring(q.f), tostring(q.s),
						tostring(wx), tostring(wz), tag)
				end
				out[#out+1] = "  the ride ahead, in world tiles:"
				for _, rw in ipairs(rows) do out[#out+1] = rw end
			end
			-- ⭐ THE VETERAN ON THE BRIDGE. Script file 242 has exactly three ApplyMovement calls and all
			-- three target actor UID 9. Talking to him (his own script, entry 11) applies sequence 0x0400
			-- — one action, 0x4B, an emote — and he does NOT move. The rail COORDINATE trigger (entry 10,
			-- rail rectangle line 41 front 0 = world (32,34)) applies 0x0400 and then 0x0408, which is
			-- "walk two steps, then face" — that is the one that clears the bridge. It is gated on script
			-- variable 0x40A9 being 0, so print it: if it is non-zero the scene has already run and he is
			-- not going to move again, which changes the answer completely.
			if reg.zid() == 121 then
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  VETERAN TRIGGER: var 0x40A9 = %s   (0 = the scene can still "
					.. "fire; it lives on the rail tile (32,34), step-on)", tostring(ow.gym.var(0x40A9)))
			end
			-- ⛔⛔ THE POSES AND THE LIVE DISABLED SET, IN BOTH SPACES. These two numbers decide which rides
			-- exist, so they are the first thing anyone needs when a ride fails — and they were printed
			-- only inside the grid-gated diagnostic below, which means they disappeared precisely when the
			-- player was ON THE RAILS. Ola's dump from (12,39), stuck mid-ride and told "13 down", has no
			-- dragon section at all for exactly that reason: the one failure mode where they matter most
			-- was the one where the dump stayed silent about them. A diagnostic that hides in the case it
			-- was written for is not a diagnostic (this room has now taught that three times).
			if ow.dg.here() then
				local st2 = ow.dg.state()
				local b2, bw2 = ow.dg.busy()
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  DRAGONS: poses=%s  settled=%s  busy=%s%s",
					st2 and (st2.p[0] .. "," .. st2.p[1] .. "," .. st2.p[2]) or "unreadable",
					st2 and tostring(st2.settled) or "-", tostring(b2),
					bw2 and ("  reason=" .. bw2) or "")
				local rcd2 = rail.sys()
				if rcd2 then
					local d2 = {}
					for i = 0, rcd2.nl - 1 do if rcd2.dis[i] then d2[#d2+1] = i end end
					out[#out+1] = "  live disabled lines: " .. ((#d2 > 0) and table.concat(d2, ",") or "(none)")
					-- and whether the line the route is actually riding is one of them
					local rl2, rf2, rs2 = rail.pos()
					if rl2 then
						out[#out+1] = string.format("  you are on line %d front %d side %d — that line is %s",
							rl2, rf2, rs2, rcd2.dis[rl2] and "**DISABLED**" or "enabled")
					end
					if pf and pf.rail and pf.rpos then
						local bad = {}
						for _, q in ipairs(pf.rpos) do
							if rcd2.dis[q.l] and not bad[q.l] then bad[#bad+1] = q.l; bad[q.l] = true end
						end
						out[#out+1] = "  lines this route rides that are DISABLED right now: "
							.. ((#bad > 0) and table.concat(bad, ",") or "(none)")
					end
				else
					out[#out+1] = "  live disabled lines: rail.sys() is nil"
				end
			end
		end
		-- ⛔ HYBRID ZONES GET THEIR OWN LINES, for the same reason Driftveil does below: every hop that can
		-- silence the router here is invisible to the offline harnesses, because they all supply the rail
		-- system rather than reading it. Chargestone cost four test rounds before a purpose-built dump named
		-- the failing hop in one keypress; this is that dump, written before the round rather than after it.
		if ow.HANDOFF[m] then
			local now, static = ow.space()
			local rcx = rail.sys()
			out[#out+1] = string.format("HYBRID: ow.space now=%s static=%s   rail.sys=%s   ow.grid=%s",
				tostring(now), tostring(static),
				rcx and string.format("np=%d nl=%d", rcx.np, rcx.nl) or "NIL",
				tostring(ow.grid() ~= nil))
			local H, csx = ow.HANDOFF[m], ow.grid()
			for i = 1, #H - 1, 2 do
				local hx, hz = H[i], H[i + 1]
				local l, f, s                                            -- (never `= rcx and f(...)`: see pf_build)
				if rcx then l, f, s = ow.hand.rail_at(rcx, hx, hz) end
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  handoff (%d,%d): tile=%s  rail=%s",
					hx, hz, csx and string.format("0x%X", pf_tile(csx, hx, hz) or 0) or "no grid",
					l and string.format("line %d front %d side %d", l, f, s) or "NO RAIL UNDER IT")
			end
			if pf and pf.warp and rcx then
				local d = ow.hand.dist(rcx, pf.warp)
				local px, pz = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
				local hx, hz = ow.hand.pick(rcx, pf.warp, px, pz)
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  route %q: dist=%s  pick=%s  cross=%s",
					tostring(pf.name), d and "ok" or "NIL",
					hx and string.format("(%d,%d)", hx, hz) or "NONE",
					-- csx may be nil — that is one of the things this dump exists to REPORT, so it must not
					-- be the thing that kills it. ow.hand.cross now fails closed on a nil grid too.
					(hx and csx) and tostring(ow.hand.cross(rcx, pf.warp, csx, hx, hz)) or "-")
			end
		end
		-- ⛔ DRIFTVEIL'S SHAFT NEEDS ITS OWN LINE IN HERE, because nothing else in this dump describes it:
		-- its floor is not in the tile grid, so the collision block below shows the bottom of the mine
		-- whatever storey you are standing on. Ola's first go at the room cost a screenshot and a round
		-- trip to work out that a route had walked him into a control post; with this it is one keypress.
		if ow.dv.here() then
			local s = ow.dv.state()
			if not s then
				out[#out+1] = "DRIFTVEIL: gimmick state UNREADABLE (so every reader here is silent)"
			else
				local bits = 0
				for i = 1, 6 do bits = bits + s.sv[i] * (1 << (i - 1)) end
				local px, pz = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
				out[#out+1] = string.format("DRIFTVEIL: player Y=%d storey=%s  moving=%s  surface=%s",
					s.y, tostring(ow.dv.floor(s.y)), tostring(s.moving),
					tostring(ow.dv.sat(px, pz, s.y, bits)))
				local L = {}
				for i = 1, 6 do
					local p = ow.dv.plat[i]
					L[#L+1] = string.format("lift%d save=%d flag=%d Y=%d (stops %d/%d) box x%d..%d z%d..%d ctl up(%d,%d) down(%d,%d)",
						i, s.sv[i], s.fl[i], ow.dv.paty(s, i), p[5], p[6], p[1], p[2], p[3], p[4], p[7], p[8], p[9], p[10])
				end
				for _, t in ipairs(L) do out[#out+1] = "  " .. t end
				local a, why = ow.dv.leg(px, pz, 15, 5, ow.dv.ground)
				out[#out+1] = "  plan to Clay: " .. (a and string.format("lift %d %s, walk to (%d,%d)",
					a.i, a.up and "up" or "down", a.x, a.z) or (why and ("refused: " .. why) or "ordinary walk"))
				local row = {}                                          -- the storey you are on, as tiles
				for z = 18, 27 do
					local r = {}
					for x = 6, 24 do r[#r+1] = ow.dv.walk(s, x, z, s.y) and ((x == px and z == pz) and "@" or ".") or "#" end
					row[#row+1] = string.format("  z%2d %s", z, table.concat(r))
				end
				out[#out+1] = "  walkable on this storey, OUR MODEL (x6..24, @ = you, # = wall or post):"
				for _, t in ipairs(row) do out[#out+1] = t end
				-- ⛔ AND THE SAME WINDOW STRAIGHT OUT OF THE GAME'S OWN CHUNKS, bypassing the model. The
				-- ROM's static land file for this zone describes only the bottom of the shaft, so either
				-- the gimmick PATCHES the live collision per storey — in which case the model is redundant
				-- and the ordinary reader was right all along — or it does not, and the model is the only
				-- thing that can answer. Printing both settles it in one keypress instead of a guess.
				local cs = pf_chunks(true)
				if cs then
					out[#out+1] = "  the same window RAW from the game's chunks (? = chunk not loaded):"
					for z = 18, 27 do
						local r = {}
						for x = 6, 24 do
							local v
							for _, c in ipairs(cs) do
								local lx, lz = (x - c.cx) + cs.K, (z - c.cz) + cs.K
								if lx >= 0 and lz >= 0 and lx < cs.w and lz < cs.w then
									-- ow.cand, not a hand-rolled 8-byte read: 'RD' chunks (Black City,
									-- White Forest) are 0x18 bytes with the tile at +0x14, and a dump
									-- that decodes those as noise is worse than no dump at all.
									v = ow.cand(cs, c, x, z)
								end
							end
							r[#r+1] = (v == nil) and "?" or (pf_walk(v) and ((x == px and z == pz) and "@" or ".") or "#")
						end
						out[#out+1] = string.format("  z%2d %s", z, table.concat(r))
					end
				end
			end
		end
		local sys = u32(0x02146284 + DELTA)          -- Black 0x02146264 (-0x20)
		if inram(sys) then
			local hdr = sys + 0x04
			local parent, raw1a = u16(hdr + 0x18), u16(hdr + 0x1a)
			out[#out+1] = string.format("parent town (+0x18): %d (%q)   name idx(+0x1a&0x3ff)=%d  disp=%d",
				parent, zone_name(parent), raw1a % 0x400, math.floor(raw1a / 0x400))
			local hx = {}
			for o = 0, 0x2e, 2 do hx[#hx+1] = string.format("%04x", u16(hdr + o)) end
			out[#out+1] = "zonedata header: " .. table.concat(hx, " ")
		else
			out[#out+1] = "zonedata header: (system not loaded)"
		end
		local E = evd_find(m)
		if E and inram(E) then
			local nW, pW = u16(E + 0x16), u32(E + 0x24)
			local dz = {}
			for i = 0, math.min(nW, 40) - 1 do dz[#dz+1] = u16(pW + i*0x14) end
			out[#out+1] = string.format("warps (%d): dest zones = %s", nW, table.concat(dz, ", "))
			local nA, pA = u16(E + 0x14), u32(E + 0x20)
			local md = {}
			for i = 0, math.min(nA, 40) - 1 do md[#md+1] = u16(pA + i*0x24 + 0x02) end
			out[#out+1] = string.format("static NPCs (%d): model ids = %s", nA, table.concat(md, ", "))
			out[#out+1] = string.format("signs/triggers: %d   furniture: %d", u16(E + 0x18), u16(E + 0x12))
			-- Quicksand pits, with what the LIVE collision says about the tile you would step on — so a
			-- dump taken beside one tells us whether the class we baked the whole rule on (0x7C) is
			-- really what the game has loaded there, not just what the ROM said offline.
			for _, q in ipairs(ow.PIT[m] or {}) do
				local cs2 = pf_chunks()
				local cx, cz = q[1] + (q[3] >> 1), q[2] + (q[4] >> 1)   -- the engine's guaranteed fall point
				local function tinf(x, z)
					local p = cs2 and pf_tile(cs2, x, z)
					if not p then return "not loaded" end
					return string.format("class=0x%02X walk=%s h=%s", p % 0x10000, tostring(pf_walk(p)),
						tostring(ow.height(cs2, x, z)))
				end
				out[#out+1] = string.format("QUICKSAND pit (%d,%d) %dx%d -> zone %d (%s) | rim(%d,%d) %s | CENTRE(%d,%d) %s",
					q[1], q[2], q[3], q[4], q[5], transition_name(q[5]),
					q[1], q[2], tinf(q[1], q[2]), cx, cz, tinf(cx, cz))
			end
			-- Are you standing on sand right now, and what does the engine think of the step you just took?
			-- This is the line that answers the open question about walking versus being dragged.
			do
				local cs2 = pf_chunks()
				local px2, pz2 = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
				local p = cs2 and pf_tile(cs2, px2, pz2)
				if p and p % 0x10000 == 0x7C then
					out[#out+1] = string.format("YOU ARE ON QUICKSAND at (%d,%d): h=%s  forced=%s  moving=%s",
						px2, pz2, tostring(ow.height(cs2, px2, pz2)), tostring(ow.forced()), tostring(ow.moving()))
				end
			end
		else
			out[#out+1] = "events: (EventDataSystem not found this frame)"
		end
		-- LIVE actors (the FieldActorManager) — full identity per spawned actor (handy for pinning obstacle/sign
		-- models we haven't mined yet). model@+0xC (110/210/216=item), EvType@+0x10 (1=trainer), SpawnFlag@+0x12,
		-- SCRID@+0x14, MoveCode@+0xE; pos = +0x3c/+0x40. Stand next to a thing and press N: the "1 east" actor is it.
		local gd = find_gd()
		local mgr = gd and u32(gd + 0x1A8)
		if mgr and inram(mgr) then
			local arr = u32(mgr + 0x18)
			local cap = u16(mgr + 0x04); if cap < 1 or cap > 64 then cap = 64 end
			local px, py = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
			out[#out+1] = string.format("LIVE actors (manager=%08X cap=%d objCnt=%d array=%08X):", mgr, cap, u16(mgr+0x06), arr)
			if inram(arr) then
				for i = 0, cap - 1 do
					local o = arr + i * 0x100
					local st = u32(o)
					if st % 2 == 1 then
						-- ⛔ IN A RAIL ZONE THE GRID TILE PAIR IS UNSET, AND THIS DUMP WAS PRINTING IT.
						-- +0x3c/+0x40 is the cached GRID tile; on Victory Road it reads (0,0) for every
						-- actor, so Ola's dump showed nine NPCs all "43 N 42 W" and looked as though the
						-- game had lost them. live_actors() has always read the world VecFx32 at +0x44/
						-- +0x4C there (the pair the engine actually maintains), so the NAV LIST was right
						-- and only the instrument was lying -- the worst kind of bug in a diagnostic,
						-- because it sends the next investigation somewhere there is nothing wrong.
						-- Print what the nav list uses, and the actor's own rail position beside it.
						local rcA = rail.sys()
						local x, z = u16(o+0x3c), u16(o+0x40)
						local arl
						if rcA then
							x, z = rail.wtile(u32(o + 0x44)), rail.wtile(u32(o + 0x4C))
							arl = rail.actor_pos(rcA, o)
						end
						local de, dn = x - px, z - py
						local dir = ((dn~=0 and math.abs(dn).." "..(dn>0 and "S" or "N") or "")
							.. ((dn~=0 and de~=0) and " " or "")
							.. (de~=0 and math.abs(de).." "..(de>0 and "E" or "W") or ""))
						-- the EXACT name the nav list computes for this actor (so a mislabel is self-diagnosing:
						-- press N next to the wrong NPC and this line shows its scrid+model AND why it got that name)
						local scr, model, evt = u16(o+0x14), u16(o+0x0C), u16(o+0x10)
						local sty = STORY_NPC[m]
						local zr = ZONE_ROLE[m]
						-- ⚠ ow.CHAR_MODEL FIRST, exactly as live_actors does it. An instrument that computes the
						-- name a different way stops being an instrument the moment the two disagree.
						-- ⚠ AND HM_MODELS BEFORE EVERYTHING, because that is the first test live_actors makes.
						-- Leaving it out is how a Strength boulder printed as NAME="Person" in Ola's own
						-- Mistralton Cave dump while the nav list was about to be taught to call it a rock.
						-- ⚠ AND IT SAYS WHETHER THE LIST WILL SHOW IT. Since obstacles are now held back
						-- until the HM is owned (ow.can_clear), a dump that only printed the name would
						-- leave "the boulder is right there and the list is empty" unexplained — the same
						-- reason the DARK line above prints in lit rooms too.
						local nm = (HM_MODELS[model] and ("[HM] " .. HM_MODELS[model]
								.. (ow.can_clear(model) and "" or " — HIDDEN, HM not in the bag")))
							or (evt == 1) and trainer_label(scr)
							or (ow.CHAR_MODEL[model] and MODEL_NPC[model])
							or ((sty and sty[scr]) or ROLE_SCRIPTS[scr] or (zr and zr[scr])
							or (ZONE_MODEL_NPC[m] and ZONE_MODEL_NPC[m][model])
							or (OBJ_MODELS[model] and ("[object] " .. OBJ_MODELS[model]))
							or (PROP_MODELS[model] and "[prop, skipped]") or MODEL_NPC[model] or "Person")
						local az = u16(o+0x0A)                          -- owning zone (nav lists only az==zone)
						-- flags/footprint/gridY are the ENGINE's own collision inputs (see ow.aocc): it blocks
						-- on bit 0 set + bit 0x80 clear, over the w x h box, when the grid Ys match. Printed
						-- because the Chargestone bug was invisible without them — the actor standing in
						-- Ola's way looked identical to eleven others in this list.
						out[#out+1] = string.format(
							"  slot %2d  zone=%-3d model=%-4d evType=%-3d spawnFlag=%-3d script=%-5d moveCode=%-3d  pos(%d,%d) %s%s%s"
							.. "  flags=%08X%s wh=%dx%d y=%d  -> NAME=%q",
							i, az, model, evt, u16(o+0x12), scr, u16(o+0x0E),
							x, z, (dir == "") and "here" or dir, (math.floor(st/0x200)%2==1) and "  [HIDDEN]" or "",
							(az ~= m) and "  [OTHER ZONE, not listed]" or "",
							st, (math.floor(st/0x80)%2==1) and " [0x80 EXEMPT]" or "",
							math.max(u8(o+0x7C), 1), math.max(u8(o+0x7D), 1), ow.s16(o+0x3E), nm)
						if rcA then                                     -- rail zones: the exact position the
							out[#out] = out[#out] .. string.format(     -- nav list and the pocket filter use
								"  rail=%s", arl and string.format("L%d f%d s%+d piece=%s", arl.l, arl.f, arl.s,
								tostring(rail.pieces(rcA)[rail.pkey(arl.l, arl.f, arl.s)])) or "nil")
						end
						-- ⭐ WHY IS THIS ONE NOT IN THE NAV LIST? Ola, 2026-09-03, Pinwheel Forest: a Team
						-- Plasma standing THREE TILES EAST of him was missing from the list, and reproducing
						-- the shipped filter offline said it should be there — my model agreed with the live
						-- list on 24 of 28 actors and disagreed on exactly that one. Guessing at the 25th
						-- costs a test round each time, so make the dump say it: print the same five probes
						-- reach.filter makes (the actor's tile and its four neighbours) against the flood's
						-- own set, plus whether the model is one the list drops on sight. Between them those
						-- two answers separate "the filter cannot reach it" from "the list never offered it".
						local RS = reach.cache and reach.cache.set
						if RS then
							local hit, msk = nil, nil
							for _, dd in ipairs({ {0,0}, {1,0}, {-1,0}, {0,1}, {0,-1} }) do
								local mm = RS[(x + dd[1]) * 100000 + (z + dd[2])]
								if mm and (msk == nil or mm < msk) then hit, msk = dd, mm end
							end
							out[#out] = out[#out] .. string.format("  | flood=%s%s  listed-model=%s",
								hit and "REACHABLE" or "unreachable",
								hit and string.format("(via %+d%+d mask %d)", hit[1], hit[2], msk) or "",
								(PROP_MODELS[model] or OBJ_MODELS[model] or ITEM_MODELS[model]
								 or HM_MODELS[model]) and "no (dropped by model)" or "yes")
						end
					end
				end
			end
		else
			out[#out+1] = "LIVE actors: (manager not found this frame)"
		end
		-- LIVE COLLISION around the player, read from the ENGINE's loaded chunks (pf reader) — this sees any
		-- runtime land-data patches, unlike the static a/0/0/8 bake. Added 2026-07-22 to settle the Nacrene
		-- south-west exit question (the ROM file says that seam is walled; Ola walks it — the live grid decides).
		-- WHICH SPACE ARE WE IN? A zone can be grid, rail, or both at once (Castelia's Central Plaza), and
		-- which one is driving the player decides everything the pathfinder does. One line, so the answer
		-- is in the dump for any zone Ola ever pressed N in.
		do
			local now, static = ow.space()
			local rcd = rail.sys()
			local l, f, sd = rail.pos()
			local wx, wz
			if rcd and l then wx, wz = rail.world(rcd, l, f, sd) end
			local t = nil
			local csd = pf_chunks(true)
			if csd then t = pf_tile(csd, ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)) end
			out[#out+1] = string.format(
				"SPACE: zone is %s, driving you now = %s | railSys=%s lines=%s | railPos=%s | rail->world=%s"
				.. " | railPosValid=%s | gridUsable=%s | tileClass=%s | you=(%d,%d)",
				tostring(static), tostring(now), rcd and "yes" or "no", rcd and tostring(rcd.nl) or "-",
				l and string.format("line %d front %d side %d", l, f, sd) or "nil",
				wx and string.format("(%d,%d)", wx, wz) or "-",
				(rcd and rail.onrail(rcd)) and "yes" or "no", ow.grid() and "yes" or "no",
				t and string.format("%02X", t % 0x10000) or "-", ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y))
		end
		local cs = pf_chunks(true)                                     -- (true: show a MIXED area's grid too — the
		if cs then                                                     -- dump is a diagnostic, so show what is there)
			local px, py = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
			out[#out+1] = string.format(
				"LIVE COLLISION (for Claude, skip): 21 wide x 17 tall around (%d,%d); X=you, .=walk, #=wall, ^v<>=ledge, ?=not loaded", px, py)
			for dz = -8, 8 do
				local row = {}
				for dx = -10, 10 do
					local tx, tz = px + dx, py + dz
					local ch
					if tx == px and tz == py then ch = "X"
					else
						local p = pf_tile(cs, tx, tz)
						if not p then ch = "?"
						else
							local hop = PF_LEDGE_HOP[p % 0x10000]
							if hop then ch = (hop == "up" and "^") or (hop == "down" and "v") or (hop == "left" and "<") or ">"
							elseif pf_walk(p) then ch = "."
							else ch = "#" end
						end
					end
					row[#row+1] = ch
				end
				out[#out+1] = table.concat(row)
			end
			-- ⭐⭐ AND THE SAME WINDOW WITH THE FLOOD'S OWN ANSWER ON IT. Three rounds of this arc were
			-- spent reasoning about WHY the reachability set stops where it does; a picture of where it
			-- stops answers that in one press. Same 21x17 window, so the two maps line up row for row:
			-- `o` = the flood reached this tile freely, a digit = it reached it only through that gate
			-- mask, `-` = walkable ground the flood did NOT reach, `#` = not walkable anyway.
			-- The tile to look at is the first `-` next to an `o`: that edge is the one being refused.
			local RS = reach.cache and reach.cache.set
			if RS then
				out[#out+1] = string.format(
					"FLOOD REACH (same window): X=you, o=reached, digit=reached through that gate mask, "
					.. "-=walkable but NOT reached, #=wall     [%d tiles total]", (function()
						local c = 0; for _ in pairs(RS) do c = c + 1 end; return c end)())
				for dz = -8, 8 do
					local row = {}
					for dx = -10, 10 do
						local tx, tz = px + dx, py + dz
						local m = RS[tx * 100000 + tz]
						if tx == px and tz == py then row[#row+1] = "X"
						elseif m == 0 then row[#row+1] = "o"
						elseif m then row[#row+1] = (m < 10) and tostring(m) or "+"
						else row[#row+1] = pf_walk(pf_tile(cs, tx, tz)) and "-" or "#" end
					end
					out[#out+1] = table.concat(row)
				end
			end
		else
			out[#out+1] = "LIVE COLLISION: (chunks not readable this frame)"
		end
		-- ── REACHABILITY DIAGNOSTIC (2026-08-31) — EVERY ZONE, not just the one that broke ─────────────
		-- Ola on Route 1: "the reachability filter doesn't seem to work there ... the surf spot here isn't
		-- even being picked up". Offline the map is unambiguous — Route 1 is TWO land islands, the western
		-- beach (with the Route Gate to Route 17, three trainers and two item balls) has no land path to
		-- the main route, and the one way across is a Surf entry at (781, 707..711) pressing west. So the
		-- gate must not be offered from the main path and the Surf spot must be. Both are wrong in game,
		-- and there are FOUR independent layers that could do it: pf_chunks going nil, the flood
		-- overflowing (set=nil hides nothing), the MapMatrix read failing so the flood is not zone-limited,
		-- or the exit's chunk simply not being streamed, which makes the filter fail OPEN by design.
		-- Reasoning cannot separate those, and this project has paid for guessing at exactly this kind of
		-- fork before — so print all four at once and let the failing layer name itself in one keypress.
		-- ⚠ Deliberately NOT gated on a zone: the Chargestone block below already proved its worth and was
		-- unavailable here only because it was scoped to one cave.
		do
			out[#out+1] = "--- REACHABILITY ---"
			local zR = reg.zid()
			local pxR, pzR = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
			local nowS, statS = ow.space()
			local csR = pf_chunks()
			if not csR then
				out[#out+1] = string.format("pf_chunks = NIL (space now=%s static=%s)  <-- reach.for_ returns nil"
					.. " and the filter hides NOTHING", tostring(nowS), tostring(statS))
			else
				local cl = {}
				for _, c in ipairs(csR) do cl[#cl+1] = string.format("(%d,%d)", c.cx, c.cz) end
				out[#out+1] = string.format("pf_chunks = %d loaded (space now=%s static=%s), chunk centres %s",
					#csR, tostring(nowS), tostring(statS), table.concat(cl, " "))
				-- ⭐ WHAT EACH CHUNK'S CONTAINER ACTUALLY SAYS, because the whole two-layer read hangs off
				-- it and I have twice reasoned about it instead of looking. Ola, 2026-09-04: "can you dig
				-- into the actual game code instead of theorising?" In zone 155 the flood answers exactly
				-- the layer-0 number, so either these containers are not being recognised as 'GC' at
				-- runtime or the second offset is not where we read it. Print the raw header and the
				-- derived pointers and the question stops being a guess.
				for i, c in ipairs(csR) do
					local cont = c.cont
					out[#out+1] = string.format(
						"   chunk %d centre(%d,%d) cont=%s sig=%s sections=%s perm0=%s perm1=%s -> layer=%08X l2=%s",
						i, c.cx, c.cz,
						cont and string.format("%08X", cont) or "nil",
						cont and inram(cont) and string.format("%q", string.char(cont and u8(cont) or 0)
							.. string.char(cont and u8(cont + 1) or 0)) or "?",
						cont and inram(cont) and tostring(u16(cont + 2)) or "?",
						cont and inram(cont) and string.format("0x%X", u32(cont + 8)) or "?",
						cont and inram(cont) and string.format("0x%X", u32(cont + 0x0C)) or "?",
						c.layer, c.l2 and string.format("%08X", c.l2) or "NONE")
				end
			end
			-- the zone restriction: without a readable MapMatrix the flood is NOT limited to this zone and
			-- can run away over the seamless overworld until it hits the 6000-tile cap.
			local gR = find_gd()
			local mR = gR and u32(gR + 0x1B0)
			local okM = false
			if mR and inram(mR) then
				local wM, hM = u16(mR + 0x08), u16(mR + 0x0a)
				if wM >= 1 and hM >= 1 and wM <= 64 and hM <= 64 then
					local rM, cM = math.floor(pzR / 32), math.floor(pxR / 32)
					local zAt = (rM >= 0 and cM >= 0 and rM < hM and cM < wM)
						and reg.ez(u16(mR + 0x14 + (rM * wM + cM) * 2)) or nil   -- cells are RAW; resolve
					okM = (zAt == zR)
					out[#out+1] = string.format("MapMatrix %08X %dx%d, cell(%d,%d) zone=%s vs zid=%d -> %s",
						mR, wM, hM, cM, rM, tostring(zAt), zR,
						okM and "flood LIMITED to this zone" or "NOT limited  <-- flood can run away")
				else
					out[#out+1] = string.format("MapMatrix %08X has implausible %dx%d  <-- flood NOT limited", mR, wM, hM)
				end
			else
				out[#out+1] = "MapMatrix unreadable  <-- flood NOT limited to this zone"
			end
			local RR = reach.for_(zR, nil)
			if not RR then
				out[#out+1] = "reach.for_ = NIL  <-- the filter hides NOTHING"
			elseif not RR.set then
				out[#out+1] = "reach.for_ = overflowed (>6000 tiles), set=nil  <-- the filter hides NOTHING"
			else
				local c = 0
				for _ in pairs(RR.set) do c = c + 1 end
				out[#out+1] = string.format("reach.for_ = %d tiles reachable from (%d,%d)", c, pxR, pzR)
			end
			-- every warp record, judged the way reach.filter judges it, with the reason spelled out
			local ER = evd_find(zR)
			if ER and inram(ER) then
				local nWR, pWR = u16(ER + 0x16), u32(ER + 0x24)
				for i = 0, math.min(nWR, 16) - 1 do
					local r = pWR + i * 0x14
					if u16(r) ~= 0xFFFF and u16(r + 2) ~= 0xFFFF and u16(r + 6) == 0 then
						local wx, wz = math.floor(u16(r + 0x08) / 16), math.floor(u16(r + 0x0C) / 16)
						local best, allread = nil, true
						for _, d in ipairs({ { 0, 0 }, { 1, 0 }, { -1, 0 }, { 0, 1 }, { 0, -1 } }) do
							local k = (wx + d[1]) * 100000 + (wz + d[2])
							local mm = RR and RR.set and RR.set[k]
							if mm and (not best or mm < best) then best = mm end
							-- ⚠ csR may be nil and pf_tile indexes it unguarded (ow.dv.over does cs.dv), so this
							-- must not reach it — a diagnostic that crashes the dump is worse than no dump.
							if not (csR and pf_tile(csR, wx + d[1], wz + d[2])) then allread = false end
						end
						local verdict
						if not (RR and RR.set) then verdict = "KEPT (no flood)"
						elseif best then verdict = (best > 0) and ("KEPT, gated: " .. reach.wordstr(best)) or "KEPT (walkable)"
						elseif allread then verdict = "HIDDEN (unreachable, all 5 tiles readable)"
						else verdict = "KEPT (unreachable BUT a tile is not loaded -> fails open)" end
						out[#out+1] = string.format("  warp %d -> zone %d at (%d,%d) dist %d: %s",
							i, u16(r), wx, wz, math.abs(wx - pxR) + math.abs(wz - pzR), verdict)
					end
				end
			end
			-- ELITE FOUR HALL: the four "beaten" flags, and what the list therefore does with each door.
			-- Printed so one press of N confirms the flag->room mapping instead of Ola discovering it by
			-- standing in front of a door that is not listed.
			if zR == ow.E4_HALL then
				local gdE = find_gd()
				local ewE = gdE and u32(gdE + 0x1AC)
				out[#out+1] = string.format("--- ELITE FOUR HALL --- EventWork=%s  flag byte @+0x3A9 = %s",
					(ewE and inram(ewE)) and string.format("%08X", ewE) or "nil",
					(ewE and inram(ewE)) and string.format("0x%02X", u8(ewE + 0x3A9)) or "-")
				for _, dz in ipairs({ 140, 141, 142, 143 }) do
					local f = ow.E4_FLAG[dz]
					local v = ow.evflag(f)
					out[#out+1] = string.format("  room zone %d  flag 0x%04X = %s  -> %s",
						dz, f, tostring(v),
						(v == true) and "HIDDEN (beaten)" or (v == nil and "listed (flag unreadable)" or "listed"))
				end
			end
			-- and the Surf side: `any` reports what is THERE even when the progress gate is off, because a
			-- detector that goes quiet exactly when the feature is off explains nothing.
			local sp = reach.surf_spots(csR, pxR, pzR, true)
			out[#out+1] = string.format("ow.can_surf() = %s  (HM03 qty %s);  surf spots in range %d = %d",
				tostring(ow.can_surf()), tostring(ow.bag_qty(422, ow.BAG_TMHM)), reach.SURF_R, #sp)
			for _, e in ipairs(sp) do
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  surf entry (%d,%d) press %s, dist %d",
					e.x, e.z, tostring(e.sdir), math.abs(e.x - pxR) + math.abs(e.z - pzR))
			end
		end
		-- ── CHARGESTONE DIAGNOSTIC (2026-08-24) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		-- The nav list offered all five transitions from (21,42), where only three are reachable. The
		-- shipping ow.cs.reach, run offline against that exact frame, gives the RIGHT answer — so the fault
		-- is somewhere between the two, and there are six places it can be. Reasoning could not separate
		-- them; this prints every one, so the failing hop names itself in one keypress. Same medicine as the
		-- gym diagnostic below, and prompted by the same kind of blind alley.
		if ow.cs.here() then
			out[#out+1] = "--- CHARGESTONE DIAGNOSTIC ---"
			local g = find_gd()
			local z2 = reg.zid()
			local want = ow.cs.zone[z2]
			out[#out+1] = string.format("GameData=%s  gimmickID=%s (want %s)  initByte=%s",
				g and string.format("%08X", g) or "nil",
				g and tostring(u32(g + 0x5EC)) or "-", tostring(want),
				g and tostring(u8(g + 0x5F0)) or "-")
			local wk2 = g and u32(g + 0x5F4)
			local gs2 = u32(GAMESYS_PTR)
			out[#out+1] = string.format("work=%s  hdr.zone=%s(want %d)  hdr.user=%s(want %s)  hdr.field=%s(want %s)",
				wk2 and string.format("%08X", wk2) or "nil",
				(wk2 and inram(wk2)) and tostring(u32(wk2)) or "-", z2,
				(wk2 and inram(wk2)) and string.format("%08X", u32(wk2 + 4)) or "-",
				g and string.format("%08X", g + 0x5F0) or "-",
				(wk2 and inram(wk2)) and string.format("%08X", u32(wk2 + 8)) or "-",
				inram(gs2) and string.format("%08X", u32(gs2 + 0x20)) or "-")
			if wk2 and inram(wk2) then
				local seen2 = 0
				for i = 0, 11 do
					local a = u32(wk2 + 0x0C + 8 * i)
					if inram(a) then
						seen2 = seen2 + 1
						local st2 = u32(a)
						out[#out+1] = string.format(
							"  stone %2d @%08X uid=%04X flags=%08X pos(%d,%d) y=%d origin(%d,%d) oy=%d wh=%dx%d%s",
							i, a, u16(a + 0x08), st2, ow.s16(a + 0x3C), ow.s16(a + 0x40), ow.s16(a + 0x3E),
							ow.s16(a + 0x36), ow.s16(a + 0x3A), ow.s16(a + 0x38),
							u8(a + 0x7C), u8(a + 0x7D),
							(u16(a + 0x08) ~= 0x00FD and "  <-- WRONG UID" or "")
							.. (st2 % 2 ~= 1 and "  <-- NOT LIVE" or "")
							.. (math.floor(st2 / 0x80) % 2 == 1 and "  <-- 0x80 EXEMPT" or "")
							.. ((ow.s16(a + 0x36) ~= ow.s16(a + 0x3C)
							     or ow.s16(a + 0x3A) ~= ow.s16(a + 0x40)) and "  <-- MID-STEP" or ""))
					end
				end
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  stones readable: %d (need exactly %s)", seen2, tostring(ow.cs.n[z2]))
			end
			local sig2 = ow.cs.sig()
			out[#out+1] = "ow.cs.state() = " .. (ow.cs.state() and "OK" or "NIL  <-- everything below fails from here")
				.. "   sig=" .. (sig2 and ("\"" .. sig2 .. "\"") or "nil")
			local hard2, hardok2 = ow.aocc(nil, true)
			local hn = 0; for _ in pairs(hard2 or {}) do hn = hn + 1 end
			out[#out+1] = string.format("ow.aocc(nil,true): ok=%s  %d hard tiles", tostring(hardok2), hn)
			local cs3 = pf_chunks()
			out[#out+1] = "pf_chunks() (the reach filter's own gate) = " ..
				(cs3 and (#cs3 .. " chunks") or "NIL  <-- reach.for_ returns nil and the filter hides NOTHING")
			if cs3 and hardok2 then
				local U = ow.cs.reach(function(x, z) return pf_tile(cs3, x, z) end, pf_walk,
					hard2, ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y))
				local un = 0; for _ in pairs(U or {}) do un = un + 1 end
				out[#out+1] = "ow.cs.reach = " .. (U and (un .. " tiles") or "NIL  <-- no restriction applied")
				local E = evd_find(z2)
				if U and E and inram(E) then
					local nW, pW = u16(E + 0x16), u32(E + 0x24)
					for i = 0, math.min(nW, 12) - 1 do
						local r = pW + i * 0x14
						local wx, wz = math.floor(u16(r + 0x08) / 16), math.floor(u16(r + 0x0C) / 16)
						local hit = U[wx * 100000 + wz]
						for _, d in ipairs(ow.cs.D4) do
							if U[(wx + d[1]) * 100000 + (wz + d[2])] then hit = true end
						end
						out[#out+1] = string.format("  warp %d -> zone %d at (%d,%d): %s",
							i, u16(r), wx, wz, hit and "offered" or "NOT reachable")
					end
				end
			end
			local R2 = reach.for_(z2, nil)
			local rmsg
			if not R2 then rmsg = "NIL -- the filter then hides NOTHING"
			elseif not R2.set then rmsg = "set is nil, overflowed -- the filter then hides NOTHING"
			else
				local c = 0
				for _ in pairs(R2.set) do c = c + 1 end
				rmsg = c .. " tiles"
			end
			out[#out+1] = "reach.for_ = " .. rmsg
		end
		-- ── CASTELIA GYM DIAGNOSTIC (2026-08-18) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		-- Routing to Burgh answered "Can't reach". That can fail at four different layers and reasoning
		-- cannot separate them, so this prints all four at once. Press N in the gym and the failing layer
		-- names itself: unreadable variables, wrong wall state, tiles the engine has not loaded, or A*
		-- genuinely unable to reach a switch.
		if ow.gym.here() then
			out[#out+1] = "--- GYM DIAGNOSTIC ---"
			local g = find_gd()
			local ew = g and u32(g + 0x1AC)
			out[#out+1] = string.format("GameData=%s  EventWork(+0x1AC)=%s  inram=%s",
				g and string.format("%08X", g) or "nil",
				ew and string.format("%08X", ew) or "nil", tostring(ew and inram(ew)))
			local vs = {}
			for i = 0, 9 do
				local v = ow.gym.var(0x4000 + i)
				vs[#vs+1] = string.format("%X=%s", 0x4000 + i, v and tostring(v) or "nil")
			end
			out[#out+1] = "vars: " .. table.concat(vs, " ")
			local sh = ow.gym.shut()
			if not sh then
				out[#out+1] = "shut() = NIL  <-- this alone makes pf_build refuse; the variable read failed"
			else
				local l = {}
				for _, w in ipairs(ow.gym.walls) do
					local k = w[1] * 100000 + w[2]
					l[#l+1] = string.format("(%d,%d)=%s", w[1], w[2], sh[k] and "SHUT" or "open")
				end
				out[#out+1] = "walls: " .. table.concat(l, " ")
			end
			out[#out+1] = string.format("facing=%s", tostring(ow.facing()))
			local cs2 = pf_chunks()
			if not cs2 then
				out[#out+1] = "pf_chunks = NIL"
			else
				local cl = {}
				for _, c in ipairs(cs2) do cl[#cl+1] = string.format("(%d,%d)", c.cx, c.cz) end
				out[#out+1] = string.format("pf_chunks: %d loaded, w=%d K=%d, centres %s",
					#cs2, cs2.w, cs2.K, table.concat(cl, " "))
				local px2, pz2 = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
				local function probe(label, tx, tz)
					local p = pf_tile(cs2, tx, tz)
					local mv, rr = pf_astar(cs2, px2, pz2, tx, tz, nil)
					out[#out+1] = string.format("  %-22s (%2d,%2d) tile=%s walk=%s  astar_reached=%s moves=%s",
						label, tx, tz, p and "loaded" or "NOT LOADED", p and tostring(pf_walk(p)) or "-",
						tostring(rr), mv and tostring(#mv) or "nil")
				end
				probe("BURGH", 32, 4)
				for i, s in ipairs(ow.gym.switches) do probe("switch " .. i, s[1], s[2]) end
				for i, w in ipairs(ow.gym.walls) do probe("wall " .. i, w[1], w[2]) end
			end
		end
		-- ── WHY DID A ROUTE FAIL? LIVE REACHABILITY, WITH AND WITHOUT THE HEIGHT GATE ────────────────
		-- Ola in zone 227: "I started the path finder to 7F but it just said this is as close I can get
		-- you, but trying again continued it correctly ... I have never had that issue before."
		-- His route log said `term=exhausted` BOTH times, which means A* emptied its open set -- it saw
		-- everything it could reach and the goal was not in it. Plain walkability says that room is fully
		-- connected (139 tiles, checked against the ROM and matching his own collision dump row for row),
		-- so something in the STEP RULE is cutting the graph, and the only candidate that varies here is
		-- the height gate.
		-- ⛔ AND THIS MUST BE MEASURED WITH THE SHIPPED READER ON LIVE DATA, NOT REPRODUCED OFFLINE. My
		-- Python reproduction of ow.hrec disagrees with the running game on sloped tiles -- it returns
		-- -1540246 where the game returns 98154 for (12,35) -- so an offline answer about WHICH steps are
		-- refused cannot be trusted. That is the Opelucid lesson inverted: there the model was right and
		-- the reader wrong, here the reader is right and my model wrong, and either way the way to tell
		-- is to ask the thing that actually runs.
		-- Two floods from where the player stands, using pf_walk and ow.height exactly as A* does: one
		-- with the height gate, one without. If they differ, the gate is the cut, and the tiles it costs
		-- are named. Capped, because this also runs on 96-tile-wide outdoor maps.
		do
			local rcs = pf_chunks(true)
			if rcs then
				local rpx, rpz = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
				local rsy = ow.s32(A.map_x + 0x02)
				if not rsy or rsy > 0x400000 or rsy < -0x400000 then rsy = ow.height(rcs, rpx, rpz) end
				local function rflood(useh)
					local seen, q, head, n = { [rpx * 100000 + rpz] = true }, { { rpx, rpz, rsy } }, 1, 1
					while head <= #q and n < 4000 do
						local cur = q[head]; head = head + 1
						for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
							local nx, nz = cur[1] + d[2], cur[2] + d[3]
							local k = nx * 100000 + nz
							if not seen[k] then
								local tt = pf_tile(rcs, nx, nz, cur[3])
								local ny = ow.height(rcs, nx, nz, cur[3])
								local ok = pf_walk(tt)
								if ok and useh and ny and cur[3]
								   and (ny - cur[3] >= 0x14000 or cur[3] - ny >= 0x14000) then ok = false end
								if ok then
									seen[k] = true; n = n + 1
									q[#q + 1] = { nx, nz, ny or cur[3] }
								end
							end
						end
					end
					return n, seen
				end
				local nfree = rflood(false)
				local ngate, sgate = rflood(true)
				out[#out+1] = string.format(
					"REACHABLE from (%d,%d): walkable-only %d ; with the height gate %d%s   startY=%s",
					rpx, rpz, nfree, ngate,
					(ngate < nfree) and "   <-- THE HEIGHT GATE IS CUTTING THIS ROOM" or "",
					tostring(rsy))
				if pf and pf.gx then
					out[#out+1] = string.format("  active goal (%d,%d) inside the height-gated set: %s",
						pf.gx, pf.gz, tostring(sgate[pf.gx * 100000 + pf.gz] == true))
				end
				-- ⛔⛔ AND RUN A* ITSELF, IN THIS SAME FRAME. The flood above said 139 tiles reachable
				-- INCLUDING the goal, while A* had just answered NO ROUTE from the very same tile -- but
				-- those two ran in different frames, so the disagreement could be real or could be
				-- history. Measuring both in one press is the only way to tell, and if A* still fails
				-- while the flood succeeds then the fault is in A*'s own expansion, not in the map.
				-- ow.PFGOAL is set by pf_build whenever a route is attempted, so this replays the LAST
				-- goal asked for even after the route was dropped.
				if ow.PFGOAL then
					local gx0, gz0 = ow.PFGOAL[1], ow.PFGOAL[2]
					local ha, hok = ow.aocc(nil, true)
					local m1, r1, t1 = pf_astar(rcs, rpx, rpz, gx0, gz0, nil, nil)
					local m2, r2, t2 = pf_astar(rcs, rpx, rpz, gx0, gz0, hok and ha or nil, hok and ha or nil)
					out[#out+1] = string.format(
						"  A* REPLAY to (%d,%d) from here: no-occ %s (reached=%s term=%s, %s moves)"
						.. " | hard-occ %s (reached=%s term=%s)",
						gx0, gz0, m1 and "route" or "NO ROUTE", tostring(r1), tostring(t1),
						m1 and tostring(#m1) or "-",
						m2 and "route" or "NO ROUTE", tostring(r2), tostring(t2))
					out[#out+1] = string.format("  goal tile walkable=%s  startY=%s  goal in flood set=%s",
						tostring(pf_walk(pf_tile(rcs, gx0, gz0))), tostring(rsy),
						tostring(sgate[gx0 * 100000 + gz0] == true))
				end
			end
		end
		-- ── VICTORY ROAD DIAGNOSTIC (2026-08-28) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		-- The two things that can go wrong here are invisible from the outside and look identical to each
		-- other: the terrace filter silently not engaging (rail.isle_now returned nil, so the list keeps
		-- everything and he sees all eighteen entrances again), or engaging with the WRONG terrace (he
		-- sees somebody else's doors, or none). Both read as "the list is wrong"; only this tells them
		-- apart. Print the island we decided on, WHY if we could not, and the island of every entry we
		-- judged — so one press names the fault instead of costing a round.
		if ow.RAILISLE[reg.zid()] then
			local vrc = rail.sys()
			out[#out+1] = "--- VICTORY ROAD ---"
			if not vrc then
				out[#out+1] = "  no rail system this frame -> filter OFF, whole mountain listed"
			else
				local pl, pf_, ps = rail.pos()
				local on = rail.onrail(vrc)
				local isl = rail.isle_now(vrc)
				out[#out+1] = string.format(
					"  islands=%d  railPos=%s  onrail=%s  MY ISLAND=%s%s",
					vrc.nisle or -1,
					pl and string.format("line %d front %d side %d", pl, pf_, ps)
					   or "nil (not line-form / mid-junction)",
					on and "yes" or "NO (stale position: world cross-check failed)",
					tostring(isl), isl and "" or "   <-- FILTER OFF, everything listed")
				if isl then
					local n = 0
					for _, run in ipairs(ow.SLIDE[reg.zid()] or {}) do
						if vrc.isle[run[1][1]] == isl then n = n + 1 end
					end
					-- any_dis matters: a disabled line can move where a slide LANDS, so the names fall
					-- back to bare "Slide" when it is set. Printing it means a run of unnamed slides has
					-- a visible cause instead of looking like the naming broke.
					out[#out+1] = string.format("  slides on this island: %d   disabled lines: %s",
						n, vrc.any_dis and "YES (slide names suppressed)" or "none")
				end
				for _, e in ipairs((lists and lists.Transitions) or {}) do
					out[#out+1] = string.format("  listed: %-28s island=%s", e.label or e.name,
						tostring(e.warp and e.warp.l and vrc.isle[e.warp.l]))
				end
				for _, e in ipairs((lists and lists.Slides) or {}) do
					out[#out+1] = string.format("  slide : %-28s at (%d,%d) rail L%d f%d s%+d",
						e.label or e.name, e.x, e.z, e.rl.l, e.rl.f, e.rl.s)
				end
				-- Every slide of this island with the island it lands on, whether or not it made the
				-- list — so a slide that went missing can be told apart from one that was never there.
				for i, run in ipairs(ow.SLIDE[reg.zid()] or {}) do
					if vrc.isle[run[1][1]] == isl then
						out[#out+1] = string.format("  baked slide %2d: %d cells, lands on island %s (%sF)",
							i, #run, tostring(run.dest),
							tostring(ow.ISLE_FLOOR[reg.zid()]
							         and ow.ISLE_FLOOR[reg.zid()][run.dest]))
					end
				end
			end
		end
		-- ── ICE DIAGNOSTIC (2026-08-20) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		-- Ola, in Cold Storage: "it slides me back to the same spot I can't go ... I cannot move more left,
		-- and it wants me to move more left." Something the engine refuses is something our map calls
		-- possible, and no amount of reasoning here will say which tile — five attempts at exactly this
		-- shape of question failed on the party screens and ONE keypress settled it. So dump, from where he
		-- is standing, everything the router believed: the tile under him, and for each direction the raw
		-- tile, each of the three tests ow.slide makes, and the resting tile it predicts. Compare that with
		-- what actually happens when he presses the direction and the disagreement names itself.
		local ics = ow.grid()
		if ics then
			local px, pz = ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)
			local function tinfo(t)
				if not t then return "NOT LOADED" end
				return string.format("class=0x%02X flags=0x%04X walk=%s ice=%s",
					t % 0x10000, math.floor(t / 0x10000), tostring(pf_walk(t)), tostring(ow.ice(t)))
			end
			out[#out+1] = "--- ICE DIAGNOSTIC ---"
			-- ⛔ PRINT THE HEIGHTS, NOT JUST THE CLASSES. The whole Opelucid diagnosis turns on whether a
			-- step the model calls a 2.00-tile wall really is one, and class/flags cannot say — a tile can
			-- be perfectly walkable and still be refused on height. Without this the dump could not tell
			-- "the map disagrees with me" from "the map agrees and the room is genuinely stuck", which is
			-- exactly the question that cost Ola a test round.
			local pyy = ow.dg.here() and ow.dg.y0(ics, px, pz) or nil
			out[#out+1] = string.format("standing on (%d,%d): %s  height=%s", px, pz,
				tinfo(pf_tile(ics, px, pz)), tostring(ow.height(ics, px, pz, pyy)))
			for _, gg in ipairs({ {0,-1,"up"}, {0,1,"down"}, {-1,0,"left"}, {1,0,"right"} }) do
				local nx2, nz2 = px + gg[1], pz + gg[2]
				local hh = ow.height(ics, nx2, nz2, pyy)
				local hs = ow.hstep(ics, px, pz, nx2, nz2, pyy)
				out[#out+1] = string.format("   %-5s (%2d,%2d) height=%-9s hstep_ok=%-5s  %s",
					gg[3], nx2, nz2, tostring(hh), tostring(hs), tinfo(pf_tile(ics, nx2, nz2, pyy)))
			end
			out[#out+1] = string.format("ow.forced()=%s  ow.moving()=%s  facing=%s",
				tostring(ow.forced()), tostring(ow.moving()), tostring(ow.facing()))
			-- ⛔ OPELUCID GYM: PRINT THE THREE DRAGON RECORDS RAW, AND THE LIVE LINE SET BESIDE THEM.
			-- The whole model rests on the saved half and the live half agreeing at rest, so the dump has
			-- to show BOTH and let them be compared by eye — printing our derived answer alone would be a
			-- diagnostic that can only confirm what it already assumed. It is also the one number no
			-- amount of ROM reading could settle: which poses a real save actually starts the gym in.
			if ow.dg.here() then
				local dgb, dgwhy = ow.dg.busy()
				local st = ow.dg.state()
				out[#out+1] = string.format("ow.dg.busy()=%s%s   (true = a neck is mid-swing; nil = could not tell)",
					tostring(dgb), dgwhy and ("  reason=" .. dgwhy) or "")
				if st then
					for d = 0, 2 do
						local pr = ow.dg.neck[d][st.p[d]]
						out[#out+1] = string.format("  dragon %d: pose=%d  flags={%d,%d}  neck enables %s",
							d, st.p[d], st.f[d][0], st.f[d][1],
							pr and ("lines " .. pr[1] .. "+" .. pr[2]) or "NOTHING (pose 1)")
					end
					out[#out+1] = "  settled=" .. tostring(st.settled) ..
						"   (false = the flag has flipped but the pose has not yet followed)"
				else
					out[#out+1] = "  ow.dg.state() = nil — gimmick id not 10, a field out of range, or GameData unreadable"
				end
				local rcd = rail.sys()
				if rcd then
					local dis = {}
					for i = 0, rcd.nl - 1 do if rcd.dis[i] then dis[#dis+1] = i end end
					out[#out+1] = "  live disabled lines: " .. (table.concat(dis, ",") ~= "" and table.concat(dis, ",") or "(none)")
				else
					out[#out+1] = "  live disabled lines: rail.sys() is nil"
				end
				-- ⛔ PRINT THE LIVE HEIGHT BESIDE THE BAKED ONE. The offline harness feeds the planner heights
				-- decoded in Python, so it structurally CANNOT catch a disagreement between that decode and
				-- main.lua's own ow.hrec — and if the two ever diverged, ow.dg.at would reject every tile and
				-- the gym would go quiet with no reason given. Printing both turns that invisible failure
				-- into one line of the dump. They should match to within a unit or two.
				for _, s in ipairs(ow.dg.sw) do
					local lh = ow.height(ics, s[1], s[2], s[6])
					out[#out+1] = string.format("  switch (%2d,%2d) dragon %d side %d: baked h=%d live h=%s%s  reachable now = %s",
						s[1], s[2], s[3], s[4], s[6], tostring(lh),
						(lh and math.abs(lh - s[6]) >= 0x8000) and "  ** DISAGREES WITH THE BAKE **" or "",
						tostring(ow.dg.at(ow.dg.flood(ics, function(x, z, y) return pf_tile(ics, x, z, y) end,
						                     pf_walk, reach.edge_ok, px, pz,
						                     ow.dg.y0(ics, px, pz)), { s[1], s[2], s[6] })))
				end
			end
			if ow.ig.here() then
				local igb, igwhy = ow.ig.busy()
				out[#out+1] = string.format("ow.ig.busy()=%s%s   (true = an arm is mid-swing; nil = could not tell)",
					tostring(igb), igwhy and ("  reason=" .. igwhy) or "")
				local st = ow.ig.state()
				out[#out+1] = string.format("ow.ig.state()=%s   var 0x%04X=%s (selok=%s)  var 0x%04X=%s (swok=%s)",
					st and ("{" .. st.w[1] .. "," .. st.w[2] .. "," .. st.w[3] .. "}") or "nil",
					ow.ig.SELVAR, tostring(ow.gym.var(ow.ig.SELVAR)), tostring(st and st.selok),
					ow.ig.SWVAR, tostring(ow.gym.var(ow.ig.SWVAR)), tostring(st and st.swok))
				-- ⭐ EVERY SELECTOR, ITS REQUIRED ENTRY DIRECTION, AND WHETHER THE MECHANISM ARMS ALLOW IT.
				-- Designed for exactly the frame Ola dumps from: standing on one that will not fire, the
				-- line that says WHY is the one with YOU ARE HERE on it.
				out[#out+1] = "  selectors: tile | enter moving | mechanism gate | live now?"
				for _, se in ipairs(ow.ig.sel) do
					local live = st and (st.w[se[3]] == se[4]) and st.selok
					out[#out+1] = string.format("    (%2d,%2d) sel %d  enter moving %-5s  needs word%d==%d (now %s)  live=%s%s",
						se[1], se[2], se[8], se[7], se[3], se[4],
						st and tostring(st.w[se[3]]) or "?", tostring(live and true or false),
						(se[1] == px and se[2] == pz) and "   <== YOU ARE HERE" or "")
				end
				for i, sw in ipairs(ow.ig.sw) do
					out[#out+1] = string.format("    (%2d,%2d) switch %d  enter from any side  toggles word%d%s",
						sw[1], sw[2], i, sw[3],
						(sw[1] == px and sw[2] == pz) and "   <== YOU ARE HERE" or "")
				end
			end
			local occ = pf_occupied(-1)                        -- every actor; -1 can never be a real tile key
			local dhard = pf_occupied(-1, true)                -- only the ones that never step aside
			for _, d in ipairs(PF_DIRS) do
				local dir, dx, dz = d[1], d[2], d[3]
				local nx, nz = px + dx, pz + dz
				local here, tt = pf_tile(ics, px, pz), pf_tile(ics, nx, nz)
				local ex, ez, n = ow.slide(ics, px, pz, dir, dx, dz, nil, dhard)
				local exo, ezo, no = ow.slide(ics, px, pz, dir, dx, dz, occ, dhard)
				local hop = tt and PF_LEDGE_HOP[tt % 0x10000]
				local hopinfo = ""
				if hop then
					local lx, lz = px + 2 * dx, pz + 2 * dz
					hopinfo = string.format(" | LEDGE(%s) land(%d,%d) walk=%s hstep=%s", hop, lx, lz,
						tostring(pf_walk(pf_tile(ics, lx, lz))), tostring(ow.hstep(ics, px, pz, lx, lz)))
				end
				out[#out+1] = string.format(
					"  %-5s next(%d,%d) %s | edge_ok=%s | occupied=%s | slide-> %s | +people -> %s | hard=%s%s",
					dir, nx, nz, tinfo(tt), tostring(reach.edge_ok(here, tt, dir)),
					tostring(occ ~= nil and occ[nx * 100000 + nz] == true),
					ex and string.format("(%d,%d) %d tiles", ex, ez, n) or "NO MOVE",
					exo and string.format("(%d,%d) %d tiles", exo, ezo, no) or "NO MOVE",
					tostring(dhard ~= nil and dhard[nx * 100000 + nz] == true), hopinfo)
			end
			-- the live route, so a "recalculating" complaint can be matched against what it was aiming at
			if pf then
				local wp = {}
				for i = math.max(1, pf.pi - 1), math.min(#pf.tiles, pf.pi + 4) do
					wp[#wp+1] = string.format("%s[%d](%d,%d)", (i == pf.pi) and ">" or "", i,
						pf.tiles[i].x, pf.tiles[i].z)
				end
				out[#out+1] = string.format("route to %q: pi=%d/%d  next=%q  waypoints %s",
					pf.name or "?", pf.pi, #pf.tiles, pf.segAt[pf.pi] or "-", table.concat(wp, " "))
				out[#out+1] = string.format("  goal=(%s,%s) reached=%s  (best-effort means A* aimed at the "
					.. "closest tile it could get to, which is what a two-cycle usually is)",
					tostring(pf.gx), tostring(pf.gz), tostring(pf.reached))
			else
				out[#out+1] = "route: none active"
			end
			if #ow.PFLOG > 0 then
				out[#out+1] = "the last route builds, oldest first (start -> goal, how A* ended, first instruction):"
				for _, l in ipairs(ow.PFLOG) do out[#out+1] = "  " .. l end
			end
			-- ⛔ THIS LINE MUST COME FROM THE SAME PLACE THE ROUTER GETS IT. It used to be built from
			-- live_actors()'s model-classified lists, so after occupancy moved to the engine's own rule it
			-- was reporting a set nothing used any more — it showed Chargestone's stones as occupying
			-- nothing at all, and cost a round of diagnosis chasing that (2026-08-24). A diagnostic that
			-- does not read what the code reads is worse than no diagnostic.
			local occ2, occok2 = ow.aocc(-1)
			if occok2 then
				local who = {}
				for k in pairs(occ2 or {}) do
					who[#who+1] = string.format("(%d,%d)", math.floor(k / 100000), k % 100000)
				end
				table.sort(who)
				out[#out+1] = "actors occupying tiles (the router's own set): "
					.. (next(who) and table.concat(who, " ") or "none")
			else
				out[#out+1] = "actors occupying tiles: UNREADABLE -- pf_build refuses every route while this is so"
			end
			local pa = ow.pactor()
			out[#out+1] = string.format("player actor: %s  gridY=%s",
				pa and string.format("%08X", pa) or "nil", pa and tostring(ow.s16(pa + 0x3E)) or "-")
		end
		out[#out+1] = ""
		local f = io.open(DUMP_PATH, "a")
		if f then
			f:write(table.concat(out, "\n") .. "\n"); f:close()
			say("Dumped zone " .. m, false)            -- one brief confirmation; the data goes to the file
		else
			say("Dump failed: cannot write the file", false)
		end
	end
	-- ⛔ RIDING A COASTER IS ITS OWN CONTEXT, because during the ride the player is NOT free to walk —
	-- the gimmick drives their position, so can_walk() is false and the overworld handler above is gated
	-- OFF. That is exactly why the first build went silent in the car: there was nothing running to speak.
	-- One line, one job: say "down" the moment the car stops. Registered BEFORE the overworld module so it
	-- wins the context, which is harmless even if control turns out to be ours during the dwell — while the
	-- coaster has us there is nothing else worth saying anyway.
	-- ⚠ Its detect is deliberately narrow: one zone check, then the gimmick's own riding flag. It reads no
	-- field-wide global, so it cannot starve another module the way the starter-selection detect once
	-- starved the party reader (accessibility-docs/19). Everything registered before it is a title, gender,
	-- intro or Musical screen — none of which can be up while a coaster is carrying you.
	MODULES[#MODULES + 1] = {
		name = "coaster ride",
		detect = function() local s = ow.rc.here() and ow.rc.state() or nil; return (s and s.riding) or false end,
		on_exit = function() ow.rc.ph = nil end,
		update = function() ow.rc.exit_step() end,
	}
	MODULES[#MODULES + 1] = {
		name = "overworld",
		detect = overworld_active,
		on_exit = function() lists = nil                               -- rebuild fresh next time (positions move)
			ow.wait = NAME_SETTLE                                      -- gate closed / another screen took over:
			-- ⛔ RE-ANNOUNCE AFTER *ANY* INTERRUPTION, not just a battle (Ola 2026-08-18, Castelia Gym:
			-- "when a switch is pressed there is a little animation and a pause, you need to figure this out
			-- then tell me the next steps when those have ended right away").
			-- A gym switch script runs LockAll, swings the camera to the gate it opens, animates it, then
			-- ReleaseAll — several seconds during which this module is gated off. On the way back the player
			-- has not MOVED, so pf_update's "hasn't moved" test returned silently and you were left standing
			-- there with no instruction until you took a step. The same held for dialogue and every menu.
			-- Arming pf_reenter re-plans from where you stand and speaks the next turn on the first frame we
			-- have control again, which is also exactly right after a switch: a wall has just opened, so the
			-- fresh plan is genuinely shorter than the old one.
			if pf then pf_reenter = true end end,                      -- re-arm the queued name's countdown.
		update = function(pressed)                                     -- (battle interrupted a route -> re-announce)
			local m = reg.zid()
			if m ~= prev_map then                                   -- zone id changed: invalidate the nav cache, and
				local from = prev_map                               -- QUEUE the name (announce only when the place NAME
				prev_map = m; lists = nil; pf = nil                 -- changes, so a town's many sub-zones / interiors
				ow.gym.lastk, ow.gym.from = nil, nil                -- ⚠ codex-review: drop the honey-wall tracker's
				                                                    -- remembered tile on ANY zone change, so a stale
				                                                    -- position from a previous visit can never be
				                                                    -- read as a crossing when we come back.
				local nm = zone_name(m)                             -- don't repeat it).
				local area = zone_area[m]
				if area then
					-- CASTELIA (Ola's design, 2026-08-18). Its twelve outdoor zones all share one place name,
					-- so the plain rule above would say "Castelia City" once and then go silent for the whole
					-- city. Instead: the CITY name is spoken only when you ARRIVE among the streets from
					-- somewhere else — the bridge gate, or out of any door, since every Castelia interior is
					-- its own zone and is not in zone_area — and the street QUEUES behind it. Crossing from
					-- one street to another speaks the street alone. `zone_area[from]` is the whole test;
					-- `from` is nil on the very first frame after a load, which reads as "arrived from
					-- outside" and correctly gives you the full "Castelia City, <street>".
					if zone_area[from] then
						-- ⚠ codex-review 2026-08-18: if the "<city> then <street>" pair from an arrival is
						-- still WAITING (pend2 set), crossing another street before it is spoken must not
						-- throw the city away — correct which street it lands on and keep the city.
						if ow.pend2 then
							ow.pend2 = area; ow.parea = area; ow.wait = NAME_SETTLE
						elseif area ~= ow.parea then
							ow.parea = area; ow.pend = area; ow.pend2 = nil; ow.wait = NAME_SETTLE
						end
					else
						ow.parea = area; ow.pend = nm; ow.pend2 = area; ow.wait = NAME_SETTLE
					end
					-- ⚠ codex-review 2026-08-18: prev_name must keep meaning "the PLACE name last decided",
					-- never a street. Castelia's interiors are generic HOUSE zones whose zone_name is plain
					-- "Castelia City", so parking a street here made stepping into the Battle Company
					-- announce "Castelia City" where it had always, correctly, been silent.
					prev_name = nm
				else
					ow.parea = nil
					if nm ~= prev_name then
						prev_name = nm; ow.pend = nm; ow.pend2 = nil; ow.wait = NAME_SETTLE
					elseif ow.pend2 then
						-- Same place name as before, so the pending line still stands — but we have LEFT the
						-- streets (a door, mid-countdown), and its street half is now a lie. Drop just that:
						-- "Castelia City" is still true of the interior we are standing in. Fail closed.
						ow.pend2 = nil
					end
				end
			end
			-- SPEAK IT ONLY ONCE THE GATE HAS HELD SINCE QUEUING (Ola's design; corrected by codex-review
			-- 2026-07-31). The module goes live the instant you have control — so the nav list, the pathfinder
			-- and the coordinate keys are as responsive as ever — but the NAME waits and re-checks.
			-- WHY AT ALL: Ola's U-dump proved can_walk() is right in steady state (false all through the locked
			-- museum scene, true while roaming). What leaked was a TRANSIENT: stepping out of the museum there
			-- is a genuinely free moment before the cutscene grabs you, and we spoke into it.
			-- ⚠ WHY A DEDICATED COUNTDOWN rather than a global "frames the gate has been open": the review
			-- caught that a global streak is INHERITED. Cross a SEAMLESS outdoor boundary (BW's overworld is
			-- seamless — Nuvema into Route 1 never closes the gate) and the streak is already past the
			-- threshold, so the name fires instantly — straight back into the same bug if a scripted event
			-- triggers at that boundary, which is exactly where rivals tend to appear. The countdown is reset
			-- when the name is QUEUED and again in on_exit whenever this module stops being the active screen,
			-- so it always means "N consecutive open frames since we decided to say this".
			-- This update() only runs while the gate is open, so ticking here IS counting open frames.
			-- ⚠ NOTE ON N: Ola suggested 1-2. It is 8, because 8 is what the OLD gate used and the museum leak
			-- was never reported while that was in force — so the free window is under 8 frames. At 60fps that
			-- is about an eighth of a second, inaudible. One constant to change if it ever feels sluggish.
			if ow.pend then
				ow.wait = (ow.wait or NAME_SETTLE) - 1
				if ow.wait <= 0 then
					local nm, nm2 = ow.pend, ow.pend2
					ow.pend, ow.pend2 = nil, nil
					say(nm)
					if nm2 then say(nm2, false) end                  -- the street QUEUES behind the city name
				end
			end
			ow.gym.step()                                           -- Castelia Gym: "Through" / "Pushed back"
			ow.dv.step()                                            -- Driftveil Gym: the storey, when it changes
			ow.vr.step()                                            -- Victory Road: the terrace, when it changes
			ow.rc.exit_step()                                       -- Nimbasa Gym: "Down", the moment a car we
			                                                        -- are riding stops (also runs from the
			                                                        -- "coaster ride" module, for the frames
			                                                        -- where control is NOT ours; whichever
			                                                        -- context is live, the latch speaks once)
			if pressed["C"] then                                    -- coords: name the street too, where there is one
				local a = zone_area[m]
				local where = a and (zone_name(m) .. ", " .. a) or zone_name(m)
				say(string.format("%s. X %d, Y %d.", where, ow.s16(A.map_x), ow.s16(A.map_y)))
			end
			if pressed["N"] then building_dump(m) end   -- dev: append a building-curation record to the dump file
			pf_update()                                 -- pathfinder: drive the continuous turn-by-turn as the player walks
			if pressed["P"] then pf_start() end         -- p: pathfind to the selected nav item (press again on it = off)
			-- ⛔ IN A DARK CAVE THE LIST GOES QUIET TOO (Ola's answer B, questions_dark_caves.md Q2,
			-- 2026-09-01). I built only the pathfinder refusal and asked whether browsing should survive;
			-- his answer was that the whole overworld reader stands down until Flash is used, because a
			-- sighted player in there cannot see any of it either. So every nav key answers with the same
			-- one line, which doubles as the explanation for why nothing is being listed.
			-- ⚠ SCOPE, deliberately narrow to what he named ("no list, no routing"): the place-name
			-- announcement on entering the cave still speaks (you must be able to tell where you have
			-- arrived), C still gives your own coordinates (you know where you are standing whatever the
			-- light), and N still dumps (it is the instrument, and it prints the DARK line that explains
			-- this very state). Routing is refused in pf_start, which additionally lets a toggle-OFF
			-- through so a route can never become impossible to cancel.
			-- ⚠ Speaks only on a positive read of both halves — see ow.too_dark.
			-- ⚠ THE KEY TEST COMES FIRST, and the order is not cosmetic: `and` evaluates left to right, so
			-- putting ow.too_dark() on the left would run its pointer chain on EVERY FRAME in every zone in
			-- the game, in the one block whose whole design is "lazy build on first use; no per-frame work".
			if (pressed["O"] or pressed["I"] or pressed["J"] or pressed["K"] or pressed["L"])
				and ow.too_dark(m) then
				say("This area is too dark to see in.", true); return
			end
			-- Nav-list keys (lazy build on first use; no per-frame work). i/o = category, j/k/l = item.
			if pressed["O"] or pressed["I"] or pressed["J"] or pressed["K"] or pressed["L"] then
				local t0 = os.clock and os.clock()                     -- console-only lag tracer (Ola report 2026-07-22):
				local ok = refresh()                                   -- refresh() is the only variable-cost part of a nav
				if t0 then                                             -- press (evd scan fallback / live actors); a slow
					local ms = (os.clock() - t0) * 1000                -- press prints here, silent otherwise.
					if ms > 50 then dbg(string.format("nav: SLOW refresh %d ms", math.floor(ms + 0.5))) end
				end
				if not ok or #active == 0 then say("Nothing nearby", true); return end
				if not entered then                                    -- first nav key: land on the current category + item
					entered = true                                     -- (refresh() already sorted the active list)
					local l = cur_list()                               -- same split as switch_cat: this is the same
					say(cur_cat(), true)                               -- phrase from the same key press, so it follows
					if #l > 0 then say(item_line(l[1], 1, #l), false) end   -- the same interrupt/queue rule
				elseif pressed["O"] then switch_cat(1)
				elseif pressed["I"] then switch_cat(-1)
				elseif pressed["J"] then ii = ii - 1; announce_item(true)
				elseif pressed["L"] then ii = ii + 1; announce_item(true)
				elseif pressed["K"] then announce_item(true) end
			end
		end,
	}
end

-- 1d) NAME-ENTRY KEYBOARD — EVERY naming screen in the game, not just the intro (2026-09-01; Codex RE
-- dispatch, report-nameentry-everywhere.md, cross-validated by Claude before wiring). Ola: "it works in
-- the intro for your player, but not anywhere else."
-- ⭐ THE CAUSE WAS NOT THE OVERLAY. ov194 is one reusable naming APP with eight launch sites, and the
-- reader was wrong in two independent ways at once:
--   (a) THE GATE described ov88. It demanded the intro proc be the CurrentProc at EventState 3, so every
--       other launcher — the post-catch nickname, the Name Rater, Box rename, a hatched egg — failed at
--       the first hop. There is no single manager to swap in: the keyboard sits on the SUB manager for
--       script naming, on a private manager for five apps, and on ov88's for the intro. See naming_sub().
--   (b) THE THREE READ ADDRESSES WERE NEVER STATICS. 0x0224FA50 / 0x0224FBBC / 0x0224FBC0 sit in the
--       static band and were found by RAM search in June, which is why they looked like globals — but
--       Ghidra has ZERO xrefs to any of them. They are naming_sub +0x68 / +0x1D4 / +0x1D8 at the heap
--       address the INTRO's instance happens to get, so they would have read another screen's memory
--       even if the gate had let us through. Both halves had to be fixed for any of it to work.
-- The cursor is u32, not u8; the editor is inline in the 0x2d4 subsystem, so it never moves or is freed
-- while the proc is RUN; the prompt is now the game's own localized String (bank 81 by naming mode, with
-- the species already inserted for a nickname) instead of a hardcoded English "Your name?".
-- The page (UPPER/lower/Others/qwerty) is naming_sub+0x1FC. Geometry: 13x6 (UPPER/lower/Others)
-- or 11x4 (qwerty), with row 5 = the home row (page tabs + DELETE + OK). The keyboard is glyph-rendered
-- (no charcode grid in RAM), so labels are a HARDCODED layout. The UPPER + lower pages are fully mapped
-- (letters; the right-side comma/period/apostrophe/hyphen and the male/female signs; the digit row;
-- and the home row of page tabs + DELETE + OK). Still "row R, column C": the Others page and the qwerty
-- page (alternate layouts — map them if ever needed). Empty cells (row 2 cols 6-9, all of row 3, row 4
-- cols 10-12) read "blank"; col 10 = space (verified with Ola).
do
	local PAGES = { [0] = "UPPER", [1] = "lower", [2] = "Others", [3] = "qwerty" }
	local UP = {   -- UPPER keys by (row, col). Cols 0-9 = letters; col 10 = space; cols 11-12 = the
		           -- right-side keys (comma/period, apostrophe/hyphen, gender signs). Verified with Ola.
		[0] = { [0]="A",[1]="B",[2]="C",[3]="D",[4]="E",[5]="F",[6]="G",[7]="H",[8]="I",[9]="J",[10]="space",[11]="comma",[12]="period" },
		[1] = { [0]="K",[1]="L",[2]="M",[3]="N",[4]="O",[5]="P",[6]="Q",[7]="R",[8]="S",[9]="T",[10]="space",[11]="apostrophe",[12]="hyphen" },
		[2] = { [0]="U",[1]="V",[2]="W",[3]="X",[4]="Y",[5]="Z",[6]="blank",[7]="blank",[8]="blank",[9]="blank",[10]="space",[11]="male sign",[12]="female sign" },
	}
	local LO = {}   -- lower page = same positions, lower-cased (standard B/W layout)
	for r = 0, 2 do LO[r] = {}; for c, ch in pairs(UP[r]) do LO[r][c] = ch:lower() end end
	local DIGITS = {}                                       -- digit row (row 4): 0-9, then blanks 10-12
	for i = 0, 9 do DIGITS[i] = tostring(i) end
	for i = 10, 12 do DIGITS[i] = "blank" end
	local HOME = { [0]="UPPER",[1]="lower",[2]="Others",[3]="qwerty",[4]="DELETE",[5]="OK" }  -- row 5
	-- (page,row,col) -> label. Confirmed with Ola: row 4 = digits, row 5 = the home row, on the 13x6
	-- pages (UPPER/lower/Others). UPPER letters verified; lower derived by lower-casing. Returns
	-- "row R, column C" for anything not yet mapped (right-side keys, Others symbols, qwerty page).
	local function key_label(page, r, c)
		if page ~= 3 then                                  -- qwerty (page 3) is 11x4 — different layout
			if r == 5 and HOME[c] then return HOME[c] end
			if r == 4 and DIGITS[c] then return DIGITS[c] end
			if r == 3 then return "blank" end              -- the empty spacer row under the letters
		end
		local letters = (page == 0 and UP) or (page == 1 and LO) or nil
		if letters and letters[r] and letters[r][c] then return letters[r][c] end
		return "row " .. r .. ", column " .. c
	end
	-- ── THE GATE. ov194 IS A REUSABLE APP, SO THE GATE IS A BOUNDED PARENT DISPATCH ────────────────
	-- There is NO single manager the keyboard always lives on, so we test three shapes in order. Every
	-- one is a FIXED-ROOT pointer chain of two to four hops — no scan, and constant work per frame.
	--   1. the GameSystem SUB manager's CurrentProc IS ov194 — ov021's script command 0x0105 launches it
	--      straight onto that manager (ordinary "give a nickname", the Name Rater, and the passerby
	--      greeting/thanks/message fields);
	--   2. the SUB CurrentProc is one of five known APPS, each of which built a PRIVATE manager and put
	--      the keyboard on it — the table below is exactly those five, at their proven work offsets;
	--   3. the TOP manager's CurrentProc is the ov88 intro proc, private manager at work+0x30 — the
	--      intro "Your name?", i.e. the one screen this module used to handle. Unchanged.
	-- ⛔ THE OLD EventState==3 CHECK IS GONE ON PURPOSE. It described ov88's state machine, so it could
	-- never generalise; the final predicate below is strictly stronger anyway, because it proves the
	-- KEYBOARD PROC ITSELF is alive (vtable + overlay id + RUN) rather than proving its parent's mood.
	local NAME_PARENTS = {                          -- SUB parent vtable -> the work offset holding its manager
		[C.evbattleret_vtable] = 0x40,              -- ov092 event_battle_return.c: the POST-CATCH nickname
		[C.pdcreturn_vtable]   = 0x34,              -- ov227 event_pdc_return.c: gift/special-Pokémon nickname
		[C.boxapp_vtable]      = 0x04,              -- ov170 box2: "Box name?"
		[C.friendname_vtable]  = 0x3F8,             -- ov128 field app 8: "Your friend's name?"
		[C.egghatch_vtable]    = 0x88,              -- ov215 egg_demo: the hatchling's nickname
	}
	-- The naming subsystem of a LIVE ov194 proc, or nil. Everything here is a predicate the engine
	-- itself maintains, so a wrong address on either game gives SILENCE, never a wrong word:
	-- vtable + overlayId + RUN identify the proc (the banked GameProc rule: never identify a screen by
	-- buffer contents), and the bounds below are the keyboard's own invariants — the launcher-supplied
	-- max (7 player / 10 nickname / 8 box), the length that can never exceed it, the 0xFFFF terminator
	-- sitting exactly where that length says, the mode the launcher passed (0..7), and the page geometry
	-- FUN_021F258C installs (13x6 for UPPER/lower/Others, 11x4 for qwerty).
	local function ov194_sub(p)
		-- ⛔ `p and` IS LOAD-BEARING: both callers pass reg.walk(...), which returns **nil** when any hop
		-- fails, and inram(nil) is a Lua ERROR ("attempt to compare number with nil") that kills the whole
		-- script. That is exactly what happened to Ola on 2026-09-01: the moment a trainer battle ended,
		-- ov092 event_battle_return became the SUB CurrentProc, NAME_PARENTS matched it, its keyboard
		-- manager was legitimately EMPTY, and the tool died mid-session. Every reader went silent, which
		-- looked like a gating bug and is why the field messages after that battle said nothing.
		if not (p and inram(p)) then return nil end
		if u32(p) ~= C.name_child_vtable then return nil end         -- 0x021F55A0 (Black 0x021F5580)
		if u32(p + 0x04) ~= 194 or u32(p + 0x08) ~= 2 then return nil end   -- overlay 194, status RUN
		local ns = u32(p + 0x14); if not inram(ns) then return nil end      -- the 0x2d4 naming subsystem
		local args = u32(ns + 0x2C4)
		if not (inram(args) and u32(args) <= 7) then return nil end  -- the launcher's args + naming mode
		local max, len = u16(ns + 0x8A), u16(ns + 0x86)
		if max < 1 or max > 10 or len > max then return nil end
		if u16(ns + 0x68 + len * 2) ~= 0xFFFF then return nil end    -- the editor's own terminator
		local page = u32(ns + 0x1FC); if page > 3 then return nil end
		local col, row = u32(ns + 0x1D4), u32(ns + 0x1D8)
		if page == 3 then
			if col > 10 or row > 3 then return nil end
		elseif col > 12 or row > 5 then return nil end
		return ns
	end
	local function naming_sub()
		local gs = u32(GAMESYS_PTR)
		if inram(gs) then
			local sub = u32(gs + 0x08)                               -- the SUB proc manager (field apps)
			if inram(sub) then
				local cp = u32(sub + 0x08)
				local ns = ov194_sub(cp); if ns then return ns end    -- (1) launched straight onto SUB
				if inram(cp) and u32(cp + 0x08) == 2 then             -- (2) an app's PRIVATE manager
					local off = NAME_PARENTS[u32(cp)]
					if off then
						ns = ov194_sub(reg.walk(cp, 0x14, off, 0x08))
						if ns then return ns end
					end
				end
			end
		end
		local top = cur_proc()                                       -- (3) the ov88 intro keyboard
		if top and u32(top) == C.gender_vtable then
			return ov194_sub(reg.walk(top, 0x14, 0x30, 0x08))
		end
		return nil
	end
	-- The typed name, live. ⛔ This used to read a HARDCODED 0x0224FA50, and the cursor 0x0224FBC0 /
	-- 0x0224FBBC — three addresses that were never statics at all: they are this subsystem's +0x68 /
	-- +0x1D8 / +0x1D4 at the heap address the INTRO's instance happened to get. That is exactly why the
	-- keyboard only ever spoke in the intro. The editor is INLINE in the 0x2d4 subsystem, so it cannot
	-- move or be freed while the proc is RUN, and every launcher gets it for free.
	local function typed_name(ns)
		local out, len = {}, u16(ns + 0x86)
		for i = 0, len - 1 do out[#out + 1] = utf8c(u16(ns + 0x68 + i * 2)) end
		return table.concat(out)
	end
	local last_rc, last_name, last_page, landed, said_prompt, prompt_wait
	reg.naming_active = function() return naming_sub() ~= nil end   -- so the overworld gate can stand down
	MODULES[#MODULES + 1] = {
		name = "name entry",
		detect = function() return naming_sub() ~= nil end,
		on_enter = function()
			last_rc, last_name, last_page, landed, said_prompt, prompt_wait = nil, nil, nil, false, false, 0
		end,
		update = function()
			local ns = naming_sub(); if not ns then return end
			-- THE PROMPT IS THE GAME'S OWN, LIVE. ov194 Init expands bank 81 by the naming mode into the
			-- String at ns+0x24C — "Your name?", "<species>'s nickname?" (species already inserted),
			-- "Box name?", "Your friend's name?", the passerby lines. So this replaces the hardcoded
			-- English "Your name?" that used to be spoken on EVERY naming screen. Queued, and retried
			-- each frame until readable, so a not-yet-built String costs nothing but a frame.
			if not said_prompt then
				local prompt = reg.str_text(u32(ns + 0x24C), 0xFF)
				if prompt then
					say(prompt, false); said_prompt = true
				else
					-- ⛔ HOLD THE REST BACK WHILE WE WAIT — but not for ever (codex-review 2026-09-01).
					-- If the String is not built on the entry frame, announcing the name and the landed
					-- key first would put the prompt permanently out of order. Waiting fixes the order;
					-- the frame budget means a prompt that never becomes readable costs half a second of
					-- silence instead of silencing the whole keyboard.
					prompt_wait = prompt_wait + 1
					if prompt_wait < 30 then return end
					said_prompt = true
				end
			end
			local page = u32(ns + 0x1FC)
			if last_page ~= nil and page ~= last_page then
				say(PAGES[page] or ("page " .. page), false)   -- queue (the tab label, on-screen content)
			end
			last_page = page
			-- Auto announcements (the name/edit field, then the key you LAND on) QUEUE so they play in
			-- order without cutting each other; once you're navigating, each cursor move INTERRUPTS so
			-- fast moves collapse to the latest key.
			local nm = typed_name(ns)
			if nm ~= last_name then last_name = nm; say(#nm > 0 and nm or "blank", false) end
			local r, c = u32(ns + 0x1D8), u32(ns + 0x1D4)      -- row, column — u32, not u8
			local rc = r .. "," .. c
			if rc ~= last_rc then
				last_rc = rc
				say(key_label(page, r, c), landed)   -- entry: landed=false (queue); after: true (interrupt)
				landed = true
			end
		end,
	}
end

-- 1e) POST-CATCH POKÉDEX REGISTRATION + THE "GIVE A NICKNAME?" PROMPT — overlay **ov207**
-- ("zukan_toroku_graphic.c" + "zukan_nickname.c"), the two screens you land on after "Gotcha! X was
-- caught!" (Ola 2026-09-01: "it transitions to a poke dex entry page, this isn't speaking, after that it
-- transitions to the name question … that with the buttons also don't speak").
-- ⭐ IT IS NOT A BATTLE SCREEN, which is why no battle reader could ever have seen it. The battle
-- overlays are torn down first: ov092 "event_battle_return.c" — the FIELD event that runs after a battle
-- — creates ov207 as a child, and ov206 "zukan_info.c" (which draws the dex page) is loaded at
-- 0x021B9E00, i.e. straight ON TOP of where ov093 the battle overlay lives. They cannot coexist, so this
-- had to be reached from the field side.
-- THE CHAIN (fixed root, offsets only; the root is the SAME one the PC-box reader already uses, which is
-- how it was cross-checked — ov000 launches the box app through the identical FUN_02012444 call):
--   gs   = u32(GAMESYS_PTR)            -- 0x02146268 (Black 0x02146248)
--   mgr  = u32(gs + 0x08)              -- the SUB proc manager, where field apps and events live
--   par  = u32(mgr + 0x08)             -- CurrentProc: ov092 event_battle_return (or ov227 event_pdc_return)
--   p    = u32(u32(par + 0x14) + OFF) + 0x08   -- OFF = its PRIVATE child manager (ov092 0x40, ov227 0x34)
--   w    = u32(p + 0x14)               -- ov207's 0x34-byte work (GFL_ProcInitSubsystem, heap 0x6a)
--   w+0x10 = PHASE (0 = the dex page, 1 = the nickname flow; ov207 Init sets 1 directly when the species
--            was already registered, so the page is simply skipped and we never speak it)
--   w+0x14 = the phase's state ; w+0x20 = the ov206 zukan_info object ; w+0x24 = the zukan_nickname object
-- THE DEX PAGE. zukan_info+0x0C is the live species and +0x04 is the game's own "National Dex obtained"
-- flag — the page prints the NATIONAL number when it is set and the UNOVA number otherwise, and prints no
-- number at all when the Unova lookup returns 999. We reproduce exactly that, using the game's own table
-- (a/0/1/6 FILE 668, the one ARM9 FUN_0201AF9C loads, with 999 as its own DAT_0201AFEC sentinel).
-- Every other word on the page is the game's own text, baked by research/re/gen_dexpage.py from the
-- banks **ov206's own table names** (White 0x021BC1E4 / Black 0x021BC1C4): category 260, height 245,
-- weight 268, HT/WT labels 234[10]/[11], and the description — **bank 235 on White, 236 on Black**, which
-- is the version-exclusive dex entry and was MEASURED in each ROM's own copy of that table, never guessed.
-- ⛔ WE DO NOT READ THE PAGE'S OWN STRINGS. ov206 builds each one, draws it and FREES it in the same
-- function (String_Delete at 0x021BABA6), so a reader built on them would be reading dead memory — the
-- use-after-free trap this project has already been bitten by twice. The species id is the only live read.
-- THE NICKNAME PROMPT. zukan_nickname+0x2C is the game's own expanded String ("Give a nickname to the
-- caught <species>!" — bank 234[175], species inserted by the game), and it is re-created for the second
-- message, so reg.str_text's magic check is what keeps a freed one silent.
-- ⛔ WE SPEAK ON THE GAME'S OWN STATE EDGES, never on a content diff (the bag's shared-scratch lesson):
--   nick+0x3C is ov207's message machine — 0 idle, **1 = message 1 printing**, 2 = the buttons are up,
--   **3 = message 2** ("… was transferred to … in someone's PC!"), 4 = a hold, 5 = done. Its jump table
--   at 0x0220B766 is where those six states come from.
--   nick+0x4C is the button machine — 0 idle, 1 = open it, **2 = up and being polled**, 3 = answered.
-- THE BUTTONS are the SAME widget the battle command menu uses: ov207 calls FUN_02202078, which is a
-- sibling wrapper of the battle's own btlv_input ctor FUN_0220209C (both tail into FUN_022020C0), and
-- opens it in **mode 4** — two big buttons — with bank 233[0] "YES" and 233[1] "NO". The mode-4 renderer
-- FUN_02205360 draws params[0] at y=0x10 and params[1] at y=0x40, so **cursor 0 = YES, cursor 1 = NO**;
-- the handler agrees independently (poll result 0 takes the yes path at 0x0220BAA0).
-- ⛔ THE READY GATE IS THE GAME'S OWN — every clause is a test FUN_02202DE4 itself performs before it
-- will look at the cursor, decompiled for this screen rather than copied:
--   u32(nick+0x4C) == 2   ov207's own "the prompt is up and I am polling it" state (the SUBMENU hard rule:
--                         gate on the widget owner's accepting-input state, not on which screen is active)
--   u32(o+0x58) == 4      it really is in the two-button mode
--   bit0 of o+0x68 clear  FUN_0220272C SETS bit0 when it opens the widget, so this is exactly "the open
--                         animation has finished"; FUN_02202DE4 returns -1 while it is set
--   bit31 of o+0x68 clear its other early-out
--   (u16(u32(o+0x64)+0x118) >> 2) == 0    the window is not animating (FUN_02021EF4)
--   cursor < 2 and u8(o + 0x25A + cursor) == 1   the button is enabled, as FUN_02205360 marked it
-- ⚠ DELIBERATELY *NOT* the engage flag u8([o+0x274]) that the battle command menu uses. That flag is
-- mode-1 state ("the cursor is not on anything until your first press"); mode 4's open path (0x02202B32)
-- and its renderer never touch +0x274, so requiring it here would have meant permanent silence. Noted in
-- todo.md: module 4g's shift prompt DOES require it, and may be silent for that very reason.
do
